window.ATLAS_WORKS = [{"id":"plato-apology","title":"Apology","original":"Apologia Sokratous (Greek)","author":"plato","authorName":"Plato","year":-390,"approx":true,"form":"dialogue","length":"about 30 pages in English","level":1,"topics":["ethics","knowledge","life and death"],"summary":"Plato's version of the speech Socrates gave at his trial in Athens in 399 BCE, when he was charged with corrupting the young and not honouring the city's gods. Socrates explains that he questions people because the oracle at Delphi called him the wisest man, and he decided this could only mean he knew that he did not know. He refuses to stop doing philosophy, even to save his life.","why":"It is the classic picture of the philosopher as someone who follows an argument wherever it leads, whatever it costs. Its idea that a life without self-questioning is not worth living has shaped the whole Western idea of what philosophy is for.","inside":"Three speeches: the main defence, Socrates' proposal for his own penalty after the guilty verdict, and his final words to the jury about death. The oracle story comes early in the main defence.","start":"Read it straight through in one sitting; it is short, it needs no background, and it is the best first book of philosophy there is.","line":{"text":"the unexamined life is not worth living","where":"38a","tr":"Jowett"},"islands":["hemlock","agora"],"related":["plato-crito","plato-phaedo","plato-euthyphro"],"shelf":"greece","tags":["socratic method","socratic ignorance","trial of socrates","oracle at delphi","the unexamined life","wisdom","death","civil disobedience","ethics","virtue"]},{"id":"plato-crito","title":"Crito","original":"Kriton (Greek)","author":"plato","authorName":"Plato","year":-395,"approx":true,"form":"dialogue","length":"about 15 pages in English","level":1,"topics":["ethics","politics","society"],"summary":"Socrates' rich friend Crito visits him in prison and begs him to escape before his execution. Socrates argues that we must never do wrong, even in return for wrong, and that escaping would break an agreement he made with the laws of Athens by living there all his life.","why":"It is one of the earliest arguments about why citizens should obey the law, and an early form of the idea of a social contract. Readers still argue about whether it fits with the Apology, where Socrates says he would disobey an order to stop philosophising.","inside":"A short conversation in two parts. The best-known part is the closing speech, where Socrates imagines the Laws of Athens speaking to him.","start":"Read it right after the Apology, since it continues the same story and raises a question the Apology leaves open.","line":null,"islands":["hemlock"],"related":["plato-apology","plato-phaedo"],"shelf":"greece","tags":["social contract","political obligation","civil disobedience","rule of law","justice","trial of socrates","ethics","political philosophy","never return wrong for wrong"]},{"id":"plato-euthyphro","title":"Euthyphro","original":"Euthyphron (Greek)","author":"plato","authorName":"Plato","year":-395,"approx":true,"form":"dialogue","length":"about 20 pages in English","level":1,"topics":["ethics","religion","knowledge"],"summary":"On his way to court, Socrates meets Euthyphro, who is prosecuting his own father and is sure he knows what piety (being right with the gods) is. Socrates tests each of his definitions and shows that none works. The conversation ends with no answer.","why":"It contains the Euthyphro dilemma: is something good because the gods love it, or do the gods love it because it is good? Philosophers still use this question when they ask whether morality can depend on God.","inside":"A single short conversation that tries out about five definitions in turn. The dilemma comes about halfway through.","start":"Read the whole thing, paying close attention to the middle section where Socrates asks whether the pious is loved because it is pious.","line":null,"islands":["agora"],"related":["plato-apology","plato-meno"],"shelf":"greece","tags":["euthyphro dilemma","piety","divine command theory","socratic method","definition","ethics","philosophy of religion","god"]},{"id":"plato-meno","title":"Meno","original":"Menon (Greek)","author":"plato","authorName":"Plato","year":-385,"approx":true,"form":"dialogue","length":"about 40 pages in English","level":1,"topics":["knowledge","ethics","mind"],"summary":"Meno asks Socrates whether virtue can be taught. This leads to a puzzle: how can you search for something if you do not already know what it is? Socrates answers that learning is really recollection, remembering what the soul knew before birth, and he tries to show this by questioning an enslaved boy about geometry.","why":"Meno's puzzle about inquiry and the question of where knowledge comes from are still discussed. Near the end it also draws the famous line between knowledge and true belief, which became a starting point for the theory of knowledge.","inside":"Three movements: failed definitions of virtue, the geometry lesson, and a final discussion of whether virtue can be taught. The geometry lesson is the best-known part.","start":"Start from Meno's puzzle and read on through the geometry lesson; draw the squares yourself as you go.","line":null,"islands":["academy","agora"],"related":["plato-theaetetus","plato-phaedo","plato-euthyphro"],"shelf":"greece","tags":["meno's paradox","theory of recollection","virtue","can virtue be taught","slave boy geometry","innate ideas","epistemology","knowledge vs true belief","socratic method"]},{"id":"plato-gorgias","title":"Gorgias","original":"Gorgias (Greek)","author":"plato","authorName":"Plato","year":-387,"approx":true,"form":"dialogue","length":"about 120 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["ethics","politics","language"],"summary":"Socrates questions the famous teacher of speech-making Gorgias, then two younger men, about what rhetoric (the art of persuasion) is good for. He argues that it is better to suffer wrong than to do it. The sharpest opponent, Callicles, replies that by nature the strong should rule and take more, and that conventional morality is a trick of the weak.","why":"Callicles is one of the strongest challenges to morality in ancient writing, and later thinkers such as Nietzsche have been compared to him. The dialogue also starts a long debate about persuasion versus truth in public life.","inside":"Three conversations of growing heat, with Gorgias, Polus and Callicles, ending in a myth about judgement after death. The Callicles section is the most famous.","start":"Skim the Gorgias section, then read the Callicles section closely, since that is where the real fight is.","line":null,"islands":["sophists","agora"],"related":["plato-republic","plato-apology"],"shelf":"greece","tags":["rhetoric","might makes right","better to suffer wrong than do it","justice","hedonism","pleasure","conventional morality","afterlife","ethics","political philosophy"]},{"id":"plato-phaedo","title":"Phaedo","original":"Phaidon (Greek)","author":"plato","authorName":"Plato","year":-380,"approx":true,"form":"dialogue","length":"about 80 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["life and death","metaphysics","mind","religion"],"summary":"Set on the day Socrates dies, it shows him calmly arguing with friends that the soul is immortal and that a philosopher should not fear death. He gives several arguments, including that learning is recollection and that the soul is akin to the unchanging Forms. It ends with Socrates drinking the hemlock.","why":"It set the terms for the long Western debate about whether the mind or soul can exist without the body. Its death scene is one of the most widely read passages in ancient literature.","inside":"A frame story told by Phaedo, around a chain of arguments for immortality and two objections (from Simmias and Cebes), then a myth of the afterlife and the death scene. The final pages are the best known.","start":"Read the opening and the death scene at the end first, then go back for the arguments in between.","line":{"text":"Crito, I owe a cock to Asclepius; will you remember to pay the debt?","where":"118a","tr":"Jowett"},"islands":["hemlock","academy"],"related":["plato-apology","plato-crito","plato-meno"],"shelf":"greece","tags":["immortality","soul","death","theory of forms","theory of recollection","afterlife","death of socrates","mind-body dualism","philosophy as preparation for death","metaphysics"]},{"id":"plato-symposium","title":"Symposium","original":"Symposion (Greek)","author":"plato","authorName":"Plato","year":-380,"approx":true,"form":"dialogue","length":"about 70 pages in English","level":1,"topics":["beauty","ethics","self"],"summary":"At a dinner party, each guest gives a speech in praise of Eros, the god of love and desire. Socrates reports what a wise woman, Diotima, taught him: love is a longing for what we lack, and it can climb from the beauty of one body to beauty itself. Then the drunk Alcibiades bursts in and gives a speech about Socrates.","why":"It is the root of the idea of 'Platonic love' and one of the most influential texts on love and beauty ever written. It is also a fine piece of literature, funny and moving.","inside":"Seven speeches in a row. Aristophanes' myth of humans split in half and Diotima's 'ladder of love' are the best-known parts.","start":"Read Aristophanes' speech and then Socrates' report of Diotima; they offer two very different pictures of love to compare.","line":null,"islands":["academy"],"related":["plato-republic","plato-phaedo","plotinus-enneads"],"shelf":"greece","tags":["love","eros","beauty","ladder of love","aristophanes' myth","soulmates","platonic love","desire","theory of forms","aesthetics"]},{"id":"plato-republic","title":"Republic","original":"Politeia (Greek)","author":"plato","authorName":"Plato","year":-375,"approx":true,"form":"dialogue","length":"about 350 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["politics","ethics","knowledge","metaphysics"],"summary":"Socrates sets out to show that justice is worth having for its own sake, not just for its rewards. To find justice in a person he first designs an ideal city, ruled by philosophers, and argues that a just soul is one where reason rules over spirit and appetite. Along the way he presents his theory of the Forms, the unchanging realities behind the world we see.","why":"It is probably the most read work of Western philosophy, and it raised questions about justice, education, censorship and expert rule that political thought has argued about ever since. Critics such as Karl Popper have attacked it as a blueprint for authoritarian rule; defenders read it more as a study of the soul.","inside":"Ten books. Book I is a debate with Thrasymachus, Book II has the ring of Gyges, Book VII holds the allegory of the cave, and Book X ends with the myth of Er.","start":"Read Book I as a self-contained dialogue, then jump to the cave at the start of Book VII before going back to the middle.","line":null,"islands":["republic","academy"],"related":["plato-gorgias","aristotle-politics","plato-symposium"],"shelf":"greece","tags":["allegory of the cave","philosopher kings","theory of forms","justice","ring of gyges","tripartite soul","utopia","myth of er","ideal state","political philosophy","noble lie","education"]},{"id":"plato-theaetetus","title":"Theaetetus","original":"Theaitetos (Greek)","author":"plato","authorName":"Plato","year":-369,"approx":true,"form":"dialogue","length":"about 100 pages in English","level":3,"topics":["knowledge","mind","language"],"summary":"Socrates and the young mathematician Theaetetus try to say what knowledge is. They test three answers: knowledge is perception, knowledge is true belief, and knowledge is true belief with an account (an explanation). All three fail, and the dialogue ends without a definition.","why":"It is the founding text of epistemology, the theory of knowledge. Its third definition is close to the 'justified true belief' view that Edmund Gettier challenged in 1963, and its criticism of Protagoras' claim that 'man is the measure' is still the classic reply to relativism.","inside":"One long conversation in three parts, one per definition. Socrates' description of himself as an intellectual midwife comes near the start.","start":"Read the opening and the midwife passage, then the discussion of Protagoras, which is the most lively section.","line":null,"islands":["academy","sophists"],"related":["plato-meno","sextus-outlines-of-pyrrhonism"],"shelf":"greece","tags":["epistemology","knowledge","justified true belief","perception","relativism","man is the measure of all things","socratic midwife","definition of knowledge","socratic method"]},{"id":"aristotle-nicomachean-ethics","title":"Nicomachean Ethics","original":"Ethika Nikomacheia (Greek)","author":"aristotle","authorName":"Aristotle","year":-340,"approx":true,"form":"lectures","length":"about 300 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["ethics","self","society"],"summary":"Aristotle asks what the best life for a human being is, and answers: eudaimonia, usually translated 'happiness' or 'flourishing', which is a life of activity in line with virtue. Virtues of character, like courage, are settled habits that hit the mean between too much and too little, and they are learned by practice. Practical wisdom, friendship and the life of study all play their part.","why":"It is the founding text of virtue ethics, which judges actions by what a good person would do rather than by rules or results. Virtue ethics returned strongly in the 20th century, and many people still find Aristotle's picture of a good life the most down-to-earth.","inside":"Ten books, probably based on his lecture notes and perhaps named after his son Nicomachus. Book II explains virtue as a mean, Books VIII–IX are on friendship and Book X praises the life of contemplation.","start":"Start with Books I and II, which set out the whole plan and the idea of virtue as a mean.","line":{"text":"For one swallow does not make a summer, nor does one day; and so too one day, or a short time, does not make a man blessed and happy.","where":"Book I","tr":"Ross"},"islands":["nicomachean"],"related":["aristotle-politics","plato-republic","aristotle-de-anima"],"shelf":"greece","tags":["virtue ethics","eudaimonia","happiness","golden mean","practical wisdom","friendship","character","habit","contemplation","moral virtue","the good life","ethics"]},{"id":"aristotle-politics","title":"Politics","original":"Politika (Greek)","author":"aristotle","authorName":"Aristotle","year":-340,"approx":true,"form":"lectures","length":"about 300 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["politics","society","ethics"],"summary":"Aristotle studies the polis, the Greek city-state, and argues that humans are by nature creatures who live in political communities. He compares different constitutions, sorting them by whether one, a few or many rule and whether they rule for the common good. He favours a mixed constitution with a strong middle class.","why":"Its comparisons of constitutions started political science as a field, and its ideas about citizenship and the common good are still used. It also defends 'natural slavery' and the rule of men over women, which critics from ancient times to now have rejected, and readers must face these parts honestly.","inside":"Eight books. Book I covers the household and slavery, Book II criticises Plato's Republic, Book III defines the citizen and sorts constitutions, and Books VII–VIII describe an ideal city.","start":"Begin with Book I for the 'political animal' argument, then read Book III, the core of his theory of constitutions.","line":{"text":"Hence it is evident that the state is a creation of nature, and that man is by nature a political animal.","where":"Book I","tr":"Jowett"},"islands":["polis"],"related":["aristotle-nicomachean-ethics","plato-republic"],"shelf":"greece","tags":["political philosophy","man is a political animal","polis","constitutions","democracy","oligarchy","mixed constitution","citizenship","slavery","middle class","ideal state"]},{"id":"aristotle-metaphysics","title":"Metaphysics","original":"Ta meta ta physika (Greek)","author":"aristotle","authorName":"Aristotle","year":-340,"approx":true,"form":"treatise","length":"14 books, about 300 pages in English","level":3,"topics":["metaphysics","logic","religion","knowledge"],"summary":"Aristotle studies 'being as being': what it is for anything to exist at all. He asks what substance (the basic kind of thing that exists) is, defends the law of non-contradiction, and argues that the order of the cosmos depends on an unmoved mover, a first cause that moves things without itself changing.","why":"It gave the subject of metaphysics its name and many of its basic ideas, such as substance, form and matter, and actuality and potentiality. Medieval Islamic, Jewish and Christian thinkers built their own philosophies on it.","inside":"Fourteen books, labelled with Greek letters and put together by later editors, which is why the title only means 'the books after the Physics'. Book I opens with a history of earlier philosophy; Book XII (Lambda) has the unmoved mover.","start":"Read Book I, which is clear and gives a short history of the thinkers before Aristotle, before trying the harder books.","line":{"text":"All men by nature desire to know.","where":"Book I","tr":"Ross"},"islands":["lyceum"],"related":["aristotle-categories","aristotle-de-anima","parmenides-on-nature"],"shelf":"greece","tags":["metaphysics","being qua being","substance","unmoved mover","law of non-contradiction","four causes","form and matter","essence","first cause","potentiality and actuality","god"]},{"id":"aristotle-poetics","title":"Poetics","original":"Peri poietikes (Greek)","author":"aristotle","authorName":"Aristotle","year":-335,"approx":true,"form":"treatise","length":"about 50 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["beauty","mind","society"],"summary":"Aristotle explains poetry and especially tragedy as mimesis, the imitation of human action. He argues that plot matters most, that a good tragedy shows a person brought down by some error rather than by pure wickedness, and that it produces catharsis, a release or cleansing of pity and fear.","why":"It is the oldest surviving work of literary theory, and ideas such as unity of plot and the tragic flaw shaped European drama and criticism for centuries. Scholars still disagree about exactly what Aristotle meant by catharsis.","inside":"Twenty-six short chapters, mostly on tragedy. A second book, probably on comedy, is lost.","start":"Read chapters 6 to 14, where he defines tragedy and explains what makes a good plot.","line":null,"islands":["lyceum"],"related":["plato-republic","aristotle-nicomachean-ethics"],"shelf":"greece","tags":["tragedy","catharsis","mimesis","aesthetics","plot","tragic flaw","pity and fear","art","literary theory","poetry"]},{"id":"aristotle-de-anima","title":"On the Soul","original":"Peri psyches (Greek); De Anima (Latin)","author":"aristotle","authorName":"Aristotle","year":-335,"approx":true,"form":"treatise","length":"3 books, about 120 pages in English","level":3,"topics":["mind","science","metaphysics"],"summary":"Aristotle asks what makes living things alive. He defines the soul not as a separate ghost but as the form of a living body: its organisation and its powers to grow, perceive, move and think. Plants, animals and humans have different levels of these powers.","why":"It is the first systematic work on the mind. Some modern philosophers see its view as an early form of functionalism, the idea that the mind is defined by what it does, while others reject that reading.","inside":"Three books. Book I reviews earlier views, Book II gives his definition and discusses the senses, and Book III treats imagination and the intellect, including the much-debated 'active intellect'.","start":"Begin with the opening chapters of Book II, where he gives his own definition of the soul.","line":null,"islands":["lyceum"],"related":["plato-phaedo","aristotle-metaphysics"],"shelf":"greece","tags":["soul","philosophy of mind","hylomorphism","form and matter","perception","senses","imagination","intellect","active intellect","life"]},{"id":"aristotle-categories","title":"Categories","original":"Kategoriai (Greek)","author":"aristotle","authorName":"Aristotle","year":-350,"approx":true,"form":"treatise","length":"about 30 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["logic","metaphysics","language"],"summary":"A short work that sorts everything we can say about a thing into ten kinds, such as substance, quantity, quality, relation, place and time. It argues that individual things, like this man or this horse, are the most basic realities, which it calls primary substances.","why":"It became the first book of the Organon, Aristotle's logical works, and for over a thousand years it was one of the first texts students of philosophy read. Most scholars accept it as Aristotle's, though a few have doubted parts of it.","inside":"Fifteen short chapters. Chapters 4 and 5, which list the categories and explain substance, are the core.","start":"Read chapters 1 to 5; they are short and give the whole central idea.","line":null,"islands":["lyceum"],"related":["aristotle-metaphysics"],"shelf":"greece","tags":["categories","substance","primary substance","ontology","metaphysics","logic","predication","philosophy of language","essence"]},{"id":"parmenides-on-nature","title":"On Nature","original":"Peri physeos (Greek)","author":"parmenides","authorName":"Parmenides","year":-475,"approx":true,"form":"poem","length":"about 150 lines survive, a few pages in English","level":3,"topics":["metaphysics","logic","knowledge"],"summary":"In a poem in Greek verse, a goddess tells a young man that there are only two paths of inquiry: 'it is' and 'it is not'. Since what is not cannot even be thought, she argues that real being cannot come to be, change, move or be divided. The ordinary world of change is only appearance.","why":"It is the first sustained argument in Western philosophy, reasoning step by step from premises to shocking conclusions. Almost every later Greek thinker, from Plato to the atomists, had to answer it.","inside":"Survives only in fragments quoted by later writers. It has three parts: an opening journey to the goddess, the 'Way of Truth', and the 'Way of Opinion', of which little survives.","start":"Read the 'Way of Truth' fragments slowly with a commentary beside you, since the Greek is compressed and every translation is an interpretation.","line":null,"islands":["elea"],"related":["heraclitus-fragments","plato-theaetetus","aristotle-metaphysics"],"shelf":"greece","tags":["being","monism","appearance and reality","change is impossible","way of truth","nothing comes from nothing","metaphysics","pre-socratic philosophy","logic"]},{"id":"heraclitus-fragments","title":"Fragments","original":null,"author":"heraclitus","authorName":"Heraclitus","year":-500,"approx":true,"form":"fragments","length":"about 130 short fragments, a few pages","level":2,"topics":["metaphysics","knowledge","language"],"summary":"Short, riddling sayings that survive from a lost book. Heraclitus claims that everything is in constant change, that opposites such as day and night are bound together, and that a single logos (an underlying order or account) governs the world, even though most people fail to grasp it.","why":"He is the classic thinker of change and conflict, the opposite pole to Parmenides. Plato, the Stoics, Hegel and Nietzsche all drew on him.","inside":"No original order survives; editors number the fragments differently, most often in the Diels-Kranz system. The river sayings are the best known.","start":"Read a modern edition that groups the fragments by theme, and take a few at a time.","line":{"text":"You cannot step twice into the same rivers; for fresh waters are ever flowing in upon you.","where":"Fragment 41 (Burnet's numbering)","tr":"Burnet"},"islands":["elea"],"related":["parmenides-on-nature","marcus-aurelius-meditations"],"shelf":"greece","tags":["flux","change","unity of opposites","logos","you cannot step into the same river twice","fire","pre-socratic philosophy","metaphysics","becoming"]},{"id":"epicurus-letter-to-menoeceus","title":"Letter to Menoeceus","original":"Epistole pros Menoikea (Greek)","author":"epicurus","authorName":"Epicurus","year":-300,"approx":true,"form":"letters","length":"about 5 pages in English","level":1,"topics":["ethics","life and death","religion"],"summary":"A short summary of Epicurus' advice for a happy life. The gods are not to be feared, death is nothing to us because when death is here we are not, and pleasure is the goal of life. But by pleasure he means mainly the absence of pain in the body and of trouble in the mind, reached through a simple life, friendship and clear thinking.","why":"It is the clearest surviving statement of Epicureanism in Epicurus' own words, and its argument that death cannot harm us is still debated. It also shows that 'epicurean' originally meant something close to the opposite of luxury.","inside":"One letter, preserved by Diogenes Laertius in Book X of his Lives of the Eminent Philosophers. It moves from the gods to death to desire and pleasure.","start":"Read the whole letter in one go, then reread the paragraph on death and ask whether the argument works.","line":null,"islands":["garden"],"related":["lucretius-on-the-nature-of-things","seneca-letters-to-lucilius"],"shelf":"greece-rome","tags":["epicureanism","death is nothing to us","pleasure","hedonism","ataraxia","happiness","fear of death","gods","desire","simple living","friendship"]},{"id":"lucretius-on-the-nature-of-things","title":"On the Nature of Things","original":"De rerum natura (Latin)","author":"lucretius","authorName":"Lucretius","year":-55,"approx":true,"form":"poem","length":"6 books, about 7,400 lines of verse","level":2,"topics":["science","life and death","religion","mind"],"summary":"A long Latin poem that explains Epicurus' philosophy. Everything is made of atoms moving in empty space, the world was not made by gods, and the soul is made of atoms too and dies with the body. Lucretius argues that understanding this frees us from fear of the gods and of death.","why":"It is the fullest surviving account of ancient atomism. A copy was rediscovered in 1417, and the poem influenced Renaissance and early modern thinkers interested in a natural, godless explanation of the world.","inside":"Six books on atoms, the soul, perception, the world, and natural events, apparently unfinished. Book III, on why death is nothing to fear, is the best known.","start":"Start with Book III; it stands on its own and carries the poem's main message.","line":null,"islands":["garden"],"related":["epicurus-letter-to-menoeceus"],"shelf":"greece-rome","tags":["atomism","epicureanism","materialism","fear of death","death is nothing to us","the swerve","free will","mortality of the soul","religion","philosophy of nature","void"]},{"id":"cicero-on-duties","title":"On Duties","original":"De officiis (Latin)","author":"cicero","authorName":"Marcus Tullius Cicero","year":-44,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"3 books, about 200 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["ethics","politics","society"],"summary":"Written as advice to his son, Cicero sets out how a good person should act in public and private life. He discusses what is honourable, what is useful, and what to do when the two seem to clash, arguing that nothing truly useful can be dishonourable. He draws heavily on the Stoic Panaetius.","why":"It was one of the most read books of moral philosophy in Europe from the Middle Ages to the 18th century, and shaped ideas of public service and the duties of leaders. Cicero wrote it in the months after Julius Caesar's murder, shortly before his own death.","inside":"Three books: on the honourable, on the useful, and on conflicts between them. Book III, with its hard cases, is the liveliest.","start":"Begin with Book III, where Cicero tests his principles on tricky examples about honesty in trade and keeping promises.","line":null,"islands":["stoa"],"related":["seneca-letters-to-lucilius","aristotle-nicomachean-ethics"],"shelf":"greece-rome","tags":["duty","stoicism","the honourable and the useful","moral dilemmas","virtue","justice","public life","ethics","political philosophy","natural law"]},{"id":"seneca-letters-to-lucilius","title":"Letters to Lucilius","original":"Epistulae morales ad Lucilium (Latin)","author":"seneca","authorName":"Seneca","year":64,"approx":true,"form":"letters","length":"124 letters, about 500 pages in English","level":1,"topics":["ethics","life and death","self"],"summary":"In letters to his friend Lucilius, written near the end of his life, Seneca gives practical Stoic advice: how to use time well, face death and loss calmly, choose friends, and focus on what is within our control. Each letter usually starts from an everyday event and ends with a lesson.","why":"They are among the most readable works of ancient philosophy and a main reason Stoicism is popular today. Montaigne and many later essayists learned from their style.","inside":"124 letters grouped in 20 books, written c. 62–65 CE. The first letter, on taking control of one's time, sets the tone.","start":"Read the first dozen letters in order; they are short and need no background.","line":null,"islands":["stoa"],"related":["seneca-on-the-shortness-of-life","epictetus-handbook","marcus-aurelius-meditations"],"shelf":"greece-rome","tags":["stoicism","time","death","friendship","dichotomy of control","adversity","practical wisdom","the good life","virtue","ethics"]},{"id":"seneca-on-the-shortness-of-life","title":"On the Shortness of Life","original":"De brevitate vitae (Latin)","author":"seneca","authorName":"Seneca","year":49,"approx":true,"form":"essays","length":"about 30 pages in English","level":1,"topics":["ethics","life and death","self"],"summary":"Seneca argues that life is not short; we make it short by wasting it on busyness, ambition and other people's demands. Time is the one thing we cannot get back, so we should spend it on philosophy and on living well now rather than postponing life.","why":"Its simple message about time speaks to anyone who feels busy, and it is one of the most popular Stoic texts today. Its date is uncertain; scholars place it around 49 CE or a few years later.","inside":"A single essay addressed to Paulinus, an official in charge of Rome's grain supply. The opening chapters state the whole argument.","start":"Read it straight through; it is short and works well as a first taste of Stoicism.","line":null,"islands":["stoa"],"related":["seneca-letters-to-lucilius","marcus-aurelius-meditations"],"shelf":"greece-rome","tags":["stoicism","time","shortness of life","busyness","procrastination","the good life","mortality","leisure","living in the present"]},{"id":"epictetus-handbook","title":"Handbook","original":"Encheiridion (Greek)","author":"epictetus","authorName":"Epictetus (compiled by Arrian)","year":125,"approx":true,"form":"handbook","length":"53 short chapters, about 30 pages","level":1,"topics":["ethics","self","mind"],"summary":"A short guide to Stoic living. Its central teaching is that some things are up to us (our judgements, desires and choices) and some are not (our body, property, reputation). We suffer when we treat the second as if it were the first, so peace of mind comes from caring only about what is truly ours.","why":"It has been used as a practical guide for nearly two thousand years, by Christian monks, soldiers and modern readers. Its focus on changing how we judge events helped inspire cognitive behavioural therapy.","inside":"Fifty-three short chapters drawn from the teachings of Epictetus, a former slave, by his student Arrian. Chapter 1 on what is and is not up to us is the key.","start":"Read chapter 1 several times, then dip into the rest in any order.","line":{"text":"Of things some are in our power, and others are not.","where":"Chapter 1","tr":"Long"},"islands":["stoa"],"related":["epictetus-discourses","marcus-aurelius-meditations","seneca-letters-to-lucilius"],"shelf":"greece-rome","tags":["stoicism","dichotomy of control","what is up to us","judgement","inner freedom","emotions","acceptance","tranquility","self-control","ethics"]},{"id":"epictetus-discourses","title":"Discourses","original":"Diatribai (Greek)","author":"epictetus","authorName":"Epictetus (recorded by Arrian)","year":108,"approx":true,"form":"lectures","length":"4 books, about 300 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["ethics","self","religion"],"summary":"Notes of Epictetus' informal talks to his students, taken down by Arrian. He presses the same Stoic themes as the Handbook, freedom through right judgement and accepting what is not in our control, but with stories, jokes and sharp exchanges with students and visitors.","why":"It shows Stoicism as a living practice taught face to face, and is the fullest source for Epictetus. Marcus Aurelius read and quoted it.","inside":"Four books survive of what was probably a longer work. The first chapter of Book I, on what is in our power, is the starting point.","start":"Begin with Book I, chapter 1, then read chapters whose titles catch your eye, since each stands alone.","line":null,"islands":["stoa"],"related":["epictetus-handbook","marcus-aurelius-meditations"],"shelf":"greece-rome","tags":["stoicism","dichotomy of control","inner freedom","judgement","acceptance","providence","virtue","self-control","ethics","emotions"]},{"id":"marcus-aurelius-meditations","title":"Meditations","original":"Ta eis heauton, 'To Himself' (Greek)","author":"marcus_aurelius","authorName":"Marcus Aurelius","year":175,"approx":true,"form":"other","length":"12 short books, about 150 pages in English","level":1,"topics":["ethics","self","life and death"],"summary":"The private notebook of a Roman emperor, written to remind himself how to live as a Stoic. He tells himself to do his duty, accept what fate brings, treat difficult people with patience, and remember how brief life is and how small one person is in the whole of nature.","why":"It is a rare record of a powerful ruler trying to live by a philosophy, and it is one of the most widely read ancient books today. It was never meant for publication, which gives it unusual honesty.","inside":"Twelve books of short notes, probably written c. 170–180 CE, partly on military campaign. Book I lists what he learned from family and teachers.","start":"Start with Book II, which opens with his famous morning reminder, then read freely.","line":{"text":"Begin the morning by saying to thyself, I shall meet with the busy-body, the ungrateful, arrogant, deceitful, envious, unsocial.","where":"Book II","tr":"Long"},"islands":["stoa"],"related":["epictetus-handbook","seneca-letters-to-lucilius","boethius-consolation"],"shelf":"greece-rome","tags":["stoicism","duty","acceptance","fate","mortality","impermanence","cosmos","self-control","virtue","anger","the view from above"]},{"id":"sextus-outlines-of-pyrrhonism","title":"Outlines of Pyrrhonism","original":"Pyrrhoneioi hypotyposeis (Greek)","author":"sextus","authorName":"Sextus Empiricus","year":200,"approx":true,"form":"treatise","length":"3 books, about 250 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["knowledge","logic","mind"],"summary":"A guide to Pyrrhonian scepticism. For any claim, the sceptic sets equally strong arguments on the other side, suspends judgement, and finds that peace of mind follows. Sextus gives standard patterns of argument, the 'modes', for producing this balance, then turns them against the theories of other schools.","why":"It is the main surviving source for ancient scepticism. Its rediscovery in the 16th century fed into the doubts of Montaigne and Descartes and helped shape modern debates about knowledge.","inside":"Three books. Book I explains what scepticism is and sets out the Ten Modes and the Five Modes; Books II and III attack dogmatic logic, physics and ethics.","start":"Read Book I, which is a clear self-contained account of the sceptical way of life.","line":null,"islands":["sceptics"],"related":["plato-theaetetus","cicero-on-duties"],"shelf":"greece-rome","tags":["skepticism","pyrrhonism","suspension of judgement","ataraxia","ten modes","five modes","agrippa's trilemma","dogmatism","epistemology","relativism"]},{"id":"plotinus-enneads","title":"Enneads","original":"Enneades (Greek)","author":"plotinus","authorName":"Plotinus (edited by Porphyry)","year":301,"approx":true,"form":"treatise","length":"54 treatises, about 600 pages in English","level":3,"topics":["metaphysics","religion","mind","beauty"],"summary":"Plotinus develops Plato's ideas into a picture of reality flowing from a single source, the One, which is beyond all description. From the One comes Intellect, and from Intellect comes Soul, which shapes the physical world. The human soul can turn inward and rise back towards the One.","why":"It founded what is now called Neoplatonism, which shaped Augustine, much medieval Christian, Islamic and Jewish thought, and Renaissance philosophy. It is also a major text of Western mysticism.","inside":"Fifty-four treatises written c. 253–270 CE and arranged by his student Porphyry into six groups of nine ('ennead' means a group of nine). The treatise On Beauty (I.6) is the usual way in.","start":"Begin with I.6, On Beauty, which is short and links straight back to Plato's Symposium.","line":null,"islands":["plotinus"],"related":["plato-symposium","augustine-confessions"],"shelf":"christian-platonists","tags":["neoplatonism","the one","emanation","intellect","world soul","mysticism","beauty","ascent of the soul","metaphysics","evil as privation"]},{"id":"augustine-confessions","title":"Confessions","original":"Confessiones (Latin)","author":"augustine","authorName":"Augustine of Hippo","year":400,"approx":true,"form":"autobiography","length":"13 books, about 300 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["religion","self","mind","ethics"],"summary":"Written as a long prayer to God, Augustine tells the story of his restless youth, his search through different philosophies, and his conversion to Christianity. The later books turn to philosophy, asking about memory, the self and the nature of time.","why":"It is often called the first Western autobiography, and its close attention to inner life shaped later writing about the self. Book XI's question 'what is time?' is still discussed by philosophers.","inside":"Thirteen books, written c. 397–400 CE. Books I–IX tell his life, with the theft of pears in Book II and the conversion in a garden in Book VIII; Book X is on memory and Book XI on time.","start":"Read Books I–IX as a story first, then try Book XI on time.","line":{"text":"Thou madest us for Thyself, and our heart is restless, until it repose in Thee.","where":"Book I","tr":"Pusey"},"islands":["augustine"],"related":["augustine-city-of-god","plotinus-enneads"],"shelf":"christian-platonists","tags":["autobiography","conversion","christianity","sin","time","memory","the self","god","grace","theft of the pears","problem of evil","restless heart"]},{"id":"augustine-city-of-god","title":"The City of God","original":"De civitate Dei (Latin)","author":"augustine","authorName":"Augustine of Hippo","year":426,"approx":true,"form":"treatise","length":"22 books, over 1,000 pages in English","level":3,"topics":["religion","politics","society","ethics"],"summary":"After Rome was sacked in 410 CE, some blamed Christianity for its fall. Augustine replies by contrasting two 'cities': the earthly city, built on love of self, and the city of God, built on love of God. History is the story of these two cities mixed together until the end of time.","why":"It shaped medieval ideas about church and state and the Christian view of history. It also contains influential discussions of evil, free will and just war.","inside":"Twenty-two books written over about 413–426 CE. Books I–X answer pagan critics; Books XI–XXII trace the two cities from creation to the end.","start":"Begin with Book XIV for the two loves and Book XIX for his view of peace and politics, using a good abridged edition.","line":null,"islands":["augustine"],"related":["augustine-confessions","plato-republic","boethius-consolation"],"shelf":"christian-platonists","tags":["christianity","two cities","philosophy of history","political philosophy","sin","providence","fall of rome","just war","theology","free will","problem of evil"]},{"id":"upanishads","title":"The Upanishads","original":"Upanishad (Sanskrit)","author":null,"authorName":"Various sages (anonymous)","year":-600,"approx":true,"form":"scripture","length":"about 13 principal texts, about 400 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["metaphysics","self","religion","knowledge"],"summary":"A collection of texts, often in the form of conversations between teachers and students, that ask what the ultimate reality is. Their central idea is that Brahman, the reality behind the whole world, and atman, a person's deepest self, are in some sense one. Knowing this brings freedom from the cycle of rebirth.","why":"They are the foundation of Vedanta, the most influential family of Hindu philosophies, and later thinkers such as Shankara built whole systems from them. Schopenhauer and other Europeans also read them closely.","inside":"The oldest, such as the Brihadaranyaka and Chandogya, probably date from c. 700–500 BCE; others are later. In the Chandogya, Uddalaka teaches his son with salt dissolved in water and the words 'you are that' (tat tvam asi).","start":"Start with the sixth chapter of the Chandogya Upanishad, where Uddalaka teaches Shvetaketu, then read the short Katha Upanishad.","line":null,"islands":["upanishads"],"related":["bhagavad-gita","patanjali-yoga-sutras"],"shelf":"east-sages","tags":["brahman","atman","tat tvam asi","hinduism","vedanta","reincarnation","liberation","consciousness","the self","karma","metaphysics","mysticism"]},{"id":"bhagavad-gita","title":"Bhagavad Gita","original":"Bhagavadgita, 'Song of the Lord' (Sanskrit)","author":null,"authorName":"Traditionally Vyasa (part of the Mahabharata)","year":-200,"approx":true,"form":"poem","length":"18 chapters, 700 verses","level":1,"topics":["ethics","religion","self","life and death"],"summary":"On a battlefield, the warrior Arjuna refuses to fight against his own relatives. His charioteer, the god Krishna, teaches him that the true self cannot be killed, that he must do his duty, and that he should act without attachment to the results of his actions. Krishna sets out paths to liberation through action, knowledge and devotion.","why":"It is one of the most widely read Hindu texts and has been commented on by every major school of Vedanta. Gandhi called it his guide, and its question of how to act well without clinging to outcomes still speaks to readers everywhere.","inside":"Eighteen chapters, part of the epic Mahabharata; its date is uncertain, often placed around the 2nd century BCE. Chapter 2 sets out the core teaching; Chapter 11 shows Krishna's overwhelming cosmic form.","start":"Read chapters 1 and 2 to see Arjuna's crisis and Krishna's first answer.","line":null,"islands":["upanishads"],"related":["upanishads","dhammapada"],"shelf":"india-classical","tags":["duty","dharma","karma yoga","detachment","hinduism","the self","devotion","liberation","action without attachment","yoga","war","reincarnation"]},{"id":"dhammapada","title":"Dhammapada","original":"Dhammapada (Pali)","author":"buddha","authorName":"The Buddha (as remembered by his followers)","year":-250,"approx":true,"form":"sayings","length":"26 chapters, 423 verses","level":1,"topics":["ethics","mind","religion","self"],"summary":"A collection of short verses traditionally said to be the Buddha's own words. They teach that our minds shape our lives, that hatred is ended only by non-hatred, and that freedom from suffering comes from training the mind and letting go of craving.","why":"It is one of the most loved Buddhist texts and often the first that people read. It gives Buddhist ethics in a form anyone can memorise.","inside":"Twenty-six themed chapters, such as the mind, anger and the wise, in the Pali canon. When it was compiled is uncertain. The first chapter of paired verses is the most famous.","start":"Read the first chapter, the 'Twin Verses', and then the chapter on the mind.","line":{"text":"All that we are is the result of what we have thought: it is founded on our thoughts, it is made up of our thoughts.","where":"Verse 1","tr":"Max Müller"},"islands":["buddha"],"related":["nagarjuna-mulamadhyamakakarika","bhagavad-gita"],"shelf":"east-sages","tags":["buddhism","mind","craving","suffering","non-attachment","mindfulness","hatred","karma","nirvana","self-control","ethics"]},{"id":"nagarjuna-mulamadhyamakakarika","title":"Fundamental Verses on the Middle Way","original":"Mulamadhyamakakarika (Sanskrit)","author":"nagarjuna","authorName":"Nagarjuna","year":200,"approx":true,"form":"poem","length":"27 chapters, about 450 short verses","level":3,"topics":["metaphysics","logic","knowledge","religion"],"summary":"Nagarjuna argues that nothing has svabhava, an independent nature of its own: everything exists only in dependence on other things. He tests ideas such as cause, motion, time and the self and shows that each leads to contradiction if taken as independently real. This 'emptiness' is the Middle Way between saying things truly exist and saying they do not exist at all.","why":"It founded the Madhyamaka school, which shaped Buddhism in India, Tibet, China and Japan. Modern philosophers have compared it with sceptics, Wittgenstein and others, though how to read it is still debated.","inside":"Twenty-seven chapters of compressed verse. Chapter 1 on causation opens the work; Chapter 24 explains emptiness and the two truths, conventional and ultimate.","start":"Read Chapter 24 first with a commentary, since it explains what the rest is for.","line":null,"islands":["nagarjuna"],"related":["dhammapada","sextus-outlines-of-pyrrhonism"],"shelf":"india-classical","tags":["emptiness","madhyamaka","buddhism","middle way","dependent origination","two truths","causation","no-self","metaphysics","nirvana"]},{"id":"umasvati-tattvartha-sutra","title":"Tattvartha Sutra","original":"Tattvarthasutra (Sanskrit)","author":"umasvati","authorName":"Umasvati","year":300,"approx":true,"form":"sutra","length":"10 chapters, about 350 short aphorisms","level":2,"topics":["metaphysics","ethics","religion","knowledge"],"summary":"The first systematic summary of Jain philosophy in Sanskrit. It sets out the basic realities: souls, non-living matter, and karma, which Jains see as fine matter that sticks to the soul. It explains how karma binds the soul and how right faith, right knowledge and right conduct together free it.","why":"It is the one text accepted by both main branches of Jainism, and many commentaries have been written on it. It is the best single doorway into Jain thought on knowledge, non-violence and liberation.","inside":"Ten chapters of brief aphorisms (sutras); its date is uncertain, placed anywhere from the 2nd to the 5th century CE. It opens by naming the three jewels and ends with liberation.","start":"Read Chapter 1 with a translation that includes a commentary, since the bare sutras are very brief.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["dhammapada","akshapada-nyaya-sutras"],"shelf":"india-classical","tags":["jainism","karma","soul","three jewels","liberation","non-violence","right knowledge","metaphysics","asceticism","reincarnation"]},{"id":"akshapada-nyaya-sutras","title":"Nyaya Sutras","original":"Nyayasutra (Sanskrit)","author":"akshapada","authorName":"Akshapada Gautama","year":150,"approx":true,"form":"sutra","length":"5 books, about 530 short aphorisms","level":3,"topics":["logic","knowledge","language"],"summary":"The founding text of the Nyaya school, which studies how we come to know things and how to argue well. It names four sources of knowledge: perception, inference, comparison and reliable testimony. It also sets out a five-step model of argument and lists the errors that spoil a debate.","why":"It began India's long tradition of logic and theory of knowledge, which other schools, including Buddhists, debated for centuries. The text probably grew over time, and dating it is disputed.","inside":"Five books. Book I lists sixteen topics of study and defines the sources of knowledge; the rest defends them against objections. It is usually read with Vatsyayana's commentary.","start":"Read Book I with Vatsyayana's commentary, which explains the four sources of knowledge clearly.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["nagarjuna-mulamadhyamakakarika","aristotle-categories"],"shelf":"india-classical","tags":["nyaya","epistemology","sources of knowledge","perception","inference","testimony","logic","debate","fallacies","indian philosophy"]},{"id":"patanjali-yoga-sutras","title":"Yoga Sutras","original":"Yogasutra (Sanskrit)","author":"patanjali","authorName":"Patanjali","year":300,"approx":true,"form":"sutra","length":"4 chapters, about 195 short aphorisms","level":2,"topics":["mind","self","religion"],"summary":"A compact guide to yoga as a discipline of the mind. Yoga is defined as stilling the movements of the mind, so that the pure conscious self can stand apart from the changing world of matter and thought. It sets out an eight-limbed path from ethical restraints and posture to breath, concentration and meditation.","why":"It is the classical text of the Yoga school of Indian philosophy and lies behind many modern ideas of meditation. Its date is uncertain; scholars place it anywhere from c. 200 BCE to c. 400 CE.","inside":"Four chapters (padas). The definition of yoga comes at the start of Chapter 1; the eight limbs are in Chapter 2.","start":"Read the first few sutras of Chapter 1 and the eight limbs in Chapter 2, using a translation with commentary.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["upanishads","bhagavad-gita"],"shelf":"india-classical","tags":["yoga","meditation","eight limbs of yoga","consciousness","samkhya","mind","liberation","concentration","the self","hinduism"]},{"id":"confucius-analects","title":"Analects","original":"Lunyu (Chinese)","author":"confucius","authorName":"Confucius (compiled by his followers)","year":-400,"approx":true,"form":"sayings","length":"20 short books, about 500 passages","level":1,"topics":["ethics","politics","society","self"],"summary":"Short sayings and conversations of Confucius, collected by his followers over several generations. They describe the junzi, the exemplary person, who cultivates ren (humaneness), respects ritual and family, loves learning, and leads by moral example rather than by force.","why":"It became the core of Chinese education for about two thousand years and shaped the cultures of East Asia. It is still a central text for discussions of character, family and good government.","inside":"Twenty books with little order between passages. Book I opens with the joy of learning, and Book XV includes his version of the golden rule.","start":"Read Books I and II slowly, with a translation that has notes, before dipping in elsewhere.","line":{"text":"What you do not want done to yourself, do not do to others.","where":"Book XV","tr":"Legge"},"islands":["confucius"],"related":["mencius","xunzi","laozi-daodejing"],"shelf":"east-sages","tags":["confucianism","junzi","ren","ritual","filial piety","golden rule","virtue ethics","learning","leadership by example","self-cultivation","political philosophy"]},{"id":"laozi-daodejing","title":"Daodejing","original":"Daodejing (Chinese)","author":"laozi","authorName":"Laozi (traditionally)","year":-400,"approx":true,"form":"poem","length":"81 short chapters, about 5,000 Chinese characters","level":1,"topics":["metaphysics","politics","ethics","language"],"summary":"A book of short poetic chapters about the Dao, the 'way' behind all things, which cannot be captured in words. It praises softness, simplicity and wu wei (acting without forcing), and advises rulers to govern lightly so that people can follow their own nature.","why":"It is one of the most translated books in the world and a founding text of Daoism. Whether a single person called Laozi wrote it is doubted by most scholars; parts of it appear on bamboo slips from c. 300 BCE.","inside":"Eighty-one brief chapters, often divided into a 'Dao' part and a 'De' (virtue) part. Chapter 1, on the Dao that cannot be named, is the best known.","start":"Read the first ten chapters and compare two different translations, since the Chinese is open to many readings.","line":{"text":"The Tao that can be trodden is not the enduring and unchanging Tao. The name that can be named is not the enduring and unchanging name.","where":"Chapter 1","tr":"Legge"},"islands":["dao"],"related":["zhuangzi","confucius-analects","hanfeizi"],"shelf":"east-sages","tags":["taoism","dao","wu wei","simplicity","the way","non-action","nature","ineffability","political philosophy","yin and yang","humility"]},{"id":"zhuangzi","title":"Zhuangzi","original":"Zhuangzi (Chinese)","author":"zhuangzi","authorName":"Zhuangzi and later followers","year":-300,"approx":true,"form":"other","length":"33 chapters, about 300 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["self","knowledge","language","life and death"],"summary":"A collection of stories, jokes and arguments that question our fixed ideas of right and wrong, useful and useless, life and death. It suggests that every point of view is limited, and that the wise person drifts freely with the changes of nature instead of clinging to one perspective.","why":"It is the second great Daoist classic and one of the most enjoyable books of Chinese literature. Its playful scepticism about knowledge has drawn comparison with Western sceptics, and it later influenced Chan (Zen) Buddhism.","inside":"Thirty-three chapters in the standard edition by Guo Xiang (c. 300 CE). The first seven, the 'Inner Chapters', are usually thought to be closest to Zhuangzi himself; the butterfly dream ends Chapter 2 and Cook Ding is in Chapter 3.","start":"Read the Inner Chapters, starting with Chapter 1 and its giant bird, then Chapter 2.","line":{"text":"Once upon a time, I, Chuang Tzu, dreamt I was a butterfly, fluttering hither and thither, to all intents and purposes a butterfly.","where":"Chapter 2","tr":"Giles"},"islands":["dao"],"related":["laozi-daodejing","sextus-outlines-of-pyrrhonism"],"shelf":"east-sages","tags":["taoism","butterfly dream","perspectivism","relativism","skepticism","spontaneity","cook ding","uselessness","death","dao","wu wei"]},{"id":"mencius","title":"Mencius","original":"Mengzi (Chinese)","author":"mencius","authorName":"Mencius","year":-300,"approx":true,"form":"dialogue","length":"7 books, about 250 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["ethics","politics","self"],"summary":"Conversations of Mencius with rulers, students and rivals. He argues that human nature is good: everyone has 'sprouts' of compassion, shame, respect and a sense of right, which grow into virtue if they are nourished. A ruler who governs with benevolence will win the people, and one who oppresses them loses the right to rule.","why":"Mencius' view became the official Confucian position for later centuries, and his debate with Xunzi on human nature is one of the great debates in Chinese thought. His example of the child about to fall into a well is still used to argue for natural moral feeling.","inside":"Seven books, each in two parts. The child at the well is in Book 2A; the debate with Gaozi on human nature is in Book 6A.","start":"Read Book 1A for his talks with King Hui of Liang and King Xuan of Qi, then the child-at-the-well passage in 2A.","line":{"text":"The feeling of commiseration is the principle of benevolence.","where":"Book II, Part I","tr":"Legge"},"islands":["confucius"],"related":["confucius-analects","xunzi","mozi"],"shelf":"east-sages","tags":["confucianism","human nature is good","four sprouts","child at the well","compassion","benevolent government","mandate of heaven","self-cultivation","virtue ethics","political philosophy"]},{"id":"mozi","title":"Mozi","original":"Mozi (Chinese)","author":"mozi","authorName":"Mozi and the Mohist school","year":-400,"approx":true,"form":"essays","length":"53 surviving chapters, about 350 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["ethics","politics","logic","society"],"summary":"The writings of Mozi and his followers argue that we should judge actions by whether they benefit the world. They call for jian ai, impartial care for everyone rather than favouring one's own family and state, and they condemn aggressive war, wasteful funerals and costly music.","why":"It contains one of the earliest arguments that resembles consequentialism, the view that right actions are those with the best results. It was the main rival to Confucianism for two centuries, and its later chapters include early Chinese logic.","inside":"Of 71 original chapters, 53 survive. The core chapters, often in three versions each, include 'Impartial Caring' and 'Against Offensive Warfare'; the later 'Dialectical' chapters deal with logic.","start":"Read the 'Impartial Caring' and 'Against Offensive Warfare' chapters, which are the clearest statements of his ethics.","line":null,"islands":["mozi_xunzi"],"related":["mencius","xunzi","confucius-analects"],"shelf":"east-sages","tags":["mohism","impartial care","universal love","consequentialism","against war","frugality","meritocracy","logic","ethics","political philosophy"]},{"id":"xunzi","title":"Xunzi","original":"Xunzi (Chinese)","author":"xunzi","authorName":"Xunzi","year":-250,"approx":true,"form":"essays","length":"32 chapters, about 400 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["ethics","society","politics","knowledge"],"summary":"Xunzi argues, against Mencius, that human nature is bad: left alone, people's desires lead to conflict. Goodness comes from deliberate effort, through learning, ritual and music, much as a craftsman straightens wood. He also argues that Heaven is the natural order and does not respond to human prayers.","why":"It is the most carefully argued Confucian text of its time, and its view that morality is built by culture is still a live position. Two of his students, Han Fei and Li Si, became leading Legalists.","inside":"Thirty-two essay-like chapters. Chapter 1 is 'Encouraging Learning', Chapter 17 is on Heaven, and Chapter 23 argues that human nature is bad.","start":"Read Chapter 1 on learning and then Chapter 23 on human nature.","line":{"text":"The nature of man is evil; his goodness is only acquired training.","where":"Chapter 23","tr":"Dubs"},"islands":["mozi_xunzi"],"related":["mencius","hanfeizi","confucius-analects"],"shelf":"china-classical","tags":["confucianism","human nature is bad","ritual","education","self-cultivation","music","heaven as nature","naturalism","moral education","political philosophy"]},{"id":"hanfeizi","title":"Han Feizi","original":"Han Feizi (Chinese)","author":"hanfeizi","authorName":"Han Fei","year":-240,"approx":true,"form":"essays","length":"55 chapters, about 600 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["politics","society","ethics"],"summary":"Han Fei argues that a ruler cannot rely on virtue or tradition. Instead he must govern through clear laws, strict rewards and punishments, careful techniques for controlling his ministers, and the power of his position. People act from self-interest, so a state must be built to work with that fact.","why":"It is the fullest statement of Legalism, the theory that guided the Qin state as it unified China in 221 BCE. It is often compared with Machiavelli for its hard-headed view of power.","inside":"Fifty-five chapters, not all by Han Fei himself. 'The Two Handles' (on reward and punishment) and 'The Five Vermin' (with the story of the farmer waiting by a tree stump) are the best known.","start":"Read 'The Two Handles' and 'The Five Vermin', which give his core ideas in lively form.","line":null,"islands":["mozi_xunzi"],"related":["xunzi","laozi-daodejing","machiavelli-prince"],"shelf":"china-classical","tags":["legalism","rule of law","reward and punishment","statecraft","power","self-interest","realpolitik","political philosophy","authoritarianism"]},{"id":"ecclesiastes","title":"Ecclesiastes","original":"Qoheleth (Hebrew)","author":null,"authorName":"Qoheleth, 'the Teacher' (traditionally Solomon)","year":-250,"approx":true,"form":"scripture","length":"12 short chapters, about 15 pages","level":1,"topics":["life and death","ethics","religion"],"summary":"A book of the Hebrew Bible in which a speaker called Qoheleth reflects on a life spent seeking wisdom, pleasure and success. He finds that everything is hevel, a breath or vapour: fleeting and hard to grasp, and death comes to the wise and foolish alike. His advice is to enjoy simple goods, work, and fear God.","why":"It is one of the oldest sustained reflections on the meaning of life and death, and it is unusual in scripture for its doubts. Readers still argue about whether its outlook is despairing, sceptical or quietly hopeful.","inside":"Twelve short chapters. Tradition names Solomon as the author, but most scholars date it to c. 450–200 BCE. The poem on 'a time for every purpose' in Chapter 3 is the best-known part.","start":"Read Chapters 1 to 3 together, which give the opening complaint and the famous poem on time.","line":{"text":"Vanity of vanities, saith the Preacher, vanity of vanities; all is vanity.","where":"Chapter 1","tr":"King James Version"},"islands":[],"related":["marcus-aurelius-meditations","epicurus-letter-to-menoeceus"],"shelf":"greece-rome","tags":["vanity","meaning of life","mortality","death","wisdom","a time for every purpose","futility","judaism","enjoyment of life","god"]},{"id":"boethius-consolation","title":"The Consolation of Philosophy","original":"De consolatione philosophiae (Latin)","author":"boethius","authorName":"Boethius","year":524,"approx":true,"form":"dialogue","length":"about 150 pages in English","level":1,"topics":["ethics","religion","life and death","self"],"summary":"Written in prison while Boethius waited to be executed, the book imagines Philosophy appearing to him as a woman and talking him out of despair. She argues that wealth, power and fame are gifts of Fortune that can be taken away, so true happiness can only be found in the highest good, which is God. The last book asks how God can know the future without taking away human free will.","why":"For about a thousand years it was one of the most widely read books in Europe, translated by King Alfred, Chaucer and Elizabeth I. Its picture of Fortune's turning wheel and its answer to the problem of foreknowledge shaped medieval thought.","inside":"Five books in which prose conversation alternates with short poems. The best-known part is Book II, where Philosophy speaks for Fortune and her wheel.","start":"Start with Books I and II, which set the scene and deal with Fortune; save Book V on free will for when you are warmed up.","line":null,"islands":["boethius"],"related":["augustine-confessions","aquinas-summa-theologiae"],"shelf":"christian-platonists","tags":["wheel of fortune","happiness","highest good","god","free will","divine foreknowledge","providence","consolation","christianity","problem of evil","fate"]},{"id":"dharmakirti-pramanavarttika","title":"Commentary on Valid Knowledge","original":"Pramāṇavārttika (Sanskrit)","author":"dharmakirti","authorName":"Dharmakirti","year":640,"approx":true,"form":"commentary","length":"a long work in Sanskrit verse; most English readers meet it in partial translations and studies","level":3,"topics":["knowledge","logic","language","religion"],"summary":"A verse commentary on the work of the earlier Buddhist logician Dignaga, setting out what counts as a reliable source of knowledge. Dharmakirti accepts only two: perception and inference. He also defends the Buddhist views that things last only a moment and that words do not name real general essences.","why":"It became the main textbook of Buddhist logic and theory of knowledge in India and especially in Tibet, where it is still studied in monasteries. Hindu and Jain thinkers spent centuries arguing against it.","inside":"Four chapters dealing with inference, the authority of the Buddha, perception, and inference used in debate; the order of the chapters is itself debated by scholars.","start":"Begin with a modern introduction to Buddhist epistemology (theory of knowledge) and then read the chapter on perception in translation.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["shankara-brahmasutra-bhashya"],"shelf":"india-schools","tags":["buddhism","epistemology","perception","inference","momentariness","apoha","logic","universals","philosophy of language","sources of knowledge"]},{"id":"shankara-brahmasutra-bhashya","title":"Commentary on the Brahma Sutras","original":"Brahmasūtrabhāṣya (Sanskrit)","author":"shankara","authorName":"Adi Shankara","year":720,"approx":true,"form":"commentary","length":"two large volumes in the classic English translation","level":3,"topics":["metaphysics","religion","self","knowledge"],"summary":"Shankara explains the short, puzzling verses of the Brahma Sutras to defend Advaita (non-dual) Vedanta. He argues that only Brahman, pure consciousness, is ultimately real, that your true self (atman) is identical with Brahman, and that the world of many separate things is an appearance produced by ignorance. Liberation comes from knowing this, not from ritual.","why":"It is the founding text of Advaita Vedanta, still the best-known school of Hindu philosophy, and every later Vedanta thinker, including Ramanuja, defined himself by agreeing or disagreeing with it. Shankara's dates are uncertain; many scholars place him around the early 8th century.","inside":"It follows the Brahma Sutras verse by verse in four chapters, arguing with rival schools along the way. The best-known part is the short preface on 'superimposition', the mistake of taking one thing for another, like seeing a snake in a rope.","start":"Read the preface on superimposition first: it is only a few pages long and contains the core of his whole view.","line":null,"islands":["upanishads"],"related":["ramanuja-sri-bhashya"],"shelf":"india-schools","tags":["advaita vedanta","non-dualism","brahman","atman","maya","illusion","snake and rope","liberation","consciousness","hinduism","ignorance"]},{"id":"alfarabi-virtuous-city","title":"The Virtuous City","original":"Mabādiʾ ārāʾ ahl al-madīna al-fāḍila (Arabic)","author":"alfarabi","authorName":"Al-Farabi","year":942,"approx":true,"form":"treatise","length":"about 150 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["politics","metaphysics","religion","society"],"summary":"Al-Farabi describes how the universe flows down in stages from a perfect First Cause, and then how human beings should live in a city that copies that order. The best city is ruled by a person who is both a philosopher and a prophet and who guides citizens towards true happiness. He contrasts it with ignorant, immoral and mistaken cities.","why":"It joined Plato's ideal state with Islamic ideas of prophecy and revelation, and set the agenda for political philosophy in the Islamic world. Later writers called al-Farabi the 'Second Teacher', after Aristotle.","inside":"The first half covers God, the heavens and the human soul; the second half turns to the city and its ruler. The best-known part is the list of qualities the first ruler must have.","start":"Skip ahead to the chapters on the need for society and the virtuous city, then go back to the cosmology if you want to see what supports it.","line":null,"islands":["falsafa"],"related":["plato-republic","avicenna-healing"],"shelf":"islam-jewish-early","tags":["islamic philosophy","ideal state","philosopher kings","prophecy","emanation","happiness","political philosophy","neoplatonism","first cause","virtuous city"]},{"id":"avicenna-healing","title":"The Book of Healing","original":"Kitāb al-Shifāʾ (Arabic)","author":"avicenna","authorName":"Avicenna (Ibn Sina)","year":1027,"approx":true,"form":"treatise","length":"an encyclopedia of many thousands of pages; most readers meet it in extracts","level":3,"topics":["metaphysics","mind","logic","science"],"summary":"Despite its name, this is not a medical book: it aims to heal the soul of ignorance by covering all of philosophy, from logic and nature to mathematics and metaphysics. Avicenna argues that God is the one Necessary Existent, whose existence follows from its very essence, while everything else needs a cause. In the section on the soul he describes the 'floating man', a thought experiment meant to show that we know our own self exists even without any bodily sensation.","why":"It became the standard statement of Islamic philosophy, and in Latin translation it shaped European thinkers such as Aquinas. His distinction between what a thing is and the fact that it exists became central to later metaphysics.","inside":"Four large parts: logic, natural philosophy (including psychology), mathematics, and metaphysics. The best-known passage is the floating man near the start of the book On the Soul.","start":"Read the floating man passage from On the Soul in an anthology, then the early chapters of the Metaphysics on the Necessary Existent.","line":null,"islands":["falsafa"],"related":["alghazali-incoherence","descartes-meditations"],"shelf":"islam-jewish-early","tags":["floating man","necessary existent","essence and existence","islamic philosophy","self-awareness","soul","existence of god","metaphysics","philosophy of mind","logic","contingency"]},{"id":"alghazali-incoherence","title":"The Incoherence of the Philosophers","original":"Tahāfut al-falāsifa (Arabic)","author":"alghazali","authorName":"Al-Ghazali","year":1095,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 250 pages in English","level":3,"topics":["religion","metaphysics","science","knowledge"],"summary":"Al-Ghazali attacks the philosophers who followed Aristotle, especially al-Farabi and Avicenna, on their own terms, by showing that many of their proofs do not work. He judges three of their claims to be unbelief: that the world has always existed, that God knows only general truths and not particular things, and that the body is not raised at the resurrection. Famously he argues that we never see fire cause cotton to burn; we only see one follow the other, and God could make it otherwise.","why":"It is one of the sharpest critiques of philosophy written by an insider, and its argument about cause and effect anticipates Hume's by more than six centuries. Averroes wrote a whole book in reply.","inside":"Twenty 'discussions', each stating a philosophers' thesis and then dismantling it. The best-known is the seventeenth, on causation and the cotton and the fire.","start":"Go straight to the seventeenth discussion on causation; it is self-contained and the argument is easy to follow.","line":null,"islands":["incoherence"],"related":["averroes-incoherence","alghazali-deliverance","hume-enquiry"],"shelf":"islamic","tags":["causation","occasionalism","cotton and fire","islamic philosophy","eternity of the world","god","miracles","critique of philosophy","divine knowledge","resurrection","faith and reason"]},{"id":"alghazali-deliverance","title":"Deliverance from Error","original":"al-Munqidh min al-ḍalāl (Arabic)","author":"alghazali","authorName":"Al-Ghazali","year":1108,"approx":true,"form":"autobiography","length":"about 70 pages in English","level":1,"topics":["knowledge","religion","self"],"summary":"Late in life al-Ghazali tells the story of his search for certain knowledge. He describes falling into doubt about both the senses and reason, being cured not by argument but by a light God cast into his heart, and then testing four groups of seekers: theologians, philosophers, the followers of an infallible teacher, and the Sufis (Muslim mystics). He concludes that the Sufis' direct experience reaches what argument cannot.","why":"It is one of the earliest intellectual autobiographies and a classic account of a crisis of doubt. Readers often compare its opening to Descartes' Meditations, though no direct influence has been shown.","inside":"A short continuous narrative. The best-known part is the opening account of his scepticism and how it ended.","start":"Read it straight through in one sitting; it is short, personal and the best way into al-Ghazali.","line":null,"islands":["incoherence"],"related":["alghazali-incoherence","descartes-meditations"],"shelf":"islamic","tags":["skepticism","certainty","doubt","sufism","mysticism","islamic philosophy","autobiography","faith and reason","religious experience","epistemology"]},{"id":"averroes-decisive-treatise","title":"The Decisive Treatise","original":"Faṣl al-maqāl (Arabic)","author":"averroes","authorName":"Averroes (Ibn Rushd)","year":1179,"approx":true,"form":"treatise","length":"about 40 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["religion","knowledge","society"],"summary":"Writing as a judge of Islamic law, Averroes asks whether the law allows philosophy, and answers that it actually requires it of those able to do it. Since truth cannot contradict truth, where scripture and sound demonstration seem to clash, scripture should be interpreted. He divides people into those persuaded by strict proof, by debate, and by rhetoric, and says each should be addressed in the right way.","why":"It is a short, clear defence of reason within a religious tradition, and its question of how faith and science fit together is still argued over today.","inside":"One continuous argument in three stages: is philosophy permitted, how should scripture be interpreted, and who should hear which kind of argument.","start":"Read the whole thing: it is short, and the opening pages state the question very clearly.","line":null,"islands":["incoherence"],"related":["averroes-incoherence","maimonides-guide"],"shelf":"islamic","tags":["faith and reason","islamic philosophy","interpretation of scripture","truth cannot contradict truth","philosophy and religion","demonstration","rhetoric","islamic law"]},{"id":"averroes-incoherence","title":"The Incoherence of the Incoherence","original":"Tahāfut al-tahāfut (Arabic)","author":"averroes","authorName":"Averroes (Ibn Rushd)","year":1180,"approx":true,"form":"commentary","length":"two volumes in the standard English translation","level":3,"topics":["metaphysics","religion","science"],"summary":"Averroes quotes al-Ghazali's Incoherence of the Philosophers passage by passage and replies to each point. He argues that al-Ghazali misunderstood Aristotle, that Avicenna had sometimes strayed from Aristotle too, and that denying real causes in nature would destroy all knowledge. The title turns al-Ghazali's own word back on him.","why":"It is the classic defence of philosophy in medieval Islam. In Latin Europe Averroes became known simply as 'the Commentator' on Aristotle, and his views sparked fierce debates at the University of Paris.","inside":"It follows the same twenty discussions as al-Ghazali's book, so the two can be read side by side. The reply on causation is the most famous section.","start":"Read al-Ghazali's seventeenth discussion first, then Averroes' reply to it.","line":null,"islands":["incoherence"],"related":["alghazali-incoherence","averroes-decisive-treatise"],"shelf":"islamic","tags":["causation","islamic philosophy","aristotelianism","eternity of the world","faith and reason","critique of occasionalism","metaphysics","philosophy of nature"]},{"id":"halevi-kuzari","title":"The Kuzari","original":"Kitāb al-radd wa-l-dalīl fī l-dīn al-dhalīl (Judeo-Arabic)","author":null,"authorName":"Judah Halevi","year":1140,"approx":true,"form":"dialogue","length":"about 300 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["religion","knowledge","ethics"],"summary":"A king of the Khazars is told in a dream that his intentions please God but his actions do not. He questions a philosopher, a Christian and a Muslim, and finally a rabbi, who convinces him. Halevi uses the story to argue that a religion grounded in history and in revelation witnessed by a whole people is more reliable than the abstract God of the philosophers.","why":"It is one of the great works of medieval Jewish thought and an early, pointed critique of the idea that philosophy can replace religious tradition.","inside":"Five parts of conversation between the king and the rabbi, after a short opening in which the other three speak. The opening exchange with the philosopher is the best-known part.","start":"Read Part I, which contains the frame story and the case against the philosopher.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["maimonides-guide","alghazali-incoherence"],"shelf":"jewish","tags":["judaism","revelation","god of the philosophers","faith and reason","philosophy of religion","religious dialogue","jewish philosophy","history"]},{"id":"maimonides-guide","title":"The Guide for the Perplexed","original":"Dalālat al-ḥāʾirīn (Judeo-Arabic)","author":"maimonides","authorName":"Maimonides","year":1190,"approx":true,"form":"treatise","length":"about 650 pages in English","level":3,"topics":["religion","metaphysics","language","knowledge"],"summary":"Maimonides writes for a student who knows both the Bible and Aristotle and is troubled because they seem to disagree. He shows that many biblical words about God are meant figuratively, and argues that we can say only what God is not, never what God is. He also discusses whether the world was created, what prophecy is, why evil exists, and the reasons behind the commandments.","why":"It is the most important work of medieval Jewish philosophy and was read closely by Aquinas and later by Spinoza. Its idea of 'negative theology' is still central to debates about how we can talk about God.","inside":"Three parts. Maimonides warns in the introduction that he has written in a deliberately guarded way and may contradict himself on purpose. The chapters on negative attributes in Part I are the best known.","start":"Read the introduction, with its warning about how the book is written, and then the chapters in Part I on why God has no positive attributes.","line":null,"islands":["maimonides"],"related":["aquinas-summa-theologiae","spinoza-ttp","averroes-decisive-treatise"],"shelf":"jewish","tags":["jewish philosophy","negative theology","divine attributes","faith and reason","creation","prophecy","problem of evil","interpretation of scripture","aristotelianism","god"]},{"id":"ramanuja-sri-bhashya","title":"Sri Bhashya","original":"Śrībhāṣya (Sanskrit)","author":"ramanuja","authorName":"Ramanuja","year":1100,"approx":true,"form":"commentary","length":"several hundred pages in English","level":3,"topics":["metaphysics","religion","self"],"summary":"Ramanuja's commentary on the Brahma Sutras defends Vishishtadvaita, or 'qualified non-dualism'. He argues against Shankara that the world and individual souls are real, not illusions; they relate to God as a body relates to its soul. Liberation comes through loving devotion and God's grace, and the freed soul stays distinct from God while sharing in God's bliss.","why":"It gave a full philosophical basis to devotional (bhakti) Hinduism and remains the main text of the Sri Vaishnava tradition. Its debate with Advaita about whether the world is real is one of the great arguments of Indian philosophy. Ramanuja's traditional dates are uncertain.","inside":"It follows the Brahma Sutras verse by verse. The long discussion of the first sutra, where he attacks Shankara's theory of illusion, is the best-known part.","start":"Read a modern summary of the debate with Shankara first, then the opening discussion of the first sutra.","line":null,"islands":["upanishads"],"related":["shankara-brahmasutra-bhashya"],"shelf":"india-schools","tags":["vishishtadvaita","qualified non-dualism","vedanta","hinduism","devotion","bhakti","grace","brahman","the self","liberation","critique of illusion"]},{"id":"zhuxi-reflections","title":"Reflections on Things at Hand","original":"Jinsilu 近思錄 (Classical Chinese)","author":"zhuxi","authorName":"Zhu Xi and Lü Zuqian","year":1175,"approx":false,"form":"sayings","length":"about 350 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["ethics","metaphysics","self","society"],"summary":"Zhu Xi and his friend Lü Zuqian gathered short passages from four Song-dynasty thinkers (Zhou Dunyi, Cheng Hao, Cheng Yi and Zhang Zai) into a handbook of Neo-Confucian thought. The passages explain that everything is made of qi (vital energy) and ordered by li (principle), and that a person becomes good by studying the principle in things and cultivating a reverent, attentive mind.","why":"It became the standard introduction to Neo-Confucianism for centuries in China, Korea and Japan. Zhu Xi's teaching was later made the basis of the Chinese civil service examinations.","inside":"Fourteen chapters arranged as a course of study: from the nature of the Way, through learning and self-cultivation, to family life, government and teaching. The opening chapter on the Way is the most philosophical and the hardest.","start":"Start with the chapters on learning and self-cultivation, which are more concrete than the opening chapter on the Way.","line":null,"islands":["eastlight"],"related":["wangyangming-instructions","confucius-analects"],"shelf":"asia-medieval","tags":["neo-confucianism","li and qi","principle","self-cultivation","investigation of things","confucianism","moral education","metaphysics","reverence"]},{"id":"dogen-shobogenzo","title":"Treasury of the True Dharma Eye","original":"Shōbōgenzō 正法眼蔵 (Japanese)","author":"dogen","authorName":"Dōgen","year":1253,"approx":true,"form":"essays","length":"several volumes in full English translation","level":3,"topics":["religion","self","metaphysics","life and death"],"summary":"A collection of essays written between 1231 and Dōgen's death in 1253, explaining Zen Buddhist practice. Dōgen teaches that sitting meditation (zazen) is not a means to enlightenment but is itself enlightenment in action. He bends language to show that time and existence are not separate things, and that to study the self is finally to forget the self.","why":"It is the founding text of the Sōtō school of Zen and one of the most original works of Japanese philosophy. In the 20th century philosophers such as those of the Kyoto School read it alongside Heidegger.","inside":"Dozens of separate essays, collected in different versions after his death. The best known are 'Genjōkōan' (Actualising the Fundamental Point) and 'Uji' (Being-Time).","start":"Start with 'Genjōkōan': it is short, it is usually placed first, and it states his central ideas.","line":null,"islands":["eastlight"],"related":["zhuxi-reflections","kenko-essays-in-idleness"],"shelf":"asia-medieval","tags":["zen","buddhism","zazen","meditation","enlightenment","being-time","time","no-self","impermanence","forget the self"]},{"id":"aquinas-summa-theologiae","title":"Summa Theologiae","original":"Summa theologiae (Latin)","author":"aquinas","authorName":"Thomas Aquinas","year":1265,"approx":true,"form":"treatise","length":"several thousand pages; most readers use selections","level":2,"topics":["religion","metaphysics","ethics","politics"],"summary":"Aquinas set out to give beginners in theology an orderly guide to the whole of Christian teaching, using Aristotle's philosophy wherever reason could reach. He argues that some truths about God, such as God's existence, can be shown by reason, while others need revelation. Along the way he covers the soul, happiness, the virtues, law, war and much more.","why":"It is the greatest synthesis of Christian faith and Aristotelian philosophy, and it remains the basis of Catholic philosophy. His account of natural law still shapes debates in ethics and law.","inside":"Written from 1265 until 1273 and left unfinished; three parts, divided into questions and then articles. Each article states objections, a counter-authority, Aquinas' answer, and replies. The 'Five Ways' of proving God's existence in Part I, Question 2 are the best-known passage.","start":"Read Part I, Question 2, Article 3 (the Five Ways); it is two pages long and shows how every article works.","line":{"text":"The existence of God can be proved in five ways.","where":"Part I, Question 2, Article 3","tr":"Fathers of the English Dominican Province"},"islands":["aquinas"],"related":["anselm-proslogion","aristotle-metaphysics","maimonides-guide"],"shelf":"christian-europe","tags":["five ways","existence of god","natural law","thomism","faith and reason","just war","virtue ethics","cosmological argument","christianity","scholasticism","soul","happiness"]},{"id":"anselm-proslogion","title":"Proslogion","original":"Proslogion (Latin)","author":"anselm","authorName":"Anselm of Canterbury","year":1078,"approx":true,"form":"treatise","length":"about 30 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["religion","metaphysics","logic"],"summary":"Written as a prayer, it offers the 'ontological argument': God is that than which nothing greater can be thought. Anselm argues that such a being cannot exist only in the mind, because then something greater, the same being existing in reality, could be thought. So God must really exist.","why":"The argument has been attacked and defended ever since, by Aquinas, Descartes, Leibniz, Kant and modern logicians. A monk called Gaunilo replied at once that the same reasoning would prove a perfect island exists, and Anselm answered him.","inside":"Twenty-six short chapters, usually printed with Gaunilo's reply and Anselm's response. Chapters 2 to 4 contain the famous argument.","start":"Read chapters 2 to 4, then Gaunilo's 'perfect island' reply, which is the quickest way to see what is at stake.","line":{"text":"we believe that thou art a being than which nothing greater can be conceived.","where":"Chapter 2","tr":"S. N. Deane (1903)"},"islands":["anselm"],"related":["aquinas-summa-theologiae","descartes-meditations"],"shelf":"christian-platonists","tags":["ontological argument","existence of god","that than which nothing greater can be thought","gaunilo's island","faith seeking understanding","christianity","philosophy of religion","scholasticism"]},{"id":"ockham-summa-logicae","title":"Summa of Logic","original":"Summa logicae (Latin)","author":"ockham","authorName":"William of Ockham","year":1323,"approx":true,"form":"treatise","length":"a long textbook; most readers use selections from Part I","level":3,"topics":["logic","language","metaphysics"],"summary":"Ockham's textbook of logic explains how words and concepts signify things. He uses it to defend nominalism: only individual things exist, and 'universals' such as 'humanity' are just concepts or signs that stand for many individuals. He trims away entities that other philosophers thought they needed.","why":"It helped end the dominance of the older realist view of universals and shaped late medieval logic. His habit of refusing unnecessary entities became known as 'Ockham's razor', though the famous slogan form is not found in his writings.","inside":"Three parts: on terms, on propositions, and on arguments. The chapters in Part I on universals are the most read.","start":"Read the chapters on universals in Part I, ideally with a guide, since the logical vocabulary takes getting used to.","line":null,"islands":["ockham"],"related":["aquinas-summa-theologiae"],"shelf":"christian-europe","tags":["nominalism","universals","ockham's razor","logic","philosophy of language","signification","medieval philosophy","scholasticism","metaphysics"]},{"id":"kenko-essays-in-idleness","title":"Essays in Idleness","original":"Tsurezuregusa 徒然草 (Japanese)","author":"kenko","authorName":"Yoshida Kenkō","year":1331,"approx":true,"form":"essays","length":"about 150 pages in English","level":1,"topics":["beauty","life and death","self"],"summary":"A Buddhist monk jots down passing thoughts on manners, nature, friendship, death and taste. Again and again he returns to impermanence: because nothing lasts, things are precious, and beauty lies in what is fading, unfinished or imperfect.","why":"It became a classic of Japanese literature and helped shape Japanese ideas of beauty, such as finding value in the incomplete and the transient.","inside":"243 short sections in no fixed order, some a single sentence long. The passages on impermanence and on enjoying blossoms that are not in full bloom are the best known.","start":"Dip in anywhere; sections 7 and 137, on impermanence and on imperfect beauty, are good places to begin.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["dogen-shobogenzo","montaigne-essays"],"shelf":"asia-medieval","tags":["impermanence","aesthetics","mono no aware","wabi-sabi","buddhism","death","beauty of imperfection","japanese philosophy","nature","essays"]},{"id":"ibn-khaldun-muqaddimah","title":"The Muqaddimah","original":"al-Muqaddima (Arabic)","author":"ibn_khaldun","authorName":"Ibn Khaldun","year":1377,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 1,200 pages in full; about 450 in the standard abridgement","level":2,"topics":["society","politics","knowledge","science"],"summary":"Written as the introduction to a world history, it asks why historians make mistakes and what laws govern societies. Ibn Khaldun argues that tough desert peoples have strong 'asabiyya' (group solidarity), which lets them found dynasties; city luxury then weakens that solidarity until the dynasty falls, usually within a few generations. He also discusses economics, labour, cities and the sciences.","why":"It is often called a founding work of sociology and of the philosophy of history, and its ideas about cycles of rise and decline are still discussed by historians and economists.","inside":"An introduction on the errors of historians, then six chapters on civilisation, nomads, dynasties, cities, economic life and the sciences. The material on group solidarity and the life cycle of dynasties is the best known. A first draft was finished in 1377 and revised later.","start":"Read the opening on historians' errors, then the chapters on desert life and dynasties in an abridged translation.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["machiavelli-discourses","montesquieu-spirit-of-the-laws"],"shelf":"islamic","tags":["asabiyya","group solidarity","rise and fall of dynasties","philosophy of history","sociology","economics","civilisation","islamic philosophy","cyclical history","historiography"]},{"id":"christine-city-of-ladies","title":"The Book of the City of Ladies","original":"Le Livre de la Cité des Dames (Middle French)","author":null,"authorName":"Christine de Pizan","year":1405,"approx":true,"form":"other","length":"about 250 pages in English","level":1,"topics":["society","ethics","self"],"summary":"Upset after reading a book that insults women, Christine is visited by three ladies, Reason, Rectitude and Justice. Together they build an allegorical city whose stones are the stories of brave, wise and good women from history and legend. The book argues that misogynist writers are wrong and that women are as capable of virtue and learning as men.","why":"It is one of the first books by a woman writing against the tradition of attacks on women, and a key text in the 'quarrel about women' that ran for centuries in Europe.","inside":"Three books, one for each lady, made up of conversations and short stories of famous women. The opening scene of Christine in her study is the best-known part.","start":"Read the opening chapters of Book I, which set out the problem and Reason's first arguments.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["astell-serious-proposal"],"shelf":"christian-europe","tags":["feminism","women","misogyny","allegory","virtue","education of women","gender equality","medieval philosophy"]},{"id":"erasmus-praise-of-folly","title":"The Praise of Folly","original":"Moriae encomium (Latin)","author":null,"authorName":"Desiderius Erasmus","year":1511,"approx":false,"form":"other","length":"about 150 pages in English","level":1,"topics":["religion","society","ethics"],"summary":"Folly herself gives a speech in her own praise, pointing out that without her no one would marry, have children or enjoy life. The joke grows sharper as she turns to theologians, monks, princes and popes and mocks their pride and hypocrisy. At the end she praises the 'folly' of Christian faith, which looks foolish to the worldly.","why":"It was a bestseller that helped prepare the ground for the Reformation by mocking abuses in the Church, though Erasmus himself stayed Catholic and later argued with Luther over free will. It shows how satire can do philosophy.","inside":"One continuous speech, written in 1509 while Erasmus stayed with Thomas More; the Latin title puns on More's name. The attack on theologians and monks is the best-known part.","start":"Read it straight through; it is short, but a modern edition with notes helps with the classical jokes.","line":null,"islands":["reformation"],"related":["more-utopia","luther-freedom-of-a-christian"],"shelf":"humanists","tags":["satire","folly","humanism","religious hypocrisy","christianity","church reform","renaissance","wisdom"]},{"id":"machiavelli-prince","title":"The Prince","original":"Il Principe (Italian)","author":"machiavelli","authorName":"Niccolò Machiavelli","year":1513,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 100 pages in English","level":1,"topics":["politics","ethics","society"],"summary":"A short handbook for a new ruler on how to win and keep power. Machiavelli argues that a prince must learn how not to be good when necessary, because acting on ideals in a world of bad people leads to ruin. Success depends on virtù (skill and boldness) meeting fortuna (luck).","why":"It broke with the long tradition of advice books that told rulers to be virtuous, and it began the habit of studying politics as it really is. Readers still disagree about whether it is cynical advice, a warning, or satire.","inside":"Twenty-six short chapters, written in 1513 and published in 1532 after his death. The chapters on whether it is better to be loved or feared and on the lion and the fox are the most famous.","start":"Read chapters 15 to 18, where he sets out his break with traditional morality.","line":{"text":"it is much safer to be feared than loved","where":"Chapter XVII","tr":"W. K. Marriott (1908)"},"islands":["machiavelli"],"related":["machiavelli-discourses","hobbes-leviathan"],"shelf":"power-law","tags":["realpolitik","power","better to be feared than loved","virtu and fortuna","lion and the fox","the ends justify the means","statecraft","political philosophy","political realism","leadership"]},{"id":"machiavelli-discourses","title":"Discourses on Livy","original":"Discorsi sopra la prima deca di Tito Livio (Italian)","author":"machiavelli","authorName":"Niccolò Machiavelli","year":1517,"approx":true,"form":"treatise","length":"about 450 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["politics","society"],"summary":"Using the Roman historian Livy, Machiavelli studies how the Roman Republic grew great and stayed free. He argues that a mixed constitution and even conflict between nobles and common people kept Rome free, and that republics need good laws, citizen armies and periodic renewal.","why":"It shows that the author of The Prince was also a serious defender of republics, and it influenced later republican thinkers in England, America and France.","inside":"Three books: on the founding and internal order of states, on expansion and war, and on how states renew themselves and on great individuals. It was published in 1531, after his death.","start":"Read the first ten chapters of Book I, where he sets out his case for the mixed republic.","line":null,"islands":["machiavelli"],"related":["machiavelli-prince","montesquieu-spirit-of-the-laws"],"shelf":"power-law","tags":["republicanism","mixed constitution","liberty","civic virtue","roman republic","political conflict","political philosophy","citizen armies","political realism"]},{"id":"more-utopia","title":"Utopia","original":"Utopia (Latin)","author":null,"authorName":"Thomas More","year":1516,"approx":false,"form":"dialogue","length":"about 130 pages in English","level":1,"topics":["politics","society","ethics"],"summary":"A traveller named Raphael Hythloday criticises the injustice of European society, then describes an island where property is shared, everyone works a short day, and a wide religious tolerance is practised. The name means 'no place' in Greek and puns on 'good place'.","why":"It gave the world the word 'utopia' and started a whole genre of imagined societies. Scholars still argue about how far More, a devout Catholic and royal servant, agreed with the society he described.","inside":"Book I is a dialogue about whether a philosopher should advise kings and about the causes of crime in England. Book II is Hythloday's description of the island.","start":"Read Book I first, because its argument about poverty and crime explains why Book II matters.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["plato-republic","erasmus-praise-of-folly"],"shelf":"power-law","tags":["utopia","ideal state","common property","communism","religious tolerance","social justice","crime and punishment","satire","political philosophy","humanism"]},{"id":"wangyangming-instructions","title":"Instructions for Practical Living","original":"Chuanxilu 傳習錄 (Classical Chinese)","author":"wangyangming","authorName":"Wang Yangming","year":1518,"approx":false,"form":"sayings","length":"about 300 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["ethics","knowledge","self"],"summary":"Conversations and letters recorded by Wang's students. He argues that principle is not out in things to be studied, but in the mind itself, and that everyone has an inborn knowledge of good. He insists that knowledge and action are one: if you do not act on what you know, you do not really know it.","why":"It challenged Zhu Xi's dominant teaching and launched the 'School of Mind', which spread widely in China and Japan. His idea that knowing and doing cannot be separated is still widely quoted.","inside":"Three parts; the first was published in 1518 and the others added later. The early exchanges on the unity of knowledge and action and his account of failing to find principle by staring at bamboo are the best known.","start":"Start with the opening conversations in Part I, where a student questions him about knowledge and action.","line":null,"islands":["eastlight"],"related":["zhuxi-reflections"],"shelf":"asia-medieval","tags":["neo-confucianism","unity of knowledge and action","innate knowledge","school of mind","moral intuition","self-cultivation","bamboo","confucianism","ethics"]},{"id":"luther-freedom-of-a-christian","title":"On the Freedom of a Christian","original":"Von der Freiheit eines Christenmenschen (German); De libertate christiana (Latin)","author":null,"authorName":"Martin Luther","year":1520,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 40 pages in English","level":1,"topics":["religion","ethics","self"],"summary":"Luther states a paradox: a Christian is a free lord of all, subject to none, and a dutiful servant of all, subject to all. He argues that a person is made right with God by faith alone, not by good works, and that good works then flow freely from faith as service to one's neighbour.","why":"It is the clearest short statement of the ideas behind the Protestant Reformation, which split Western Christianity and changed ideas about conscience and individual faith.","inside":"A short essay in two halves: the inner person, freed by faith, and the outer person, who serves others. It was issued with an open letter to Pope Leo X.","start":"Read it in one go; it is short and the opening paradox organises everything that follows.","line":null,"islands":["reformation"],"related":["erasmus-praise-of-folly","locke-letter-concerning-toleration"],"shelf":"humanists","tags":["faith alone","christian freedom","grace","good works","protestantism","reformation","justification","christianity","service to others","paradox"]},{"id":"montaigne-essays","title":"Essays","original":"Essais (French)","author":"montaigne","authorName":"Michel de Montaigne","year":1580,"approx":false,"form":"essays","length":"about 1,200 pages in English","level":1,"topics":["self","knowledge","life and death","ethics"],"summary":"Montaigne 'tries out' his judgement on everything from friendship, cruelty and cannibals to death, education and his own kidney stones. His main subject is himself, and through himself the changeable, uncertain human condition. He argues that we know far less than we think and should be slow to condemn other customs.","why":"He invented the essay as a form, and his scepticism and frankness about the self influenced Descartes, Pascal, Shakespeare and modern writing. His defence of other cultures against European arrogance still reads as fresh.","inside":"107 essays in three books: the first edition of 1580 had Books I and II, Book III was added in 1588, and a posthumous edition followed in 1595. The longest and most philosophical is the 'Apology for Raymond Sebond' (Book II, chapter 12).","start":"Start with short essays such as 'Of Cannibals' and 'That to Philosophise Is to Learn to Die'; save the long 'Apology' for later.","line":{"text":"Que sais-je? (What do I know?)","where":"Book II, chapter 12, 'Apology for Raymond Sebond'","tr":"original French"},"islands":["montaigne"],"related":["pascal-pensees","descartes-discourse-on-method","kenko-essays-in-idleness"],"shelf":"humanists","tags":["skepticism","the self","human condition","cultural relativism","of cannibals","death","friendship","education","what do i know","essays","humanism"]},{"id":"bacon-new-organon","title":"The New Organon","original":"Novum Organum (Latin)","author":"bacon","authorName":"Francis Bacon","year":1620,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 250 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["science","knowledge","logic"],"summary":"Bacon argues that knowledge had stalled because people relied on Aristotle's logic and leapt from a few observations to grand conclusions. He proposes a new method: collect many careful observations, compare cases where something is present, absent or varies, and rise step by step to general laws. First, though, the mind must be cleared of 'idols', the errors built into human nature, personal bias, language and philosophical systems.","why":"It became a manifesto for experimental science and inspired the founders of the Royal Society. His list of the idols is still one of the best short guides to how we fool ourselves.","inside":"Two books of numbered aphorisms. It was meant as one part of a much larger, unfinished project, the Great Instauration. The four idols in Book I are the best-known part.","start":"Read the aphorisms on the idols in Book I (roughly 38 to 68).","line":{"text":"Human knowledge and human power meet in one","where":"Book I, Aphorism 3","tr":"James Spedding and others (1858)"},"islands":["bacon"],"related":["descartes-discourse-on-method","hume-treatise"],"shelf":"new-science","tags":["scientific method","induction","idols of the mind","empiricism","bias","philosophy of science","experimentation","knowledge is power","critique of aristotle"]},{"id":"descartes-discourse-on-method","title":"Discourse on the Method","original":"Discours de la méthode (French)","author":"descartes","authorName":"René Descartes","year":1637,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 60 pages in English","level":1,"topics":["knowledge","science","mind","self"],"summary":"Descartes tells the story of how, unsatisfied with what he was taught, he decided to rebuild his beliefs from the ground up. He gives four simple rules of method, a temporary moral code to live by while doubting, and a first version of his proof that he exists because he thinks. He also sketches his science, including the claim that animals are complex machines.","why":"Written in French rather than Latin so that ordinary readers could follow, it contains the first appearance of the famous 'I think, therefore I am'.","inside":"Six parts, first published anonymously as a preface to three scientific essays on optics, weather and geometry. Part IV, with the cogito, is the best known.","start":"Read Parts I, II and IV: his life story, his rules of method, and the cogito.","line":{"text":"Je pense, donc je suis (I think, therefore I am)","where":"Part IV","tr":"original French"},"islands":["descartes"],"related":["descartes-meditations","montaigne-essays"],"shelf":"rationalists","tags":["cogito","i think therefore i am","method of doubt","rationalism","rules of method","provisional morality","animals as machines","mind-body dualism","epistemology","philosophy of science"]},{"id":"descartes-meditations","title":"Meditations on First Philosophy","original":"Meditationes de prima philosophia (Latin)","author":"descartes","authorName":"René Descartes","year":1641,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 70 pages in English, plus several hundred pages of Objections and Replies","level":2,"topics":["knowledge","mind","metaphysics","religion"],"summary":"Over six imagined days, Descartes doubts everything that can be doubted, even imagining that an evil demon deceives him. He finds one certainty, that he exists while he thinks, and builds from it proofs that God exists and is no deceiver, that mind and body are distinct, and that the physical world is real.","why":"It set the agenda of modern philosophy: the problem of knowledge, scepticism and the mind-body problem. Critics have pressed hard ever since, for example that his proof of God relies on the very clear thinking that God is supposed to guarantee.","inside":"Six meditations, published with objections from leading thinkers, including Hobbes, Arnauld and Gassendi, and Descartes' replies. The first two meditations, with the demon, the cogito and the piece of wax, are the most famous.","start":"Read Meditations I and II first; they are short and need no background.","line":{"text":"I am, I exist, is necessarily true each time that I pronounce it, or that I mentally conceive it.","where":"Meditation II","tr":"Elizabeth S. Haldane (1911)"},"islands":["descartes"],"related":["elisabeth-correspondence-with-descartes","descartes-discourse-on-method","avicenna-healing"],"shelf":"rationalists","tags":["cogito","evil demon","method of doubt","mind-body dualism","existence of god","wax argument","skepticism","rationalism","dream argument","certainty","clear and distinct ideas","epistemology"]},{"id":"elisabeth-correspondence-with-descartes","title":"Correspondence with Descartes","original":null,"author":"elisabeth","authorName":"Elisabeth of Bohemia","year":1643,"approx":false,"form":"letters","length":"a few dozen letters","level":2,"topics":["mind","metaphysics","ethics"],"summary":"In her first letter the princess asks Descartes a question he never fully answered: how can the soul, which on his view is only a thinking thing with no extension, move the body? Over the following years they also discuss emotions, illness, happiness and how to live well in politics and misfortune.","why":"Her objection is still the classic statement of the problem facing mind-body dualism. Descartes dedicated his Principles of Philosophy to her, and their exchange on the emotions fed into his Passions of the Soul (1649).","inside":"Letters running from 1643 until Descartes' death in 1650. The first exchanges of 1643 on mind-body interaction are the best known.","start":"Read the first three or four letters of 1643; her objection is stated in the very first one.","line":null,"islands":["descartes"],"related":["descartes-meditations","spinoza-ethics"],"shelf":"rationalists","tags":["mind-body problem","interaction problem","mind-body dualism","emotions","passions","happiness","women philosophers","philosophy of mind","stoicism"]},{"id":"hobbes-leviathan","title":"Leviathan","original":null,"author":"hobbes","authorName":"Thomas Hobbes","year":1651,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 500 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["politics","ethics","mind","religion"],"summary":"Writing during and after the English Civil War, Hobbes imagines life without government, a 'state of nature' in which everyone fears everyone else. To escape it, people agree to hand their power to a sovereign whose authority is almost unlimited, because the alternative is endless war. He builds this on a materialist account of human beings as bodies driven by desire and fear.","why":"It founded modern social contract theory: the idea that political authority rests on agreement between individuals. Locke and Rousseau, and later Rawls, all wrote in response to it.","inside":"Four parts: Of Man, Of Commonwealth, Of a Christian Commonwealth, and Of the Kingdom of Darkness. Chapter 13 on the state of nature is the most famous.","start":"Read chapters 13 to 17, from the state of nature to the creation of the commonwealth.","line":{"text":"the life of man, solitary, poore, nasty, brutish, and short.","where":"Chapter 13","tr":"original English"},"islands":["hobbes"],"related":["locke-two-treatises","machiavelli-prince","rousseau-social-contract"],"shelf":"power-law","tags":["social contract","state of nature","war of all against all","nasty brutish and short","sovereignty","absolutism","political authority","materialism","political philosophy","self-interest","fear"]},{"id":"pascal-pensees","title":"Pensées","original":"Pensées (French)","author":"pascal","authorName":"Blaise Pascal","year":1670,"approx":false,"form":"fragments","length":"about 300 pages in English","level":1,"topics":["religion","self","knowledge","life and death"],"summary":"Notes for a defence of Christianity that Pascal did not live to finish. He paints human beings as caught between greatness and wretchedness, lost between the infinitely large and the infinitely small, and distracting themselves to avoid thinking about death. In the famous wager he argues that a reasonable gambler should bet that God exists, since the possible gain is infinite and the possible loss small.","why":"The wager is an early use of probability and decision theory, and is still debated. His portrait of restlessness and distraction anticipates existentialism.","inside":"Hundreds of fragments, published after his death; editors number and order them differently, so check which numbering your edition uses. The wager and the 'thinking reed' are the best-known passages.","start":"Read the fragments on the thinking reed and on diversion, then the wager.","line":{"text":"Man is but a reed, the most feeble thing in nature; but he is a thinking reed.","where":"Fragment 347 (Brunschvicg numbering)","tr":"W. F. Trotter (1910)"},"islands":["pascal"],"related":["montaigne-essays","descartes-meditations"],"shelf":"rationalists","tags":["pascal's wager","existence of god","thinking reed","human condition","diversion","the heart has its reasons","faith","infinity","christianity","death","misery and greatness"]},{"id":"spinoza-ttp","title":"Theological-Political Treatise","original":"Tractatus theologico-politicus (Latin)","author":"spinoza","authorName":"Baruch Spinoza","year":1670,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 250 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["religion","politics","knowledge"],"summary":"Spinoza argues that the Bible should be read like any other old book, by studying its language and history. He concludes that the prophets had vivid imaginations rather than special knowledge, that miracles in the sense of breaks in nature's laws are impossible, and that scripture teaches obedience and charity, not philosophy. The state should therefore leave people free to think and speak.","why":"It is a founding text of modern biblical criticism and of the case for freedom of thought. It was published anonymously and quickly banned, and made Spinoza notorious.","inside":"Twenty chapters: the first fifteen on prophecy, miracles and how to read scripture, the last five on the state. Chapter 20, on freedom of thought and speech, is the best known.","start":"Read the preface and chapter 20, which together state what the book is for.","line":null,"islands":["spinoza"],"related":["spinoza-ethics","maimonides-guide","locke-letter-concerning-toleration"],"shelf":"rationalists","tags":["freedom of speech","freedom of thought","biblical criticism","miracles","prophecy","secularism","democracy","religious tolerance","political philosophy","interpretation of scripture"]},{"id":"cavendish-observations","title":"Observations upon Experimental Philosophy","original":null,"author":null,"authorName":"Margaret Cavendish","year":1666,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 300 pages","level":2,"topics":["science","metaphysics","knowledge","mind"],"summary":"Cavendish challenges the new experimental science of the Royal Society, especially its trust in microscopes, which she argues distort what they show. She defends her own view that nature is made only of matter, but that all matter is self-moving and in some degree perceptive and rational.","why":"She was one of the very few women of her time to publish natural philosophy under her own name, and her criticisms of early science and her view of living, thinking matter are now taken seriously by historians of philosophy.","inside":"A long series of short chapters responding to the scientists of her day, especially Robert Hooke's book on the microscope. It was published together with her imaginative fiction The Blazing World.","start":"Start with the early chapters criticising microscopes, which show her method and her sense of humour.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["bacon-new-organon","descartes-meditations"],"shelf":"new-science","tags":["materialism","panpsychism","vitalism","philosophy of science","critique of experimental science","microscope","self-moving matter","women philosophers","philosophy of nature","perception"]},{"id":"spinoza-ethics","title":"Ethics","original":"Ethica, ordine geometrico demonstrata (Latin)","author":"spinoza","authorName":"Baruch Spinoza","year":1677,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 250 pages in English","level":3,"topics":["metaphysics","ethics","mind","religion"],"summary":"Spinoza argues, step by step like a geometry book, that there is only one substance, which can be called God or Nature. Everything follows necessarily from it, so there is no free will and no purpose in nature. Freedom and happiness come from understanding why things happen, which turns passive emotions into active ones and leads to an 'intellectual love of God'.","why":"It offers one of the boldest systems in philosophy, influencing German thinkers such as Goethe and Hegel and, more recently, debates about emotion and the mind. It was published only after his death because of its radical views.","inside":"Five parts: on God, the mind, the emotions, human bondage, and human freedom. Each is built from definitions, axioms, propositions and proofs. The appendix to Part I, attacking the idea that nature has purposes, is the most readable part.","start":"Read the appendix to Part I first; it is plain prose and shows what the system is fighting against.","line":{"text":"But all things excellent are as difficult as they are rare.","where":"Part V, Proposition 42, Scholium","tr":"R. H. M. Elwes (1883)"},"islands":["spinoza"],"related":["spinoza-ttp","descartes-meditations","leibniz-monadology"],"shelf":"rationalists","tags":["pantheism","god or nature","monism","substance","determinism","free will","emotions","intellectual love of god","geometric method","rationalism","freedom","mind-body problem"]},{"id":"locke-essay","title":"An Essay Concerning Human Understanding","original":null,"author":"locke","authorName":"John Locke","year":1689,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 700 pages","level":2,"topics":["knowledge","mind","language","self"],"summary":"Locke asks what the human mind can know and how. He argues that there are no inborn ideas: all our ideas come from experience, either through the senses or by reflecting on our own thinking. He then traces how complex ideas are built from simple ones, and what this means for language, knowledge and belief.","why":"It founded British empiricism, the view that knowledge rests on experience, and set up the problems Berkeley and Hume worked on. His account of personal identity as continuity of consciousness is still central to debates about the self.","inside":"Four books: against innate ideas; on ideas; on words; on knowledge and opinion. The chapter on personal identity was added to Book II in the second edition (1694). It was published in December 1689 with 1690 on the title page.","start":"Read the first chapters of Book II, where he sets out the 'white paper' picture of the mind.","line":{"text":"Let us then suppose the mind to be, as we say, white paper, void of all characters, without any ideas","where":"Book II, chapter 1, section 2","tr":"original English"},"islands":["locke"],"related":["berkeley-principles","hume-treatise","descartes-meditations"],"shelf":"empiricists","tags":["empiricism","tabula rasa","blank slate","innate ideas","personal identity","primary and secondary qualities","simple and complex ideas","philosophy of language","epistemology","substance"]},{"id":"locke-two-treatises","title":"Two Treatises of Government","original":null,"author":"locke","authorName":"John Locke","year":1689,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 250 pages","level":2,"topics":["politics","ethics","society"],"summary":"The First Treatise attacks Robert Filmer's claim that kings inherit their power from Adam. The Second argues that people are naturally free and equal, with rights to life, liberty and property, and that they set up government by consent to protect those rights. A government that betrays this trust may be resisted and replaced.","why":"It became a key source for the American Declaration of Independence and for liberal ideas of rights and limited government. Its theory of property, based on mixing your labour with nature, is still debated, including its use to justify taking land in the colonies.","inside":"Published anonymously; scholars now think much of it was written several years before the Revolution of 1688. The Second Treatise, especially the chapters on the state of nature and on property, is what most people read.","start":"Read chapters 2 and 5 of the Second Treatise, on the state of nature and on property.","line":{"text":"being all equal and independent, no one ought to harm another in his life, health, liberty, or possessions","where":"Second Treatise, chapter 2, section 6","tr":"original English"},"islands":["locke"],"related":["hobbes-leviathan","locke-letter-concerning-toleration","rousseau-social-contract"],"shelf":"rights","tags":["natural rights","social contract","state of nature","consent of the governed","property","liberalism","right of revolution","life liberty and property","political philosophy","divine right of kings"]},{"id":"locke-letter-concerning-toleration","title":"A Letter Concerning Toleration","original":"Epistola de tolerantia (Latin)","author":"locke","authorName":"John Locke","year":1689,"approx":false,"form":"letters","length":"about 60 pages","level":1,"topics":["religion","politics","society"],"summary":"Locke argues that the state exists only to protect civil goods such as life, liberty and property, while a church is a voluntary society for saving souls. Since force can make people say things but cannot make them believe, the state should not punish religious belief. He still excluded atheists and those whose religion demands loyalty to a foreign ruler, a description widely read as aimed at Catholics.","why":"It is a classic argument for separating church and state and for religious freedom, though critics point out its limits.","inside":"One long letter, written in Latin in exile in Holland and translated into English by William Popple in the same year.","start":"Read it straight through; it is short and the argument about belief and force comes early.","line":null,"islands":["locke"],"related":["spinoza-ttp","locke-two-treatises","luther-freedom-of-a-christian"],"shelf":"rights","tags":["religious tolerance","separation of church and state","freedom of religion","liberalism","political philosophy","conscience","belief cannot be coerced"]},{"id":"astell-serious-proposal","title":"A Serious Proposal to the Ladies","original":null,"author":null,"authorName":"Mary Astell","year":1694,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 200 pages for both parts","level":1,"topics":["society","knowledge","religion","self"],"summary":"Astell argues that women seem less capable than men only because they are denied education. She proposes a kind of academic retreat where women could study, think and grow in faith away from the pressures of fashion and marriage. Part II gives a method for thinking clearly, drawing on Descartes and the logic of Port-Royal.","why":"It is one of the earliest English arguments for women's education based on the equality of minds, and it anticipates later feminist thinkers such as Mary Wollstonecraft.","inside":"Two parts: Part I (1694) sets out the proposal; Part II (1697) is a guide to good reasoning. Part I is the best known.","start":"Read Part I, which is short, lively and states the main argument.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["christine-city-of-ladies","descartes-discourse-on-method","wollstonecraft-vindication-woman"],"shelf":"rights","tags":["feminism","education of women","women","rationalism","reasoning","marriage","religion","women philosophers","gender equality"]},{"id":"leibniz-theodicy","title":"Theodicy","original":"Essais de Théodicée sur la bonté de Dieu, la liberté de l'homme et l'origine du mal (French)","author":"leibniz","authorName":"Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz","year":1710,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 450 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["religion","metaphysics","ethics"],"summary":"Leibniz answers the sceptic Pierre Bayle, who had argued that reason cannot reconcile God's goodness with the evil in the world. Leibniz argues that God, being wise and good, chose the best of all possible worlds; evils exist because a world without them would be worse overall. He also defends human freedom against fate.","why":"It gave the problem of evil its modern name ('theodicy' means justifying God) and provoked Voltaire's satire Candide. It is usually described as the only full-length philosophical book Leibniz published in his lifetime.","inside":"A preface, a discussion of how faith fits with reason, and three long parts on God's goodness, freedom and evil. The claim that this is the best possible world is the best-known idea.","start":"Read the preliminary discussion and the opening of Part I, where the best-world argument is set out.","line":null,"islands":["leibniz"],"related":["leibniz-monadology","voltaire-candide"],"shelf":"germany-enl","tags":["theodicy","problem of evil","best of all possible worlds","optimism","free will","faith and reason","god","philosophy of religion","possible worlds","determinism"]},{"id":"leibniz-monadology","title":"Monadology","original":"La Monadologie (French; the title was added by later editors)","author":"leibniz","authorName":"Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz","year":1714,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 20 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["metaphysics","mind","religion"],"summary":"A compact summary of Leibniz's mature system. Reality is made of 'monads', simple, unextended substances that perceive. They do not really act on one another; each unfolds its own perceptions, kept in step by a harmony God set up from the start.","why":"It shows how far pure reasoning can push metaphysics, and its 'mill' argument, that walking inside a thinking machine you would find only parts pushing parts and never a perception, is still cited in debates about consciousness.","inside":"Ninety short numbered sections. Leibniz wrote it in French and never published it; it first appeared in a German translation in 1720. The 'windows' and the 'mill' passages are the most famous.","start":"Read sections 1 to 30 first; they set out what monads are and include the mill argument.","line":{"text":"The Monads have no windows, through which anything could come in or go out.","where":"Section 7","tr":"Robert Latta (1898)"},"islands":["leibniz"],"related":["leibniz-theodicy","spinoza-ethics"],"shelf":"germany-enl","tags":["monads","pre-established harmony","principle of sufficient reason","mill argument","rationalism","metaphysics","substance","perception","best of all possible worlds","god","consciousness"]},{"id":"berkeley-principles","title":"A Treatise Concerning the Principles of Human Knowledge","original":null,"author":"berkeley","authorName":"George Berkeley","year":1710,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 100 pages","level":2,"topics":["metaphysics","knowledge","mind","religion"],"summary":"Berkeley argues that the objects we perceive are nothing but collections of ideas, so to exist is to be perceived (or to perceive). There is no 'matter' behind our ideas; the order of nature comes from God, who perceives everything continually. He presents this as the cure for scepticism and atheism.","why":"It is the classic defence of idealism, the view that reality is fundamentally mental. Many readers find it absurd at first, yet his arguments against Locke's picture of matter have proved hard to refute.","inside":"An introduction attacking 'abstract ideas', followed by Part I (the only part he published), in numbered sections that state his view and then answer objections.","start":"Read the first 33 sections of Part I, where the whole position is set out.","line":{"text":"Their esse is percipi, nor is it possible they should have any existence out of the minds or thinking things which perceive them.","where":"Part I, section 3","tr":"original English"},"islands":["berkeley"],"related":["berkeley-three-dialogues","locke-essay","hume-treatise"],"shelf":"empiricists","tags":["idealism","immaterialism","esse est percipi","to be is to be perceived","abstract ideas","empiricism","god","critique of matter","skepticism","metaphysics"]},{"id":"berkeley-three-dialogues","title":"Three Dialogues between Hylas and Philonous","original":null,"author":"berkeley","authorName":"George Berkeley","year":1713,"approx":false,"form":"dialogue","length":"about 100 pages","level":1,"topics":["metaphysics","knowledge","mind"],"summary":"Berkeley restates the case of his Principles as a friendly argument between Hylas, who believes in matter, and Philonous ('lover of mind'), who does not. Step by step Philonous shows that heat, colour, shape and the rest exist only as perceived, and Hylas is brought to agree that there is no need for matter.","why":"Written because the Principles had been ridiculed or ignored, it is the most approachable route into idealism and a model of philosophical dialogue.","inside":"Three dialogues. The first, which begins with the argument that great heat is just a kind of pain, is the most famous.","start":"Read the First Dialogue; it does most of the work and needs no background.","line":null,"islands":["berkeley"],"related":["berkeley-principles","locke-essay"],"shelf":"empiricists","tags":["idealism","immaterialism","esse est percipi","to be is to be perceived","primary and secondary qualities","empiricism","critique of matter","perception","dialogue"]},{"id":"hume-treatise","title":"A Treatise of Human Nature","original":null,"author":"hume","authorName":"David Hume","year":1739,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 600 pages","level":3,"topics":["knowledge","mind","ethics","self"],"summary":"Hume tries to build a science of human nature by observation, much as Newton did for physics. He argues that all ideas come from impressions, that our belief in cause and effect rests on habit rather than reason, that the self is only a bundle of perceptions, and that morality comes from feeling, not reason.","why":"It contains many of the most discussed arguments in modern philosophy, including the problem of induction and the gap between 'is' and 'ought'. Hume later complained it fell dead-born from the press and rewrote parts of it in plainer books.","inside":"Three books: Of the Understanding and Of the Passions (1739), and Of Morals (1740), published anonymously. Book I's sections on causation and personal identity are the most famous.","start":"Start with the short Abstract Hume wrote to explain the book, then Book I, Part III, on causation.","line":{"text":"Reason is, and ought only to be the slave of the passions","where":"Book II, Part III, section 3","tr":"original English"},"islands":["hume"],"related":["hume-enquiry","locke-essay","kant-critique-pure-reason"],"shelf":"empiricists","tags":["empiricism","impressions and ideas","causation","bundle theory","personal identity","is-ought problem","reason is the slave of the passions","moral sentiment","skepticism","the self","induction"]},{"id":"hume-enquiry","title":"An Enquiry Concerning Human Understanding","original":null,"author":"hume","authorName":"David Hume","year":1748,"approx":false,"form":"essays","length":"about 150 pages","level":2,"topics":["knowledge","religion","science","mind"],"summary":"A shorter, clearer reworking of Book I of the Treatise. Hume argues that we cannot justify by reason our expectation that the future will resemble the past, and that the idea of necessary connection between cause and effect comes from habit. He also argues that it is almost never reasonable to believe reports of miracles.","why":"Kant said that remembering Hume interrupted his 'dogmatic slumber'. The problem of induction it states is still unsolved to many philosophers' satisfaction.","inside":"Twelve sections; first published as Philosophical Essays Concerning Human Understanding, it took its present title in 1758. Sections IV, VII and X (on miracles) are the best known.","start":"Read Sections II to V, which set out the theory of ideas and the problem of induction.","line":{"text":"Commit it then to the flames: for it can contain nothing but sophistry and illusion.","where":"Section XII, Part III","tr":"original English"},"islands":["hume"],"related":["hume-treatise","alghazali-incoherence","kant-critique-pure-reason"],"shelf":"empiricists","tags":["problem of induction","causation","necessary connection","empiricism","miracles","skepticism","hume's fork","custom and habit","free will","epistemology"]},{"id":"montesquieu-spirit-of-the-laws","title":"The Spirit of the Laws","original":"De l'esprit des lois (French)","author":null,"authorName":"Montesquieu","year":1748,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 750 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["politics","society","science"],"summary":"Montesquieu argues that laws are not arbitrary but fit a people's form of government, climate, religion, trade and customs. He sorts governments into republics, run on virtue, monarchies, run on honour, and despotisms, run on fear. Liberty is safest where legislative, executive and judicial power are held by separate bodies, as he believed they were in England.","why":"His idea of the separation of powers shaped the United States Constitution and many later constitutions. His comparative method helped found the social sciences, though his claims about climate now look crude.","inside":"Thirty-one books, published anonymously in Geneva. Book XI, chapter 6, on the English constitution, is the best-known part.","start":"Read Books I to III on the kinds of government, then Book XI, chapter 6.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["locke-two-treatises","machiavelli-discourses","rousseau-social-contract"],"shelf":"continental-enl","tags":["separation of powers","checks and balances","forms of government","despotism","liberty","climate","political philosophy","law","republicanism","enlightenment"]},{"id":"rousseau-discourse-inequality","title":"Discourse on the Origin of Inequality","original":"Discours sur l'origine et les fondements de l'inégalité parmi les hommes (French)","author":"rousseau","authorName":"Jean-Jacques Rousseau","year":1755,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 100 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["politics","society","self"],"summary":"Rousseau imagines humans in a 'state of nature' before society: solitary, simple and moved by self-love and pity rather than greed. He argues that most inequality is not natural but grows out of settled life, private property, comparison with others and laws that protect the rich.","why":"It turned the question of inequality into a question about history and institutions rather than nature, and it shaped later radical thought, including Marx. Critics reply that his picture of early humans is guesswork.","inside":"It was written for a prize competition set by the Academy of Dijon (it did not win). Part One describes natural humans; Part Two tells the story of how society, property and inequality arose.","start":"Start with the opening of Part Two, a short and vivid passage that states the whole argument in miniature.","line":{"text":"The first man who, having enclosed a piece of ground, bethought himself of saying This is mine, and found people simple enough to believe him, was the real founder of civil society.","where":"Part Two, opening","tr":"G. D. H. Cole"},"islands":["rousseau"],"related":["rousseau-social-contract","rousseau-emile","hobbes-leviathan"],"shelf":"continental-enl","tags":["inequality","state of nature","noble savage","private property","amour-propre","pity","civilisation","political philosophy","social critique","enlightenment"]},{"id":"rousseau-social-contract","title":"The Social Contract","original":"Du contrat social ou Principes du droit politique (French)","author":"rousseau","authorName":"Jean-Jacques Rousseau","year":1762,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 150 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["politics","society","ethics"],"summary":"Rousseau asks how people can live under laws and still be free. His answer is that legitimate laws must come from the 'general will', the shared will of citizens aiming at the common good, so that in obeying the law each person obeys a rule they have given themselves.","why":"It is a founding text for the idea of popular sovereignty, the view that ultimate political authority belongs to the people. Critics from Benjamin Constant onward have worried that the general will can be used to justify crushing individual dissent.","inside":"Four short books: Book I on the social pact, Book II on sovereignty and law, Book III on forms of government, Book IV on voting and civil religion. Its best-known part is the opening chapter and the claim that someone may be 'forced to be free'.","start":"Read Book I straight through: it is short and contains the core idea of the whole work.","line":{"text":"Man is born free; and everywhere he is in chains.","where":"Book I, Chapter 1","tr":"G. D. H. Cole"},"islands":["rousseau"],"related":["rousseau-discourse-inequality","kant-perpetual-peace"],"shelf":"continental-enl","tags":["social contract","general will","sovereignty","forced to be free","man is born free","popular sovereignty","democracy","freedom","civil religion","political philosophy"]},{"id":"rousseau-emile","title":"Emile, or On Education","original":"Émile, ou De l'éducation (French)","author":"rousseau","authorName":"Jean-Jacques Rousseau","year":1762,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 450 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["self","society","religion","ethics"],"summary":"Through the story of an imaginary pupil, Rousseau argues that education should follow the child's natural development, protect them from the corrupting habits of society and let them learn from experience rather than books. It ends with the education of Sophie, Emile's future wife, whom he trains to please and serve.","why":"It changed thinking about childhood as a stage with its own needs and influenced later educators such as Pestalozzi. Its treatment of girls drew a direct reply from Mary Wollstonecraft, and its religious section led to the book being condemned in Paris and Geneva.","inside":"Five books follow Emile from infancy to marriage. Book IV contains the 'Profession of Faith of the Savoyard Vicar', a defence of natural religion without church authority.","start":"Begin with the opening pages of Book I, which set out Rousseau's view of nature and society, then dip into the Savoyard Vicar in Book IV.","line":{"text":"God makes all things good; man meddles with them and they become evil.","where":"Book I, opening","tr":"Barbara Foxley"},"islands":["rousseau","wollstonecraft"],"related":["rousseau-social-contract","wollstonecraft-vindication-woman"],"shelf":"continental-enl","tags":["education","philosophy of education","natural education","childhood","natural religion","savoyard vicar","gender roles","enlightenment","human nature"]},{"id":"burke-sublime-beautiful","title":"A Philosophical Enquiry into the Origin of Our Ideas of the Sublime and Beautiful","original":null,"author":"burke","authorName":"Edmund Burke","year":1757,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 150 pages","level":2,"topics":["beauty","mind"],"summary":"Burke separates two kinds of aesthetic experience. The beautiful pleases us through qualities like smallness, smoothness and gentle variation, and is tied to love; the sublime overwhelms us through vastness, darkness, power and danger, and is tied to a kind of 'delightful horror' felt when we are safe from real harm.","why":"It made the sublime a central topic in aesthetics and influenced Kant's later account, as well as Romantic painting and poetry. It is also an early attempt to explain taste by looking at the body and the passions rather than at rules of art.","inside":"Five parts, made up of many short numbered sections. Part II, on the sublime and its sources such as terror, obscurity and power, is the best known.","start":"Start with Part I's short sections on pain and pleasure, then read Part II on the sublime.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["kant-critique-judgement","hume-standard-of-taste"],"shelf":"sentiment","tags":["sublime","beauty","aesthetics","terror","emotions","delightful horror","taste","romanticism","art"]},{"id":"hume-standard-of-taste","title":"Of the Standard of Taste","original":null,"author":"hume","authorName":"David Hume","year":1757,"approx":false,"form":"essays","length":"about 20 pages","level":1,"topics":["beauty","knowledge"],"summary":"Hume admits that taste in art varies widely and that beauty lies in the feelings of the observer, yet he argues that some judgements are still better than others. The standard of taste is the shared verdict of 'true judges' who have delicate perception, practice, a wide range of comparison, good sense and freedom from prejudice.","why":"It is still the classic starting point for the debate over whether aesthetic judgements can be objective. Critics ask how we can pick out the true judges without already knowing which works are good.","inside":"A single essay, first published in Hume's Four Dissertations. Its best-known passage is the story from Don Quixote of two wine tasters who detect a faint taste of leather and iron, later explained by a key on a leather strap found in the barrel.","start":"Read it whole in one sitting; it is short and clearly argued.","line":{"text":"Strong sense, united to delicate sentiment, improved by practice, perfected by comparison, and cleared of all prejudice, can alone entitle critics to this valuable character","where":"on the qualities of a true judge","tr":"original English"},"islands":["hume"],"related":["burke-sublime-beautiful","kant-critique-judgement"],"shelf":"sentiment","tags":["aesthetics","taste","beauty","aesthetic judgement","true judges","subjectivity","art criticism","relativism"]},{"id":"smith-moral-sentiments","title":"The Theory of Moral Sentiments","original":null,"author":"smith","authorName":"Adam Smith","year":1759,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 400 pages","level":2,"topics":["ethics","self","society"],"summary":"Smith explains morality through sympathy: our ability to imagine ourselves in another person's place and share their feelings. We judge our own conduct by imagining how an 'impartial spectator' would see it, and over time this imagined spectator becomes our conscience.","why":"It shows that the author of The Wealth of Nations did not think people are moved only by self-interest. It is now read alongside Hume as a major account of how moral judgement grows out of social life.","inside":"Seven parts, revised several times; the sixth edition of 1790 added a new Part VI on virtue. The opening chapters on sympathy and the discussion of the impartial spectator in Part III are the best known.","start":"Start with Part I, Section I, Chapter 1, which sets out the idea of sympathy in plain, concrete examples.","line":{"text":"How selfish soever man may be supposed, there are evidently some principles in his nature, which interest him in the fortune of others, and render their happiness necessary to him, though he derives nothing from it except the pleasure of seeing it.","where":"Part I, Section I, Chapter 1","tr":"original English"},"islands":["wollstonecraft"],"related":["smith-wealth-of-nations","hume-treatise"],"shelf":"sentiment","tags":["sympathy","impartial spectator","moral sentiment","conscience","empathy","virtue","moral psychology","ethics","scottish enlightenment"]},{"id":"voltaire-candide","title":"Candide, or Optimism","original":"Candide, ou l'Optimisme (French)","author":"voltaire","authorName":"Voltaire","year":1759,"approx":false,"form":"novel","length":"about 100 pages in English","level":1,"topics":["religion","life and death","ethics"],"summary":"A comic novel in which the innocent Candide and his tutor Pangloss, who insists that all is for the best in the best of all possible worlds, suffer war, earthquake, shipwreck and cruelty across the globe. The story mocks philosophical optimism, the view linked with Leibniz that this world is the best God could have made.","why":"It remains the best-known literary attack on attempts to explain away suffering, written partly in response to the Lisbon earthquake of 1755. Defenders of Leibniz point out that Pangloss is a caricature of his actual position.","inside":"Thirty short, fast-moving chapters. It ends with the characters settling on a small farm and Candide's advice that we should cultivate our garden.","start":"Read it from the start; the chapters are short and the jokes depend on the build-up.","line":null,"islands":["leibniz"],"related":["leibniz-theodicy","hume-dialogues"],"shelf":"continental-enl","tags":["optimism","best of all possible worlds","problem of evil","satire","cultivate our garden","suffering","enlightenment","lisbon earthquake","theodicy"]},{"id":"smith-wealth-of-nations","title":"The Wealth of Nations","original":null,"author":"smith","authorName":"Adam Smith","year":1776,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 1,000 pages","level":2,"topics":["politics","society","ethics"],"summary":"Smith asks what makes nations rich. He argues that wealth comes from productive labour, increased by the division of labour, and that free exchange in markets usually serves the public good better than government controls, monopolies and trade restrictions.","why":"It is a founding text of economics and of the case for free markets. Smith also warned about merchants conspiring against the public and about the dulling effect of repetitive work, points often left out by later admirers and critics alike.","inside":"Five books, starting with the division of labour and ending with the duties and revenue of the state. The pin factory example in Book I, Chapter 1 is the best-known passage; the famous 'invisible hand' appears only once, in Book IV.","start":"Read the first two chapters of Book I, which explain the division of labour and why people trade.","line":{"text":"It is not from the benevolence of the butcher, the brewer, or the baker, that we expect our dinner, but from their regard to their own interest.","where":"Book I, Chapter 2","tr":"original English"},"islands":["wollstonecraft"],"related":["smith-moral-sentiments","marx-capital"],"shelf":"rights","tags":["invisible hand","free market","capitalism","division of labour","economics","self-interest","free trade","pin factory","political economy","laissez-faire"]},{"id":"hume-dialogues","title":"Dialogues Concerning Natural Religion","original":null,"author":"hume","authorName":"David Hume","year":1779,"approx":false,"form":"dialogue","length":"about 100 pages","level":2,"topics":["religion","knowledge","metaphysics"],"summary":"Three characters, Cleanthes, Demea and Philo, debate whether we can know God through reason. Cleanthes defends the argument from design, which says the order of nature shows an intelligent maker; Philo, the sceptic, shows how weak the analogy is and presses the problem of evil.","why":"It is still the most important critique of the design argument, written before Darwin offered a natural explanation of biological order. Hume worked on it for decades but arranged for it to appear only after his death.","inside":"Twelve parts. Parts II to VIII take apart the design argument; Parts X and XI deal with suffering and evil; Part XII contains a puzzling final turn that readers still argue over.","start":"Start with Part II, where Cleanthes states the design argument and Philo begins his reply.","line":{"text":"Is he willing to prevent evil, but not able? then is he impotent. Is he able, but not willing? then is he malevolent. Is he both able and willing? whence then is evil?","where":"Part X (Philo, repeating Epicurus' old questions)","tr":"original English"},"islands":["hume"],"related":["hume-enquiry","darwin-origin-of-species","voltaire-candide"],"shelf":"empiricists","tags":["argument from design","existence of god","problem of evil","natural religion","skepticism","philosophy of religion","analogy","cosmological argument","natural theology"]},{"id":"kant-critique-pure-reason","title":"Critique of Pure Reason","original":"Kritik der reinen Vernunft (German)","author":"kant","authorName":"Immanuel Kant","year":1781,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 700 pages in English","level":3,"topics":["knowledge","metaphysics","mind"],"summary":"Kant asks what reason can know without relying on experience. He argues that space, time and basic concepts like cause are forms the mind brings to experience, so we can have certain knowledge of the world as it appears to us but never of things as they are in themselves.","why":"It tried to settle the dispute between rationalists and empiricists and set the agenda for almost all later philosophy, including Hegel and modern debates about the mind's role in shaping experience. Its claim that traditional metaphysics oversteps the limits of reason remains hotly contested.","inside":"After the Introduction come the Transcendental Aesthetic (space and time), the Analytic (the categories) and the Dialectic (where reason tangles itself in contradictions, the 'antinomies'). A heavily revised second edition appeared in 1787.","start":"Start with the Preface to the second edition, where Kant explains his 'Copernican' idea that objects must conform to our knowledge, then read the Introduction.","line":null,"islands":["kant"],"related":["hume-enquiry","kant-critique-practical-reason","kant-critique-judgement"],"shelf":"germany-enl","tags":["transcendental idealism","things in themselves","synthetic a priori","categories","space and time","antinomies","copernican revolution","phenomena and noumena","epistemology","metaphysics","limits of reason","rationalism vs empiricism"]},{"id":"kant-what-is-enlightenment","title":"An Answer to the Question: What is Enlightenment?","original":"Beantwortung der Frage: Was ist Aufklärung? (German)","author":"kant","authorName":"Immanuel Kant","year":1784,"approx":false,"form":"essays","length":"about 10 pages in English","level":1,"topics":["politics","knowledge","self"],"summary":"Kant defines enlightenment as humanity's way out of a self-imposed immaturity: the habit of letting priests, officials and books do our thinking for us. He calls on people to dare to use their own understanding and argues that rulers should allow free public use of reason.","why":"It is the classic short statement of Enlightenment ideals and is still cited in debates about free speech and intellectual independence. Michel Foucault later wrote a well-known reply with the same title.","inside":"A single short essay published in a Berlin monthly magazine. Its best-known part is the opening definition and the motto 'Sapere aude', dare to know.","start":"Read it whole; it is the easiest way into Kant.","line":null,"islands":["kant"],"related":["kant-groundwork","mill-on-liberty"],"shelf":"germany-enl","tags":["enlightenment","sapere aude","dare to know","autonomy","public use of reason","freedom of thought","immaturity","political philosophy"]},{"id":"kant-groundwork","title":"Groundwork of the Metaphysics of Morals","original":"Grundlegung zur Metaphysik der Sitten (German)","author":"kant","authorName":"Immanuel Kant","year":1785,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 80 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["ethics","self"],"summary":"Kant searches for the supreme principle of morality. He argues that an action has moral worth only when done from duty, and that duty is set by the 'categorical imperative': act only on principles you could will everyone to follow, and treat humanity never merely as a means but always also as an end.","why":"It is the founding text of deontology, the view that some acts are right or wrong regardless of their consequences, and the main rival to utilitarianism. Its idea of human dignity runs through modern thinking about human rights.","inside":"A preface and three sections, moving from ordinary moral thinking to philosophy and then to the question of freedom. The Second Section, with the different formulas of the categorical imperative and its four examples, is the best known.","start":"Read the First Section, on the good will and duty: it starts from everyday moral intuitions.","line":{"text":"Nothing can possibly be conceived in the world, or even out of it, which can be called good without qualification, except a Good Will.","where":"First Section, opening","tr":"Thomas Kingsmill Abbott"},"islands":["kant"],"related":["kant-critique-practical-reason","mill-utilitarianism","bentham-principles-morals-legislation"],"shelf":"germany-enl","tags":["categorical imperative","deontology","duty","good will","universalizability","humanity as an end in itself","autonomy","moral law","hypothetical imperative","kingdom of ends","ethics"]},{"id":"kant-critique-practical-reason","title":"Critique of Practical Reason","original":"Kritik der praktischen Vernunft (German)","author":"kant","authorName":"Immanuel Kant","year":1788,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 200 pages in English","level":3,"topics":["ethics","religion","self"],"summary":"Kant argues that the moral law is a 'fact of reason' that shows we are free. He then argues that, to make sense of morality's demand that virtue and happiness go together, we must assume freedom, the immortality of the soul and the existence of God, even though theoretical reason cannot prove them.","why":"It completes Kant's moral philosophy and gives his distinctive answer to how religion relates to morality: faith rests on moral need, not on proof. Many readers find the postulates of God and immortality the least convincing part of his system.","inside":"An Analytic, on the moral law and moral motivation, and a Dialectic, on the 'highest good'. The short Conclusion is the best-known passage.","start":"Read the Conclusion first for its spirit, then the opening of the Analytic once you know the Groundwork.","line":{"text":"Two things fill the mind with ever new and increasing admiration and awe, the oftener and the more steadily we reflect on them: the starry heavens above and the moral law within.","where":"Conclusion","tr":"Thomas Kingsmill Abbott"},"islands":["kant"],"related":["kant-groundwork","kant-critique-pure-reason"],"shelf":"germany-enl","tags":["moral law","deontology","free will","fact of reason","highest good","immortality","existence of god","postulates of practical reason","autonomy","starry heavens above","ethics"]},{"id":"kant-critique-judgement","title":"Critique of Judgement","original":"Kritik der Urteilskraft (German)","author":"kant","authorName":"Immanuel Kant","year":1790,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 350 pages in English","level":3,"topics":["beauty","science","mind"],"summary":"Kant examines judgements of beauty and of purpose in nature. He argues that a judgement of beauty rests on a feeling of disinterested pleasure yet claims everyone's agreement, and that the sublime reveals the power of our reason over overwhelming nature; the second half asks why we cannot help seeing living things as if designed.","why":"It is the most influential work in modern aesthetics and shaped Romanticism and later art theory. Its account of living things is still discussed in the philosophy of biology.","inside":"Two parts: the Critique of Aesthetic Judgement (the beautiful and the sublime) and the Critique of Teleological Judgement (purpose in nature). The 'Analytic of the Beautiful' at the start is the best-known part.","start":"Start with the first few sections of the Analytic of the Beautiful, which define a judgement of taste.","line":null,"islands":["kant"],"related":["burke-sublime-beautiful","hume-standard-of-taste","kant-critique-pure-reason"],"shelf":"germany-enl","tags":["aesthetics","beauty","sublime","aesthetic judgement","disinterested pleasure","taste","teleology","purposiveness","genius","philosophy of biology"]},{"id":"kant-perpetual-peace","title":"Perpetual Peace: A Philosophical Sketch","original":"Zum ewigen Frieden. Ein philosophischer Entwurf (German)","author":"kant","authorName":"Immanuel Kant","year":1795,"approx":false,"form":"essays","length":"about 50 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["politics","ethics","society"],"summary":"Written in the form of a peace treaty, it sets out conditions for ending war between states for good. Kant proposes that states should have republican constitutions, join a voluntary federation of free states, and grant foreigners a right of 'hospitality', meaning they may visit without being treated as enemies.","why":"It is often cited as an ancestor of the League of Nations, the United Nations and the 'democratic peace' idea that republics rarely fight each other, though scholars debate how close the link is. It also shows Kant's political thought in compact form.","inside":"Six preliminary articles (such as no standing armies in the long run), three definitive articles, two supplements and appendices on morality and politics. The three definitive articles are the core.","start":"Read the three definitive articles, which take only a few pages and contain the main proposal.","line":null,"islands":["kant"],"related":["kant-what-is-enlightenment","rousseau-social-contract"],"shelf":"germany-enl","tags":["perpetual peace","war and peace","cosmopolitanism","international relations","republicanism","league of nations","democratic peace","hospitality","political philosophy"]},{"id":"bentham-principles-morals-legislation","title":"An Introduction to the Principles of Morals and Legislation","original":null,"author":"bentham","authorName":"Jeremy Bentham","year":1789,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 350 pages","level":2,"topics":["ethics","politics"],"summary":"Bentham argues that the right action and the right law are those that produce the greatest balance of pleasure over pain for everyone affected. He offers a method for measuring pleasures and pains by their intensity, duration, certainty, nearness and other features, and applies it to punishment and law.","why":"It is the first systematic statement of classical utilitarianism and pushed for legal reform based on consequences rather than tradition. A famous footnote extending moral concern to animals because they can suffer is still cited in animal ethics.","inside":"Seventeen chapters, moving from the principle of utility to motives, intentions and the classification of offences. It was printed in 1780 but not published until 1789. Chapter 1 and the footnote on animals in Chapter 17 are the best known.","start":"Read Chapter 1, 'Of the Principle of Utility', which is short and lays out the whole view.","line":{"text":"Nature has placed mankind under the governance of two sovereign masters, pain and pleasure.","where":"Chapter 1, opening","tr":"original English"},"islands":["mill","singer"],"related":["mill-utilitarianism","sidgwick-methods-ethics","kant-groundwork"],"shelf":"rights","tags":["utilitarianism","principle of utility","greatest happiness principle","hedonic calculus","pleasure","consequentialism","punishment","animal rights","law","hedonism"]},{"id":"burke-reflections","title":"Reflections on the Revolution in France","original":null,"author":"burke","authorName":"Edmund Burke","year":1790,"approx":false,"form":"letters","length":"about 300 pages","level":2,"topics":["politics","society"],"summary":"Written as a long letter to a young Frenchman, it attacks the French Revolution for tearing down institutions built up over centuries in the name of abstract rights. Burke argues that society is a partnership between the living, the dead and the unborn, and that reform should be gradual and respect inherited customs.","why":"It is usually seen as the founding text of modern conservatism. It provoked famous replies from Thomas Paine and Mary Wollstonecraft, who accused Burke of defending privilege.","inside":"One continuous letter with no chapter divisions. The passage lamenting the treatment of Marie Antoinette and the end of the 'age of chivalry' is the most quoted.","start":"Read the pages on the 'partnership' of generations and on inherited rights, which give the heart of Burke's case.","line":{"text":"But the age of chivalry is gone. That of sophisters, economists, and calculators, has succeeded; and the glory of Europe is extinguished for ever.","where":"passage on Marie Antoinette","tr":"original English (modern spelling)"},"islands":[],"related":["paine-rights-of-man","wollstonecraft-vindication-woman"],"shelf":"rights","tags":["conservatism","french revolution","tradition","gradual reform","critique of abstract rights","social contract","political philosophy","society","prejudice"]},{"id":"paine-rights-of-man","title":"Rights of Man","original":null,"author":"paine","authorName":"Thomas Paine","year":1791,"approx":false,"form":"pamphlet","length":"about 250 pages","level":1,"topics":["politics","society"],"summary":"Paine defends the French Revolution against Burke, arguing that each generation has the right to govern itself and that rights belong to every person by nature, not by inheritance or royal grant. The second part sets out practical plans, including public support for the poor, the old and children, paid for by taxing large estates.","why":"It was one of the most widely read political works of its time and helped spread the language of universal rights. Its proposals anticipate parts of the modern welfare state; the British government prosecuted Paine for seditious libel.","inside":"Part One (1791) answers Burke directly; Part Two (1792) turns to constructive proposals. Chapter 5 of Part Two, with its welfare plans, is the most original.","start":"Start with the opening of Part One, where Paine attacks the idea that one generation can bind the next.","line":{"text":"My country is the world, and my religion is to do good.","where":"Part Two, Chapter 5","tr":"original English"},"islands":[],"related":["burke-reflections","wollstonecraft-vindication-woman"],"shelf":"rights","tags":["natural rights","human rights","french revolution","republicanism","welfare state","democracy","critique of monarchy","political philosophy","radicalism"]},{"id":"wollstonecraft-vindication-woman","title":"A Vindication of the Rights of Woman","original":null,"author":"wollstonecraft","authorName":"Mary Wollstonecraft","year":1792,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 250 pages","level":1,"topics":["ethics","politics","society","self"],"summary":"Wollstonecraft argues that women seem weaker in reason only because they are educated to be pleasing and dependent. If women are rational beings, they deserve the same education and the chance to develop virtue as independent people, which would also make them better citizens, partners and mothers.","why":"It is one of the earliest major works of feminist philosophy. It applies Enlightenment ideas of reason and rights to women and takes on Rousseau's account of female education in Emile.","inside":"Thirteen chapters, dedicated to the French statesman Talleyrand. Chapter 5, a sustained attack on writers like Rousseau who held women in contempt, is the best known.","start":"Start with the Introduction and Chapter 2, where she sets out her case about education and reason.","line":{"text":"I do not wish them to have power over men; but over themselves.","where":"Chapter 4","tr":"original English"},"islands":["wollstonecraft"],"related":["rousseau-emile","mill-subjection-women","burke-reflections"],"shelf":"rights","tags":["feminism","women's rights","education of women","gender equality","reason","virtue","independence","critique of rousseau","enlightenment","political philosophy"]},{"id":"hegel-phenomenology","title":"Phenomenology of Spirit","original":"Phänomenologie des Geistes (German)","author":"hegel","authorName":"G. W. F. Hegel","year":1807,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 600 pages in English","level":3,"topics":["knowledge","mind","self","society"],"summary":"Hegel traces the journey of consciousness from simple sense-certainty to 'absolute knowing'. Each stage proves inadequate by its own standards and gives way to a richer one, and along the way he argues that self-consciousness depends on recognition by others and that knowledge is shaped by history and culture.","why":"It introduced ideas that shaped Marx, existentialism, critical theory and modern debates about recognition and identity. It is also notoriously difficult, and readers disagree sharply about how to interpret it.","inside":"It moves through Consciousness, Self-Consciousness, Reason, Spirit, Religion and Absolute Knowing. The section on 'lordship and bondage', often called the master and slave dialectic, is the best-known part.","start":"Read the lordship and bondage section with a guide; it is short and its influence is easy to trace.","line":null,"islands":["hegel"],"related":["hegel-philosophy-of-right","kant-critique-pure-reason","marx-capital"],"shelf":"hegel-marx","tags":["master-slave dialectic","dialectic","self-consciousness","recognition","spirit","absolute knowing","german idealism","consciousness","unhappy consciousness","history"]},{"id":"schopenhauer-world-as-will","title":"The World as Will and Representation","original":"Die Welt als Wille und Vorstellung (German)","author":"schopenhauer","authorName":"Arthur Schopenhauer","year":1818,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 550 pages in English for Volume I; Volume II is longer","level":2,"topics":["metaphysics","life and death","beauty","ethics"],"summary":"Building on Kant, Schopenhauer argues that the world we perceive is only our representation, while the inner reality of everything is a blind, endless striving he calls 'will'. Because will always wants more, life is mostly suffering; relief comes briefly through art, especially music, and more deeply through compassion and the denial of the will.","why":"It was one of the first major Western works to draw seriously on Indian thought, and it influenced Nietzsche, Wagner, Tolstoy and later thinkers about the unconscious. Its pessimism remains a standing challenge to optimistic views of life.","inside":"Volume I has four books: representation, will, art and ethics. Volume II, added in 1844, has supplementary essays on each book. Book IV on suffering and compassion is the best known. The first volume appeared in late 1818, though its title page says 1819.","start":"Start with Book IV in Volume I, which is the most direct and gives his view of life and ethics.","line":{"text":"The world is my idea","where":"Volume I, Book I, §1","tr":"R. B. Haldane and J. Kemp"},"islands":["schopenhauer"],"related":["kant-critique-pure-reason","nietzsche-gay-science"],"shelf":"hegel-marx","tags":["will","pessimism","suffering","representation","aesthetics","music","compassion","denial of the will","asceticism","metaphysics","buddhism"]},{"id":"hegel-philosophy-of-right","title":"Elements of the Philosophy of Right","original":"Grundlinien der Philosophie des Rechts (German)","author":"hegel","authorName":"G. W. F. Hegel","year":1820,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 400 pages in English","level":3,"topics":["politics","ethics","society"],"summary":"Hegel argues that freedom is not just doing what you like but is realised in social institutions. He moves from property and contract, through individual conscience, to 'ethical life': the family, civil society (the world of work and markets) and the state, which he sees as the fullest expression of freedom.","why":"It shaped later political thought on both left and right, and Marx's early work began as a critique of it. Readers still argue over whether it defends the Prussian state of his day or a more liberal ideal.","inside":"Three parts: Abstract Right, Morality and Ethical Life, in numbered paragraphs with Hegel's remarks and students' added notes. The Preface, with its claim that philosophy understands its age only when that age is ending, is the most quoted part. It appeared in late 1820, though its title page says 1821.","start":"Read the Preface and then the opening paragraphs of Ethical Life on the family and civil society.","line":null,"islands":["hegel"],"related":["hegel-phenomenology","marx-communist-manifesto","rousseau-social-contract"],"shelf":"hegel-marx","tags":["freedom","ethical life","civil society","the state","property","family","owl of minerva","german idealism","political philosophy","recognition"]},{"id":"tocqueville-democracy-america","title":"Democracy in America","original":"De la démocratie en Amérique (French)","author":null,"authorName":"Alexis de Tocqueville","year":1835,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 900 pages in English across two volumes","level":2,"topics":["politics","society"],"summary":"After visiting the United States, Tocqueville describes how a society built on 'equality of conditions' works, from local government to manners and religion. He praises American habits of forming voluntary associations but warns of new dangers in democracy, including the 'tyranny of the majority' and a soft, caring despotism that could make citizens passive.","why":"It is still read as one of the best analyses of what democracy does to people's habits and minds. John Stuart Mill reviewed it and took up its worry about majority tyranny in On Liberty.","inside":"Volume I (1835) is mostly about American institutions; Volume II (1840) is about democratic ideas, feelings and customs. The chapters on the tyranny of the majority and on associations are the best known.","start":"Start with the chapters in Volume I on the omnipotence and tyranny of the majority.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["mill-on-liberty","rousseau-social-contract"],"shelf":"americas","tags":["democracy","tyranny of the majority","equality","civil society","voluntary associations","soft despotism","individualism","liberty","political philosophy","religion"]},{"id":"emerson-self-reliance","title":"Self-Reliance","original":null,"author":null,"authorName":"Ralph Waldo Emerson","year":1841,"approx":false,"form":"essays","length":"about 30 pages","level":1,"topics":["self","ethics","religion"],"summary":"Emerson urges readers to trust their own thoughts and inner voice rather than conform to society, tradition or the expectations of others. He argues that each person has direct access to truth and that consistency and conformity stifle genuine individuality.","why":"It is the classic statement of American individualism and a key text of Transcendentalism. Critics worry that it can slide into excusing selfishness, a charge Emerson's admirers dispute.","inside":"A single essay in Emerson's Essays: First Series. Its opening pages and the remarks on 'foolish consistency' are the most quoted.","start":"Read it whole; it is made of short, quotable paragraphs that can be taken slowly.","line":{"text":"Whoso would be a man must be a nonconformist.","where":"early in the essay","tr":"original English"},"islands":[],"related":["thoreau-civil-disobedience","nietzsche-gay-science"],"shelf":"americas","tags":["self-reliance","individualism","nonconformity","transcendentalism","authenticity","intuition","foolish consistency","the self"]},{"id":"kierkegaard-either-or","title":"Either/Or","original":"Enten – Eller (Danish)","author":"kierkegaard","authorName":"Søren Kierkegaard","year":1843,"approx":false,"form":"other","length":"about 600 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["ethics","self","life and death"],"summary":"Presented as papers found in a secret drawer, the book sets two ways of life side by side. The first part is written by a young aesthete who lives for pleasure and interest and fears boredom; the second consists of letters from Judge William urging him to choose himself and commit to an ethical life of duty and marriage.","why":"It began Kierkegaard's method of writing under pseudonyms to show views from the inside, leaving readers to choose for themselves. It is a founding text for existentialism's focus on choice and commitment.","inside":"Two volumes, edited by the pseudonymous 'Victor Eremita'. The 'Diary of a Seducer' at the end of Part One and the Judge's long letter on the balance between the aesthetic and the ethical are the best-known parts.","start":"Start with the short aphorisms ('Diapsalmata') and the essay 'Rotation of Crops' in Part One, which show the aesthete's mood quickly.","line":null,"islands":["kierkegaard"],"related":["kierkegaard-fear-trembling","hegel-phenomenology"],"shelf":"inner-life","tags":["existentialism","aesthetic life","ethical life","choice","boredom","despair","marriage","seduction","the self","stages of life"]},{"id":"kierkegaard-fear-trembling","title":"Fear and Trembling","original":"Frygt og Bæven (Danish)","author":"kierkegaard","authorName":"Søren Kierkegaard","year":1843,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 130 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["religion","ethics","self"],"summary":"Writing as 'Johannes de silentio', Kierkegaard returns again and again to the story of Abraham being told to sacrifice his son Isaac. He asks whether faith can require a 'teleological suspension of the ethical', setting aside universal moral rules for a higher purpose, and insists that real faith is far harder than his comfortable contemporaries think.","why":"It is one of the most discussed works on the relation between faith and morality. Readers still disagree about whether Kierkegaard endorses Abraham, and many find the idea of suspending ethics deeply troubling.","inside":"A short lyrical opening retelling the Abraham story in several versions, a 'speech in praise of Abraham', a preliminary section on the 'knight of faith', and three 'Problemata'. Problema I, on the suspension of the ethical, is the most famous.","start":"Read the opening retellings of the Abraham story, then Problema I.","line":null,"islands":["kierkegaard"],"related":["kierkegaard-either-or","kant-groundwork"],"shelf":"inner-life","tags":["abraham and isaac","faith","knight of faith","teleological suspension of the ethical","leap of faith","existentialism","paradox","christianity","anxiety","religion and ethics"]},{"id":"douglass-narrative","title":"Narrative of the Life of Frederick Douglass, an American Slave","original":null,"author":null,"authorName":"Frederick Douglass","year":1845,"approx":false,"form":"autobiography","length":"about 120 pages","level":1,"topics":["politics","ethics","self","society"],"summary":"Douglass tells the story of his life under slavery in Maryland and his escape to freedom. He shows how slavery depended on keeping enslaved people ignorant and how learning to read, and later resisting a brutal overseer, made him see himself as free in mind before he was free in body.","why":"It was a best-selling and powerful argument against slavery, written by someone who had lived it at a time when many claimed enslaved people could not write such works. It is also read as philosophy on freedom, literacy and human dignity.","inside":"Eleven chapters with prefaces by the abolitionists William Lloyd Garrison and Wendell Phillips. Chapter 7, on learning to read, and Chapter 10, with the fight against the overseer Edward Covey, are the best known.","start":"Read Chapter 7 on learning to read, which shows how knowledge and freedom are tied together.","line":{"text":"You have seen how a man was made a slave; you shall see how a slave was made a man.","where":"Chapter 10","tr":"original English"},"islands":["margins"],"related":["thoreau-civil-disobedience","mill-on-liberty"],"shelf":"americas","tags":["slavery","abolitionism","freedom","literacy","resistance","autobiography","racism","human dignity","self-emancipation"]},{"id":"marx-communist-manifesto","title":"The Communist Manifesto","original":"Manifest der Kommunistischen Partei (German)","author":"marx","authorName":"Karl Marx and Friedrich Engels","year":1848,"approx":false,"form":"pamphlet","length":"about 50 pages in English","level":1,"topics":["politics","society"],"summary":"Marx and Engels argue that history is driven by struggles between economic classes. Capitalism, they say, has transformed the world at astonishing speed but has also created a working class, the proletariat, that will overthrow it and abolish private ownership of the means of production.","why":"It is one of the most widely read political texts ever written and shaped socialist and communist movements worldwide. Critics point out that many of its predictions, such as ever-deepening poverty for workers in rich countries, did not come true.","inside":"Four sections: bourgeois and proletarians, proletarians and communists, a critique of other socialist writings, and a brief statement of tactics. Section I, with its portrait of capitalism's power, is the best known.","start":"Read Section I, 'Bourgeois and Proletarians', which contains the core theory in vivid form.","line":{"text":"The history of all hitherto existing society is the history of class struggles.","where":"Section I, opening","tr":"Samuel Moore"},"islands":["marx"],"related":["marx-capital","hegel-philosophy-of-right","mill-on-liberty"],"shelf":"hegel-marx","tags":["communism","class struggle","capitalism","proletariat","bourgeoisie","revolution","historical materialism","private property","socialism","workers of the world unite"]},{"id":"thoreau-civil-disobedience","title":"Civil Disobedience","original":null,"author":null,"authorName":"Henry David Thoreau","year":1849,"approx":false,"form":"essays","length":"about 30 pages","level":1,"topics":["politics","ethics","self"],"summary":"Thoreau explains why he refused to pay a tax to a government that supported slavery and waged the Mexican-American War, and why he spent a night in jail as a result. He argues that individuals must not lend their support to injustice and that conscience, not majority vote, should guide what we do.","why":"It is the classic statement of principled law-breaking and influenced later movements of nonviolent resistance, including Gandhi and Martin Luther King Jr. Critics question whether the view gives too much weight to individual conscience over democratic decisions.","inside":"A single essay, first published under the title 'Resistance to Civil Government'. The account of his night in jail is the best-known part.","start":"Read it whole; it is short and moves from principle to personal story.","line":{"text":"Under a government which imprisons any unjustly, the true place for a just man is also a prison.","where":"middle of the essay","tr":"original English"},"islands":[],"related":["emerson-self-reliance","douglass-narrative","plato-crito"],"shelf":"americas","tags":["civil disobedience","conscience","non-violent resistance","slavery","injustice","individualism","transcendentalism","political obligation","anarchism"]},{"id":"mill-on-liberty","title":"On Liberty","original":null,"author":"mill","authorName":"John Stuart Mill","year":1859,"approx":false,"form":"essays","length":"about 120 pages","level":1,"topics":["politics","ethics","society","self"],"summary":"Mill argues that society may limit a person's freedom only to prevent harm to others, never just for their own good or because others disapprove. He defends freedom of thought and speech, since even false opinions help us understand the truth, and freedom to live in unusual ways, since individuality is part of human wellbeing.","why":"The 'harm principle' remains the starting point for debates about free speech, drug laws and personal freedom. Critics ask what counts as harm and whether Mill's view fits his utilitarianism.","inside":"Five chapters: an introduction, liberty of thought and discussion, individuality, the limits of society's authority, and applications. Chapter 2, on free speech, is the best known.","start":"Read Chapter 1 for the harm principle, then Chapter 2 for the argument about free discussion.","line":{"text":"If all mankind minus one, were of one opinion, and only one person were of the contrary opinion, mankind would be no more justified in silencing that one person, than he, if he had the power, would be justified in silencing mankind.","where":"Chapter 2","tr":"original English"},"islands":["mill"],"related":["mill-utilitarianism","tocqueville-democracy-america","mill-subjection-women"],"shelf":"britain","tags":["harm principle","liberty","freedom of speech","individuality","tyranny of the majority","liberalism","paternalism","marketplace of ideas","political philosophy","utilitarianism"]},{"id":"darwin-origin-of-species","title":"On the Origin of Species","original":null,"author":null,"authorName":"Charles Darwin","year":1859,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 500 pages","level":2,"topics":["science","religion","life and death"],"summary":"Darwin argues that species are not fixed but change over long periods, and that natural selection explains how: individuals vary, more are born than can survive, and those with helpful variations leave more offspring. Over time this produces new species and the appearance of design without a designer.","why":"It is a work of science, not philosophy, but it changed philosophy deeply: it undercut the design argument for God, reshaped questions about human nature, ethics and mind, and inspired pragmatists like Peirce, James and Dewey.","inside":"Fourteen chapters in the first edition, building from variation under domestication to natural selection, objections, and the evidence from fossils and geography. Darwin revised it through six editions.","start":"Read Chapters 3 and 4, on the struggle for existence and natural selection, then the final paragraph of the book.","line":{"text":"There is grandeur in this view of life","where":"final paragraph","tr":"original English"},"islands":[],"related":["hume-dialogues","nietzsche-genealogy","james-principles-psychology"],"shelf":"britain","tags":["evolution","natural selection","survival of the fittest","philosophy of biology","argument from design","struggle for existence","common descent","science and religion","variation"]},{"id":"mill-utilitarianism","title":"Utilitarianism","original":null,"author":"mill","authorName":"John Stuart Mill","year":1861,"approx":false,"form":"essays","length":"about 70 pages","level":1,"topics":["ethics","politics"],"summary":"Mill defends the view that right actions are those that tend to promote happiness, understood as pleasure and the absence of pain. Against the charge that this is a philosophy fit for pigs, he argues that some pleasures, those of the intellect and feelings, are higher in quality than others.","why":"It is the most widely read defence of utilitarianism and is still the standard first text on the theory. Its 'proof' of the principle of utility in Chapter 4 is famous for being controversial.","inside":"Five chapters, first published in Fraser's Magazine in 1861 and as a book in 1863. Chapter 2, which answers common objections, is the best known; Chapter 5 links utility and justice.","start":"Read Chapter 2, 'What Utilitarianism Is', which explains the theory and the higher and lower pleasures.","line":{"text":"It is better to be a human being dissatisfied than a pig satisfied; better to be Socrates dissatisfied than a fool satisfied.","where":"Chapter 2","tr":"original English"},"islands":["mill","singer"],"related":["bentham-principles-morals-legislation","kant-groundwork","sidgwick-methods-ethics"],"shelf":"britain","tags":["utilitarianism","greatest happiness principle","higher and lower pleasures","consequentialism","happiness","justice","hedonism","proof of utility","ethics"]},{"id":"mill-subjection-women","title":"The Subjection of Women","original":null,"author":"mill","authorName":"John Stuart Mill","year":1869,"approx":false,"form":"essays","length":"about 100 pages","level":1,"topics":["politics","ethics","society"],"summary":"Mill argues that the legal subordination of women to men is wrong in itself and holds back human progress. He says we cannot know what women's 'nature' is while they live under such constraints, and calls for equal rights in marriage, property, education, work and voting.","why":"It was one of the strongest arguments for women's equality by a leading philosopher of the time. Mill credited much of his thinking on the subject to his wife, Harriet Taylor Mill; later feminists have praised it while noting its limits on marriage roles.","inside":"Four chapters: the case against the current system, the position of wives in marriage, women's exclusion from public life and work, and the benefits equality would bring.","start":"Read Chapter 1, which sets out the whole argument and answers the claim that the existing order is natural.","line":null,"islands":["mill","wollstonecraft"],"related":["wollstonecraft-vindication-woman","mill-on-liberty"],"shelf":"britain","tags":["feminism","women's rights","gender equality","marriage","women's suffrage","liberalism","oppression","political philosophy","human nature"]},{"id":"marx-capital","title":"Capital, Volume I","original":"Das Kapital. Kritik der politischen Ökonomie (German)","author":"marx","authorName":"Karl Marx","year":1867,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 900 pages in English","level":3,"topics":["politics","society"],"summary":"Marx analyses how capitalism works, starting from the commodity. He argues that profit comes from 'surplus value', the extra value workers produce beyond what they are paid, so capitalism rests on exploitation, and he traces how it drives longer hours, machinery and the concentration of wealth.","why":"It is the central work of Marxist economics and social theory, and its ideas about exploitation, alienation and commodity fetishism are still debated in philosophy and sociology. Most economists reject its labour theory of value.","inside":"Divided into parts (seven in the German, eight in the standard English translation), moving from commodities and money to the working day, machinery and the violent origins of capitalism ('primitive accumulation'). Volumes II and III were edited by Engels after Marx's death. The section on the 'fetishism of commodities' at the end of Chapter 1 is the best known.","start":"Read the chapter on the working day in Part Three first, which is concrete and vivid, before the harder opening chapters.","line":null,"islands":["marx"],"related":["marx-communist-manifesto","smith-wealth-of-nations","hegel-phenomenology"],"shelf":"hegel-marx","tags":["capitalism","surplus value","exploitation","labour theory of value","commodity fetishism","alienation","primitive accumulation","class struggle","political economy","historical materialism"]},{"id":"fukuzawa-encouragement-learning","title":"An Encouragement of Learning","original":"学問のすゝめ Gakumon no susume (Japanese)","author":null,"authorName":"Fukuzawa Yukichi","year":1872,"approx":false,"form":"pamphlet","length":"about 120 pages in English","level":1,"topics":["society","politics","self"],"summary":"Fukuzawa argues that people are born equal and that differences in rank should come from learning, not birth. He urges ordinary Japanese to study practical, useful knowledge, to become independent in mind and livelihood, and so to secure the independence of Japan itself.","why":"It was one of the most widely read books of Meiji Japan and helped spread Western ideas of equality, rights and education there. It shows how Enlightenment ideas were adapted outside Europe.","inside":"Seventeen short pamphlets published between 1872 and 1876. The first, which opens with a saying that heaven does not create one person above or below another, is the best known.","start":"Read the first pamphlet, which states his main ideas on equality and learning in a few pages.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["kant-what-is-enlightenment","mill-on-liberty"],"shelf":"asia-modern","tags":["education","equality","independence","japanese philosophy","practical learning","modernisation","meritocracy","enlightenment","self-reliance"]},{"id":"sidgwick-methods-ethics","title":"The Methods of Ethics","original":null,"author":"sidgwick","authorName":"Henry Sidgwick","year":1874,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 500 pages","level":3,"topics":["ethics","knowledge"],"summary":"Sidgwick carefully compares three methods of deciding what we ought to do: egoism (seek your own greatest happiness), intuitionism (follow self-evident moral rules) and utilitarianism (seek the greatest happiness overall). He argues that utilitarianism best systematises common-sense morality but cannot fully defeat egoism, leaving a 'dualism of practical reason'.","why":"Many philosophers, including John Rawls and Derek Parfit, have regarded it as one of the best works of ethics ever written. Its careful style became a model for later analytic moral philosophy.","inside":"Four books: an introduction, then one book each on egoism, intuitionism and utilitarianism, with a concluding chapter. Book III's close examination of common-sense moral rules is especially admired. It went through seven editions; Sidgwick revised it up to the sixth (1901), which appeared after his death in 1900, and the seventh (1907) made only small changes.","start":"Read the Preface to the sixth edition, where Sidgwick explains how he came to his view, then the Concluding Chapter.","line":null,"islands":["mill","singer"],"related":["mill-utilitarianism","bentham-principles-morals-legislation"],"shelf":"britain","tags":["utilitarianism","egoism","intuitionism","common-sense morality","dualism of practical reason","point of view of the universe","consequentialism","metaethics","ethics"]},{"id":"peirce-fixation-belief","title":"The Fixation of Belief","original":null,"author":"peirce","authorName":"Charles Sanders Peirce","year":1877,"approx":false,"form":"essays","length":"about 15 pages","level":1,"topics":["knowledge","science","logic"],"summary":"Peirce argues that inquiry begins with the uncomfortable state of doubt and aims at settled belief. He compares four ways of fixing belief: tenacity (clinging to what you already think), authority, the 'a priori' method (believing what seems agreeable to reason), and the method of science, the only one that lets beliefs be corrected by reality.","why":"It is a founding text of American pragmatism and a clear, lasting defence of scientific method. It appeared in the series 'Illustrations of the Logic of Science', followed by 'How to Make Our Ideas Clear' (1878).","inside":"A short essay in five sections, first published in Popular Science Monthly. The comparison of the four methods is the best-known part.","start":"Read it whole; it is short and written for a general audience.","line":{"text":"The irritation of doubt causes a struggle to attain a state of belief.","where":"on doubt and belief","tr":"original English"},"islands":["pragmatists"],"related":["james-will-to-believe","clifford-ethics-belief"],"shelf":"pragmatism","tags":["pragmatism","scientific method","belief","doubt","inquiry","tenacity","authority","epistemology","philosophy of science"]},{"id":"clifford-ethics-belief","title":"The Ethics of Belief","original":null,"author":"clifford","authorName":"W. K. Clifford","year":1877,"approx":false,"form":"essays","length":"about 20 pages","level":1,"topics":["knowledge","ethics","religion"],"summary":"Clifford argues that believing is a moral matter: we are responsible for what we believe, and it is wrong to hold beliefs without good evidence. His opening example is a shipowner who sends an unsafe ship to sea after talking himself into believing it is sound.","why":"It is the classic statement of 'evidentialism', the view that belief should match evidence, and is still central in debates about religious faith. William James wrote The Will to Believe largely as a reply.","inside":"A single essay first published in the Contemporary Review, in three parts: the duty of inquiry, the weight of authority, and the limits of inference. The shipowner story is the best-known passage.","start":"Read the first part, with the shipowner example, which states the whole case.","line":{"text":"It is wrong always, everywhere, and for anyone, to believe anything upon insufficient evidence.","where":"end of Part One","tr":"original English"},"islands":["pragmatists"],"related":["james-will-to-believe","peirce-fixation-belief"],"shelf":"britain","tags":["ethics of belief","evidentialism","evidence","shipowner","belief","religious belief","intellectual responsibility","epistemology","skepticism"]},{"id":"nietzsche-gay-science","title":"The Gay Science","original":"Die fröhliche Wissenschaft (German)","author":"nietzsche","authorName":"Friedrich Nietzsche","year":1882,"approx":false,"form":"fragments","length":"about 350 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["religion","ethics","life and death","self"],"summary":"In hundreds of short sections, Nietzsche explores what follows from the 'death of God': the collapse of belief in the Christian God and the values that rested on it. He calls for a cheerful, experimental attitude to life and introduces the thought of eternal recurrence, asking whether you could will to live your life again endlessly.","why":"It contains Nietzsche's most famous statements on the death of God and eternal recurrence, which shaped existentialism and later debates about meaning without religion. The title means something like 'the joyful knowledge'.","inside":"A prelude of poems and four books of aphorisms (1882), with a fifth book and preface added in 1887. Section 125, 'The Madman', and section 341, 'The Greatest Weight', are the best known.","start":"Start with section 125, 'The Madman', then read section 341 on eternal recurrence.","line":null,"islands":["nietzsche"],"related":["nietzsche-zarathustra","nietzsche-beyond-good-evil","schopenhauer-world-as-will"],"shelf":"inner-life","tags":["god is dead","eternal recurrence","the madman","amor fati","nihilism","perspectivism","revaluation of values","atheism","affirmation of life","aphorisms"]},{"id":"nietzsche-zarathustra","title":"Thus Spoke Zarathustra","original":"Also sprach Zarathustra (German)","author":"nietzsche","authorName":"Friedrich Nietzsche","year":1883,"approx":false,"form":"other","length":"about 300 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["ethics","religion","self","life and death"],"summary":"In poetic, biblical-sounding prose, the prophet Zarathustra comes down from his mountain to teach humanity. He announces the 'Übermensch' (overhuman), a goal for people to create new values after the death of God, and wrestles with the thought of eternal recurrence.","why":"Nietzsche regarded it as his most important book, and it is the main source for ideas like the Übermensch. Its symbolic style makes it open to very different readings.","inside":"Four parts published between 1883 and 1885, made up of Zarathustra's speeches and episodes. The Prologue, with the tightrope walker and the 'last man', is the best-known part.","start":"Read the Prologue, which introduces the main themes through a single story.","line":null,"islands":["nietzsche"],"related":["nietzsche-gay-science","nietzsche-beyond-good-evil"],"shelf":"inner-life","tags":["ubermensch","god is dead","eternal recurrence","the last man","will to power","nihilism","creating values","amor fati","self-overcoming","atheism"]},{"id":"frege-foundations-arithmetic","title":"The Foundations of Arithmetic","original":"Die Grundlagen der Arithmetik (German)","author":"frege","authorName":"Gottlob Frege","year":1884,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 120 pages in English","level":3,"topics":["logic","language","metaphysics"],"summary":"Frege asks what numbers are. He criticises the views that numbers are physical things, mental ideas or (as Mill thought) generalisations from experience, and argues that statements of number are about concepts and that numbers are objects that can be defined using logic alone.","why":"It is a founding work of analytic philosophy and of logicism, the attempt to show that arithmetic is a branch of logic. Its principle that a word has meaning only in the context of a sentence influenced later philosophy of language.","inside":"An introduction, criticism of earlier views, and Frege's own account, written mostly in plain prose rather than symbols. The early sections attacking Mill and the 'context principle' in the introduction are the best known.","start":"Read the Introduction, which states his three guiding principles in a few pages.","line":null,"islands":["logic"],"related":["frege-sense-reference","kant-critique-pure-reason","russell-on-denoting"],"shelf":"logic","tags":["philosophy of mathematics","logicism","number","context principle","concepts and objects","analytic philosophy","logic","abstract objects","platonism"]},{"id":"nietzsche-beyond-good-evil","title":"Beyond Good and Evil","original":"Jenseits von Gut und Böse. Vorspiel einer Philosophie der Zukunft (German)","author":"nietzsche","authorName":"Friedrich Nietzsche","year":1886,"approx":false,"form":"fragments","length":"about 250 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["ethics","knowledge","religion","society"],"summary":"Nietzsche attacks past philosophers for dressing up their prejudices and desires as objective truths, and calls for 'philosophers of the future' who will create new values. He contrasts 'master morality', which grows from self-affirmation, with 'slave morality', which grows from resentment.","why":"It is one of the clearest ways into Nietzsche's mature thought and his critique of morality, truth and religion. It strongly influenced later thinkers who treated philosophy as an expression of power or interest.","inside":"A preface, nine parts of numbered sections and a closing poem. Part One, 'On the Prejudices of Philosophers', and Part Nine, 'What Is Noble?', are the best known.","start":"Start with Part One, which shows Nietzsche's method of unmasking philosophers.","line":{"text":"He who fights with monsters should be careful lest he thereby become a monster. And if thou gaze long into an abyss, the abyss will also gaze into thee.","where":"Section 146","tr":"Helen Zimmern"},"islands":["nietzsche"],"related":["nietzsche-genealogy","nietzsche-gay-science"],"shelf":"inner-life","tags":["master morality","slave morality","will to power","perspectivism","critique of philosophers","revaluation of values","nobility","free spirits","truth","christianity"]},{"id":"nietzsche-genealogy","title":"On the Genealogy of Morality","original":"Zur Genealogie der Moral (German)","author":"nietzsche","authorName":"Friedrich Nietzsche","year":1887,"approx":false,"form":"essays","length":"about 150 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["ethics","religion","self","society"],"summary":"Nietzsche tells a historical story about where our moral values come from. He argues that 'good and evil' arose from a 'slave revolt' in which the weak, out of resentment, condemned the values of the strong; that guilt and bad conscience grew from debt and cruelty turned inward; and that ascetic ideals give suffering a meaning.","why":"It is Nietzsche's most sustained argument and the model for 'genealogy' as a method of questioning values by tracing their history, later used by Foucault. Critics question its historical accuracy and whether origins settle the value of anything.","inside":"A preface and three essays: on 'good and evil' versus 'good and bad', on guilt and bad conscience, and on ascetic ideals. The First Essay is the best known.","start":"Read the First Essay, which is the most direct and sets up the rest.","line":null,"islands":["nietzsche"],"related":["nietzsche-beyond-good-evil","darwin-origin-of-species"],"shelf":"inner-life","tags":["genealogy","slave morality","master morality","ressentiment","guilt","bad conscience","ascetic ideal","good and evil","critique of morality","christianity","punishment"]},{"id":"james-principles-psychology","title":"The Principles of Psychology","original":null,"author":"james","authorName":"William James","year":1890,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 1,400 pages in two volumes","level":2,"topics":["mind","self","science"],"summary":"James sets out a broad science of mental life, from the brain and habit to attention, memory, emotion and will. He describes consciousness as a continuous 'stream of thought' rather than a chain of separate ideas and argues that emotions are our feeling of bodily changes.","why":"It helped establish psychology as a science separate from philosophy while remaining full of philosophical ideas about the self and consciousness. Its descriptions of experience are still cited by philosophers of mind.","inside":"Twenty-eight chapters, written over about twelve years. 'The Stream of Thought', 'The Consciousness of Self' and 'Habit' are the best-known chapters; James also published a shorter version in 1892.","start":"Read the chapter on 'Habit', which is short, practical and engaging.","line":{"text":"Habit is thus the enormous fly-wheel of society, its most precious conservative agent.","where":"Chapter 4, Habit","tr":"original English"},"islands":["pragmatists"],"related":["james-will-to-believe","darwin-origin-of-species"],"shelf":"pragmatism","tags":["stream of consciousness","consciousness","psychology","habit","the self","emotions","james-lange theory","attention","memory","free will","philosophy of mind"]},{"id":"frege-sense-reference","title":"On Sense and Reference","original":"Über Sinn und Bedeutung (German)","author":"frege","authorName":"Gottlob Frege","year":1892,"approx":false,"form":"essays","length":"about 25 pages in English","level":3,"topics":["language","logic","mind"],"summary":"Frege asks how 'the morning star is the evening star' can tell us something new when both names refer to the same planet, Venus. He answers that expressions have both a reference (the thing they pick out) and a sense (the way that thing is presented), and applies the distinction to whole sentences and to reported speech.","why":"It is a founding text of the philosophy of language, and the sense and reference distinction is still central to debates about meaning and thought. Later philosophers such as Russell and Kripke offered rival accounts of names.","inside":"A single journal article. The opening puzzle about identity statements and the example of the morning star and the evening star are the best-known parts.","start":"Read the first few pages, which pose the puzzle and give the core distinction.","line":null,"islands":["logic"],"related":["frege-foundations-arithmetic","russell-on-denoting"],"shelf":"logic","tags":["sense and reference","morning star and evening star","identity statements","meaning","philosophy of language","analytic philosophy","reference","propositional attitudes","logic"]},{"id":"james-will-to-believe","title":"The Will to Believe","original":null,"author":"james","authorName":"William James","year":1896,"approx":false,"form":"essays","length":"about 25 pages","level":1,"topics":["religion","knowledge","ethics"],"summary":"Replying to Clifford, James argues that when we face a genuine choice between beliefs, one that is live, forced and momentous, and evidence cannot settle it, we are entitled to let our feelings and will decide. He applies this especially to religious faith.","why":"It is the classic defence of the right to believe beyond the evidence and remains central in the philosophy of religion. Critics say it licenses wishful thinking; James insisted it applies only to narrow cases.","inside":"Delivered as an address in 1896 and published as the title essay of a collection in 1897. The definitions of live, forced and momentous options at the start are the best-known part.","start":"Read the opening sections that define a 'genuine option'; they make the rest easy to follow.","line":{"text":"Our passional nature not only lawfully may, but must, decide an option between propositions, whenever it is a genuine option that cannot by its nature be decided on intellectual grounds","where":"statement of the thesis","tr":"original English"},"islands":["pragmatists"],"related":["clifford-ethics-belief","pascal-pensees","peirce-fixation-belief"],"shelf":"pragmatism","tags":["will to believe","faith","religious belief","ethics of belief","pragmatism","genuine options","evidence","epistemology","philosophy of religion"]},{"id":"vivekananda-raja-yoga","title":"Raja Yoga","original":null,"author":"vivekananda","authorName":"Swami Vivekananda","year":1896,"approx":false,"form":"lectures","length":"about 250 pages","level":2,"topics":["religion","mind","self"],"summary":"Vivekananda presents the yoga of meditation as a practical, almost scientific method of controlling the mind and realising the divine nature of the self. The book combines his own lectures with a translation of and commentary on Patanjali's Yoga Sutras.","why":"It played a major part in introducing yoga and Indian philosophy to Western audiences. Scholars note that it presents yoga through Vivekananda's own modern Vedanta and tends to downplay its other traditions.","inside":"Based on talks given in New York in 1895–96: a series of lectures on breath, concentration and meditation, followed by Patanjali's aphorisms with comments. The opening chapter explains his approach.","start":"Read the Introductory chapter, which sets out why he treats yoga as a practical science of the mind.","line":null,"islands":["upanishads"],"related":["schopenhauer-world-as-will","james-principles-psychology"],"shelf":"asia-modern","tags":["yoga","meditation","raja yoga","concentration","breath control","vedanta","hinduism","the self","mind","consciousness"]},{"id":"husserl-logical-investigations","title":"Logical Investigations","original":"Logische Untersuchungen (German)","author":"husserl","authorName":"Edmund Husserl","year":1900,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"two volumes, roughly 900 pages in English","level":3,"topics":["logic","knowledge","mind"],"summary":"Husserl first argues that the laws of logic cannot be reduced to facts about how human minds happen to work, a view called psychologism. He then studies, with great care, how experiences such as perceiving, meaning and judging are always directed at something. This patient description of experience became the method he called phenomenology.","why":"It launched phenomenology, the movement that Heidegger, Sartre, Merleau-Ponty and Beauvoir all grew out of. Its attack on psychologism also shaped debates about logic in the early analytic tradition.","inside":"The first volume, the Prolegomena to Pure Logic (1900), is the attack on psychologism; the second (1901) contains six separate investigations into meaning, parts and wholes, and the structure of consciousness.","start":"Start with the Prolegomena, because its argument against reducing logic to psychology is the clearest and most self-contained part.","line":null,"islands":["phenomenology"],"related":["husserl-ideas","heidegger-being-and-time"],"shelf":"phenomenology","tags":["phenomenology","intentionality","psychologism","logic","meaning","consciousness","parts and wholes","philosophy of logic","epistemology"]},{"id":"husserl-ideas","title":"Ideas I","original":"Ideen zu einer reinen Phänomenologie und phänomenologischen Philosophie (German)","author":"husserl","authorName":"Edmund Husserl","year":1913,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 400 pages","level":3,"topics":["mind","knowledge","metaphysics"],"summary":"Husserl asks us to set aside, or 'bracket', our everyday assumption that the world exists independently of us, not to deny it but to look closely at how things show up in experience. He calls this suspension the epoché. What remains to study is consciousness and the meanings it gives to its objects.","why":"It set out phenomenology as a rigorous method and turned it in an idealist direction that many of his students, including Heidegger, resisted. The idea of bracketing is still used in philosophy, psychology and qualitative research.","inside":"The book moves from general remarks on facts and essences to the 'phenomenological reduction' and then to a detailed account of the structure of conscious acts and their objects.","start":"Begin with the sections on the 'natural attitude' and its suspension in Part Two, which explain the method everything else depends on.","line":null,"islands":["phenomenology"],"related":["husserl-logical-investigations","heidegger-being-and-time"],"shelf":"phenomenology","tags":["phenomenology","epoche","bracketing","phenomenological reduction","intentionality","consciousness","noema","transcendental ego","essences","experience"]},{"id":"du-bois-souls-of-black-folk","title":"The Souls of Black Folk","original":null,"author":"du_bois","authorName":"W. E. B. Du Bois","year":1903,"approx":false,"form":"book","length":"about 200 pages","level":1,"topics":["society","self","politics"],"summary":"A collection of essays on Black life in the United States after the end of slavery, mixing history, sociology, memoir and music. Du Bois describes 'double consciousness', the experience of seeing yourself through the eyes of a society that looks down on you, and the 'veil' that separates Black and white America. He also argues that the colour line is the central problem of the twentieth century.","why":"It gave philosophy and social thought lasting tools for describing how racism shapes a person's sense of self. Its disagreement with Booker T. Washington about education and civil rights set the terms of a long debate.","inside":"Fourteen chapters, each opening with lines of poetry and a few bars of a spiritual; the first, 'Of Our Spiritual Strivings', introduces double consciousness, and the third criticises Washington.","start":"Read the first chapter, 'Of Our Spiritual Strivings', which is short and contains the book's most famous ideas.","line":null,"islands":["margins"],"related":["fanon-wretched-of-the-earth","ambedkar-annihilation-of-caste"],"shelf":"society-20","tags":["double consciousness","the veil","the color line","race","racism","african american experience","identity","philosophy of race","education","sociology"]},{"id":"moore-principia-ethica","title":"Principia Ethica","original":null,"author":"moore","authorName":"G. E. Moore","year":1903,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 230 pages","level":2,"topics":["ethics","language"],"summary":"Moore argues that 'good' names a simple property that cannot be defined in terms of anything else, such as pleasure or what we desire. Anyone who tries to define it commits what he calls the 'naturalistic fallacy'. His 'open question argument' says that for any proposed definition, it still makes sense to ask whether that thing is really good.","why":"It set the agenda for much of twentieth-century moral philosophy, which spent decades debating whether moral words describe facts at all. Its closing praise of friendship and beauty also influenced the Bloomsbury Group of writers and artists.","inside":"Six chapters: the first introduces the indefinability of good and the naturalistic fallacy, the middle chapters criticise rival theories, and the last, 'The Ideal', names personal affection and the enjoyment of beauty as the greatest goods.","start":"Start with Chapter 1, especially the sections on why good cannot be defined; the rest of the book builds on them.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["ayer-language-truth-and-logic","mackie-ethics-inventing-right-and-wrong"],"shelf":"cambridge-oxford","tags":["naturalistic fallacy","open question argument","metaethics","intuitionism","the good","non-naturalism","ideal utilitarianism","friendship","beauty","analytic philosophy"]},{"id":"russell-on-denoting","title":"On Denoting","original":null,"author":"russell","authorName":"Bertrand Russell","year":1905,"approx":false,"form":"paper","length":"a 15-page paper","level":3,"topics":["language","logic","metaphysics"],"summary":"Russell asks how a sentence like 'The present King of France is bald' can be meaningful when there is no such king. His answer, the theory of descriptions, says that phrases like 'the so-and-so' are not names at all: the sentence really claims that there is exactly one King of France and that he is bald, which is simply false.","why":"It became a model of how logical analysis can dissolve a puzzle by showing that a sentence's grammar hides its true form. Later philosophers, notably P. F. Strawson in 1950, argued that Russell got ordinary usage wrong, and the debate shaped philosophy of language.","inside":"A short, dense journal article in Mind that states the theory, applies it to three puzzles, and criticises the rival views of Meinong and Frege.","start":"Read a modern introduction to the theory of descriptions first, then the paper's statement of the three puzzles, which is the clearest stretch.","line":null,"islands":["logic"],"related":["russell-problems-of-philosophy","kripke-naming-and-necessity"],"shelf":"logic","tags":["theory of descriptions","king of france is bald","definite descriptions","philosophy of language","reference","logical analysis","analytic philosophy","logic","existence","meaning"]},{"id":"russell-problems-of-philosophy","title":"The Problems of Philosophy","original":null,"author":"russell","authorName":"Bertrand Russell","year":1912,"approx":false,"form":"book","length":"about 150 pages","level":1,"topics":["knowledge","metaphysics","logic"],"summary":"A short introduction written for general readers. Russell starts by asking whether we can know that the table in front of us really exists, then works through matter, idealism, induction, a priori knowledge, universals and truth. He ends by arguing that philosophy is valuable less for its answers than for widening what we think is possible.","why":"It is still one of the most widely used first books in philosophy. Its distinction between knowledge by acquaintance and knowledge by description influenced later theories of meaning and knowledge.","inside":"Fifteen short chapters, each on one problem; the final chapter, 'The Value of Philosophy', is the most often read.","start":"Read Chapter 1 on appearance and reality, then jump to the last chapter, and fill in the middle as your interest leads.","line":null,"islands":["logic"],"related":["russell-on-denoting","descartes-meditations"],"shelf":"logic","tags":["appearance and reality","skepticism","idealism","induction","a priori knowledge","universals","truth","knowledge by acquaintance","value of philosophy","epistemology","sense data"]},{"id":"james-pragmatism","title":"Pragmatism","original":null,"author":"james","authorName":"William James","year":1907,"approx":false,"form":"lectures","length":"about 180 pages","level":1,"topics":["knowledge","religion","metaphysics"],"summary":"Lectures given to general audiences in Boston and New York, subtitled 'A New Name for Some Old Ways of Thinking'. James proposes that the meaning of an idea lies in the practical difference it would make, and that true ideas are those that work out well when we act on them and test them. He uses this to cut through old disputes, including those about religion and free will.","why":"It made pragmatism, the most influential American philosophical movement, known to a wide public. Critics such as Russell objected that it confused truth with usefulness, and the argument about that point continues.","inside":"Eight lectures; the first contrasts 'tender-minded' and 'tough-minded' temperaments, the second explains the pragmatic method with the story of a squirrel and a tree, and the sixth gives the theory of truth.","start":"Start with Lecture II, 'What Pragmatism Means', which contains the squirrel example and states the method plainly.","line":{"text":"'The true,' to put it very briefly, is only the expedient in the way of our thinking, just as 'the right' is only the expedient in the way of our behaving.","where":"Lecture VI, Pragmatism's Conception of Truth","tr":"original English"},"islands":["pragmatists"],"related":["dewey-democracy-and-education"],"shelf":"pragmatism","tags":["pragmatism","pragmatic theory of truth","truth","squirrel and the tree","tender-minded and tough-minded","free will","religion","cash value","meaning"]},{"id":"bergson-creative-evolution","title":"Creative Evolution","original":"L'Évolution créatrice (French)","author":null,"authorName":"Henri Bergson","year":1907,"approx":false,"form":"book","length":"about 400 pages","level":2,"topics":["metaphysics","science","mind"],"summary":"Bergson argues that life cannot be fully explained either as a machine or as the working out of a fixed plan. Evolution is driven by a creative impulse he calls the élan vital, and reality is a flow of change, or 'duration', that the intellect chops into still frames. Intuition, he says, can grasp this flow better than analysis.","why":"It made Bergson one of the most famous thinkers in the world before the First World War and influenced writers, artists and later philosophers such as Deleuze. Most biologists and analytic philosophers rejected the élan vital as explaining nothing, and his reputation fell sharply after the 1920s.","inside":"Four chapters: on the evolution of life, on the paths of instinct and intellect, on the meaning of life, and a final critique of how philosophers have misunderstood change, which compares thought to a cinema film.","start":"Start with the first chapter, where Bergson explains why both mechanical and purpose-driven accounts of evolution fail.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["nishida-inquiry-into-the-good","james-pragmatism"],"shelf":"phenomenology","tags":["elan vital","evolution","duration","time","intuition","vitalism","process philosophy","life","instinct and intellect","change"]},{"id":"nishida-inquiry-into-the-good","title":"An Inquiry into the Good","original":"Zen no kenkyū, 善の研究 (Japanese)","author":"nishida","authorName":"Nishida Kitarō","year":1911,"approx":false,"form":"book","length":"about 200 pages","level":3,"topics":["metaphysics","ethics","religion","self"],"summary":"Nishida builds a philosophy around 'pure experience', the moment of experience before it is split into a subject who knows and an object that is known. He argues that reality itself is this unified activity, that the good is the full realisation of the self within it, and that religion is the deepest form of that unity. He draws on Western thinkers such as William James alongside his own Zen practice.","why":"It is usually seen as the first major original work of modern Japanese philosophy and the founding text of the Kyoto School. It showed that Western philosophical methods could be used to express ideas rooted in East Asian traditions.","inside":"Four parts: pure experience, reality, the good, and religion, each building on the idea of pure experience set out in the first.","start":"Begin with Part One on pure experience, which is short and sets up every later argument.","line":null,"islands":["eastlight"],"related":["james-pragmatism","bergson-creative-evolution"],"shelf":"asia-20","tags":["pure experience","kyoto school","zen","japanese philosophy","the good","self-realisation","subject and object","religion","non-dualism","metaphysics"]},{"id":"dewey-democracy-and-education","title":"Democracy and Education","original":null,"author":"dewey","authorName":"John Dewey","year":1916,"approx":false,"form":"book","length":"about 400 pages","level":2,"topics":["society","politics","knowledge"],"summary":"Dewey argues that education should not be the passive storing of facts but growth through experience, active problem-solving and shared activity. Democracy, for him, is more than a way of voting: it is a way of living together in which people communicate freely and learn from one another. Schools should therefore be small democratic communities.","why":"It shaped 'progressive' education across the world, including in China, where Dewey lectured from 1919 to 1921. Critics have blamed his followers for weakening the teaching of knowledge, though whether Dewey himself held such views is disputed.","inside":"Twenty-six chapters moving from education as a social necessity, through theories of method, subject matter and interest, to the philosophy of education and morality.","start":"Read the first two chapters on education as a necessity of life and a social function, then Chapter 7, 'The Democratic Conception in Education'.","line":null,"islands":["pragmatists"],"related":["james-pragmatism"],"shelf":"society-20","tags":["philosophy of education","democracy","pragmatism","experiential learning","progressive education","growth","community","learning by doing","communication"]},{"id":"wittgenstein-tractatus","title":"Tractatus Logico-Philosophicus","original":"Logisch-philosophische Abhandlung (German)","author":"wittgenstein","authorName":"Ludwig Wittgenstein","year":1921,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 80 pages","level":3,"topics":["language","logic","metaphysics"],"summary":"Wittgenstein argues that language can say something only by picturing possible facts in the world. Statements of logic say nothing about the world, and claims about ethics, value, the meaning of life and the 'mystical' cannot be said at all, only shown. The book ends by saying that its own sentences are, strictly, nonsense: a ladder to be thrown away after climbing it.","why":"It inspired the Vienna Circle's logical positivism, though Wittgenstein thought they misread its point about ethics. Wittgenstein himself later rejected much of it in the Philosophical Investigations.","inside":"Numbered remarks arranged under seven main propositions, with decimals showing which remark comments on which; the final remarks, from 6.4 on, on ethics, death and silence, are the most quoted.","start":"Read the seven main numbered propositions first to see the skeleton, then the closing remarks from 6.4 onwards.","line":null,"islands":["wittgenstein","logic"],"related":["wittgenstein-philosophical-investigations","ayer-language-truth-and-logic","russell-on-denoting"],"shelf":"logic","tags":["picture theory of meaning","logical atomism","philosophy of language","limits of language","saying and showing","whereof one cannot speak","the mystical","the ladder","logic","analytic philosophy","facts"]},{"id":"heidegger-being-and-time","title":"Being and Time","original":"Sein und Zeit (German)","author":"heidegger","authorName":"Martin Heidegger","year":1927,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 450 pages","level":3,"topics":["metaphysics","life and death","self"],"summary":"Heidegger asks what it means for anything to be, and starts with the one being for whom that question matters: us, whom he calls Dasein. We are not minds looking at objects, but beings already absorbed in a world of tasks, tools and other people. Facing our own death through anxiety can pull us out of living as 'anyone' does and into an authentic life of our own.","why":"It reshaped European philosophy, feeding into existentialism, hermeneutics and deconstruction, and influenced psychology and architecture. Many analytic philosophers found it obscure, and Heidegger's membership of the Nazi party has made his legacy bitterly disputed.","inside":"Only two of a planned six divisions were written: Division One analyses everyday being-in-the-world, including the famous example of using a hammer, and Division Two turns to death, conscience, authenticity and time.","start":"Start with a guide and the Division One sections on 'worldhood' and equipment, where the hammer example shows his method at its most concrete.","line":null,"islands":["phenomenology"],"related":["husserl-ideas","sartre-being-and-nothingness"],"shelf":"phenomenology","tags":["being","dasein","being-in-the-world","authenticity","being-towards-death","anxiety","the they","ready-to-hand","existentialism","phenomenology","time","death"]},{"id":"ambedkar-annihilation-of-caste","title":"Annihilation of Caste","original":null,"author":"ambedkar","authorName":"B. R. Ambedkar","year":1936,"approx":false,"form":"essay","length":"about 90 pages","level":1,"topics":["society","politics","religion","ethics"],"summary":"Written as a speech for a Hindu reform group in Lahore, which cancelled the invitation after reading it, so Ambedkar published it himself. He argues that caste is not merely a division of labour but a division of labourers into ranked groups, that it destroys any sense of shared public life, and that it rests on the authority of Hindu scriptures. Real reform, he concludes, means rejecting that authority, not just tinkering with customs.","why":"It remains a central text of the anti-caste movement in India and is widely read on equality and social reform. Gandhi published a reply in his paper Harijan defending the ideal of varna (hereditary callings), which he distinguished from caste as it was practised, and Ambedkar's response to him is printed in later editions.","inside":"A continuous argument in numbered sections, followed in later editions by the exchange with Gandhi.","start":"Read the sections where Ambedkar answers the claim that caste is a division of labour; they show his method of meeting each defence in turn.","line":null,"islands":["margins"],"related":["du-bois-souls-of-black-folk"],"shelf":"asia-20","tags":["caste","social justice","hinduism","religious authority","equality","untouchability","social reform","democracy","oppression","political philosophy"]},{"id":"ayer-language-truth-and-logic","title":"Language, Truth and Logic","original":null,"author":"ayer","authorName":"A. J. Ayer","year":1936,"approx":false,"form":"book","length":"about 160 pages","level":1,"topics":["language","knowledge","ethics","religion"],"summary":"Ayer brought the ideas of the Vienna Circle to English readers in a short, confident book. His verification principle says that a statement is meaningful only if it is true by definition or could in principle be checked by experience. On this test, metaphysics and talk of God are neither true nor false but meaningless, and moral judgements merely express feelings.","why":"It made logical positivism famous and gave early form to emotivism in ethics. Critics soon pointed out that the verification principle seems to fail its own test, and Ayer later accepted that much of the book was mistaken.","inside":"Eight chapters; Chapter 1 attacks metaphysics, and Chapter 6, 'Critique of Ethics and Theology', is the best known.","start":"Read Chapter 1 and then Chapter 6, which together show the theory and its most startling consequences.","line":null,"islands":["vienna"],"related":["wittgenstein-tractatus","quine-two-dogmas","moore-principia-ethica"],"shelf":"logic","tags":["logical positivism","verification principle","elimination of metaphysics","emotivism","vienna circle","meaning","analytic-synthetic distinction","empiricism","atheism","metaethics"]},{"id":"popper-logic-of-scientific-discovery","title":"The Logic of Scientific Discovery","original":"Logik der Forschung (German)","author":"popper","authorName":"Karl Popper","year":1934,"approx":false,"form":"book","length":"about 500 pages in the enlarged English edition","level":2,"topics":["science","knowledge","logic"],"summary":"Popper argues that science does not prove theories by piling up confirming cases, since no number of observations can prove a general law. Instead a theory is scientific if it is falsifiable: it forbids certain observations, so it could be shown false. Science advances by bold guesses and serious attempts to refute them.","why":"Falsifiability became the most widely known answer to the question of what separates science from non-science. Kuhn, Lakatos and others argued that real scientists do not abandon theories at the first failed test.","inside":"The original German book was much shorter; the English edition of 1959 adds many appendices, and the opening chapters on induction and the problem of demarcation are the best known.","start":"Read Part I, the introductory chapters, which state the problem of induction and the falsifiability criterion.","line":null,"islands":["vienna"],"related":["kuhn-structure-of-scientific-revolutions","popper-open-society"],"shelf":"logic","tags":["falsifiability","falsification","problem of demarcation","problem of induction","philosophy of science","scientific method","conjectures and refutations","critical rationalism","black swan"]},{"id":"camus-myth-of-sisyphus","title":"The Myth of Sisyphus","original":"Le Mythe de Sisyphe (French)","author":"camus","authorName":"Albert Camus","year":1942,"approx":false,"form":"essay","length":"about 150 pages","level":1,"topics":["life and death","ethics","religion"],"summary":"Camus asks whether, if life has no meaning given from outside, suicide is the answer. He describes the 'absurd' as the clash between our demand for meaning and a silent universe. He rejects both suicide and the leap into religious faith, urging us instead to live fully while staying aware of the absurd.","why":"It became one of the defining statements of mid-century thought about meaning and is still widely read by people with no other contact with philosophy. Camus himself denied he was an existentialist and criticised Kierkegaard and other existentialists for escaping the absurd.","inside":"A long essay in four parts: an absurd reasoning, the absurd man, absurd creation, and a short final chapter on Sisyphus endlessly rolling his rock uphill.","start":"Read the opening pages, which pose the question of suicide, and then the last chapter on Sisyphus, which is only a few pages long.","line":null,"islands":["existentialists"],"related":["sartre-existentialism-is-a-humanism","kierkegaard-fear-trembling"],"shelf":"phenomenology","tags":["absurdism","the absurd","meaning of life","suicide","sisyphus","revolt","existentialism","philosophical suicide","one must imagine sisyphus happy","atheism"]},{"id":"sartre-being-and-nothingness","title":"Being and Nothingness","original":"L'Être et le néant (French)","author":"sartre","authorName":"Jean-Paul Sartre","year":1943,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 700 pages","level":3,"topics":["self","mind","metaphysics","ethics"],"summary":"Sartre divides being into things, which simply are what they are, and consciousness, which is never fixed and is always choosing what it will become. Because we are radically free, we are responsible for what we make of ourselves, and we often hide from this in 'bad faith', pretending our choices are forced on us. Other people's gaze also turns us into objects, which makes human relationships a site of conflict.","why":"It is the main technical work of French existentialism and its examples of bad faith and 'the look' are still widely taught. Critics, including Beauvoir and later Sartre himself, argued that it underplays how much social conditions limit freedom.","inside":"Four parts plus an introduction, subtitled 'An Essay on Phenomenological Ontology'; the chapter on bad faith, with the café waiter who plays at being a waiter, is the most famous.","start":"Start with the chapter on bad faith in Part One; it is concrete, vivid and readable on its own.","line":null,"islands":["existentialists"],"related":["sartre-existentialism-is-a-humanism","heidegger-being-and-time","beauvoir-second-sex"],"shelf":"phenomenology","tags":["existentialism","bad faith","radical freedom","being-for-itself","being-in-itself","the look","the other","nothingness","responsibility","phenomenology","consciousness"]},{"id":"popper-open-society","title":"The Open Society and Its Enemies","original":null,"author":"popper","authorName":"Karl Popper","year":1945,"approx":false,"form":"book","length":"two volumes, about 750 pages","level":2,"topics":["politics","society","knowledge"],"summary":"Written in New Zealand during the Second World War, it attacks 'historicism', the idea that history follows laws that let us predict and plan society's future. Popper traces this idea to Plato, Hegel and Marx and argues it leads to closed, authoritarian societies. He defends an 'open society' that reforms itself piece by piece and lets citizens criticise and replace their rulers without violence.","why":"It became a classic of post-war liberal thought, and its note on the 'paradox of tolerance' is still widely cited. Many scholars think its readings of Plato and especially Hegel are unfair.","inside":"Volume 1, 'The Spell of Plato', and Volume 2, 'The High Tide of Prophecy', on Hegel and Marx.","start":"Start with the early chapters of Volume 1 on Plato, which make his main charge clearly and concretely.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["plato-republic","popper-logic-of-scientific-discovery","berlin-two-concepts-of-liberty"],"shelf":"cambridge-oxford","tags":["open society","historicism","totalitarianism","liberal democracy","piecemeal social engineering","critique of plato","critique of marx","paradox of tolerance","political philosophy","critical rationalism"]},{"id":"sartre-existentialism-is-a-humanism","title":"Existentialism Is a Humanism","original":"L'existentialisme est un humanisme (French)","author":"sartre","authorName":"Jean-Paul Sartre","year":1946,"approx":false,"form":"lectures","length":"about 50 pages","level":1,"topics":["ethics","self","religion"],"summary":"A public lecture given in Paris in October 1945 and published the next year, in which Sartre defends existentialism against charges of gloom and despair. Its core claim is that for human beings 'existence precedes essence': there is no fixed human nature, so we create ourselves through our choices and are responsible for them. In choosing, he argues, we also propose an image of what everyone ought to be.","why":"It became the most widely read short statement of existentialism. Sartre later distanced himself from it, and many readers find its argument that choosing for oneself means choosing for all humanity too quick.","inside":"A single lecture followed in many editions by a discussion with the audience; the best-known passage is the case of a student torn between caring for his mother and joining the Free French.","start":"Read it straight through in one sitting; it is short and was written to be heard by a general audience.","line":null,"islands":["existentialists"],"related":["sartre-being-and-nothingness","beauvoir-ethics-of-ambiguity","camus-myth-of-sisyphus"],"shelf":"phenomenology","tags":["existentialism","existence precedes essence","radical freedom","responsibility","anguish","humanism","atheism","choice","condemned to be free","moral dilemmas"]},{"id":"beauvoir-ethics-of-ambiguity","title":"The Ethics of Ambiguity","original":"Pour une morale de l'ambiguïté (French)","author":"beauvoir","authorName":"Simone de Beauvoir","year":1947,"approx":false,"form":"essay","length":"about 160 pages","level":2,"topics":["ethics","self","politics"],"summary":"Beauvoir answers the charge that existentialism, by denying fixed values, cannot have an ethics. Human beings are 'ambiguous', both free and bound by bodies and situations, and we must accept rather than escape this condition. Truly willing your own freedom means also willing the freedom of others.","why":"It is often seen as the fullest ethical statement of French existentialism. It also prepared ideas about oppression that she developed in The Second Sex.","inside":"Three parts; the second describes a gallery of attitudes that flee freedom, such as the 'serious man' who hides behind fixed values, and the 'adventurer' and 'nihilist'.","start":"Start with Part Two, whose portraits of different ways of dodging freedom are concrete and memorable.","line":null,"islands":["existentialists","critical"],"related":["beauvoir-second-sex","sartre-existentialism-is-a-humanism"],"shelf":"phenomenology","tags":["existentialism","ambiguity","freedom","existentialist ethics","the serious man","nihilism","oppression","responsibility","authenticity"]},{"id":"beauvoir-second-sex","title":"The Second Sex","original":"Le Deuxième Sexe (French)","author":"beauvoir","authorName":"Simone de Beauvoir","year":1949,"approx":false,"form":"book","length":"two volumes, about 800 pages","level":2,"topics":["society","self","politics","ethics"],"summary":"Beauvoir asks what a woman is, and argues that women have been defined as the 'Other' of men, never as subjects in their own right. Biology, psychoanalysis and history do not explain this; it is produced by society. Her famous claim, paraphrased, is that one is not born a woman but becomes one.","why":"It is a founding text of modern feminism and underlies the later distinction between sex and gender. Later feminists, including Judith Butler, built on it while questioning parts of it.","inside":"Volume 1, 'Facts and Myths', examines biology, history and myths about women; Volume 2, 'Lived Experience', follows women from childhood through marriage and old age. The first English translation (1953) was heavily abridged; a complete one appeared in 2009.","start":"Read the Introduction to Volume 1, which sets out the idea of woman as Other in about twenty pages.","line":null,"islands":["critical","existentialists"],"related":["beauvoir-ethics-of-ambiguity","butler-gender-trouble","wollstonecraft-vindication-woman"],"shelf":"phenomenology","tags":["feminism","woman as other","one is not born a woman","gender","existentialism","oppression","patriarchy","social construction","women","myth"]},{"id":"ryle-concept-of-mind","title":"The Concept of Mind","original":null,"author":null,"authorName":"Gilbert Ryle","year":1949,"approx":false,"form":"book","length":"about 330 pages","level":2,"topics":["mind","language"],"summary":"Ryle attacks the view, which he traces to Descartes, that a person is a ghostly mind living inside a body-machine: the 'ghost in the machine'. He argues this rests on a 'category mistake', like a visitor who sees Oxford's colleges and libraries and then asks where the university is. Talk about the mind, he says, is largely talk about how people behave and are disposed to behave.","why":"It made 'ghost in the machine' a household phrase and led many philosophers away from Cartesian dualism. Its account is often classed as a form of behaviourism, which Ryle resisted, and most later philosophers thought it left out inner experience.","inside":"Ten chapters on topics such as knowing how and knowing that, the will, emotion, self-knowledge and imagination; Chapter 1, 'Descartes' Myth', is the most famous.","start":"Read Chapter 1, 'Descartes' Myth', which introduces the ghost in the machine and the category mistake.","line":null,"islands":["mind"],"related":["descartes-meditations","turing-computing-machinery-and-intelligence","dennett-consciousness-explained"],"shelf":"cambridge-oxford","tags":["ghost in the machine","category mistake","behaviourism","mind-body dualism","knowing how and knowing that","philosophy of mind","ordinary language philosophy","dispositions","critique of descartes"]},{"id":"turing-computing-machinery-and-intelligence","title":"Computing Machinery and Intelligence","original":null,"author":"turing","authorName":"Alan Turing","year":1950,"approx":false,"form":"paper","length":"a 28-page paper","level":1,"topics":["mind","science"],"summary":"Turing replaces the vague question 'Can machines think?' with a test, the 'imitation game': can a machine answer typed questions so that a judge cannot tell it from a human? He predicts that machines will eventually do well at this and replies to nine objections, from religious ones to the claim that machines can only do what they are told. He ends by suggesting that machines might learn, like children.","why":"Now called the Turing test, it shaped debate about artificial intelligence for over seventy years and has become newly relevant with large language models. Critics such as Searle argue that passing it would not show real understanding.","inside":"Seven sections: the game, a description of digital computers, the objections, and a final section on learning machines.","start":"Read Section 1 on the imitation game, then Section 6 on the objections, which is the liveliest part.","line":null,"islands":["mind"],"related":["searle-minds-brains-and-programs","ryle-concept-of-mind"],"shelf":"logic","tags":["turing test","imitation game","artificial intelligence","can machines think","computation","philosophy of mind","machine learning","consciousness","lady lovelace's objection"]},{"id":"quine-two-dogmas","title":"Two Dogmas of Empiricism","original":null,"author":"quine","authorName":"W. V. O. Quine","year":1951,"approx":false,"form":"paper","length":"a 24-page paper","level":3,"topics":["knowledge","language","logic","science"],"summary":"Quine attacks two assumptions of the logical positivists. The first is that there is a sharp line between 'analytic' truths, true by meaning alone, and 'synthetic' truths, true because of the facts. The second is that each statement can be tested against experience on its own. Instead, he argues, our beliefs face experience as a whole, and any belief, even in logic, could in principle be revised.","why":"It helped end logical positivism as a movement and spread 'holism' about knowledge. Defenders of the analytic and synthetic distinction, including Carnap and later Grice and Strawson, replied that Quine had set an unfair standard of clarity.","inside":"Six sections: four on why 'analyticity' cannot be explained without going in a circle, one on reductionism, and a final section, 'Empiricism without the Dogmas', that sketches his holistic picture.","start":"Read the final section first for the picture he is arguing towards, then go back to the opening sections.","line":null,"islands":["vienna"],"related":["ayer-language-truth-and-logic","kuhn-structure-of-scientific-revolutions"],"shelf":"language-science","tags":["analytic-synthetic distinction","confirmation holism","web of belief","critique of logical positivism","reductionism","empiricism","naturalism","philosophy of language","epistemology"]},{"id":"wittgenstein-philosophical-investigations","title":"Philosophical Investigations","original":"Philosophische Untersuchungen (German)","author":"wittgenstein","authorName":"Ludwig Wittgenstein","year":1953,"approx":false,"form":"aphorisms","length":"about 250 pages","level":2,"topics":["language","mind","logic"],"summary":"Published after Wittgenstein's death, it rejects the picture of language in his own Tractatus. Words do not get meaning by naming things; their meaning lies in their use within 'language games' woven into ways of life. Many philosophical problems, he argues, arise when words are taken out of their ordinary work, and the task is to dissolve them rather than solve them.","why":"It is among the most influential works of twentieth-century philosophy, shaping ordinary language philosophy and later thinking about meaning, rules and the mind. Its private language argument, which questions whether a language only one person could understand is possible, is still debated.","inside":"Numbered remarks rather than chapters; the opening sections criticise Augustine's picture of language, sections 65–67 introduce 'family resemblance', and roughly sections 243–315 contain the private language argument.","start":"Read the first 40 or so remarks, which start from simple examples and set up everything that follows.","line":null,"islands":["wittgenstein"],"related":["wittgenstein-tractatus","kripke-naming-and-necessity"],"shelf":"cambridge-oxford","tags":["language games","meaning is use","private language argument","family resemblance","forms of life","rule-following","ordinary language philosophy","philosophy of language","beetle in a box","philosophy as therapy"]},{"id":"anscombe-intention","title":"Intention","original":null,"author":"anscombe","authorName":"G. E. M. Anscombe","year":1957,"approx":false,"form":"book","length":"about 90 pages","level":3,"topics":["mind","ethics","self"],"summary":"Anscombe asks what makes an action intentional. Her answer is that intentional actions are those to which a certain question, 'Why?', applies in the sense of asking for a reason. One and the same action can be described in many ways, such as moving an arm, pumping water, and poisoning a household, and it may be intentional under some descriptions and not others.","why":"It is often credited with founding modern philosophy of action, and later theorists such as Donald Davidson built on its questions.","inside":"Fifty-two short numbered sections; the example of a man pumping poisoned water into a house is the best known.","start":"Read with a guide and focus on the sections around the man pumping water, which show how one action carries many descriptions.","line":null,"islands":["virtue"],"related":["anscombe-modern-moral-philosophy"],"shelf":"mind","tags":["intention","philosophy of action","intentional action","reasons for action","practical knowledge","action under a description","philosophy of mind","analytic philosophy"]},{"id":"anscombe-modern-moral-philosophy","title":"Modern Moral Philosophy","original":null,"author":"anscombe","authorName":"G. E. M. Anscombe","year":1958,"approx":false,"form":"paper","length":"a 19-page paper","level":2,"topics":["ethics","religion"],"summary":"Anscombe argues that moral philosophy should be put aside until we have a better understanding of psychology: of action, intention and the virtues. The special 'moral ought' used by modern philosophers, she claims, is left over from a religious idea of divine law and no longer makes sense without it. She also criticises the leading English moral philosophers and coins the word 'consequentialism'.","why":"It is widely credited with starting the revival of virtue ethics. Its suggestion that ethics should return to Aristotle's questions about character influenced Foot, MacIntyre and many others.","inside":"A short paper built around three stated theses, which she lays out on the first page and then defends in turn.","start":"Read the first page, where the three theses are stated, then the section on the history of the moral 'ought'.","line":null,"islands":["virtue"],"related":["anscombe-intention","macintyre-after-virtue","aristotle-nicomachean-ethics"],"shelf":"good-life","tags":["virtue ethics","consequentialism","moral ought","divine law","moral psychology","critique of modern ethics","metaethics","intention"]},{"id":"arendt-human-condition","title":"The Human Condition","original":null,"author":"arendt","authorName":"Hannah Arendt","year":1958,"approx":false,"form":"book","length":"about 330 pages","level":2,"topics":["politics","society","self"],"summary":"Arendt distinguishes three kinds of human activity: labour, which keeps us alive; work, which builds a lasting world of objects; and action, through which we appear to one another in public and start something new. She argues that modern societies have let labour and consumption crowd out political action. Each birth, she stresses, brings the capacity for a new beginning.","why":"It shaped later political theory on public life, freedom and citizenship. Some readers find its distinctions too sharp or nostalgic for ancient Greek politics.","inside":"A prologue and six chapters: on the human condition, the public and private realms, labour, work, action, and the modern age.","start":"Read the Prologue and Chapter 1, which set out the three activities, then Chapter 5 on action.","line":null,"islands":["critical"],"related":["arendt-eichmann-in-jerusalem"],"shelf":"justice","tags":["labour work and action","vita activa","public and private","natality","political action","consumer society","modernity","political philosophy","plurality"]},{"id":"berlin-two-concepts-of-liberty","title":"Two Concepts of Liberty","original":null,"author":"berlin","authorName":"Isaiah Berlin","year":1958,"approx":false,"form":"lectures","length":"about 50 pages","level":1,"topics":["politics","ethics"],"summary":"Berlin's inaugural lecture at Oxford distinguishes 'negative' liberty, freedom from interference by others, from 'positive' liberty, being one's own master. He warns that positive liberty has often been twisted into the claim that people can be forced to be free in the name of their 'true' selves. He also defends value pluralism, the view that genuine values can conflict without any final way to rank them.","why":"The two concepts became standard vocabulary in political philosophy. Critics such as Charles Taylor and later republican theorists argued that the division leaves out important kinds of freedom.","inside":"A single lecture, later revised for his collection Four Essays on Liberty (1969), ending with the defence of pluralism.","start":"Read the opening sections, where the two concepts are defined, then the closing pages on pluralism.","line":null,"islands":["mill"],"related":["popper-open-society","mill-on-liberty"],"shelf":"cambridge-oxford","tags":["negative liberty","positive liberty","freedom","value pluralism","liberalism","totalitarianism","political philosophy","autonomy"]},{"id":"fanon-wretched-of-the-earth","title":"The Wretched of the Earth","original":"Les Damnés de la Terre (French)","author":"fanon","authorName":"Frantz Fanon","year":1961,"approx":false,"form":"book","length":"about 300 pages","level":2,"topics":["politics","society","self"],"summary":"Written during the Algerian war of independence, it analyses colonialism as a system of violence that shapes the minds of both colonisers and colonised. Fanon argues that decolonisation is inevitably violent, but warns that a new national elite may simply replace the colonial one. Drawing on his work as a psychiatrist, he also documents mental disorders caused by colonial war.","why":"It became a key text for anti-colonial movements and for postcolonial thought. Its view of violence has been contested from the start, including by Arendt, who criticised its influence in On Violence.","inside":"Five chapters plus a conclusion, with a preface by Sartre: 'On Violence', on spontaneity, 'The Pitfalls of National Consciousness', on national culture, and on colonial war and mental disorders.","start":"Start with 'The Pitfalls of National Consciousness', which is less often quoted than the chapter on violence but shows how carefully Fanon thought about what comes after independence.","line":null,"islands":["margins","critical"],"related":["du-bois-souls-of-black-folk","sartre-being-and-nothingness"],"shelf":"global","tags":["colonialism","decolonisation","anti-colonialism","violence","postcolonialism","national consciousness","revolution","racism","psychology of oppression","political philosophy"]},{"id":"pfstrawson-freedom-and-resentment","title":"Freedom and Resentment","original":null,"author":"pfstrawson","authorName":"P. F. Strawson","year":1962,"approx":false,"form":"paper","length":"a 25-page paper","level":2,"topics":["ethics","self","mind"],"summary":"Strawson shifts the debate about free will away from metaphysics. He starts from our 'reactive attitudes', such as resentment, gratitude and indignation, which are part of ordinary human relationships. Even if determinism were true, he argues, we could not and should not give up these attitudes, so responsibility does not depend on settling whether determinism is true.","why":"It is one of the most influential papers on moral responsibility and inspired a whole school of 'reactive attitude' theories. Critics argue that it does not show that our attitudes are justified, only that they are hard to give up.","inside":"A lecture to the British Academy, built around a contrast between 'optimists' and 'pessimists' about free will that Strawson tries to reconcile.","start":"Read the opening sections on the reactive attitudes and when we suspend them, which are the heart of the paper.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["frankfurt-freedom-of-the-will"],"shelf":"right-wrong","tags":["free will","moral responsibility","reactive attitudes","determinism","compatibilism","resentment","blame","ethics"]},{"id":"kuhn-structure-of-scientific-revolutions","title":"The Structure of Scientific Revolutions","original":null,"author":"kuhn","authorName":"Thomas Kuhn","year":1962,"approx":false,"form":"book","length":"about 200 pages","level":1,"topics":["science","knowledge","society"],"summary":"Kuhn argues that science does not grow by steady accumulation. Most of the time scientists do 'normal science', solving puzzles within a shared 'paradigm', a framework of theories, methods and examples. When unsolved anomalies build up, a crisis can lead to a revolution in which a new paradigm replaces the old, and he suggests the two may be hard to compare on neutral terms.","why":"It changed how scientists, historians and the public think about science, and the phrase 'paradigm shift' entered everyday speech. Critics, including Popper and Lakatos, said it made scientific change look irrational, which Kuhn denied.","inside":"Thirteen short sections tracing the cycle from normal science through anomaly and crisis to revolution; a postscript added in the 1970 second edition replies to critics.","start":"Read it from the start; it is short, and the early sections on normal science are needed to understand revolutions.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["popper-logic-of-scientific-discovery","quine-two-dogmas"],"shelf":"language-science","tags":["paradigm shift","scientific revolutions","normal science","incommensurability","philosophy of science","anomalies","history of science","sociology of science"]},{"id":"arendt-eichmann-in-jerusalem","title":"Eichmann in Jerusalem","original":null,"author":"arendt","authorName":"Hannah Arendt","year":1963,"approx":false,"form":"book","length":"about 300 pages","level":1,"topics":["ethics","politics","society"],"summary":"Arendt reported on the 1961 trial of Adolf Eichmann, one of the organisers of the Holocaust, first for The New Yorker and then in this book, subtitled 'A Report on the Banality of Evil'. She argues that Eichmann was not a demonic monster but a careerist who seemed unable to think from anyone else's point of view. Great evil, she suggests, can be done by ordinary people who simply fail to think.","why":"The phrase 'banality of evil' changed how people discuss responsibility under bureaucracy and dictatorship. It caused fierce controversy, especially over her remarks on Jewish councils, and later historians have argued that Eichmann was more committed to Nazi ideology than she believed.","inside":"Fifteen chapters following the trial and Eichmann's career, followed by an epilogue and a postscript replying to critics.","start":"Read the epilogue and postscript first, where Arendt states her judgement and the meaning of 'banality' most directly.","line":null,"islands":["critical"],"related":["arendt-human-condition"],"shelf":"justice","tags":["banality of evil","evil","holocaust","totalitarianism","thoughtlessness","moral responsibility","obedience","judgement","nazism"]},{"id":"gettier-justified-true-belief","title":"Is Justified True Belief Knowledge?","original":null,"author":null,"authorName":"Edmund Gettier","year":1963,"approx":false,"form":"paper","length":"a three-page paper","level":1,"topics":["knowledge","logic"],"summary":"A tiny paper that challenges the old idea that knowledge is a belief that is true and justified. Gettier gives two cases in which someone has a justified true belief that is true only by luck, so it does not seem to be knowledge. In one, Smith believes the man who will get a job has ten coins in his pocket: this turns out true, but because of Smith's own unnoticed coins.","why":"It started a decades-long search for a 'fourth condition' for knowledge, and such examples are now called Gettier cases. Some philosophers, such as Timothy Williamson, concluded that knowledge cannot be analysed into simpler parts at all.","inside":"A statement of the standard analysis, followed by two counterexamples; the ten-coins case is the better known.","start":"Read the whole paper; it is about three pages long and needs no background beyond the definition it attacks.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["plato-theaetetus"],"shelf":"language-science","tags":["gettier problem","justified true belief","epistemology","knowledge","analysis of knowledge","epistemic luck","counterexample","thought experiment"]},{"id":"foot-problem-of-abortion","title":"The Problem of Abortion and the Doctrine of the Double Effect","original":null,"author":"foot","authorName":"Philippa Foot","year":1967,"approx":false,"form":"paper","length":"an 11-page paper","level":1,"topics":["ethics","life and death"],"summary":"Foot examines the doctrine of double effect, the idea that it can be permissible to cause harm as a side effect when it would be wrong to intend it. She compares cases, including a runaway tram whose driver can steer it to kill one person instead of five. She suggests the important difference is often between negative duties not to harm and weaker positive duties to help.","why":"The tram case became the 'trolley problem', a name later given by Judith Jarvis Thomson, and has spread from ethics into law, psychology and debates about self-driving cars. Thomson and others challenged Foot's own explanation of the cases.","inside":"A short paper that moves through a series of contrasting cases, with the tram driver appearing as one example among several.","start":"Read the whole paper but pay most attention to the pairs of cases she contrasts, which show how the method of comparing examples works.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["thomson-defense-of-abortion"],"shelf":"right-wrong","tags":["trolley problem","doctrine of double effect","abortion","negative and positive duties","killing vs letting die","applied ethics","thought experiment","bioethics"]},{"id":"murdoch-sovereignty-of-good","title":"The Sovereignty of Good","original":null,"author":"murdoch","authorName":"Iris Murdoch","year":1970,"approx":false,"form":"essay","length":"three essays, about 100 pages","level":2,"topics":["ethics","self","beauty","religion"],"summary":"Murdoch argues that moral life is not just a series of choices but a continuous effort of 'attention': learning to see other people justly and lovingly. Her example is a mother-in-law who, without any outward change, comes to see her son's wife more kindly. Art, nature and study help us escape our self-absorbed ego and see what is real.","why":"It was an early and influential challenge to the choice-centred ethics of her day and fed the revival of virtue ethics. Its emphasis on moral perception is still widely discussed.","inside":"Three essays: 'The Idea of Perfection', 'On \"God\" and \"Good\"', and 'The Sovereignty of Good over Other Concepts'; the kestrel she watches from a window is a famous image of 'unselfing'.","start":"Start with 'The Idea of Perfection', which contains the mother-in-law example.","line":null,"islands":["virtue"],"related":["anscombe-modern-moral-philosophy","macintyre-after-virtue"],"shelf":"good-life","tags":["moral attention","unselfing","the good","moral vision","love","virtue ethics","art","beauty","ego","platonism"]},{"id":"rawls-theory-of-justice","title":"A Theory of Justice","original":null,"author":"rawls","authorName":"John Rawls","year":1971,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 600 pages","level":2,"topics":["politics","ethics","society"],"summary":"Rawls asks which principles of justice free and equal people would agree to if they did not know their own place in society: their class, talents or beliefs. Behind this 'veil of ignorance', he argues, they would choose equal basic liberties for all, and would allow inequalities only if they benefit the least advantaged. He presents this 'justice as fairness' as an alternative to utilitarianism.","why":"It revived political philosophy in the English-speaking world and is the reference point for nearly all later work on justice. Nozick criticised it from the right, while communitarians and Marxists challenged its assumptions about the self and society.","inside":"Three parts: theory, institutions and ends; the first three chapters, which present the original position and the two principles, are the most read. A revised edition appeared in 1999.","start":"Read Chapter 1, sections 1–4, then Chapter 3 on the original position.","line":null,"islands":["justice"],"related":["nozick-anarchy-state-and-utopia","kant-groundwork"],"shelf":"justice","tags":["veil of ignorance","original position","justice as fairness","difference principle","social contract","distributive justice","liberalism","equality","reflective equilibrium","political philosophy"]},{"id":"thomson-defense-of-abortion","title":"A Defense of Abortion","original":null,"author":"thomson","authorName":"Judith Jarvis Thomson","year":1971,"approx":false,"form":"paper","length":"a 20-page paper","level":1,"topics":["ethics","life and death"],"summary":"Thomson grants, for the sake of argument, that a foetus is a person with a right to life. She then argues that this does not settle whether abortion is wrong, because a right to life does not include a right to use another person's body. In her best-known example you wake up connected to a famous violinist who will die if you unplug yourself.","why":"It changed the abortion debate by shifting attention from whether the foetus is a person to what we owe one another. Critics argue that the violinist case differs from pregnancy in important ways, for example that parents have special duties to their children.","inside":"A series of thought experiments, including the violinist, 'people-seeds' drifting through windows, and the ill person who needs a cool hand.","start":"Read the opening with the violinist, then follow how she adjusts the case to answer objections.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["foot-problem-of-abortion"],"shelf":"right-wrong","tags":["abortion","violinist thought experiment","bodily autonomy","right to life","applied ethics","bioethics","feminism","thought experiment","good samaritan"]},{"id":"frankfurt-freedom-of-the-will","title":"Freedom of the Will and the Concept of a Person","original":null,"author":"frankfurt","authorName":"Harry Frankfurt","year":1971,"approx":false,"form":"paper","length":"a 15-page paper","level":2,"topics":["self","mind","ethics"],"summary":"Frankfurt argues that what makes us persons is our capacity to have desires about our desires. An addict who wishes he did not want the drug has a second-order desire against his first-order craving. Someone acts freely, he suggests, when the desire that moves them is one they want to be moved by.","why":"It gave a new model of free will that does not depend on whether determinism is true. Critics ask why second-order desires should have more authority than first-order ones, since they could be manipulated too.","inside":"A short journal paper that defines orders of desire, contrasts the 'unwilling addict' with the 'wanton' who has no second-order desires, and then applies this to freedom.","start":"Read it straight through; the addict examples make the abstract idea clear.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["pfstrawson-freedom-and-resentment"],"shelf":"mind","tags":["free will","second-order desires","personhood","hierarchy of desires","compatibilism","autonomy","the wanton","moral responsibility"]},{"id":"singer-famine-affluence-morality","title":"Famine, Affluence, and Morality","original":null,"author":"singer","authorName":"Peter Singer","year":1972,"approx":false,"form":"paper","length":"a 15-page paper","level":1,"topics":["ethics","society"],"summary":"Written in response to the 1971 famine in East Bengal, the paper argues that if we can prevent something very bad without giving up anything of comparable moral importance, we ought to do it. If you would wade into a shallow pond to save a drowning child, distance and the number of others who could help make no moral difference. Giving to relieve famine is therefore a duty, not charity.","why":"It is one of the most widely read papers in ethics and helped inspire the effective altruism movement. Critics argue its conclusion demands too much and ignores special duties to family and community.","inside":"A short argument built on one principle and the drowning child example, followed by replies to objections.","start":"Read the whole paper; the opening pages with the pond example carry the argument.","line":null,"islands":["singer"],"related":["singer-animal-liberation"],"shelf":"right-wrong","tags":["drowning child","global poverty","duty to aid","effective altruism","charity","utilitarianism","applied ethics","moral demandingness","famine relief"]},{"id":"williams-utilitarianism-for-and-against","title":"Utilitarianism: For and Against","original":null,"author":"williams","authorName":"J. J. C. Smart and Bernard Williams","year":1973,"approx":false,"form":"book","length":"about 150 pages","level":2,"topics":["ethics","self"],"summary":"A two-part book: Smart sets out and defends a straightforward act utilitarianism, and Williams attacks it. Williams argues that utilitarianism treats a person's own deepest commitments as just one input among many, which undermines their integrity. His cases of George, a chemist offered work on chemical weapons, and Jim, told he can save nineteen villagers by shooting one, are much discussed.","why":"Williams's critique is a standard objection to utilitarianism and helped move ethics towards questions of character and personal projects. Utilitarians reply that the cases trade on intuitions the theory can explain or correct.","inside":"Smart's essay first, then Williams's 'A Critique of Utilitarianism', whose section on integrity contains the George and Jim cases.","start":"Read the section of Williams's essay on integrity, which can be understood without reading Smart first.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["mill-utilitarianism","singer-famine-affluence-morality"],"shelf":"right-wrong","tags":["utilitarianism","integrity","jim and the indians","act utilitarianism","critique of utilitarianism","consequentialism","moral responsibility","negative responsibility","ethics"]},{"id":"nagel-what-is-it-like-to-be-a-bat","title":"What Is It Like to Be a Bat?","original":null,"author":"nagel","authorName":"Thomas Nagel","year":1974,"approx":false,"form":"paper","length":"a 15-page paper","level":1,"topics":["mind","science"],"summary":"Nagel argues that an organism has conscious experience if there is 'something it is like' to be that organism. A bat senses the world by echolocation, and however much we learn about its brain from the outside, we cannot know what that is like from the inside. So physical science, which describes things objectively, seems to leave out the subjective character of experience.","why":"It became the standard way of stating why consciousness is hard to explain physically and prepared the way for Chalmers's 'hard problem'. Physicalists such as Dennett argue that Nagel overrates what we cannot know.","inside":"A short paper that introduces the 'what it is like' test, the bat example, and closes with a proposal for an 'objective phenomenology'.","start":"Read the whole paper; it is short and its opening pages state the problem better than most textbooks.","line":null,"islands":["mind"],"related":["jackson-epiphenomenal-qualia","chalmers-conscious-mind","dennett-consciousness-explained"],"shelf":"mind","tags":["consciousness","what it is like","subjective experience","qualia","physicalism","mind-body problem","philosophy of mind","thought experiment","objectivity"]},{"id":"nozick-anarchy-state-and-utopia","title":"Anarchy, State, and Utopia","original":null,"author":"nozick","authorName":"Robert Nozick","year":1974,"approx":false,"form":"book","length":"about 370 pages","level":2,"topics":["politics","ethics","society"],"summary":"Nozick argues that only a minimal state, limited to protecting people against force, theft and fraud, is justified. On his 'entitlement theory', a distribution of wealth is just if it came about through just acquisition and free exchange, whatever pattern results. Taxation to redistribute income, he claims, is on a par with forced labour.","why":"It is the most influential philosophical defence of libertarianism and the main reply to Rawls. Critics, including G. A. Cohen, attack its account of how anyone first comes to own unowned things.","inside":"Three parts: how a state could arise from anarchy without violating rights, the critique of patterned justice with the Wilt Chamberlain example, and a closing vision of utopia as a framework for many communities. The 'experience machine' thought experiment appears in Part I.","start":"Read Chapter 7, section 1, on the entitlement theory and the Wilt Chamberlain example.","line":null,"islands":["justice"],"related":["rawls-theory-of-justice"],"shelf":"justice","tags":["libertarianism","minimal state","entitlement theory","experience machine","wilt chamberlain argument","taxation","property rights","distributive justice","political philosophy","utopia"]},{"id":"singer-animal-liberation","title":"Animal Liberation","original":null,"author":"singer","authorName":"Peter Singer","year":1975,"approx":false,"form":"book","length":"about 300 pages","level":1,"topics":["ethics","society"],"summary":"Singer argues that the capacity to suffer, not intelligence or species, is what gives a being interests that count morally. Giving less weight to animals' suffering simply because they are not human is 'speciesism', which he compares to racism and sexism. He describes factory farming and animal experiments in detail and argues for a vegetarian diet.","why":"It helped launch the modern animal rights movement. Critics argue that the comparison with racism is misleading, or that morality rests on relationships or rationality that animals lack.","inside":"Six chapters: the principle of equality, then experiments, factory farming, becoming vegetarian, the history of 'man's dominion', and replies to objections; revised in 1990 and again as Animal Liberation Now in 2023.","start":"Read Chapter 1, 'All Animals Are Equal', which contains the whole philosophical argument.","line":null,"islands":["singer"],"related":["singer-famine-affluence-morality"],"shelf":"right-wrong","tags":["animal rights","speciesism","animal welfare","factory farming","vegetarianism","suffering","utilitarianism","applied ethics","equality"]},{"id":"foucault-discipline-and-punish","title":"Discipline and Punish","original":"Surveiller et punir (French)","author":null,"authorName":"Michel Foucault","year":1975,"approx":false,"form":"book","length":"about 300 pages","level":2,"topics":["society","politics","self"],"summary":"Foucault traces the shift in Europe from punishment as public torture to the modern prison. He argues this was not simply growing kindness but a new kind of power, 'discipline', that works by constant observation, timetables, exams and records. Schools, armies, factories and hospitals all use it to shape people into useful, obedient bodies.","why":"Its analysis of surveillance and 'power/knowledge' influenced sociology, history, criminology and political theory. Historians have questioned some of its evidence and timeline, and critics such as Habermas argue it leaves no ground for saying why domination is wrong.","inside":"Four parts: torture, punishment, discipline and the prison; it opens with a gruesome account of a public execution in 1757, and its chapter on Bentham's 'Panopticon' prison design is the best known.","start":"Read the chapter on 'Panopticism' in Part Three, which states the main idea in its most vivid form.","line":null,"islands":["structures"],"related":["butler-gender-trouble"],"shelf":"critical","tags":["panopticon","power","discipline","surveillance","punishment","prison","docile bodies","biopower","social control","post-structuralism"]},{"id":"mackie-ethics-inventing-right-and-wrong","title":"Ethics: Inventing Right and Wrong","original":null,"author":"mackie","authorName":"J. L. Mackie","year":1977,"approx":false,"form":"book","length":"about 250 pages","level":2,"topics":["ethics","metaphysics"],"summary":"Mackie opens with the claim that there are no objective values. Ordinary moral talk assumes that some things are objectively required, but nothing like that exists, so all positive moral claims are false: this is called an error theory. He supports it with the 'argument from relativity', from wide moral disagreement, and the 'argument from queerness', that objective values would be strange entities unlike anything else.","why":"It made the error theory a serious position and set much of the agenda for later debates about whether morality is objective. Moral realists have spent decades answering the argument from queerness.","inside":"Part One, especially Chapter 1, makes the case against objective values; the rest of the book builds a practical morality on that basis.","start":"Read Chapter 1, 'The Subjectivity of Values', which is self-contained and contains both famous arguments.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["moore-principia-ethica","ayer-language-truth-and-logic"],"shelf":"right-wrong","tags":["error theory","moral anti-realism","argument from queerness","argument from relativity","metaethics","moral skepticism","objective values","moral disagreement"]},{"id":"kripke-naming-and-necessity","title":"Naming and Necessity","original":null,"author":"kripke","authorName":"Saul Kripke","year":1972,"approx":false,"form":"lectures","length":"about 170 pages","level":3,"topics":["language","metaphysics","logic"],"summary":"Three lectures given at Princeton in 1970, first printed in 1972 and published as a book in 1980. Kripke argues that names are not disguised descriptions but 'rigid designators' that pick out the same thing in every possible situation, and that a name refers because of a chain of use going back to the first naming. He also argues that some truths, such as that water is H2O, are necessary even though we discover them by experience.","why":"It overturned the descriptive theory of names associated with Frege and Russell and revived metaphysics in analytic philosophy. Its brief argument against identifying mind and brain is also still discussed.","inside":"Three lectures, transcribed from talks given without notes, so the style is informal; the Gödel and Schmidt example in Lecture II is the best-known argument against description theories.","start":"Start with Lecture I and the beginning of Lecture II, read alongside an introduction to Frege and Russell on names.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["russell-on-denoting","putnam-meaning-of-meaning"],"shelf":"language-science","tags":["rigid designators","causal theory of reference","necessary a posteriori","possible worlds","modal logic","philosophy of language","essentialism","names","critique of descriptivism"]},{"id":"putnam-meaning-of-meaning","title":"The Meaning of 'Meaning'","original":null,"author":"putnam","authorName":"Hilary Putnam","year":1975,"approx":false,"form":"paper","length":"a long essay","level":2,"topics":["language","mind"],"summary":"Putnam imagines Twin Earth, a planet just like Earth except that the stuff in its rivers looks and tastes like water but has a different chemical make-up. When Oscar on Earth and his twin on Twin Earth say 'water', they are in the same mental state but mean different things. So, Putnam concludes, meanings are not just in the head: they depend on the world and on the community of speakers.","why":"Twin Earth became one of the most used thought experiments in philosophy of language and mind, and launched 'externalism' about meaning and mental content. Internalists reply that there is still a 'narrow' content that the twins share.","inside":"A long essay; the Twin Earth case is in the first part, followed by the 'division of linguistic labour' and a theory of how natural-kind words work.","start":"Read the Twin Earth section near the beginning, which is clear and self-contained.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["kripke-naming-and-necessity"],"shelf":"language-science","tags":["twin earth","semantic externalism","meanings ain't in the head","philosophy of language","natural kinds","division of linguistic labour","thought experiment","philosophy of mind"]},{"id":"searle-minds-brains-and-programs","title":"Minds, Brains, and Programs","original":null,"author":"searle","authorName":"John Searle","year":1980,"approx":false,"form":"paper","length":"a 20-page paper, published with replies","level":1,"topics":["mind","science","language"],"summary":"Searle imagines himself in a room following a rulebook for matching Chinese symbols. People outside pass in questions in Chinese and get back answers that look fluent, yet Searle understands no Chinese. Since a computer running a program only manipulates symbols in the same way, he argues, running the right program can never by itself be enough for understanding.","why":"The Chinese room is one of the most discussed arguments about artificial intelligence and is often raised about large language models. Many critics reply that the whole system, room and rulebook included, might understand even if Searle does not.","inside":"A target article in Behavioral and Brain Sciences, printed with many commentaries; Searle states the thought experiment, then answers six standard replies.","start":"Read the description of the room and then the 'systems reply', the objection most people raise first.","line":null,"islands":["mind"],"related":["turing-computing-machinery-and-intelligence","dennett-consciousness-explained"],"shelf":"mind","tags":["chinese room","artificial intelligence","strong ai","understanding","syntax and semantics","philosophy of mind","thought experiment","computation","intentionality"]},{"id":"wiredu-philosophy-and-an-african-culture","title":"Philosophy and an African Culture","original":null,"author":null,"authorName":"Kwasi Wiredu","year":1980,"approx":false,"form":"book","length":"a book-length collection of essays","level":2,"topics":["knowledge","society","language"],"summary":"A collection of essays by the Ghanaian philosopher Kwasi Wiredu. He argues that traditional African thought deserves careful philosophical study, but should be examined critically, like any tradition, rather than simply celebrated or treated as the unanimous view of a whole people. He compares concepts in his own Akan language with English ones to test which philosophical problems are universal and which arise from a particular language.","why":"It helped set the agenda for modern African philosophy and for later debates about what decolonising philosophy should mean. Some critics replied that his approach risked judging African thought too much by Western standards.","inside":"A set of separate essays rather than one continuous argument; those comparing Akan and English concepts, including the concept of truth, are the most discussed.","start":"Read the opening essays on philosophy and tradition, which set out his view of how African philosophy should proceed.","line":null,"islands":["margins"],"related":["ambedkar-annihilation-of-caste","fanon-wretched-of-the-earth"],"shelf":"global","tags":["african philosophy","akan philosophy","conceptual decolonisation","ethnophilosophy","truth","tradition","philosophy of language","cultural universals"]},{"id":"macintyre-after-virtue","title":"After Virtue","original":null,"author":"macintyre","authorName":"Alasdair MacIntyre","year":1981,"approx":false,"form":"book","length":"about 290 pages","level":2,"topics":["ethics","society","self"],"summary":"MacIntyre opens with an image of a world where science has been destroyed and only fragments survive; he claims modern moral language is in a similar state. The Enlightenment attempt to justify morality without a shared idea of the human good failed, leaving endless unresolvable debates. He proposes a return to Aristotle's virtues, understood through shared practices, the narrative unity of a life, and living traditions.","why":"It was a major force in the revival of virtue ethics and in communitarian criticism of liberalism. Critics argue that its history is too selective and that it gives little guidance on choosing between traditions.","inside":"Eighteen chapters; the first describes the 'disquieting suggestion', Chapter 9 asks 'Nietzsche or Aristotle?', and Chapters 14 and 15 develop practices and narrative.","start":"Read Chapters 1 and 2 for the diagnosis, then Chapter 15 on the narrative unity of a life.","line":null,"islands":["virtue"],"related":["anscombe-modern-moral-philosophy","aristotle-nicomachean-ethics","murdoch-sovereignty-of-good"],"shelf":"good-life","tags":["virtue ethics","aristotelianism","communitarianism","critique of enlightenment","narrative self","practices","tradition","moral disagreement","emotivism","nietzsche or aristotle"]},{"id":"jackson-epiphenomenal-qualia","title":"Epiphenomenal Qualia","original":null,"author":"jackson","authorName":"Frank Jackson","year":1982,"approx":false,"form":"paper","length":"a 10-page paper","level":1,"topics":["mind","knowledge"],"summary":"Jackson imagines Mary, a brilliant scientist who knows every physical fact about colour vision but has lived her whole life in a black-and-white room. When she first sees a red tomato, she seems to learn something new: what seeing red is like. If so, not all facts are physical facts, and physicalism, the view that everything is physical, is false.","why":"The 'knowledge argument' is one of the main arguments against physicalism. Jackson himself later rejected it and became a physicalist, and replies include the view that Mary gains a new ability rather than new facts.","inside":"A short paper; Mary appears alongside a less famous case, Fred, who can see a colour no one else can.","start":"Read the section with Mary first; the rest of the paper defends the view that such conscious qualities, called qualia, have no physical effects.","line":null,"islands":["mind"],"related":["nagel-what-is-it-like-to-be-a-bat","chalmers-conscious-mind"],"shelf":"mind","tags":["mary's room","knowledge argument","qualia","physicalism","epiphenomenalism","consciousness","philosophy of mind","thought experiment"]},{"id":"parfit-reasons-and-persons","title":"Reasons and Persons","original":null,"author":"parfit","authorName":"Derek Parfit","year":1984,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 540 pages","level":3,"topics":["ethics","self","metaphysics","life and death"],"summary":"Parfit argues, using cases such as teleportation and the splitting of brains, that personal identity is not what matters. What we should care about is psychological connectedness and continuity, which can come in degrees. He also explores moral puzzles about future generations, including the non-identity problem and what he called the 'repugnant conclusion'.","why":"It changed debates about personal identity, rationality and our duties to future people, and remains a reference point in population ethics. Critics such as Schechtman argue that its picture of the self leaves out the narrative and practical side of being a person.","inside":"Four parts: self-defeating theories, rationality and time, personal identity, and future generations; Part Three on personal identity is the best known.","start":"Start with Part Three, especially the teleportation cases, which can be read on its own.","line":null,"islands":["parfit"],"related":["locke-essay","williams-utilitarianism-for-and-against"],"shelf":"right-wrong","tags":["personal identity","teleportation","psychological continuity","non-identity problem","repugnant conclusion","future generations","population ethics","rationality","what matters","consequentialism"]},{"id":"noddings-caring","title":"Caring","original":null,"author":"noddings","authorName":"Nel Noddings","year":1984,"approx":false,"form":"book","length":"about 200 pages","level":1,"topics":["ethics","society","self"],"summary":"Noddings argues that ethics should start not from abstract principles but from caring relations between particular people, such as a parent and child or a teacher and student. Caring involves attention to the other person and acting for their sake, and it is completed only when the one cared for responds. She applies this to moral education.","why":"It was a founding text of the ethics of care, alongside Carol Gilligan's In a Different Voice (1982). Critics worried that its original subtitle, 'A Feminine Approach', tied care to gender, and a later edition changed it to 'A Relational Approach'.","inside":"The book moves from the caring relation itself, through the roles of the one caring and the one cared for, to moral education; it also discusses the limits of our obligations to distant strangers and animals.","start":"Read the opening chapter on why care matters, which sets out the contrast with principle-based ethics.","line":null,"islands":["virtue"],"related":["murdoch-sovereignty-of-good"],"shelf":"good-life","tags":["ethics of care","caring","feminist ethics","relationships","moral education","empathy","attention","philosophy of education"]},{"id":"butler-gender-trouble","title":"Gender Trouble","original":null,"author":null,"authorName":"Judith Butler","year":1990,"approx":false,"form":"book","length":"about 170 pages","level":3,"topics":["society","self","politics"],"summary":"Butler questions the common view that biological sex is a natural fact on which culture builds gender. Gender, she argues, is 'performative': it is produced through repeated acts and norms rather than expressing an inner essence. She suggests that drag can show how all gender is performed.","why":"It became a founding text of queer theory and is widely cited in the humanities. Critics, including Martha Nussbaum, argued that its style is needlessly obscure and its politics too weak, and debate over its views on sex remains heated.","inside":"Three chapters: on the subject of 'woman' and the sex/gender distinction, on psychoanalysis and how gender norms are produced, and on 'subversive bodily acts', where drag is discussed.","start":"Read the preface and the final section on subversive bodily acts with a guide, since the dense style makes the main idea easy to miss.","line":null,"islands":["structures","critical"],"related":["beauvoir-second-sex","foucault-discipline-and-punish"],"shelf":"critical","tags":["gender performativity","gender","queer theory","feminism","sex and gender","drag","identity","social construction","post-structuralism"]},{"id":"dennett-consciousness-explained","title":"Consciousness Explained","original":null,"author":"dennett","authorName":"Daniel Dennett","year":1991,"approx":false,"form":"book","length":"about 500 pages","level":2,"topics":["mind","science","self"],"summary":"Dennett attacks the idea of a 'Cartesian theatre', a central place in the brain where everything comes together for an inner viewer. He replaces it with a 'multiple drafts' model in which many brain processes produce rival versions of experience, none of them final. Much of what we think we know about our inner experience, including qualia, is, he argues, a mistake.","why":"It is the best-known statement of a fully physicalist view of consciousness. Critics joked that it should have been called 'Consciousness Explained Away', arguing that it denies the very thing to be explained.","inside":"Three parts: the problems and methods, including his method of 'heterophenomenology' for studying reports of experience; the multiple drafts model; and the philosophical consequences.","start":"Start with the early chapters on the Cartesian theatre, which explain what Dennett is arguing against.","line":null,"islands":["mind"],"related":["chalmers-conscious-mind","ryle-concept-of-mind","nagel-what-is-it-like-to-be-a-bat"],"shelf":"mind","tags":["consciousness","cartesian theatre","multiple drafts model","heterophenomenology","qualia","illusionism","physicalism","philosophy of mind","cognitive science","the self"]},{"id":"chalmers-conscious-mind","title":"The Conscious Mind","original":null,"author":"chalmers","authorName":"David Chalmers","year":1996,"approx":false,"form":"book","length":"about 400 pages","level":3,"topics":["mind","metaphysics","science"],"summary":"Chalmers separates the 'easy problems' of consciousness, explaining how the brain processes information and controls behaviour, from the 'hard problem': why any of this is accompanied by experience at all. He argues that we can coherently imagine 'zombies', physical duplicates of us with no inner life, so consciousness is not fixed by physical facts alone. He defends a 'naturalistic dualism' in which experience is a basic feature of nature.","why":"It made the hard problem a central topic in philosophy and consciousness science. Physicalists such as Dennett reject the zombie argument, arguing that what we can imagine tells us little about what is possible.","inside":"Four parts: foundations, the irreducibility of consciousness with the zombie argument, a positive theory, and applications to artificial intelligence and quantum mechanics.","start":"Read the introduction and the chapter on why consciousness is not logically supervenient on the physical, which contains the zombie argument.","line":null,"islands":["mind"],"related":["nagel-what-is-it-like-to-be-a-bat","jackson-epiphenomenal-qualia","dennett-consciousness-explained"],"shelf":"mind","tags":["hard problem of consciousness","philosophical zombies","consciousness","property dualism","qualia","physicalism","philosophy of mind","panpsychism","artificial intelligence","naturalistic dualism"]},{"id":"korsgaard-sources-of-normativity","title":"The Sources of Normativity","original":null,"author":"korsgaard","authorName":"Christine Korsgaard","year":1996,"approx":false,"form":"lectures","length":"about 270 pages","level":3,"topics":["ethics","self"],"summary":"Based on Korsgaard's Tanner Lectures of 1992, it asks what gives moral demands their authority over us. She argues, in a Kantian spirit, that it comes from reflection itself: in deciding what to do we must act under some 'practical identity', and valuing any such identity commits us to valuing our humanity. That, in turn, commits us to morality.","why":"It is one of the most discussed modern defences of a Kantian, constructivist view of ethics. It was published with replies from G. A. Cohen, Raymond Geuss, Thomas Nagel and Bernard Williams, who press objections that are still debated.","inside":"Four lectures: the first surveys rival answers, the third presents her own view based on practical identity, then four replies and her response.","start":"Read Lecture 1, which is a clear survey of the main theories of where moral authority comes from.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["kant-groundwork","rawls-theory-of-justice"],"shelf":"right-wrong","tags":["normativity","practical identity","kantian ethics","reflective endorsement","metaethics","constructivism","moral obligation","autonomy","deontology"]},{"id":"nussbaum-women-and-human-development","title":"Women and Human Development","original":null,"author":"nussbaum","authorName":"Martha Nussbaum","year":2000,"approx":false,"form":"book","length":"about 300 pages","level":2,"topics":["politics","society","ethics"],"summary":"Nussbaum develops the 'capabilities approach', which judges societies by what people are actually able to do and be, not just by income or reported satisfaction. She proposes a list of central capabilities, such as health, bodily integrity, education, play and political participation, that every government should secure up to a threshold. She grounds the argument in the lives of poor women in India.","why":"With Amartya Sen's work, the capabilities approach shaped how bodies such as the United Nations measure human development. Critics question who has the authority to draw up a universal list, and whether it is fair to cultures with different values.","inside":"Four chapters: a defence of universal values, which includes the list of capabilities, then chapters on adaptive preferences, the role of religion, and love and care in families.","start":"Read Chapter 1, which explains why she thinks universal standards are needed and gives the list.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["rawls-theory-of-justice","aristotle-politics"],"shelf":"justice","tags":["capabilities approach","human development","feminism","global justice","adaptive preferences","human rights","women","political philosophy","wellbeing"]},{"id":"xenophanes-fragments","title":"Fragments of Xenophanes","original":"Greek verse fragments","author":null,"authorName":"Xenophanes of Colophon","year":-500,"approx":true,"form":"poem fragments","length":"a few pages of surviving verse","level":1,"topics":["religion","knowledge"],"summary":"Xenophanes was a wandering poet who mocked the Greek habit of picturing the gods as people. He pointed out that Ethiopians imagine their gods dark-skinned and Thracians imagine theirs blue-eyed and red-haired, and proposed instead a single god unlike humans in body or thought. He also doubted that anyone could have certain knowledge of such matters.","why":"It is one of the earliest pieces of religious criticism in Western thought and an early statement of the gap between belief and knowledge. Later skeptics claimed him as a forerunner.","inside":"About forty short fragments quoted by later writers such as Clement of Alexandria and Sextus Empiricus, covering the gods, nature and the limits of human knowledge.","start":"Read the fragments about the gods first; they are short and surprisingly modern.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["heraclitus-fragments","parmenides-on-nature"],"shelf":"greece","tags":["critique of anthropomorphism","monotheism","gods","skepticism","pre-socratic philosophy","philosophy of religion","limits of knowledge","religious relativism"]},{"id":"zeno-paradoxes","title":"Zeno's Paradoxes","original":null,"author":"zeno_elea","authorName":"Zeno of Elea","year":-460,"approx":true,"form":"arguments (reported by others)","length":"a handful of short arguments","level":1,"topics":["metaphysics","logic","science"],"summary":"Zeno defended his teacher Parmenides by arguing that motion and plurality lead to absurdities. Achilles can never catch a tortoise with a head start, because he must first reach where it was, by which time it has moved on; a flying arrow is at rest at every instant, so it never moves. His own book is lost, and the arguments survive mainly through Aristotle's Physics.","why":"The paradoxes forced philosophers and mathematicians to think hard about infinity, continuity and time. Debates about how best to answer them continued into the age of calculus and beyond.","inside":"Four famous paradoxes of motion (the Dichotomy, Achilles, the Arrow and the Stadium) plus arguments against plurality, mostly found in Aristotle's Physics Book VI and in Simplicius.","start":"Start with Achilles and the tortoise, then try to say exactly what is wrong with it before reading any solution.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["parmenides-on-nature","aristotle-physics"],"shelf":"greece","tags":["zeno's paradoxes","achilles and the tortoise","arrow paradox","dichotomy paradox","motion","infinity","pre-socratic philosophy","eleatic school","plurality","metaphysics"]},{"id":"empedocles-on-nature-and-purifications","title":"On Nature and Purifications","original":"Peri Physeos and Katharmoi (Greek)","author":null,"authorName":"Empedocles","year":-450,"approx":true,"form":"poem fragments","length":"about 150 surviving fragments","level":2,"topics":["metaphysics","science","religion"],"summary":"Empedocles taught that everything is made of four 'roots' (earth, water, air and fire) mixed and separated by two forces, Love and Strife. The cosmos swings in a great cycle between total union under Love and total separation under Strife. In a second, religious poem he describes souls falling into a cycle of rebirths as punishment and urges people not to kill animals.","why":"His four elements shaped Western science for some two thousand years. He also offered an early picture of something like natural selection, imagining random animal parts combining and only workable creatures surviving.","inside":"Fragments of two hexameter poems, quoted by later authors, plus pieces of a papyrus from Strasbourg published in 1999 that added new lines.","start":"Read the fragments about Love and Strife and the four roots, then the passages on the cycle of the cosmos.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["parmenides-on-nature","lucretius-on-the-nature-of-things","democritus-fragments"],"shelf":"greece","tags":["four elements","love and strife","cosmic cycle","reincarnation","pre-socratic philosophy","vegetarianism","philosophy of nature","cosmology","metaphysics"]},{"id":"protagoras-fragments","title":"Fragments of Protagoras","original":"Greek prose fragments","author":"protagoras","authorName":"Protagoras","year":-440,"approx":true,"form":"fragments","length":"a few sentences, plus reports by Plato and others","level":1,"topics":["knowledge","religion","society"],"summary":"Protagoras was the most famous of the Sophists, the paid teachers of argument in classical Greece. Only a few sentences of his writing survive: that 'man is the measure of all things', and that about the gods he could not know whether they exist or what they are like. Most of what we know comes from Plato, who argued against him.","why":"His measure saying is the classic early statement of relativism, the view that truth depends on the person or community judging. His agnostic remark about the gods is one of the first on record.","inside":"Two or three famous fragments, the most important preserved by Plato in the Theaetetus and by Sextus Empiricus, alongside reports of his teaching in Plato's Protagoras.","start":"Read the two main fragments, then the part of Plato's Theaetetus where Socrates examines the measure doctrine.","line":{"text":"Man is the measure of all things","where":"quoted in Plato's Theaetetus 152a","tr":null},"islands":[],"related":["plato-protagoras","plato-theaetetus"],"shelf":"greece","tags":["man is the measure of all things","relativism","sophists","agnosticism","gods","pre-socratic philosophy","rhetoric","epistemology"]},{"id":"democritus-fragments","title":"Fragments of Democritus","original":"Greek prose fragments","author":"democritus","authorName":"Democritus","year":-420,"approx":true,"form":"fragments","length":"several hundred short fragments, mostly ethical sayings","level":1,"topics":["metaphysics","science","ethics"],"summary":"Democritus, building on his teacher Leucippus, held that the world consists only of tiny indivisible bodies, atoms, moving in empty space. Colours, tastes and warmth exist only by convention, in our experience, not in the atoms themselves. He also left many sayings on living well, praising a calm cheerfulness of mind.","why":"He gave the first full atomic theory of nature, later taken up by Epicurus and Lucretius and revived in early modern science. His distinction between how things appear and how they really are anticipates the later split between secondary and primary qualities.","inside":"Scattered fragments on physics and knowledge, preserved by writers such as Sextus Empiricus, and a large collection of ethical maxims preserved in the anthology of Stobaeus.","start":"Begin with the fragments on atoms and the void, then browse the ethical sayings on cheerfulness and moderation.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["epicurus-letter-to-menoeceus","lucretius-on-the-nature-of-things","empedocles-on-nature-and-purifications"],"shelf":"greece","tags":["atomism","atoms and the void","materialism","primary and secondary qualities","cheerfulness","pre-socratic philosophy","philosophy of nature","convention","ethics"]},{"id":"buddha-dhammacakkappavattana-sutta","title":"Setting in Motion the Wheel of the Dharma","original":"Dhammacakkappavattana Sutta (Pali)","author":"buddha","authorName":"The Buddha (Siddhartha Gautama)","year":-450,"approx":true,"form":"sutra","length":"a few pages","level":1,"topics":["ethics","religion","life and death"],"summary":"Buddhist tradition holds this to be the first teaching the Buddha gave after his awakening, delivered to five former companions in the Deer Park near Varanasi. He describes a Middle Way between indulgence and harsh self-denial and sets out the Four Noble Truths: life involves suffering, suffering arises from craving, it can cease, and the Noble Eightfold Path leads there.","why":"It is the classic summary of the core of Buddhism, accepted across all major traditions. Almost every later Buddhist teaching can be read as an expansion of it.","inside":"A short discourse in the Samyutta Nikaya (SN 56.11): the Middle Way, the Four Noble Truths, and the awakening of the first listener, Kondañña. The teachings were passed down orally and written down in Pali centuries later.","start":"Read it whole in one sitting; it takes ten minutes.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["dhammapada","milindapanha"],"shelf":"east-sages","tags":["four noble truths","eightfold path","middle way","suffering","craving","buddhism","nirvana","first sermon","liberation"]},{"id":"mahavira-acaranga-sutra","title":"Acaranga Sutra","original":"Ayaranga Sutta (Ardhamagadhi Prakrit)","author":"mahavira","authorName":"Mahavira (as remembered by the Jain tradition)","year":-400,"approx":true,"form":"sutra","length":"about 100 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["ethics","religion","self"],"summary":"The first book of the Jain scriptures, setting out the conduct of a monk or nun. Its central teaching is non-violence: all living beings, down to tiny creatures in earth, water and plants, love life and fear pain, so one must avoid harming any of them. Its first part ends with an account of the hard ascetic life of Mahavira himself.","why":"It is thought to contain some of the oldest layers of Jain teaching and is the classic source for the Jain ethic of ahimsa, which later fed into Gandhi's ideas of non-violence.","inside":"Two books: the first on non-harm and renunciation, closing with the account of Mahavira's wanderings; the second, later in date, on practical rules for monks and nuns. The Digambara Jains do not accept the surviving text as authoritative.","start":"Read the first lecture of Book One on the many forms of life, then the closing account of Mahavira's practice.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["umasvati-tattvartha-sutra","dhammapada"],"shelf":"east-sages","tags":["jainism","non-violence","ahimsa","asceticism","renunciation","animal ethics","all life is sacred","monasticism","karma"]},{"id":"plato-protagoras","title":"Protagoras","original":"Protagoras (Greek)","author":"plato","authorName":"Plato","year":-380,"approx":true,"form":"dialogue","length":"about 60 pages in English","level":1,"topics":["ethics","knowledge"],"summary":"A young Athenian is eager to study with the celebrated Sophist Protagoras, and Socrates takes him along to question the great man. Can virtue be taught, and are the virtues really one thing? Socrates argues that no one does wrong knowingly, so virtue must be a kind of knowledge, and by the end the two men seem to have swapped positions.","why":"It is the liveliest portrait of the Sophists and the best place to see Socrates' claim that wrongdoing comes from ignorance, a view later debated as the problem of weakness of will.","inside":"A comic opening scene at the house of Callias, Protagoras' 'Great Speech' with a myth of Prometheus, a quarrel about how to debate, an analysis of a poem by Simonides, and a final argument about pleasure and knowledge.","start":"Read the opening scene and Protagoras' Great Speech (320c-328d), then the final argument from 351b.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["protagoras-fragments","plato-meno","plato-gorgias"],"shelf":"greece","tags":["can virtue be taught","unity of the virtues","virtue is knowledge","no one does wrong willingly","akrasia","sophists","hedonism","myth of prometheus","socratic method"]},{"id":"xenophon-memorabilia","title":"Memorabilia","original":"Apomnemoneumata (Greek)","author":null,"authorName":"Xenophon","year":-370,"approx":true,"form":"memoir","length":"about 200 pages in English","level":1,"topics":["ethics","society"],"summary":"Xenophon, a soldier and writer who knew Socrates as a young man, sets out to show that Socrates was innocent of corrupting the young and neglecting the gods. He records many practical conversations in which Socrates gives advice on friendship, self-control, family quarrels, money and public life.","why":"Alongside Plato's dialogues, it is our main source for the historical Socrates, and its Socrates is more down-to-earth and practical than Plato's. Comparing the two is a classic puzzle.","inside":"Four books: a defence against the charges, then conversations grouped by theme. Book 2 contains the famous fable of the Choice of Heracles between Virtue and Vice, borrowed from the Sophist Prodicus.","start":"Read Book 1, chapters 1 and 2, then the Choice of Heracles in Book 2, chapter 1.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["plato-apology","plato-crito"],"shelf":"greece","tags":["socrates","choice of heracles","self-control","friendship","virtue","trial of socrates","practical wisdom","ethics"]},{"id":"plato-phaedrus","title":"Phaedrus","original":"Phaidros (Greek)","author":"plato","authorName":"Plato","year":-370,"approx":true,"form":"dialogue","length":"about 60 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["beauty","mind","language"],"summary":"Socrates and young Phaedrus walk out of Athens and sit by a river to read a speech about love. Socrates gives two speeches of his own, the second describing the soul as a charioteer driving one noble and one unruly horse, and love as a divine madness that lifts the soul toward beauty. The second half asks what makes speaking and writing good.","why":"Its chariot image is one of the most famous pictures of the divided mind. Its criticism of writing, that it weakens memory and cannot answer back, is still cited in debates about new technologies.","inside":"Three speeches on love (Lysias', then two by Socrates), followed by a discussion of rhetoric and a myth about the Egyptian god Theuth inventing writing.","start":"Read Socrates' second speech, especially the chariot passage (from 246a), then the myth of Theuth (274c-275b).","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["plato-symposium","aristotle-rhetoric"],"shelf":"greece","tags":["chariot allegory","love","divine madness","beauty","rhetoric","writing","soul","myth of theuth","eros","tripartite soul"]},{"id":"plato-timaeus","title":"Timaeus","original":"Timaios (Greek)","author":"plato","authorName":"Plato","year":-360,"approx":true,"form":"dialogue","length":"about 90 pages in English","level":3,"topics":["metaphysics","science","religion"],"summary":"Most of this dialogue is a long speech by Timaeus describing how a divine craftsman, the Demiurge, shaped the cosmos by looking to the eternal Forms. The four elements are built from geometric triangles, time is a 'moving image of eternity', and the human body and soul are explained in detail. Timaeus presents it all as only a 'likely story'.","why":"For much of the Middle Ages it was the only Plato dialogue widely read in Latin, so it shaped how Europe pictured the cosmos and creation. It is also where the story of Atlantis begins.","inside":"A short frame in which Critias tells the Atlantis story, then Timaeus' cosmology: the world soul, time and the heavens, the elements as solids, and finally the human body, senses and diseases.","start":"Read the opening through the creation of the world soul and time (to about 40d); the later anatomy can be skimmed.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["plato-republic","aristotle-physics","plotinus-enneads"],"shelf":"greece","tags":["cosmology","demiurge","creation","theory of forms","atlantis","world soul","time","platonic solids","likely story","philosophy of nature"]},{"id":"plato-laws","title":"Laws","original":"Nomoi (Greek)","author":"plato","authorName":"Plato","year":-348,"approx":true,"form":"dialogue","length":"about 500 pages in English","level":3,"topics":["politics","ethics","religion"],"summary":"Plato's last and longest work. An unnamed Athenian, a Cretan and a Spartan walk to a shrine on Crete while planning the laws of a new colony. Instead of rule by philosopher-kings, as in the Republic, they design a 'second-best' city governed by detailed written laws, each with a preamble to persuade citizens rather than merely command them.","why":"It is Plato's most practical political thinking and the source of the idea that laws should explain themselves. Book 10 contains the first systematic arguments against atheism in Western philosophy.","inside":"Twelve books covering education, drinking parties, the history of constitutions, marriage, property, crime and punishment, and religion. It appears to have been left unrevised at Plato's death.","start":"Read Book 1 for the setting and Book 10 for the arguments about the gods.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["plato-republic","aristotle-politics"],"shelf":"greece","tags":["political philosophy","law","second-best city","education","legislation","punishment","religion","atheism","constitutions","persuasion"]},{"id":"aristotle-prior-analytics","title":"Prior Analytics","original":"Analytika protera (Greek)","author":"aristotle","authorName":"Aristotle","year":-350,"approx":true,"form":"treatise","length":"about 120 pages in English","level":3,"topics":["logic"],"summary":"Aristotle invents formal logic. He studies the syllogism, an argument in which two premises such as 'all humans are mortal' and 'all Greeks are humans' force a conclusion, and sorts the valid patterns into three figures. He uses letters as variables to show that validity depends on form, not content.","why":"It founded logic as a discipline and remained the core of logic teaching for over two thousand years, until the work of Frege and others in the nineteenth century.","inside":"Two books. Book I sets out the three figures of the syllogism and proves which forms are valid, including a theory of arguments about necessity and possibility; Book II covers further properties of syllogisms and common errors.","start":"Read Book I, chapters 1 to 7, which give the basic theory of the syllogism.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["aristotle-categories","aristotle-metaphysics"],"shelf":"greece","tags":["syllogism","formal logic","deduction","validity","logic","modal logic","term logic","inference"]},{"id":"aristotle-physics","title":"Physics","original":"Physike akroasis (Greek)","author":"aristotle","authorName":"Aristotle","year":-340,"approx":true,"form":"treatise","length":"about 250 pages in English","level":3,"topics":["science","metaphysics"],"summary":"A study of nature, meaning everything that changes. Aristotle explains change as matter taking on form, sets out his four causes (material, formal, efficient and final), and analyses infinity, place, void and time. He ends by arguing that motion in the universe depends on a first unmoved mover.","why":"It was the basic framework of natural philosophy in Europe and the Islamic world until Galileo and Newton. Its four causes, especially the idea of purpose in nature, are still debated.","inside":"Eight books. Book II on nature and the four causes is the most read; Books III and IV cover infinity, place, void and time; Book VI answers Zeno's paradoxes; Book VIII gives the argument for an unmoved mover.","start":"Read Book II, then the discussion of time in Book IV, chapters 10 to 14.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["aristotle-metaphysics","zeno-paradoxes","galileo-dialogue-two-world-systems"],"shelf":"greece","tags":["four causes","change","philosophy of nature","unmoved mover","teleology","infinity","time","form and matter","zeno's paradoxes","motion","void"]},{"id":"aristotle-rhetoric","title":"Rhetoric","original":"Techne rhetorike (Greek)","author":"aristotle","authorName":"Aristotle","year":-335,"approx":true,"form":"treatise","length":"about 200 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["language","politics","mind"],"summary":"Aristotle's guide to persuasion. He says a speaker can persuade through character (ethos), by stirring the audience's emotions (pathos), or by argument (logos), and he gives detailed advice on each. Along the way he offers one of the earliest careful analyses of emotions such as anger, fear and pity.","why":"It is still the starting point for the study of persuasion and communication. Its treatment of the emotions matters for the philosophy of emotion as well as for rhetoric.","inside":"Three books: Book I on the kinds of speeches and the arguments they use; Book II on character and the emotions; Book III on style and the arrangement of a speech.","start":"Read Book I, chapters 1 and 2, then the chapters on the emotions in Book II.","line":{"text":"Rhetoric is the counterpart of Dialectic.","where":"Book I, chapter 1 (1354a)","tr":"Roberts"},"islands":[],"related":["plato-gorgias","plato-phaedrus","aristotle-poetics"],"shelf":"greece","tags":["rhetoric","persuasion","ethos pathos logos","emotions","argumentation","public speaking","anger","philosophy of language"]},{"id":"gongsun-long-white-horse","title":"White Horse Dialogue","original":"Baima lun (Chinese)","author":null,"authorName":"Gongsun Long","year":-300,"approx":true,"form":"dialogue","length":"a few pages","level":2,"topics":["logic","language"],"summary":"A puzzling exchange defending the claim that 'a white horse is not a horse'. If you ask for a horse, a yellow or black one will do, but if you ask for a white horse, they will not, so 'white horse' and 'horse' cannot be the same. The dialogue probes how names relate to the things and qualities they pick out.","why":"Gongsun Long belonged to the School of Names, early Chinese thinkers interested in language and argument. The dialogue is the most famous example of Chinese logical debate and is still analysed by philosophers of language.","inside":"A short question-and-answer between an objector and a defender, one of a handful of chapters surviving in the book that bears Gongsun Long's name.","start":"Read it twice: once to enjoy the paradox, once to work out what 'is not' might mean.","line":{"text":"A white horse is not a horse.","where":"the thesis defended throughout","tr":null},"islands":[],"related":["mozi","zhuangzi"],"shelf":"china-classical","tags":["white horse paradox","school of names","logic","philosophy of language","names and things","paradox","chinese philosophy","universals"]},{"id":"zhongyong-doctrine-of-the-mean","title":"The Doctrine of the Mean","original":"Zhongyong (Chinese)","author":null,"authorName":"Traditionally attributed to Zisi, grandson of Confucius","year":-300,"approx":true,"form":"classic","length":"about 15 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["ethics","self","metaphysics"],"summary":"A short Confucian text on living in balance and harmony. It teaches that the noble person stays centred before emotions arise and responds in due measure when they do, and that sincerity or integrity (cheng) links the moral life of the individual to the order of heaven and earth.","why":"Zhu Xi made it one of the Four Books, which became the basis of the Chinese civil service examinations from the fourteenth century to 1905. It gave Confucianism much of its spiritual and metaphysical depth.","inside":"Originally a chapter of the Book of Rites, divided by Zhu Xi into 33 short sections: an opening on human nature and the Way, sayings of Confucius on the mean, and a later part on sincerity. Its date of composition is uncertain.","start":"Read the opening section and the passages on sincerity in the second half.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["confucius-analects","mencius","zhuxi-reflections"],"shelf":"china-classical","tags":["confucianism","doctrine of the mean","harmony","sincerity","balance","self-cultivation","emotions","heaven","junzi"]},{"id":"kautilya-arthashastra","title":"Arthashastra","original":"Arthaśāstra (Sanskrit)","author":null,"authorName":"Kautilya","year":-300,"approx":true,"form":"treatise","length":"15 books, several hundred pages","level":2,"topics":["politics","society"],"summary":"A detailed manual of statecraft: how a king should run his treasury, laws, spies, alliances and wars. Tradition credits it to Kautilya, adviser to the first Mauryan emperor around 300 BCE, though many scholars think the surviving text was compiled later, perhaps between the first century BCE and the second century CE. Its outlook is hard-headed: the welfare and security of the state come first.","why":"Lost for centuries, it was rediscovered in a manuscript in 1905 and changed the picture of ancient Indian political thought. It is often compared to Machiavelli's The Prince.","inside":"Fifteen books on the training of the king, administration, law, internal security, foreign policy (including the 'circle of states'), war and secret practices.","start":"Read Book 1 on the king's education, then the sections on foreign policy in Book 6.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["machiavelli-prince","hanfeizi"],"shelf":"india-classical","tags":["statecraft","realpolitik","political realism","espionage","economics","war","foreign policy","mandala theory","political philosophy","indian philosophy"]},{"id":"epicurus-principal-doctrines","title":"Principal Doctrines","original":"Kyriai Doxai (Greek)","author":"epicurus","authorName":"Epicurus","year":-300,"approx":true,"form":"maxims","length":"40 short sayings, a few pages","level":1,"topics":["ethics","life and death","society"],"summary":"Forty short maxims summing up Epicurus' philosophy. The gods are not angry or interested in us, death is nothing to fear, the pleasure we need is easy to obtain, and pain is either brief or bearable. Later maxims praise friendship and treat justice as an agreement not to harm or be harmed.","why":"The first four maxims became the 'fourfold remedy' of the Epicurean school, a simple cure for anxiety. The maxims on justice as a mutual agreement are an early form of social contract thinking.","inside":"A list of numbered sayings preserved by Diogenes Laertius at the end of his life of Epicurus. The first four are the core; the rest cover desire, safety, friendship and justice.","start":"Read all forty; they take a quarter of an hour. Then read the Letter to Menoeceus.","line":{"text":"Death is nothing to us","where":"Doctrine 2","tr":"Hicks"},"islands":[],"related":["epicurus-letter-to-menoeceus","lucretius-on-the-nature-of-things","diogenes-laertius-lives"],"shelf":"greece-rome","tags":["epicureanism","pleasure","death is nothing to us","ataraxia","friendship","justice as social contract","gods","desire","hedonism","tetrapharmakos"]},{"id":"kanada-vaisheshika-sutra","title":"Vaisheshika Sutra","original":"Vaiśeṣika Sūtra (Sanskrit)","author":null,"authorName":"Kanada","year":-200,"approx":true,"form":"sutra","length":"a few hundred short aphorisms","level":3,"topics":["metaphysics","science","knowledge"],"summary":"The founding text of the Vaisheshika school, one of the classical systems of Indian philosophy. It sorts what exists into categories such as substance, quality, motion, universal, particularity and inherence, and holds that physical things are built from indivisible, eternal atoms.","why":"It is one of the earliest atomic theories in the world, developed independently of the Greeks. Its categories became part of the shared vocabulary of Indian philosophy once the school merged with Nyaya.","inside":"Ten books of terse aphorisms meant to be explained by a teacher, so most readers rely on later commentaries. Its date is uncertain; scholars place it somewhere between the sixth and second centuries BCE.","start":"Read Book 1 with a commentary or guide, which sets out the categories.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["akshapada-nyaya-sutras","democritus-fragments"],"shelf":"india-classical","tags":["vaisheshika","atomism","categories","substance","universals","inherence","indian philosophy","ontology","metaphysics"]},{"id":"bharata-natyashastra","title":"Natyashastra","original":"Nāṭyaśāstra (Sanskrit)","author":"bharata","authorName":"Bharata Muni","year":-200,"approx":true,"form":"treatise","length":"about 36 chapters, several hundred pages","level":2,"topics":["beauty"],"summary":"A vast handbook of the performing arts in ancient India, covering theatre buildings, acting, gesture, music, dance and costume. Its most influential idea is rasa: the distinctive flavour of feeling, such as love, heroism or sorrow, that a well-made performance produces in its audience.","why":"Rasa theory became the centre of Indian aesthetics, refined about a thousand years later by Abhinavagupta. It is still used to understand Indian classical dance, music and film.","inside":"Dozens of chapters, mostly practical. Chapter 6 on rasa and chapter 7 on emotional states are the philosophical core. Its date is uncertain, often placed between 200 BCE and 200 CE.","start":"Read chapter 6, on rasa, in a modern translation or anthology.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["aristotle-poetics"],"shelf":"india-classical","tags":["rasa","aesthetics","theatre","drama","performing arts","emotions","dance","music","indian philosophy"]},{"id":"huainanzi","title":"Huainanzi","original":"Huainanzi (Chinese)","author":null,"authorName":"Liu An and scholars of his court","year":-139,"approx":false,"form":"compendium","length":"about 900 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["politics","metaphysics","science"],"summary":"A large collection of essays produced by scholars at the court of Liu An, King of Huainan, and presented to the Han emperor in 139 BCE. It aims to give a ruler everything he needs to know, from the origin of the cosmos and astronomy to warfare and government, mostly from a Daoist point of view blended with other schools.","why":"It is one of the great syntheses of early Chinese thought and a key source for Han cosmology, including yin and yang and the five phases.","inside":"Twenty-one chapters, each on a theme such as the origins of the cosmos, astronomy, topography, the art of rulership and military strategy, ending with an overview of the whole.","start":"Read chapter 1, 'Originating in the Way', and chapter 9 on rulership.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["laozi-daodejing","zhuangzi","hanfeizi"],"shelf":"china-classical","tags":["taoism","cosmology","statecraft","syncretism","dao","political philosophy","chinese philosophy","astronomy","wu wei"]},{"id":"milindapanha","title":"The Questions of King Milinda","original":"Milindapañha (Pali)","author":null,"authorName":"Unknown Buddhist authors","year":-100,"approx":true,"form":"dialogue","length":"about 300 pages in English","level":1,"topics":["self","religion","mind"],"summary":"The Greek king Menander (Milinda), who ruled in what is now Pakistan and northern India, questions the monk Nagasena about Buddhist teaching. In the most famous exchange, Nagasena compares himself to a chariot: just as no single part is 'the chariot', there is no fixed self behind the name 'Nagasena'.","why":"It is one of the clearest explanations of the Buddhist idea of not-self and a rare record of contact between Greek and Indian thought. Its chariot argument is still used in debates about personal identity.","inside":"A series of puzzles and answers on the self, rebirth, karma and nirvana. The early sections are thought to be the oldest; the rest was added over several centuries.","start":"Read the opening dialogue about the chariot and the questions on rebirth that follow.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["dhammapada","buddha-dhammacakkappavattana-sutta","parfit-reasons-and-persons"],"shelf":"india-classical","tags":["no-self","chariot analogy","buddhism","personal identity","reincarnation","karma","nirvana","dialogue"]},{"id":"cicero-on-the-nature-of-the-gods","title":"On the Nature of the Gods","original":"De Natura Deorum (Latin)","author":"cicero","authorName":"Marcus Tullius Cicero","year":-45,"approx":false,"form":"dialogue","length":"about 200 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["religion","knowledge"],"summary":"Three Romans debate whether the gods exist and what they are like. An Epicurean argues the gods are blissful beings who take no interest in us; a Stoic argues the universe is ruled by divine reason and shows design everywhere; and an Academic skeptic picks holes in both. Cicero leaves the question largely open.","why":"It is the fullest ancient survey of arguments about the gods, including an early version of the design argument. Hume modelled his Dialogues Concerning Natural Religion on it.","inside":"Three books: Velleius presents the Epicurean view and Cotta answers him; Balbus presents the Stoic view; Cotta criticises it. At the very end Cicero remarks that the Stoic view seemed to him closer to the truth.","start":"Read Book 2, the Stoic case for design, and then Cotta's reply in Book 3.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["hume-dialogues","lucretius-on-the-nature-of-things","cicero-on-duties"],"shelf":"greece-rome","tags":["existence of god","argument from design","epicureanism","stoicism","academic skepticism","philosophy of religion","providence","gods","skepticism"]},{"id":"cicero-on-friendship","title":"On Friendship","original":"Laelius de Amicitia (Latin)","author":"cicero","authorName":"Marcus Tullius Cicero","year":-44,"approx":false,"form":"dialogue","length":"about 40 pages in English","level":1,"topics":["ethics","society"],"summary":"Shortly after the death of his great friend Scipio, the Roman statesman Laelius explains what true friendship is. It grows from virtue, not from need or profit, and can exist only between good people. He also gives practical advice: how to choose friends, what you may and may not ask of them, and how to handle flattery.","why":"It was one of the most read classical texts in medieval and Renaissance Europe and, with Aristotle's Ethics, shaped Western ideas of friendship. Montaigne's essay on friendship answers it.","inside":"A short dialogue set in 129 BCE, mostly a speech by Laelius to his two sons-in-law.","start":"Read it whole; it is short and plain.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["aristotle-nicomachean-ethics","montaigne-essays","cicero-on-duties"],"shelf":"greece-rome","tags":["friendship","virtue","loyalty","flattery","love","ethics","stoicism","the good life"]},{"id":"philo-on-the-creation","title":"On the Creation of the World","original":"De Opificio Mundi (Greek)","author":null,"authorName":"Philo of Alexandria","year":30,"approx":true,"form":"commentary","length":"about 60 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["religion","metaphysics"],"summary":"Philo, a Jewish philosopher in Greek-speaking Alexandria, explains the creation story in Genesis using ideas from Plato's Timaeus. God first conceives the world as a pattern of ideas in his reason (the Logos) and then makes the visible world from it. The six days of creation stand for order, not literal time.","why":"It is the earliest major attempt to join biblical faith with Greek philosophy, a project later taken up by Christian, Jewish and Muslim thinkers. His idea of the Logos influenced early Christian writers.","inside":"A continuous commentary on the first chapters of Genesis, with long digressions on the meaning of numbers and on the creation of humans.","start":"Read the opening sections on the world of ideas and the creation of humans.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["plato-timaeus","augustine-confessions"],"shelf":"greece-rome","tags":["creation","logos","genesis","jewish philosophy","platonism","allegorical interpretation","faith and reason","numerology","cosmology"]},{"id":"seneca-on-anger","title":"On Anger","original":"De Ira (Latin)","author":"seneca","authorName":"Seneca","year":45,"approx":true,"form":"treatise","length":"about 120 pages in English","level":1,"topics":["ethics","mind","self"],"summary":"Seneca argues that anger is never useful and should be removed, not just moderated. It is a brief madness that blinds judgement, and it rests on a hasty judgement that we have been wronged. He gives practical techniques, such as delaying your response and reviewing your behaviour each night.","why":"It is the classic Stoic analysis of an emotion and a forerunner of modern cognitive approaches to managing anger. It also contains vivid stories about the cruelty of angry rulers.","inside":"Three books, addressed to his brother Novatus: what anger is and why it is bad (Book 1), how it arises and how to avoid it (Book 2), and how to cure it in ourselves and others (Book 3).","start":"Read Book 1 and the nightly self-examination near the end of Book 3.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["seneca-letters-to-lucilius","epictetus-handbook","aristotle-rhetoric"],"shelf":"greece-rome","tags":["anger","stoicism","emotions","self-control","judgement","evening review","therapy of desire","practical wisdom","ethics"]},{"id":"wang-chong-lunheng","title":"Balanced Discourses","original":"Lunheng (Chinese)","author":null,"authorName":"Wang Chong","year":80,"approx":true,"form":"essays","length":"84 surviving essays","level":2,"topics":["science","religion","knowledge"],"summary":"Wang Chong set out to weigh popular beliefs and expose the false ones. He argued that Heaven does not send omens or punish rulers, that the dead do not become ghosts, and that natural events have natural causes. He demanded evidence and checked claims against experience.","why":"It is a striking example of naturalistic and skeptical thought in early China, written against the dominant belief that Heaven responds to human behaviour.","inside":"Eighty-five chapters, one lost, each taking a belief or a story and testing it. The essays on death, ghosts and the nature of Heaven are the best known.","start":"Read the essay on death and one of the essays on omens.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["xunzi","huainanzi"],"shelf":"china-classical","tags":["skepticism","naturalism","critique of superstition","ghosts","omens","evidence","rationalism","chinese philosophy","death","heaven"]},{"id":"nagarjuna-vigrahavyavartani","title":"The Dispeller of Disputes","original":"Vigrahavyāvartanī (Sanskrit)","author":"nagarjuna","authorName":"Nagarjuna","year":200,"approx":true,"form":"verses with commentary","length":"70 verses plus Nagarjuna's own commentary","level":3,"topics":["logic","knowledge","metaphysics"],"summary":"Opponents challenge Nagarjuna: if all things are empty of independent existence, then your own words are empty too, so how can they deny anything? Nagarjuna replies that his words are indeed empty but can still work, like one illusion stopping another, and that he has no thesis of his own to defend. He also attacks the idea that knowledge rests on secure foundations.","why":"It contains Nagarjuna's sharpest discussion of whether a critic of all views must himself hold a view, and an early critique of foundations for knowledge that modern philosophers have compared with Wittgenstein.","inside":"Twenty verses of objections, many from Nyaya logicians, followed by fifty verses of replies, all explained in Nagarjuna's own prose commentary.","start":"Read the objections first, then the reply about having no thesis (around verse 29).","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["nagarjuna-mulamadhyamakakarika","akshapada-nyaya-sutras"],"shelf":"india-classical","tags":["emptiness","madhyamaka","buddhism","no thesis","epistemology","critique of foundationalism","self-refutation","nyaya","logic"]},{"id":"diogenes-laertius-lives","title":"Lives of the Eminent Philosophers","original":null,"author":null,"authorName":"Diogenes Laertius","year":230,"approx":true,"form":"biographies","length":"about 700 pages in English","level":1,"topics":["life and death","ethics","society"],"summary":"A collection of short lives of more than eighty Greek philosophers, from Thales to Epicurus. It mixes their doctrines with anecdotes, jokes, sayings and gossip, such as Diogenes the Cynic living in a large storage jar. Almost nothing is known about the author.","why":"For many philosophers it is our main or only source, and it preserves three letters of Epicurus and his Principal Doctrines in full. Much of the popular picture of ancient philosophers comes from it.","inside":"Ten books grouped roughly by school: early sages and Socratics, Plato, Aristotle, the Cynics, the Stoics, the Pythagoreans and others, and a whole final book on Epicurus.","start":"Dip into Book 6 on Diogenes the Cynic, then Book 10 on Epicurus.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["epicurus-principal-doctrines","epicurus-letter-to-menoeceus"],"shelf":"greece-rome","tags":["history of philosophy","ancient greek philosophy","cynicism","stoicism","epicureanism","biography","anecdotes","pre-socratic philosophy","diogenes in the barrel"]},{"id":"porphyry-isagoge","title":"Isagoge","original":"Eisagoge (Greek)","author":null,"authorName":"Porphyry","year":270,"approx":true,"form":"introduction","length":"about 20 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["logic","metaphysics","language"],"summary":"A short introduction to Aristotle's Categories by Porphyry, the student and editor of Plotinus. It explains five basic terms used in classifying and defining things: genus, species, difference, property and accident. At the start Porphyry raises, and then sets aside as too deep, the question of whether genera and species exist in reality or only in the mind.","why":"Translated into Latin by Boethius, it became the first logic book studied in medieval schools, and its set-aside question launched the long medieval debate over universals. It was also widely studied in Syriac and Arabic.","inside":"A preface posing the question of universals, then a section on each of the five terms and a comparison of them. The 'Porphyrian tree' of genera and species was drawn by later readers from it.","start":"Read the first page, where the problem of universals is posed, then the sections on genus and species.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["aristotle-categories","plotinus-enneads","boethius-consolation"],"shelf":"christian-platonists","tags":["problem of universals","universals","genus and species","porphyrian tree","five predicables","logic","definition","neoplatonism","classification"]},{"id":"ishvarakrishna-samkhya-karika","title":"Samkhya Karika","original":"Sāṃkhyakārikā (Sanskrit)","author":null,"authorName":"Ishvarakrishna","year":350,"approx":true,"form":"verses","length":"about 70 verses","level":2,"topics":["metaphysics","mind","self"],"summary":"The oldest surviving text of the Samkhya school. It holds that reality has two basic principles: pure consciousness (purusha), of which there are many, and matter or nature (prakriti), made of three strands called gunas. Suffering comes from confusing consciousness with the workings of matter, including the mind itself, and liberation comes from seeing the difference.","why":"Samkhya is one of the oldest Indian philosophical systems and supplied the framework behind the Yoga Sutras and much later Hindu thought. Its dualism is often compared to Descartes', though for Samkhya the thinking mind belongs to matter.","inside":"Short verses in sequence: the problem of suffering, the means of knowledge, the arguments for matter and consciousness, the unfolding of the 25 principles, and liberation. Its date is uncertain, probably the fourth or fifth century.","start":"Read the first twenty or so verses with a commentary.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["patanjali-yoga-sutras","bhagavad-gita","descartes-meditations"],"shelf":"india-schools","tags":["samkhya","purusha and prakriti","dualism","gunas","consciousness","liberation","suffering","indian philosophy","metaphysics"]},{"id":"augustine-on-free-choice","title":"On Free Choice of the Will","original":"De Libero Arbitrio (Latin)","author":"augustine","authorName":"Augustine of Hippo","year":395,"approx":true,"form":"dialogue","length":"about 150 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["religion","ethics","mind"],"summary":"Augustine and his friend Evodius ask where evil comes from if a good God made everything. Augustine argues that evil is not a thing God created but the result of creatures misusing their free will, which is itself a good gift. He also argues that God's foreknowledge of our choices does not make them unfree.","why":"It is the classic statement of the free-will defence against the problem of evil, still debated today, and an early treatment of the conflict between foreknowledge and freedom.","inside":"Three books written between 388 and about 395: Book 1 on evil and law, Book 2 on God's existence and why we have free will, Book 3 on foreknowledge and the order of the universe.","start":"Read Book 1, then the discussion of foreknowledge early in Book 3.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["augustine-confessions","boethius-consolation","leibniz-theodicy"],"shelf":"christian-platonists","tags":["free will","problem of evil","divine foreknowledge","free will defence","sin","existence of god","christianity","theodicy"]},{"id":"vasubandhu-twenty-verses","title":"Twenty Verses","original":"Viṃśatikā (Sanskrit)","author":"vasubandhu","authorName":"Vasubandhu","year":400,"approx":true,"form":"verses with commentary","length":"22 verses with Vasubandhu's own commentary","level":3,"topics":["metaphysics","mind","knowledge"],"summary":"Vasubandhu argues that there are no external physical objects, only consciousness appearing as objects. He answers objections: if experience is like a dream, why is it orderly and shared? He replies with examples such as hungry ghosts who all see the same river of pus, and argues that atoms, the supposed building blocks of matter, make no sense.","why":"It is the classic statement of the Yogacara 'consciousness-only' school of Buddhism and is often compared to Berkeley's idealism.","inside":"A sequence of objections and replies, in verse with prose commentary: on dreams and shared worlds, the argument against atoms, and how minds can affect one another without bodies.","start":"Read the first ten verses with the commentary.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["berkeley-principles","nagarjuna-mulamadhyamakakarika","dharmakirti-pramanavarttika"],"shelf":"india-schools","tags":["idealism","yogacara","mind-only","buddhism","dream argument","critique of atomism","external world","consciousness","metaphysics"]},{"id":"buddhaghosa-visuddhimagga","title":"The Path of Purification","original":"Visuddhimagga (Pali)","author":null,"authorName":"Buddhaghosa","year":430,"approx":true,"form":"treatise","length":"about 800 pages in English","level":3,"topics":["mind","ethics","religion"],"summary":"A huge manual of Theravada Buddhist practice, written in Sri Lanka by the great commentator Buddhaghosa. It lays out the path to liberation in three stages: moral conduct, concentration through meditation, and wisdom, which means seeing that all things are impermanent, unsatisfactory and without self.","why":"It is the most authoritative text of Theravada Buddhism after the Pali Canon itself and is still used by meditation teachers in Sri Lanka and Southeast Asia.","inside":"Twenty-three chapters in three parts: virtue (1-2), concentration, with forty meditation subjects (3-13), and understanding, including an analysis of mind and body and of dependent origination (14-23).","start":"Read the opening chapter on virtue and the chapter on the divine abidings, including loving-kindness (chapter 9).","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["dhammapada","milindapanha"],"shelf":"india-schools","tags":["theravada","buddhism","meditation","three marks of existence","impermanence","no-self","dependent origination","concentration","wisdom","liberation"]},{"id":"pseudo-dionysius-mystical-theology","title":"The Mystical Theology","original":"Peri Mystikes Theologias (Greek)","author":null,"authorName":"Pseudo-Dionysius the Areopagite","year":500,"approx":true,"form":"treatise","length":"about 6 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["religion","language","knowledge"],"summary":"A very short work on how the mind reaches God by going beyond all words and concepts. Since God is beyond everything, every statement about him must finally be denied, and one approaches him in a 'divine darkness' of unknowing. The author presented himself as Dionysius, a convert of St Paul, but probably wrote in Syria around 500.","why":"It is the founding text of negative (apophatic) theology in Christianity and shaped medieval mysticism and thinkers such as Aquinas and Nicholas of Cusa.","inside":"Five short chapters: a prayer and the image of Moses climbing into the cloud on Sinai, then the ways of affirmation and negation, ending with a list of everything God is not.","start":"Read it all; it is only a few pages. Chapter 1 is the heart.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["plotinus-enneads","cusa-on-learned-ignorance"],"shelf":"christian-platonists","tags":["negative theology","apophatic theology","mysticism","divine darkness","ineffability","christianity","neoplatonism","unknowing","god"]},{"id":"shantideva-bodhicaryavatara","title":"The Way of the Bodhisattva","original":"Bodhicaryāvatāra (Sanskrit)","author":null,"authorName":"Shantideva","year":700,"approx":true,"form":"poem","length":"about 900 verses, about 150 pages","level":2,"topics":["ethics","self","religion"],"summary":"A poem about becoming a bodhisattva, someone who seeks awakening in order to free all beings. Shantideva describes how to cultivate the awakening mind, patience with those who harm us, effort and meditation. His most famous argument is that since there is no real self, there is no reason to prefer relieving my own pain over anyone else's.","why":"It is one of the most loved texts of Mahayana Buddhism, especially in Tibet, and the Dalai Lama often teaches from it. Its argument about self and others interests modern philosophers working on impartiality and personal identity.","inside":"Ten chapters. Chapter 6 on patience and chapter 8 on meditation, including exchanging self and others, are the most read. Chapter 9 on wisdom is a demanding defence of Madhyamaka philosophy.","start":"Read chapter 6 on patience, then the second half of chapter 8.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["nagarjuna-mulamadhyamakakarika","parfit-reasons-and-persons"],"shelf":"india-schools","tags":["bodhisattva","compassion","mahayana buddhism","patience","exchanging self and others","no-self","altruism","madhyamaka","anger","meditation"]},{"id":"huineng-platform-sutra","title":"The Platform Sutra","original":"Liuzu Tanjing (Chinese)","author":null,"authorName":"Attributed to Huineng","year":780,"approx":true,"form":"sutra","length":"about 60 pages in English","level":1,"topics":["religion","mind","self"],"summary":"The story and teachings of Huineng, an illiterate woodcutter who became the Sixth Patriarch of Chan (Zen) Buddhism. In a poetry contest he answers the head monk's verse about keeping the mind's mirror polished by saying there is no mirror and nowhere for dust to land. He teaches that awakening is sudden and that Buddha-nature is already present in everyone.","why":"It is one of very few Chinese works given the title 'sutra', normally reserved for the Buddha's own words, and became a foundation of Chan and Zen. The mirror verses are among the most famous in Buddhism.","inside":"An autobiographical opening with the verse contest, sermons on meditation and wisdom, and dialogues with disciples. The oldest surviving version, found at Dunhuang, dates from the eighth century; later versions are longer.","start":"Read the opening story of Huineng and the verse contest.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["dogen-shobogenzo","zhuangzi"],"shelf":"china-east","tags":["zen","chan buddhism","sudden enlightenment","buddha-nature","mirror verse","no-mind","meditation","buddhism","emptiness"]},{"id":"alkindi-on-first-philosophy","title":"On First Philosophy","original":"Fi al-Falsafa al-Ula (Arabic)","author":null,"authorName":"Al-Kindi","year":840,"approx":true,"form":"treatise","length":"about 50 pages in English","level":3,"topics":["metaphysics","religion","knowledge"],"summary":"Al-Kindi, known as 'the philosopher of the Arabs', dedicates this work to the Abbasid caliph al-Mu'tasim. He defends the study of Greek philosophy as a search for truth that should be welcomed from any source, then argues that the world cannot be infinitely old and that it depends on a True One, God, who is beyond all multiplicity.","why":"It is the first major work of philosophy written in Arabic and set the agenda for the Islamic philosophical tradition. Its arguments against an infinite past were later used by al-Ghazali and revived in the modern kalam cosmological argument.","inside":"Only the first part survives, in four chapters: an opening defence of philosophy, arguments that the world had a beginning, and a long analysis of oneness leading to God as the True One.","start":"Read the opening chapter defending philosophy.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["alfarabi-virtuous-city","alghazali-incoherence","avicenna-healing"],"shelf":"islam-jewish-early","tags":["islamic philosophy","the one","creation","finite past","existence of god","truth from any source","metaphysics","neoplatonism","faith and reason"]},{"id":"saadia-beliefs-and-opinions","title":"The Book of Beliefs and Opinions","original":"Kitab al-Amanat wa-l-I'tiqadat (Judeo-Arabic)","author":null,"authorName":"Saadia Gaon","year":933,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 450 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["religion","knowledge","ethics"],"summary":"Saadia, head of a great Jewish academy in Babylonia, argues that reason and revelation lead to the same truths. He lists the sources of knowledge, argues that the world was created from nothing, defends God's unity and justice, and discusses free will, the soul, resurrection and the good life.","why":"It is the first systematic work of medieval Jewish philosophy and a model for later thinkers such as Maimonides. Its approach echoes the Islamic rational theology (kalam) of its time.","inside":"An introduction on doubt and the sources of knowledge, then ten treatises, from creation and God's unity through commandments, free will, the soul and resurrection, ending with advice on how to live.","start":"Read the introduction on doubt and knowledge, then Treatise 1 on creation.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["maimonides-guide","halevi-kuzari","alkindi-on-first-philosophy"],"shelf":"islam-jewish-early","tags":["jewish philosophy","faith and reason","creation ex nihilo","sources of knowledge","free will","soul","resurrection","divine justice","doubt"]},{"id":"avicenna-pointers-and-reminders","title":"Pointers and Reminders","original":"al-Isharat wa-l-Tanbihat (Arabic)","author":"avicenna","authorName":"Avicenna (Ibn Sina)","year":1030,"approx":true,"form":"treatise","length":"about 400 pages in English","level":3,"topics":["metaphysics","logic","religion"],"summary":"One of Avicenna's last works, a compact summary of his system written in short, hint-like sections. It covers logic, the soul, God as the Necessary Existent and how the world proceeds from him, and, in its final parts, the stages of the mystic's journey toward God.","why":"It became the main textbook of Avicenna's philosophy in the Islamic East, and commentaries on it by Fakhr al-Din al-Razi and Nasir al-Din al-Tusi kept philosophical debate alive for centuries.","inside":"Two halves: logic, then physics and metaphysics. The closing sections discuss happiness, the 'stations of the knowers' and the secrets of signs. It includes a version of his 'floating man' thought experiment about self-awareness.","start":"Read the section on the soul with the floating man passage, then the 'stations of the knowers'.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["avicenna-healing","tusi-nasirean-ethics","suhrawardi-philosophy-of-illumination"],"shelf":"islam-jewish-early","tags":["islamic philosophy","necessary existent","floating man","mysticism","logic","soul","sufism","self-awareness","metaphysics","happiness"]},{"id":"ibn-gabirol-fountain-of-life","title":"The Fountain of Life","original":"Yanbu' al-Hayat (Arabic); Fons Vitae (Latin)","author":null,"authorName":"Solomon ibn Gabirol","year":1050,"approx":true,"form":"dialogue","length":"about 300 pages in English","level":3,"topics":["metaphysics","religion"],"summary":"A Neoplatonic dialogue between a master and a disciple arguing that everything except God, even souls and angels, is made of matter and form. The divine Will links God to creation. Ibn Gabirol, a Jewish poet in Muslim Spain, wrote it with almost no reference to Jewish sources.","why":"Its idea of 'universal hylomorphism' influenced Franciscan philosophers. For centuries Latin readers knew the author only as 'Avicebron' and did not realise he was Jewish, until the scholar Salomon Munk identified him in 1846.","inside":"Five books on matter and form, moving from bodies to spiritual substances and the Will. The Arabic original is lost; it survives complete in a twelfth-century Latin translation.","start":"Read the opening of Book 1, where the plan of the work is laid out, then dip into Book 5.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["plotinus-enneads","maimonides-guide","saadia-beliefs-and-opinions"],"shelf":"islam-jewish-early","tags":["neoplatonism","universal hylomorphism","form and matter","divine will","jewish philosophy","metaphysics","emanation","spiritual matter"]},{"id":"zhangzai-western-inscription","title":"The Western Inscription","original":"Ximing (Chinese)","author":null,"authorName":"Zhang Zai","year":1070,"approx":true,"form":"essay","length":"about one page","level":1,"topics":["ethics","metaphysics","society"],"summary":"A short text Zhang Zai hung on the west wall of his study. It pictures the whole universe as one family: Heaven and Earth are our parents, all people are our siblings and all things our companions, so caring for the old, the orphaned and the sick is a family duty. Life and death are accepted calmly as part of serving one's parents.","why":"It became one of the most admired texts of Neo-Confucianism. Cheng Yi and Zhu Xi defended it against the charge that it erased Confucian distinctions in love, using the formula that 'principle is one but its manifestations are many'.","inside":"About 250 Chinese characters, usually printed with comments by Cheng Yi and Zhu Xi.","start":"Read the whole text, then the comments on 'one principle, many manifestations'.","line":{"text":"Heaven is my father and Earth is my mother","where":"opening line","tr":"Chan"},"islands":[],"related":["zhuxi-reflections","mencius","mozi"],"shelf":"china-east","tags":["neo-confucianism","unity of all things","universal family","filial piety","compassion","qi","death","confucianism"]},{"id":"anselm-monologion","title":"Monologion","original":"Monologion (Latin)","author":"anselm","authorName":"Anselm of Canterbury","year":1076,"approx":true,"form":"meditation","length":"about 80 short chapters, about 100 pages","level":2,"topics":["religion","metaphysics"],"summary":"Written for the monks of Bec, who asked Anselm to reason about God without appealing to scripture. He argues that since things are good to different degrees, there must be one supreme good through which all are good, and that this highest being is the source of everything else. He then explores how this being thinks, loves and is triune.","why":"It shows the method of 'faith seeking understanding' at work and contains a version of the argument from degrees of perfection later used by Aquinas. Anselm then wrote the Proslogion because he wanted a single, simpler argument.","inside":"A short preface followed by a long chain of brief chapters: first the arguments for a supreme being, then its nature and its creating of the world, then the Word and the Spirit, and finally the soul's love of God.","start":"Read the first four chapters, the arguments from goodness and greatness.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["anselm-proslogion","augustine-confessions","aquinas-summa-theologiae"],"shelf":"christian-platonists","tags":["existence of god","degrees of goodness","supreme being","trinity","christianity","scholasticism","natural theology","faith seeking understanding"]},{"id":"abelard-sic-et-non","title":"Yes and No","original":"Sic et Non (Latin)","author":null,"authorName":"Peter Abelard","year":1121,"approx":true,"form":"collection","length":"158 questions, with a prologue of about 20 pages","level":2,"topics":["religion","logic","knowledge"],"summary":"Abelard gathered passages from the Bible and the Church Fathers that seem to contradict each other on 158 questions, from faith and sin to marriage and lying. He did not resolve them; instead his prologue teaches readers how to: check the text, consider changes in word meaning, context and authorship, and then reason.","why":"Its method of setting authorities against one another and resolving them by reason became the basis of scholastic teaching, including the structure of Aquinas' Summa.","inside":"A prologue on rules of interpretation, followed by the questions, each with quotations on both sides.","start":"Read the prologue; the questions can be sampled.","line":{"text":"By doubting we come to inquiry, and by inquiry we perceive the truth.","where":"Prologue","tr":null},"islands":[],"related":["aquinas-summa-theologiae","abelard-heloise-letters"],"shelf":"christian-europe","tags":["scholasticism","dialectic","interpretation","contradiction","authority","critical thinking","doubt","hermeneutics","logic"]},{"id":"abelard-heloise-letters","title":"The Letters of Abelard and Heloise","original":"Historia Calamitatum and Epistolae (Latin)","author":null,"authorName":"Peter Abelard and Heloise","year":1132,"approx":true,"form":"letters","length":"about 250 pages in English","level":1,"topics":["ethics","self","religion"],"summary":"Abelard's autobiography, 'The Story of My Misfortunes', tells how he became the most famous teacher in Paris, fell in love with his brilliant student Heloise, and was castrated by men sent by her uncle. The letters that follow, written when both were in religious life, show Heloise arguing that love freely given is worth more than marriage and refusing to pretend she repents.","why":"It is one of the great love stories and a rare medieval record of a woman's philosophical voice. Heloise's arguments about intention and love connect to Abelard's ethics of intention.","inside":"The Story of My Misfortunes, then letters between Heloise and Abelard: the first personal, the later ones on the rule of life for Heloise's community of nuns.","start":"Read the Story of My Misfortunes and Heloise's first two letters.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["abelard-sic-et-non","augustine-confessions"],"shelf":"christian-europe","tags":["love","marriage","desire","autobiography","intention","repentance","women philosophers","medieval philosophy","religious life"]},{"id":"ibn-tufayl-hayy-ibn-yaqzan","title":"Hayy ibn Yaqzan","original":"Hayy ibn Yaqzan (Arabic)","author":null,"authorName":"Ibn Tufayl","year":1175,"approx":true,"form":"philosophical novel","length":"about 100 pages in English","level":1,"topics":["knowledge","religion","self"],"summary":"A child grows up alone on an island, raised by a gazelle. Through observation and reasoning he works out the natural world, the soul and the existence of God, and finally reaches mystical union. When he meets people from an inhabited island, he discovers that their religion teaches the same truths in symbols, but most of them cannot bear the plain truth.","why":"It is a philosophical novel about whether reason alone can reach truth, written in Muslim Spain by the court physician who introduced Averroes to the caliph. Its Latin translation of 1671 interested European readers in the age of Locke.","inside":"An introduction on philosophy and mystical experience, the story of Hayy's life in stages of seven years, and his meeting with Absal and the island ruled by Salaman.","start":"Read the story from Hayy's birth onward; the introduction can wait.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["averroes-decisive-treatise","alghazali-deliverance","avicenna-healing"],"shelf":"islamic","tags":["philosophical novel","natural reason","feral child","islamic philosophy","mysticism","existence of god","faith and reason","self-taught philosopher","sufism"]},{"id":"suhrawardi-philosophy-of-illumination","title":"The Philosophy of Illumination","original":"Hikmat al-Ishraq (Arabic)","author":null,"authorName":"Shihab al-Din Suhrawardi","year":1186,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 200 pages in English","level":3,"topics":["metaphysics","knowledge","religion"],"summary":"Suhrawardi rebuilds philosophy around light. Everything that exists is a degree of light or darkness, flowing from the Light of Lights. Against the Aristotelian view that knowing means defining, he argues that the deepest knowledge is direct presence, like our awareness of ourselves.","why":"It founded the Illuminationist school, which shaped Persian philosophy down to Mulla Sadra. Suhrawardi was executed in Aleppo in 1191, still in his thirties.","inside":"Two parts: a critique of Aristotelian logic and metaphysics, then the metaphysics of lights, the soul and the afterlife, introduced by the author's account of his own mystical vision.","start":"Read the author's introduction, then the opening sections of Part Two on light.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["avicenna-pointers-and-reminders","mulla-sadra-four-journeys","plotinus-enneads"],"shelf":"islamic","tags":["illuminationism","light","knowledge by presence","islamic philosophy","mysticism","critique of aristotle","sufism","metaphysics","self-awareness"]},{"id":"kamo-no-chomei-hojoki","title":"An Account of My Hut","original":"Hōjōki (Japanese)","author":null,"authorName":"Kamo no Chōmei","year":1212,"approx":false,"form":"essay","length":"about 20 pages in English","level":1,"topics":["life and death","self","religion"],"summary":"A Japanese poet who has retired to a tiny hut in the hills looks back on the disasters he witnessed in Kyoto: a great fire, a whirlwind, famine and earthquake. Seeing how fragile houses and lives are, he describes his simple life alone, then ends by asking whether even his love of the hut is a form of attachment.","why":"It is one of the classics of Japanese literature and a key expression of the Buddhist sense of impermanence, mujō. Readers interested in simple living still return to it.","inside":"A famous opening image of a flowing river, accounts of the disasters, a description of the hut and the daily life in it, and a short, self-questioning ending.","start":"Read it all; it is short.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["kenko-essays-in-idleness","dogen-shobogenzo","seneca-on-the-shortness-of-life"],"shelf":"asia-medieval","tags":["impermanence","simple living","solitude","buddhism","attachment","natural disasters","japanese philosophy","hermit","renunciation"]},{"id":"ibn-arabi-bezels-of-wisdom","title":"The Bezels of Wisdom","original":"Fusus al-Hikam (Arabic)","author":null,"authorName":"Ibn Arabi","year":1229,"approx":true,"form":"treatise","length":"about 300 pages in English","level":3,"topics":["religion","metaphysics","self"],"summary":"Ibn Arabi, the great Andalusian Sufi, said this book was given to him by the Prophet in a vision in Damascus. Each chapter takes a prophet, from Adam to Muhammad, as a 'bezel' or setting for a different aspect of divine wisdom. Behind it is the idea that God alone truly exists and that all things are his self-manifestation.","why":"It is one of the most influential and most argued-over books in Islamic mysticism. Later thinkers summed up its view as 'the unity of being' (wahdat al-wujud).","inside":"A short introduction and 27 chapters, one for each prophet. Most readers use a commentary because the language is dense and symbolic.","start":"Read the chapter on Adam with a guide or commentary.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["rumi-masnavi","suhrawardi-philosophy-of-illumination","dara-shikoh-mingling-of-two-oceans"],"shelf":"islamic","tags":["sufism","unity of being","mysticism","prophets","self-manifestation of god","islamic philosophy","perfect human","metaphysics","god"]},{"id":"tusi-nasirean-ethics","title":"The Nasirean Ethics","original":"Akhlaq-i Nasiri (Persian)","author":null,"authorName":"Nasir al-Din al-Tusi","year":1235,"approx":true,"form":"treatise","length":"about 300 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["ethics","politics","society"],"summary":"A complete practical philosophy in Persian, written by the astronomer and philosopher Tusi for an Ismaili governor named Nasir al-Din. It treats ethics as the perfecting of the soul through virtue, household management as the ordering of family and wealth, and politics as the art of organising a city for shared happiness.","why":"It became the standard book of ethics in the Persian-speaking world, read from Iran to Mughal India for centuries, and passed on Aristotle's ethics through Islamic eyes.","inside":"Three parts: ethics (drawing on Miskawayh), household economics, and politics (drawing on al-Farabi).","start":"Read the first part on the soul and the virtues.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["aristotle-nicomachean-ethics","alfarabi-virtuous-city","avicenna-pointers-and-reminders"],"shelf":"islamic","tags":["islamic philosophy","virtue ethics","household management","political philosophy","happiness","perfection of the soul","practical philosophy","ethics"]},{"id":"bonaventure-journey-of-the-mind","title":"The Journey of the Mind into God","original":"Itinerarium Mentis in Deum (Latin)","author":null,"authorName":"Bonaventure","year":1259,"approx":false,"form":"meditation","length":"about 60 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["religion","mind","beauty"],"summary":"Written on Mount La Verna, where Francis of Assisi had seen a vision of a six-winged seraph, this short book maps the mind's ascent to God in six stages. We find God first in the outer world as a trace, then within our own mind as an image, then above the mind in the ideas of being and goodness, and finally in loving ecstasy.","why":"It is the classic medieval guide to the spiritual journey and the best short introduction to Franciscan philosophy, which stressed love and illumination alongside reason.","inside":"A prologue and seven chapters: two each for God in the world, in the mind and above the mind, and a final chapter on mystical rest.","start":"Read the prologue and chapter 3 on God in the mind.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["augustine-confessions","aquinas-summa-contra-gentiles","pseudo-dionysius-mystical-theology"],"shelf":"christian-europe","tags":["mysticism","ascent to god","franciscan","christianity","contemplation","illumination","vestiges of god","scholasticism","neoplatonism"]},{"id":"aquinas-summa-contra-gentiles","title":"Summa Contra Gentiles","original":"Summa contra Gentiles (Latin)","author":"aquinas","authorName":"Thomas Aquinas","year":1264,"approx":true,"form":"treatise","length":"four books, about 1,500 pages in English","level":3,"topics":["religion","metaphysics","ethics"],"summary":"Aquinas sets out the Christian view of God and the world for readers who do not accept Christian scripture. The first three books argue from reason alone about God's existence and nature, creation, and how humans find happiness in knowing God. Only the fourth turns to truths held by faith, such as the Trinity.","why":"It is Aquinas' fullest attempt to show how far reason can go on its own, and its arguments about God, providence and the human soul are still widely studied.","inside":"Four books: God (I), creation and the human soul (II), providence and human happiness (III), and revealed truths (IV). The opening chapters of Book I explain the method.","start":"Read Book I, chapters 1 to 9, then the chapters in Book III on what human happiness consists in.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["aquinas-summa-theologiae","maimonides-guide","averroes-decisive-treatise"],"shelf":"christian-europe","tags":["natural theology","existence of god","faith and reason","thomism","providence","happiness","creation","soul","christianity","scholasticism"]},{"id":"rumi-masnavi","title":"The Masnavi","original":"Masnavi-yi Ma'navi (Persian)","author":null,"authorName":"Jalal al-Din Rumi","year":1260,"approx":true,"form":"poem","length":"six books, about 25,000 couplets","level":1,"topics":["religion","self","beauty"],"summary":"A long poem of stories, fables, jokes and teachings, dictated by the Sufi master Rumi to his disciple Husam al-Din. It opens with a reed flute lamenting its separation from the reed-bed, an image of the soul longing for God. Stories such as the elephant in the dark show how partial our knowledge is.","why":"It is one of the most read works of Islamic mysticism and is sometimes called 'the Qur'an in Persian'. Its stories carry philosophical ideas about knowledge, the self and love to a wide audience.","inside":"Six books of linked stories that interrupt one another with commentary, begun around 1258 and unfinished at Rumi's death in 1273.","start":"Read the 'Song of the Reed' at the start of Book 1, then the story of the elephant in the dark.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["ibn-arabi-bezels-of-wisdom","alghazali-deliverance"],"shelf":"islamic","tags":["sufism","mysticism","love","the reed flute","elephant in the dark","longing for god","poetry","islamic philosophy","soul","spiritual journey"]},{"id":"zohar","title":"The Zohar","original":"Sefer ha-Zohar (Aramaic)","author":null,"authorName":"Moses de León (attributed to Shimon bar Yochai)","year":1290,"approx":true,"form":"commentary","length":"several thousand pages","level":3,"topics":["religion","metaphysics"],"summary":"A vast mystical commentary on the Torah in which the second-century rabbi Shimon bar Yochai and his companions wander through Galilee revealing hidden meanings. It describes God's inner life as ten powers, the sefirot, including a feminine divine presence, and teaches that human deeds affect harmony in the divine world. Most scholars think it was written mainly by Moses de León in Castile.","why":"It is the central book of Kabbalah and for centuries stood beside the Bible and Talmud as a sacred text for many Jews. Its ideas reached Christian Renaissance thinkers such as Pico.","inside":"Long sections following the weekly Torah portions, with stories, sermons and shorter independent treatises.","start":"Start with a modern anthology or the opening passages on Genesis.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["maimonides-guide","pico-oration"],"shelf":"jewish","tags":["kabbalah","sefirot","jewish mysticism","shekhinah","mysticism","torah commentary","judaism","god","metaphysics"]},{"id":"marsilius-defender-of-the-peace","title":"The Defender of the Peace","original":"Defensor Pacis (Latin)","author":null,"authorName":"Marsilius of Padua","year":1324,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 550 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["politics","religion","society"],"summary":"Marsilius argues that the main threat to peace in Europe is the Pope's claim to power over rulers. Political authority comes from the 'legislator', the whole body of citizens or its weightier part, and the clergy should have no coercive power at all. Even church doctrine should be settled by general councils, not the Pope alone.","why":"It is one of the most radical political books of the Middle Ages, an early argument for popular sovereignty and for separating church power from the state. Pope John XXII condemned it in 1327.","inside":"Three discourses: the first on the state and law, using Aristotle; the second, much longer, on the church; and a short list of conclusions.","start":"Read Discourse I, chapters 12 and 13, on the people as legislator.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["aristotle-politics","machiavelli-discourses","hobbes-leviathan"],"shelf":"christian-europe","tags":["popular sovereignty","separation of church and state","conciliarism","critique of papal power","political philosophy","law","aristotelianism","secularism","consent of the governed"]},{"id":"gangesha-tattvacintamani","title":"Jewel of Reflection on the Truth","original":"Tattvacintāmaṇi (Sanskrit)","author":null,"authorName":"Gangesha","year":1325,"approx":true,"form":"treatise","length":"four books, very long","level":3,"topics":["knowledge","logic","language"],"summary":"Gangesha, working in Mithila in northern India, rebuilt Nyaya philosophy around the four ways of knowing: perception, inference, comparison and testimony. He developed a precise technical language for analysing how cognitions are structured and when they count as knowledge.","why":"It founded Navya-Nyaya, or 'New Logic', which became the technical language of much of Indian philosophy, law and grammar for centuries afterward.","inside":"Four books, one for each means of knowledge. The book on inference, with its analysis of invariable concomitance (vyapti), is the most influential.","start":"Read a modern introduction to Navya-Nyaya before trying the book on inference.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["akshapada-nyaya-sutras","dharmakirti-pramanavarttika"],"shelf":"asia-medieval","tags":["navya-nyaya","nyaya","epistemology","inference","vyapti","perception","testimony","sources of knowledge","logic","indian philosophy"]},{"id":"julian-revelations-of-divine-love","title":"Revelations of Divine Love","original":null,"author":null,"authorName":"Julian of Norwich","year":1395,"approx":true,"form":"visions","length":"about 200 pages","level":1,"topics":["religion","life and death"],"summary":"In 1373, while seriously ill, an English woman later known as Julian of Norwich had a series of sixteen visions. She spent much of her life as an anchoress, enclosed in a cell, reflecting on them. She sees all creation as small as a hazelnut held in God's love, speaks of God as mother, and wrestles with how sin fits into a world held in love.","why":"It is the earliest surviving book in English known to be written by a woman. Its answer to the problem of evil, trust that all will be well, has influenced many readers, including T. S. Eliot.","inside":"A Short Text written soon after the visions and a Long Text of 86 chapters written some twenty years later. The hazelnut is in chapter 5; 'all shall be well' is in chapter 27.","start":"Read chapters 1 to 7 and chapter 27 of the Long Text.","line":{"text":"All shall be well, and all shall be well, and all manner of thing shall be well.","where":"Long Text, chapter 27","tr":null},"islands":[],"related":["augustine-confessions","boethius-consolation"],"shelf":"christian-europe","tags":["mysticism","visions","divine love","all shall be well","god as mother","the hazelnut","sin","christianity","problem of evil","women philosophers"]},{"id":"cusa-on-learned-ignorance","title":"On Learned Ignorance","original":"De Docta Ignorantia (Latin)","author":null,"authorName":"Nicholas of Cusa","year":1440,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 200 pages in English","level":3,"topics":["religion","knowledge","metaphysics"],"summary":"Cusa argues that the infinite God is beyond our grasp, so wisdom means knowing that we do not know. In God, opposites such as greatest and least coincide, which he illustrates with mathematical figures: an infinite circle would also be a straight line. He also argues that the universe has no fixed centre and that the Earth is not at rest.","why":"It is a bridge between medieval and modern thought: its picture of an unbounded universe anticipates Bruno and later cosmology, and its idea of learned ignorance echoes Socrates.","inside":"Three books: on God as the absolute maximum, on the universe, and on Christ as the union of both.","start":"Read the opening chapters of Book I, then the chapters of Book II on the Earth and the universe.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["pseudo-dionysius-mystical-theology","bruno-infinite-universe","plato-apology"],"shelf":"christian-europe","tags":["learned ignorance","coincidence of opposites","infinity","negative theology","mysticism","cosmology","decentred universe","socratic ignorance","god"]},{"id":"vitoria-on-the-american-indians","title":"On the American Indians","original":"De Indis (Latin)","author":null,"authorName":"Francisco de Vitoria","year":1539,"approx":false,"form":"lecture","length":"about 60 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["politics","ethics","society"],"summary":"In a public lecture at the University of Salamanca, Vitoria asked by what right the Spanish had conquered the peoples of the Americas. He argued that the Indians had true ownership and political authority before the Spanish arrived, and that neither 'discovery', the Pope's grant nor the Indians' unbelief gave Spain a right to rule them. He then set out narrower grounds he thought could justify intervention.","why":"It is a founding text of international law and of debates about colonialism and the rights of peoples. Its mixed conclusions are still argued over.","inside":"Three parts: whether the Indians had true dominion, unjust titles for conquest, and possibly just titles, such as a right to travel and trade.","start":"Read the first part, on whether the Indians were true owners.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["las-casas-short-account","grotius-law-of-war-and-peace","aquinas-summa-theologiae"],"shelf":"power-law","tags":["colonialism","conquest of the americas","natural law","just war","international law","indigenous rights","sovereignty","school of salamanca","human rights"]},{"id":"pico-oration","title":"Oration on the Dignity of Man","original":"Oratio de hominis dignitate (Latin)","author":null,"authorName":"Giovanni Pico della Mirandola","year":1486,"approx":false,"form":"speech","length":"about 30 pages in English","level":1,"topics":["self","religion","ethics"],"summary":"The young Pico wrote this speech to open a public debate in Rome on 900 theses drawn from every tradition he knew, from Plato and Aristotle to Islamic philosophy and Kabbalah. In its famous opening, God tells Adam that, unlike every other creature, humans have no fixed place or nature and can shape themselves, sinking to the level of beasts or rising toward the divine.","why":"It is often called the manifesto of Renaissance humanism, celebrating human freedom and the unity of all wisdom. The debate never happened: the Pope condemned some of the theses, and the speech was published only after Pico's death in 1494.","inside":"A short opening on human freedom and self-making, then a longer defence of studying philosophy and of drawing on many traditions, including Jewish Kabbalah.","start":"Read the first few pages, with God's speech to Adam.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["zohar","erasmus-praise-of-folly","more-utopia"],"shelf":"humanists","tags":["dignity of man","renaissance humanism","human freedom","self-creation","syncretism","kabbalah","humanism","human nature","free will"]},{"id":"erasmus-on-free-will","title":"On Free Will","original":"De Libero Arbitrio Diatribe (Latin)","author":null,"authorName":"Desiderius Erasmus","year":1524,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 90 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["religion","ethics","knowledge"],"summary":"Erasmus, the leading humanist scholar of Europe, takes on Martin Luther's claim that human beings have no free will in matters of salvation. Weighing passages of scripture on both sides, he argues that humans contribute a little through their own choice, with God's grace doing most of the work. He also argues that where scripture is unclear, it is wiser to avoid dogmatic assertions.","why":"It sparked one of the great debates of the Reformation and marked the break between humanism and Protestantism. Its plea for moderation in uncertain questions draws on ancient skepticism.","inside":"An introduction on the limits of certainty, then passages of scripture that support free will, then passages that seem to deny it, with Erasmus' interpretations and a conclusion.","start":"Read the introduction, then the conclusion.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["luther-bondage-of-the-will","erasmus-praise-of-folly","augustine-on-free-choice"],"shelf":"humanists","tags":["free will","grace","salvation","humanism","reformation","interpretation of scripture","skepticism","christianity","predestination"]},{"id":"luther-bondage-of-the-will","title":"The Bondage of the Will","original":"De Servo Arbitrio (Latin)","author":null,"authorName":"Martin Luther","year":1525,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 300 pages in English","level":3,"topics":["religion","ethics"],"summary":"Luther's fierce reply to Erasmus. He argues that after the Fall the human will cannot choose God by its own power; it is like a beast of burden ridden either by God or by Satan. Salvation depends entirely on God's grace, and God's foreknowledge means that everything happens necessarily.","why":"It is one of the central texts of the Protestant Reformation and a classic statement of the view that human freedom is incompatible with divine sovereignty. Luther later counted it among the few of his books worth keeping.","inside":"A long rebuttal that follows Erasmus' treatise section by section, with an introduction on the clarity of scripture and a conclusion on grace.","start":"Read the introduction on certainty and the section on God's foreknowledge.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["erasmus-on-free-will","augustine-on-free-choice","luther-freedom-of-a-christian"],"shelf":"humanists","tags":["bondage of the will","free will","predestination","grace","divine foreknowledge","determinism","reformation","protestantism","salvation","original sin"]},{"id":"copernicus-on-the-revolutions","title":"On the Revolutions of the Heavenly Spheres","original":"De revolutionibus orbium coelestium (Latin)","author":null,"authorName":"Nicolaus Copernicus","year":1543,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"six books, about 400 pages","level":3,"topics":["science","knowledge"],"summary":"Copernicus argues that the Sun, not the Earth, sits near the centre of the universe, and that the Earth spins daily on its axis and travels around the Sun each year. This makes the arrangement of the planets simpler and more harmonious, though his system still used circles within circles. It was published in the year of his death.","why":"It began the Scientific Revolution and the long process by which humans stopped seeing themselves at the centre of the cosmos. An unsigned preface by the theologian Andreas Osiander presented it as a mere calculating device, which softened its reception.","inside":"A dedication to Pope Paul III, then six books: Book I gives the general picture and is readable; Books II to VI are technical astronomy.","start":"Read the dedication to the Pope and Book I.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["galileo-dialogue-two-world-systems","bruno-infinite-universe","newton-principia"],"shelf":"new-science","tags":["heliocentrism","copernican revolution","astronomy","scientific revolution","cosmology","history of science","philosophy of science","simplicity"]},{"id":"las-casas-short-account","title":"A Short Account of the Destruction of the Indies","original":"Brevísima relación de la destrucción de las Indias (Spanish)","author":null,"authorName":"Bartolomé de las Casas","year":1552,"approx":false,"form":"report","length":"about 130 pages in English","level":1,"topics":["ethics","politics","society"],"summary":"Las Casas, a former colonist who became a Dominican friar, describes the massacres, enslavement and forced labour inflicted on the native peoples of the Caribbean and the Americas by Spanish conquerors. Written in 1542 for the Spanish court and printed in 1552, it was meant to shock rulers into stopping the abuses.","why":"It is one of the first sustained protests against colonial violence and a landmark in the history of human rights. It also helped shape debates such as the Valladolid controversy of 1550-51 on how the native peoples should be treated.","inside":"A short prologue to Prince Philip, then region-by-region accounts from Hispaniola to Peru.","start":"Read the prologue and the first chapters on Hispaniola.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["vitoria-on-the-american-indians","montaigne-essays"],"shelf":"power-law","tags":["colonialism","conquest of the americas","genocide","slavery","indigenous rights","human rights","injustice","atrocities","testimony"]},{"id":"la-boetie-voluntary-servitude","title":"Discourse on Voluntary Servitude","original":"Discours de la servitude volontaire (French)","author":null,"authorName":"Étienne de La Boétie","year":1548,"approx":true,"form":"essay","length":"about 40 pages in English","level":1,"topics":["politics","society"],"summary":"Why do millions obey one tyrant who has only the power they give him? La Boétie argues that tyranny rests on the consent of the ruled, sustained by habit, entertainment and a chain of people who profit from serving the tyrant. If people simply stopped supporting him, he would fall without any violence.","why":"It is an early classic of political thought on consent and non-violent resistance, admired by later thinkers on civil disobedience and anarchism. La Boétie was Montaigne's closest friend; it was written when he was young and published after his death.","inside":"A single continuous essay: the puzzle of mass obedience, the role of custom, the ways tyrants keep people docile, and the network of beneficiaries around the tyrant.","start":"Read it whole; it takes an evening.","line":{"text":"Resolve to serve no more, and you are at once freed.","where":"middle of the essay","tr":"Kurz"},"islands":[],"related":["montaigne-essays","machiavelli-prince","thoreau-civil-disobedience"],"shelf":"power-law","tags":["voluntary servitude","tyranny","consent","obedience","non-violent resistance","power","anarchism","political philosophy","habit","civil disobedience"]},{"id":"bruno-infinite-universe","title":"On the Infinite Universe and Worlds","original":"De l'infinito, universo e mondi (Italian)","author":null,"authorName":"Giordano Bruno","year":1584,"approx":false,"form":"dialogue","length":"five dialogues, about 200 pages","level":2,"topics":["science","metaphysics","religion"],"summary":"Bruno argues that the universe is infinite, with no centre and no outer edge, and filled with countless suns and worlds like our own, perhaps inhabited. An infinite God, he says, would naturally produce an infinite creation. Characters in the dialogues defend this view against an Aristotelian.","why":"It went far beyond Copernicus in imagining the universe, and anticipated later cosmology. Bruno was burned as a heretic in Rome in 1600, largely for his theological views, and became a symbol of free thought.","inside":"Five dialogues in Italian, written in London, in which Filoteo, Bruno's spokesman, persuades others and answers Aristotle's arguments for a finite cosmos.","start":"Read the first dialogue.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["copernicus-on-the-revolutions","cusa-on-learned-ignorance","lucretius-on-the-nature-of-things"],"shelf":"new-science","tags":["infinite universe","plurality of worlds","cosmology","heliocentrism","pantheism","critique of aristotle","scientific revolution","god","extraterrestrial life"]},{"id":"li-zhi-book-to-burn","title":"A Book to Burn","original":"Fenshu (Chinese)","author":null,"authorName":"Li Zhi","year":1590,"approx":false,"form":"essays and letters","length":"about 300 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["self","society","ethics"],"summary":"A collection of letters, essays and poems by the Ming dynasty rebel Li Zhi, who named it for the fate he expected it to meet. He attacks people who repeat the Confucian classics without thinking, defends the worth of ordinary desires, merchants and women's intelligence, and urges people to follow their own genuine feelings.","why":"Its essay 'On the Childlike Mind' is a famous call for authenticity against conformity. The book was banned and burned, and Li Zhi died in prison in 1602.","inside":"Letters to friends and opponents, short essays on history, literature and moral questions, and poems. The essay on the childlike mind is the best known piece.","start":"Read 'On the Childlike Mind' first, then a few of the letters.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["wangyangming-instructions","zhuangzi","montaigne-essays"],"shelf":"beyond-europe-ren","tags":["childlike mind","authenticity","individualism","critique of confucianism","neo-confucianism","desire","women","chinese philosophy","nonconformity"]},{"id":"bacon-essays","title":"Essays, or Counsels Civil and Moral","original":null,"author":"bacon","authorName":"Francis Bacon","year":1597,"approx":false,"form":"essays","length":"58 short essays, about 150 pages","level":1,"topics":["ethics","society","self"],"summary":"Short, sharp essays of practical wisdom on topics such as truth, death, revenge, ambition, friendship, studies and gardens. Bacon writes as a statesman, often weighing how things actually work rather than how they ought to, in compressed sentences full of memorable phrases.","why":"They are among the first essays in English and some of the most quoted. They show the worldly, practical side of the man who also campaigned for a new science.","inside":"A first edition of 10 essays appeared in 1597; the collection grew to 58 by 1625. Each essay is only a page or two.","start":"Start with 'Of Truth', 'Of Studies' and 'Of Revenge'.","line":{"text":"Some books are to be tasted, others to be swallowed, and some few to be chewed and digested","where":"'Of Studies'","tr":"original English"},"islands":[],"related":["montaigne-essays","bacon-new-organon"],"shelf":"humanists","tags":["practical wisdom","essays","truth","death","revenge","friendship","ambition","statecraft","aphorisms","ethics"]},{"id":"grotius-law-of-war-and-peace","title":"On the Law of War and Peace","original":"De Jure Belli ac Pacis (Latin)","author":null,"authorName":"Hugo Grotius","year":1625,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"three books, about 900 pages in English","level":3,"topics":["politics","ethics","society"],"summary":"Grotius, a Dutch jurist writing during the Thirty Years' War, argues that there is a natural law binding all nations, rooted in human sociability and reason. War can be just only for certain causes, such as self-defence or recovering what is owed, and even a just war must be fought within limits. He famously claimed natural law would hold even if, impossibly, there were no God.","why":"It founded modern international law and shaped later natural-law thinkers such as Pufendorf and Locke. Grotius had escaped from prison in 1621 hidden in a chest of books.","inside":"A Prolegomena setting out natural law, then three books: whether war can be just, the causes of just war, and what is permitted in war.","start":"Read the Prolegomena.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["vitoria-on-the-american-indians","hobbes-leviathan","locke-two-treatises"],"shelf":"power-law","tags":["natural law","just war","international law","laws of war","sociability","even if there were no god","rights","political philosophy","war and peace"]},{"id":"mulla-sadra-four-journeys","title":"The Transcendent Philosophy of the Four Journeys of the Intellect","original":"al-Hikma al-Muta'aliya fi'l-Asfar al-'Aqliyya al-Arba'a (Arabic)","author":null,"authorName":"Mulla Sadra","year":1630,"approx":true,"form":"treatise","length":"nine volumes in Arabic","level":3,"topics":["metaphysics","religion","mind"],"summary":"Mulla Sadra's great synthesis of philosophy, theology and mysticism, built around four 'journeys' of the soul to and from God. Its central claims are that existence, not essence, is what is fundamentally real; that existence comes in degrees, like light; and that substances themselves are constantly in motion, so the world is always renewing itself.","why":"It is the most important work of later Islamic philosophy and is still the core of philosophical study in Iranian seminaries. Its idea of substantial motion is often compared to process philosophy.","inside":"Four journeys: on being and general metaphysics, on substances and accidents, on God and his attributes, and on the soul and its return to God. It was written over many years.","start":"Start with a modern introduction to Mulla Sadra; then read the opening discussions of existence.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["suhrawardi-philosophy-of-illumination","avicenna-pointers-and-reminders","ibn-arabi-bezels-of-wisdom"],"shelf":"beyond-europe-ren","tags":["primacy of existence","gradation of being","substantial motion","transcendent philosophy","islamic philosophy","mysticism","metaphysics","soul","being"]},{"id":"galileo-dialogue-two-world-systems","title":"Dialogue Concerning the Two Chief World Systems","original":"Dialogo sopra i due massimi sistemi del mondo (Italian)","author":null,"authorName":"Galileo Galilei","year":1632,"approx":false,"form":"dialogue","length":"about 450 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["science","knowledge","religion"],"summary":"Over four days, three friends debate whether the Earth or the Sun is at the centre of the world. Salviati argues for Copernicus, the thoughtful Sagredo listens and judges, and the Aristotelian Simplicio defends the traditional view and keeps losing. Galileo answers the objection that we would feel the Earth's motion with the idea that motion shared by everything is not noticed.","why":"It is a masterpiece of scientific persuasion written in Italian for a wide public. It led to Galileo's trial by the Inquisition in 1633, his forced recantation and house arrest, and the book stayed on the Index of Forbidden Books until 1835.","inside":"Four days: against Aristotle's division of heaven and earth, the Earth's daily rotation (with the famous ship experiment), its yearly orbit, and a mistaken theory of tides as proof.","start":"Read the Second Day, including the passage about experiments below deck in a ship.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["copernicus-on-the-revolutions","aristotle-physics","newton-principia"],"shelf":"new-science","tags":["heliocentrism","copernican revolution","scientific revolution","relativity of motion","critique of aristotle","science and religion","astronomy","galileo's ship","philosophy of science"]},{"id":"descartes-passions-of-the-soul","title":"The Passions of the Soul","original":"Les Passions de l'âme (French)","author":"descartes","authorName":"René Descartes","year":1649,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"212 short articles, about 130 pages","level":2,"topics":["mind","ethics"],"summary":"Descartes' last book, prompted by questions from Princess Elisabeth of Bohemia. He explains emotions as states of the soul caused by movements in the body, passed through the pineal gland. He names six basic passions, wonder, love, hatred, desire, joy and sadness, and argues that we can learn to govern them, ending with generosity as the key to all the virtues.","why":"It is a founding text of the modern study of emotion and Descartes' fullest attempt to explain how mind and body work together, the problem Elisabeth had pressed him on.","inside":"Three parts: the passions in general and the body, the six basic passions, and particular passions built from them.","start":"Read Part 1, articles 1 to 50, then the articles on generosity in Part 3 (from article 153).","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["elisabeth-correspondence-with-descartes","descartes-meditations","spinoza-ethics"],"shelf":"rationalists","tags":["emotions","passions","pineal gland","mind-body problem","mind-body dualism","generosity","wonder","self-control","philosophy of mind","virtue"]},{"id":"dara-shikoh-mingling-of-two-oceans","title":"The Mingling of the Two Oceans","original":"Majma' al-Bahrayn (Persian)","author":null,"authorName":"Dara Shikoh","year":1655,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 40 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["religion","metaphysics"],"summary":"Dara Shikoh, a Mughal prince and Sufi, sets the terms of Islamic Sufism beside those of Hindu Vedanta and argues that they express the same truths in different words. He compares, for example, the Sufi and Hindu accounts of the elements, the senses, God's names and the end of the world.","why":"It is a landmark of interreligious philosophy. Dara also had the Upanishads translated into Persian, and that translation, rendered into Latin, introduced them to Europe and to Schopenhauer. He was executed in 1659 by his brother Aurangzeb.","inside":"About twenty short chapters, each comparing a theme in the two traditions.","start":"Read the introduction and the first few chapters.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["upanishads","ibn-arabi-bezels-of-wisdom","schopenhauer-world-as-will"],"shelf":"beyond-europe-ren","tags":["sufism","vedanta","comparative religion","religious pluralism","islamic philosophy","hinduism","mysticism","interfaith dialogue","unity of religions"]},{"id":"pascal-provincial-letters","title":"The Provincial Letters","original":"Les Provinciales (French)","author":"pascal","authorName":"Blaise Pascal","year":1656,"approx":false,"form":"letters","length":"18 letters, about 300 pages in English","level":1,"topics":["ethics","religion"],"summary":"Writing under a pen name, Pascal defended his friend Antoine Arnauld, a Jansenist theologian under attack at the Sorbonne, in a series of anonymous open letters. Soon he turned to mocking the Jesuit moral theologians, whose clever reasoning about cases, he argued, could excuse almost any sin, from lying to duelling.","why":"The letters are a classic of French prose and satire and a sharp critique of casuistry, the use of case-based reasoning to bend moral rules. They remain a reference point in debates about moral loopholes.","inside":"Eighteen letters published in 1656 and 1657. Letters 1 to 4 deal with grace; 5 to 10 satirise Jesuit casuistry through a naive Jesuit father; 11 to 18 are addressed to the Jesuits directly.","start":"Read Letters 5 to 7.","line":{"text":"I have made this letter longer than usual, only because I have not had the time to make it shorter.","where":"Letter 16","tr":null},"islands":[],"related":["pascal-pensees","montaigne-essays"],"shelf":"rationalists","tags":["casuistry","jansenism","satire","grace","moral laxity","christianity","jesuits","ethics","moral reasoning"]},{"id":"huang-zongxi-waiting-for-the-dawn","title":"Waiting for the Dawn","original":"Mingyi daifang lu (Chinese)","author":null,"authorName":"Huang Zongxi","year":1663,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 200 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["politics","society"],"summary":"Written by a Ming loyalist after the Manchu conquest of China, this book argues that later emperors had turned the world into their private property, while the ancient sage kings had ruled for the good of the people. Huang proposes stronger ministers, laws that serve everyone rather than the dynasty, and schools that act as places of open public criticism of government.","why":"It is a powerful critique of absolute monarchy from within the Confucian tradition and was taken up by Chinese reformers in the late nineteenth century.","inside":"About twenty short chapters on the ruler, ministers, law, schools, land, the military, finance and other parts of government.","start":"Read the first three chapters, 'On the Prince', 'On Ministership' and 'On Law'.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["mencius","hobbes-leviathan","locke-two-treatises"],"shelf":"beyond-europe-ren","tags":["critique of autocracy","confucianism","political philosophy","rule of law","public criticism","good government","chinese philosophy","the people","reform"]},{"id":"cavendish-blazing-world","title":"The Blazing World","original":"The Description of a New World, Called the Blazing World","author":null,"authorName":"Margaret Cavendish","year":1666,"approx":false,"form":"fiction","length":"about 100 pages","level":1,"topics":["science","knowledge","society"],"summary":"A young woman is carried off to another world joined to ours at the North Pole, where she becomes empress. She questions societies of talking animal scientists about their theories, organises their research, and summons the soul of the Duchess of Newcastle, Cavendish herself, as her scribe. The two then build imaginary worlds of their own.","why":"It is one of the earliest works of science fiction and a playful critique of the new experimental science, written by a woman who argued with Hobbes and the Royal Society in her philosophy.","inside":"Published together with Cavendish's Observations upon Experimental Philosophy. Two parts: the empress's rule and scientific debates, then a war she wages to help her home country.","start":"Read the first part, up to the arrival of the Duchess's soul.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["cavendish-observations","bacon-new-organon","more-utopia"],"shelf":"new-science","tags":["utopia","science fiction","philosophy of science","imaginary worlds","women philosophers","feminism","satire","experimental science","thought experiment"]},{"id":"zera-yacob-hatata","title":"Hatata (The Inquiry)","original":"Hatata (Ge'ez)","author":null,"authorName":"Zera Yacob","year":1667,"approx":true,"form":"autobiography and treatise","length":"about 30 pages in English","level":1,"topics":["religion","knowledge","ethics"],"summary":"An Ethiopian scholar describes hiding in a cave for two years during religious persecution and using that time to think things through. Seeing that Christians, Muslims and Jews each claim their faith is true, he decides that God has given humans reason to test such claims. He rejects religious rules that contradict reason or harm human nature and argues that all people, women and men alike, are equal before God.","why":"It is a remarkable work of rationalist philosophy from seventeenth-century Africa, independent of the European Enlightenment it resembles. Its authenticity was challenged in 1920 as a later forgery by an Italian missionary; many scholars now defend it, but the debate continues.","inside":"Short chapters mixing life story with reflection: his persecution and retreat, his inquiry into faith and reason, his views on law, marriage and work, and his later life. A second work is attributed to his student Walda Heywat.","start":"Read it whole; it is short.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["descartes-discourse-on-method","alghazali-deliverance"],"shelf":"beyond-europe-ren","tags":["rationalism","faith and reason","religious tolerance","natural religion","equality","african philosophy","critique of religion","gender equality","god","ethiopian philosophy"]},{"id":"malebranche-search-after-truth","title":"The Search After Truth","original":"De la recherche de la vérité (French)","author":"malebranche","authorName":"Nicolas Malebranche","year":1674,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 600 pages in English","level":3,"topics":["knowledge","mind","religion"],"summary":"Malebranche, a priest and follower of Descartes, studies the sources of human error: the senses, the imagination, the understanding, our inclinations and our passions. Along the way he argues that we see all things 'in God', since our ideas are in God's mind, and that God is the only true cause, with physical events being only occasions for God to act.","why":"Its occasionalism and its argument that we never see a necessary connection between causes and effects strongly influenced Berkeley and Hume.","inside":"Six books, one for each source of error and a last one on method, with later 'Elucidations' answering critics.","start":"Read Book 1 on the senses, then Book 3, Part 2, on how we see all things in God.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["descartes-meditations","berkeley-principles","hume-enquiry"],"shelf":"rationalists","tags":["occasionalism","vision in god","causation","error","cartesianism","rationalism","imagination","passions","epistemology","god"]},{"id":"leibniz-discourse-on-metaphysics","title":"Discourse on Metaphysics","original":"Discours de métaphysique (French)","author":"leibniz","authorName":"Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz","year":1686,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"37 short sections, about 40 pages","level":2,"topics":["metaphysics","religion","mind"],"summary":"Leibniz's first mature sketch of his philosophy. God has created the best of all possible worlds, and each individual substance has a 'complete concept' containing everything that will ever happen to it, so that each mirrors the whole universe from its own point of view. Substances do not act on each other; they unfold in harmony.","why":"It is the best short introduction to Leibniz's system and leads to his later Monadology. Leibniz never published it; it appeared only in 1846, but a summary sent to Antoine Arnauld sparked a famous correspondence.","inside":"Thirty-seven numbered sections: God's perfection and the best world, individual substances, freedom and contingency, the relation of mind and body, and the city of God.","start":"Read sections 1 to 13, including the problem of how Caesar's crossing of the Rubicon could be free.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["leibniz-monadology","leibniz-theodicy","spinoza-ethics"],"shelf":"germany-enl","tags":["complete concept","best of all possible worlds","pre-established harmony","substance","freedom and contingency","rationalism","metaphysics","mind-body problem","god"]},{"id":"newton-principia","title":"Mathematical Principles of Natural Philosophy","original":"Philosophiae Naturalis Principia Mathematica (Latin)","author":null,"authorName":"Isaac Newton","year":1687,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"three books, about 900 pages in English","level":3,"topics":["science","knowledge"],"summary":"Newton sets out his three laws of motion and the law of universal gravitation, and shows with mathematics that the same force that makes apples fall keeps the Moon and planets in their orbits. He explains the tides, the shapes of planetary orbits and the paths of comets.","why":"It is one of the most important books in the history of science and set the model of explanation for the Enlightenment. Its 'rules of reasoning' and refusal to speculate about the cause of gravity shaped later philosophy of science.","inside":"Definitions and laws of motion, then Book I (motion of bodies), Book II (motion in resisting media) and Book III, 'The System of the World'. The second edition of 1713 added the General Scholium.","start":"Read the Definitions and Laws, the Rules of Reasoning at the start of Book III, and the General Scholium.","line":{"text":"I frame no hypotheses","where":"General Scholium (second edition, 1713)","tr":"Motte"},"islands":[],"related":["galileo-dialogue-two-world-systems","descartes-discourse-on-method","hume-enquiry"],"shelf":"empiricists","tags":["laws of motion","gravity","universal gravitation","scientific revolution","physics","philosophy of science","mechanism","hypotheses non fingo","absolute space and time"]},{"id":"conway-principles","title":"The Principles of the Most Ancient and Modern Philosophy","original":"Principia philosophiae antiquissimae et recentissimae (Latin)","author":null,"authorName":"Anne Conway","year":1690,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"nine chapters, about 70 pages","level":2,"topics":["metaphysics","religion","mind"],"summary":"Anne Conway argues against Descartes that there is no sharp divide between mind and matter: all created things are made of a single living, perceiving substance that can become more bodily or more spiritual. She also holds that all creatures can change and improve, so even suffering serves a purpose of healing. She criticises Descartes, Hobbes and Spinoza by name.","why":"It is an original metaphysics by an English woman philosopher, published anonymously after her death and admired by Leibniz. It is now read as an important alternative to both dualism and materialism.","inside":"Nine chapters: God, Christ as mediator, creatures, the possibility of change between kinds of creatures, and the critique of Descartes, Hobbes and Spinoza in chapter 9.","start":"Read chapters 1 and 9.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["elisabeth-correspondence-with-descartes","cavendish-observations","leibniz-monadology"],"shelf":"empiricists","tags":["vitalism","monism","critique of descartes","panpsychism","women philosophers","theodicy","neoplatonism","metaphysics","mind-body problem","god"]},{"id":"mandeville-fable-of-the-bees","title":"The Fable of the Bees","original":null,"author":null,"authorName":"Bernard Mandeville","year":1714,"approx":false,"form":"poem with prose commentary","length":"a short poem followed by about 250 pages of remarks","level":2,"topics":["ethics","society"],"summary":"It begins with a satirical poem about a buzzing, prosperous hive whose bees are greedy, vain and dishonest. When the bees suddenly become virtuous, trade collapses and the hive dwindles. In long prose 'Remarks', Mandeville argues that a rich society actually runs on vices like pride and love of luxury, and that moral virtue was largely invented by clever politicians who flattered human pride.","why":"It scandalised its readers and forced moral philosophers to explain how self-interest and the public good fit together. Hutcheson, Berkeley, Hume and Adam Smith all responded to it, and it anticipates later ideas about markets.","inside":"The poem 'The Grumbling Hive' (first printed in 1705) comes first, then lettered Remarks on its lines, then essays such as 'An Enquiry into the Origin of Moral Virtue'. Later editions added more essays and a second volume.","start":"Read the poem, then 'An Enquiry into the Origin of Moral Virtue'.","line":{"text":"Private Vices, Publick Benefits","where":"Subtitle of the book","tr":null},"islands":[],"related":["smith-wealth-of-nations","hutcheson-inquiry-beauty-virtue","smith-moral-sentiments"],"shelf":"sentiment","tags":["private vices public benefits","self-interest","luxury","satire","the grumbling hive","economics","moral virtue as invention","egoism","commerce","pride"]},{"id":"montesquieu-persian-letters","title":"Persian Letters","original":"Lettres persanes (French)","author":null,"authorName":"Montesquieu","year":1721,"approx":false,"form":"epistolary novel","length":"about 250 pages in English","level":1,"topics":["society","politics","religion"],"summary":"Two Persian noblemen, Usbek and Rica, travel to Paris and write letters home describing French customs, the Church, the King and fashionable society as if they were bizarre foreign rituals. Meanwhile letters from Usbek's harem in Isfahan show his own household sliding into revolt while he is away. The book uses an outsider's eyes to make the familiar look strange.","why":"It is one of the great Enlightenment satires, criticising absolute monarchy and religious intolerance under cover of fiction. Its ending, in which the wife Roxane defies Usbek, turns the critique of tyranny back on the traveller himself.","inside":"About 160 letters (the number varies by edition) arranged in time order, mixing comic sketches of Paris, short philosophical essays, a fable about the Troglodytes, and the darkening harem story.","start":"Read the Troglodyte letters (11 to 14), then dip into Rica's letters about Paris, then read the final letters from the harem.","line":{"text":"How can one be Persian?","where":"Letter 30","tr":null},"islands":[],"related":["montesquieu-spirit-of-the-laws","voltaire-candide","voltaire-letters-on-the-english"],"shelf":"continental-enl","tags":["satire","cultural relativism","outsider perspective","religious tolerance","despotism","orientalism","troglodytes","enlightenment","social criticism"]},{"id":"hutcheson-inquiry-beauty-virtue","title":"An Inquiry into the Original of Our Ideas of Beauty and Virtue","original":null,"author":null,"authorName":"Francis Hutcheson","year":1725,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 200 pages","level":2,"topics":["ethics","beauty"],"summary":"Hutcheson argues that we have an inner 'sense' of beauty, which responds to 'uniformity amidst variety', and a 'moral sense' that approves of benevolence in others. These senses work immediately, like taste or sight, and do not depend on calculating our own advantage. Against Mandeville and Hobbes, he holds that genuine kindness is part of human nature.","why":"It founded moral sense theory, which shaped Hume's and Adam Smith's ethics (Smith was Hutcheson's student at Glasgow). It also contains an early statement of the principle later made famous by the utilitarians.","inside":"Two treatises: the first on beauty, order, harmony and design; the second on moral good and evil. The second treatise is the more influential.","start":"Begin with the second treatise, on virtue, especially its first three sections.","line":{"text":"that Action is best, which procures the greatest Happiness for the greatest Numbers","where":"Treatise II, Section 3","tr":null},"islands":[],"related":["hume-enquiry-principles-morals","smith-moral-sentiments","mandeville-fable-of-the-bees"],"shelf":"sentiment","tags":["moral sense","sense of beauty","benevolence","aesthetics","moral sentiment","uniformity amidst variety","scottish enlightenment","critique of egoism","ethics"]},{"id":"vico-new-science","title":"The New Science","original":"Principi di scienza nuova (Italian)","author":null,"authorName":"Giambattista Vico","year":1725,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 400 pages in English","level":3,"topics":["society","knowledge","language"],"summary":"Vico argues that we can understand human history more deeply than nature, because humans made history. He claims that every nation passes through three ages, of gods, of heroes and of men, each with its own kind of language, law and way of thinking. He reads myths, early poetry and old laws as clues to how the first humans actually thought, treating them as 'poetic wisdom' rather than as mistakes.","why":"It is a founding work of the philosophy of history and of the human sciences, and it influenced Herder, Marx, Croce, Collingwood and the novelist James Joyce. Vico was little read in his lifetime and was rediscovered later.","inside":"The definitive third edition (1744) opens with Vico's explanation of an allegorical picture, then five books; Book 2, 'Poetic Wisdom', is the longest and most original.","start":"Read the explanation of the frontispiece, then the opening of Book 2 on poetic wisdom.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["hegel-philosophy-of-history","ibn-khaldun-muqaddimah"],"shelf":"continental-enl","tags":["philosophy of history","three ages","verum factum","poetic wisdom","myth","cyclical history","history","language","philosophy of culture"]},{"id":"amo-impassivity-of-the-human-mind","title":"On the Impassivity of the Human Mind","original":"De humanae mentis apatheia (Latin)","author":null,"authorName":"Anton Wilhelm Amo","year":1734,"approx":false,"form":"dissertation","length":"a short Latin dissertation, about 30 pages in English","level":3,"topics":["mind"],"summary":"Amo was born on the Gold Coast (in present-day Ghana), taken to Germany as a child, and became the first known African to earn a doctorate at a European university. In this dissertation, defended at Wittenberg, he argues that the human mind cannot be acted on or feel sensations: sensing belongs to the living body, while the mind is purely active. He uses the distinction between mind and body more strictly than Descartes did.","why":"It is an important document of an African philosopher working inside the early German Enlightenment, and his life is now widely studied. It also shows how hard the mind-body problem looked to Descartes's successors.","inside":"A formal academic dissertation in Latin, setting out definitions and then a series of numbered arguments for its thesis.","start":"Read a short account of Amo's life first, then the dissertation's statement of its thesis and its first arguments.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["descartes-meditations","elisabeth-correspondence-with-descartes"],"shelf":"beyond-europe-enl","tags":["mind-body dualism","philosophy of mind","sensation","african philosophy","cartesianism","impassivity of the mind","soul"]},{"id":"voltaire-letters-on-the-english","title":"Letters on the English","original":"Lettres philosophiques (French)","author":"voltaire","authorName":"Voltaire","year":1734,"approx":false,"form":"letters","length":"about 150 pages in English","level":1,"topics":["religion","politics","science"],"summary":"After living in England in the late 1720s, Voltaire wrote these letters praising English religious tolerance, Quakers and other sects, parliamentary government, trade, smallpox inoculation, and the thought of Newton and Locke. Each piece of praise is an implied criticism of France. The book closes with a letter attacking Pascal's gloomy view of human beings.","why":"It brought Newton and Locke to French readers and became a manifesto of the French Enlightenment. The French edition was condemned and publicly burned, and Voltaire had to leave Paris.","inside":"Twenty-five short letters: the first seven on religion, then politics and trade, then science and philosophy (Bacon, Locke, Descartes and Newton), then literature, then Pascal.","start":"Read the letters on the Quakers (1 to 4) and the letter on Locke (13).","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["locke-letter-concerning-toleration","voltaire-candide","pascal-pensees"],"shelf":"continental-enl","tags":["religious tolerance","enlightenment","quakers","liberty","empiricism","commerce","inoculation","critique of pascal","philosophy of science"]},{"id":"du-chatelet-foundations-of-physics","title":"Foundations of Physics","original":"Institutions de physique (French)","author":null,"authorName":"Émilie du Châtelet","year":1740,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 450 pages","level":3,"topics":["science","metaphysics","knowledge"],"summary":"Written as a physics textbook for her young son, it begins with the principles of knowledge and metaphysics before reaching space, time, motion and gravity. Du Châtelet combines Leibniz's principle of sufficient reason with Newton's physics, and in later chapters defends the Leibnizian measure of force (mass times velocity squared). It treats physics as a field that needs philosophical foundations as well as experiments.","why":"It is one of the most important works of natural philosophy by a woman in the period, and it was translated and debated across Europe. Du Châtelet also made the French translation of Newton's Principia that is still used.","inside":"Its early chapters cover the principles of knowledge, God, essence, and space and time; the later chapters move into physics proper, including motion, gravity and force.","start":"Read the preface to her son and the first chapter on the principles of our knowledge.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["leibniz-monadology","voltaire-letters-on-the-english"],"shelf":"continental-enl","tags":["principle of sufficient reason","philosophy of science","physics","vis viva","space and time","women philosophers","rationalism","enlightenment","metaphysics"]},{"id":"la-mettrie-man-a-machine","title":"Man a Machine","original":"L'Homme machine (French)","author":null,"authorName":"Julien Offray de La Mettrie","year":1747,"approx":false,"form":"essay","length":"about 60 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["mind","science"],"summary":"La Mettrie, a doctor, argues that human beings are entirely material machines and that the 'soul' is just a word for the workings of the brain. He gathers evidence from medicine: illness, food, sleep, drugs and injury all change how we think and feel. Descartes had called animals machines; La Mettrie says humans are too, only more complex.","why":"It was one of the boldest materialist statements of the Enlightenment and caused such outrage that La Mettrie had to flee to the court of Frederick the Great. It foreshadows modern scientific views of the mind as the brain.","inside":"A single continuous essay rather than a system, moving from medical observations to comparisons between humans and animals to the conclusion.","start":"Read it straight through; it is short and lively.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["descartes-discourse-on-method","ryle-concept-of-mind"],"shelf":"continental-enl","tags":["materialism","mechanism","man as machine","physicalism","atheism","philosophy of mind","enlightenment","brain","medicine"]},{"id":"rousseau-discourse-sciences-arts","title":"Discourse on the Sciences and Arts","original":"Discours sur les sciences et les arts (French)","author":"rousseau","authorName":"Jean-Jacques Rousseau","year":1750,"approx":false,"form":"essay","length":"about 30 pages in English","level":1,"topics":["society","ethics"],"summary":"The Academy of Dijon asked whether the revival of the sciences and arts had helped to purify morals. Rousseau answered no: learning and refinement had made people more polished but also more vain, idle and dishonest. He praised Sparta and the simple virtues of early Rome over Athens and modern Paris.","why":"It won the prize, made Rousseau famous overnight, and launched his lifelong critique of civilisation, which he developed in the Discourse on Inequality. It is still a sharp starting point for asking whether progress makes us better.","inside":"A short prize essay in two parts: the first draws on history, the second argues that the sciences grow out of vices.","start":"Read it straight through, then compare it with the Discourse on Inequality.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["rousseau-discourse-inequality","rousseau-social-contract"],"shelf":"continental-enl","tags":["critique of civilisation","progress","corruption","virtue","sciences and arts","luxury","sparta","enlightenment","social critique"]},{"id":"hume-enquiry-principles-morals","title":"An Enquiry Concerning the Principles of Morals","original":null,"author":"hume","authorName":"David Hume","year":1751,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 150 pages","level":2,"topics":["ethics","society"],"summary":"Hume argues that morality rests on human feeling, not pure reason. The qualities we call virtues are those we find useful or agreeable, to ourselves or to others, and we approve of them through a natural sympathy with other people. He treats benevolence and justice in detail, arguing that justice exists because of its usefulness to society.","why":"Hume himself called it the best of all his writings. It is a clear founding text of sentiment-based ethics and an ancestor of utilitarianism.","inside":"Nine sections plus four appendices; the first appendix, on whether moral judgement comes from reason or sentiment, states his central view.","start":"Read Sections 1 to 3, on benevolence and justice, and Appendix 1.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["hume-treatise","smith-moral-sentiments","hutcheson-inquiry-beauty-virtue"],"shelf":"sentiment","tags":["moral sentiment","sentimentalism","sympathy","benevolence","justice","utility","virtue","reason and emotion","ethics","scottish enlightenment"]},{"id":"beccaria-on-crimes-and-punishments","title":"On Crimes and Punishments","original":"Dei delitti e delle pene (Italian)","author":null,"authorName":"Cesare Beccaria","year":1764,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 100 pages in English","level":1,"topics":["politics","ethics","society"],"summary":"Beccaria argues that punishment is justified only to prevent future crimes, not for revenge. Punishments should be public, prompt, certain and proportionate to the harm done, and laws should be clear enough for everyone to understand. He condemns torture and argues against the death penalty.","why":"It quickly became one of the most influential books of the Enlightenment, read by Voltaire, Bentham, Catherine the Great and the American founders, and it shaped criminal law reform across Europe and America.","inside":"Forty-odd short chapters, each on one topic such as torture, the death penalty, proportion between crimes and punishments, or the prevention of crimes.","start":"Read the introduction and the chapters on torture and on the death penalty.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["bentham-principles-morals-legislation","montesquieu-spirit-of-the-laws"],"shelf":"rights","tags":["punishment","criminal justice","death penalty","torture","deterrence","proportionality","utilitarianism","rule of law","enlightenment","legal reform"]},{"id":"reid-inquiry-into-the-human-mind","title":"An Inquiry into the Human Mind on the Principles of Common Sense","original":null,"author":"reid","authorName":"Thomas Reid","year":1764,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 250 pages","level":2,"topics":["knowledge","mind"],"summary":"Reid argues that the 'theory of ideas', the view shared by Descartes, Locke, Berkeley and Hume that we only ever perceive ideas in our own minds, leads straight to Hume's scepticism. He rejects it: perception puts us directly in touch with real external things. Some beliefs, such as that the world exists and that our faculties are broadly reliable, are principles of common sense that we cannot and need not prove.","why":"It founded the Scottish school of common sense philosophy and is the classic reply to Hume's scepticism. Reid's direct realism has had a strong revival among philosophers since the twentieth century.","inside":"After an introduction, it examines the senses one by one: smelling, tasting, hearing, touch and, at greatest length, seeing, and then draws conclusions.","start":"Read the introduction and the chapter on smelling, which sets out his method in a simple case.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["hume-treatise","berkeley-principles","moore-defence-of-common-sense"],"shelf":"empiricists","tags":["common sense","direct realism","critique of the theory of ideas","perception","skepticism","scottish enlightenment","epistemology","senses","philosophy of mind"]},{"id":"paine-common-sense","title":"Common Sense","original":null,"author":"paine","authorName":"Thomas Paine","year":1776,"approx":false,"form":"pamphlet","length":"about 50 pages","level":1,"topics":["politics"],"summary":"Published anonymously in January 1776, it argued in plain, forceful language that the American colonies should declare independence from Britain. Paine attacks hereditary monarchy as absurd, argues that a continent should not be ruled by an island, and sketches a republican government.","why":"It sold in enormous numbers and helped turn colonial opinion towards independence months before the Declaration of Independence. It is a model of political argument written for ordinary readers.","inside":"Four sections: on the origin of government, on monarchy and hereditary succession, on the state of American affairs, and on America's present ability.","start":"Read the first two sections.","line":{"text":"Society in every state is a blessing, but government even in its best state is but a necessary evil; in its worst state an intolerable one.","where":"Of the Origin and Design of Government in General","tr":null},"islands":[],"related":["paine-rights-of-man","locke-two-treatises","federalist-papers"],"shelf":"rights","tags":["american revolution","independence","republicanism","critique of monarchy","hereditary succession","political philosophy","government","liberty"]},{"id":"dai-zhen-meanings-of-terms-in-the-mencius","title":"Evidential Commentary on the Meanings of Terms in the Mencius","original":"Mengzi ziyi shuzheng (Chinese)","author":null,"authorName":"Dai Zhen","year":1777,"approx":true,"form":"commentary","length":"about 150 pages in English","level":3,"topics":["ethics","metaphysics"],"summary":"Dai Zhen, a leading scholar of Qing dynasty 'evidential learning', studies key terms in the Mencius, such as li (pattern or principle), human nature and the Way. He argues that the Neo-Confucians led by Zhu Xi misread li as something above and against human feelings and desires. For Dai, li is found in the proper fulfilment of desires, and treating it as a rigid abstract rule lets the powerful oppress people in its name.","why":"It is one of the most important critiques of Neo-Confucianism, and it has been read as an early argument for taking ordinary human needs seriously in ethics.","inside":"A series of entries on philosophical terms, each set out as questions and answers backed by close reading of the classics.","start":"Start with the entries on li (principle) and on human nature.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["mencius","zhuxi-reflections","wangyangming-instructions"],"shelf":"beyond-europe-enl","tags":["critique of neo-confucianism","li","human nature","desire","evidential learning","confucianism","oppression","chinese philosophy","ethics"]},{"id":"kant-prolegomena","title":"Prolegomena to Any Future Metaphysics","original":"Prolegomena zu einer jeden künftigen Metaphysik (German)","author":"kant","authorName":"Immanuel Kant","year":1783,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 130 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["knowledge","metaphysics"],"summary":"Kant wrote this shorter book because readers found the Critique of Pure Reason too hard. It asks how 'synthetic a priori' knowledge is possible, that is, knowledge that tells us something new about the world but does not come from experience. He answers for mathematics, then natural science, then asks whether metaphysics can be a science at all.","why":"It is the most common way into Kant's theoretical philosophy, and its preface tells the famous story of how reading Hume changed his thinking.","inside":"A preface, a preamble on kinds of judgement, then three parts on mathematics, natural science and metaphysics, and a solution to the general question.","start":"Read the preface and the preamble first.","line":{"text":"I openly confess, the suggestion of David Hume was the very thing, which many years ago first interrupted my dogmatic slumber","where":"Preface","tr":"Carus"},"islands":[],"related":["kant-critique-pure-reason","hume-enquiry"],"shelf":"germany-enl","tags":["synthetic a priori","transcendental idealism","metaphysics","philosophy of mathematics","hume's problem","epistemology","copernican revolution","limits of reason"]},{"id":"federalist-papers","title":"The Federalist Papers","original":null,"author":null,"authorName":"Alexander Hamilton, James Madison and John Jay","year":1788,"approx":false,"form":"essays","length":"85 essays, about 500 pages","level":2,"topics":["politics"],"summary":"Written under the shared pen name 'Publius' in 1787 and 1788, these newspaper essays urged New Yorkers to ratify the new United States Constitution. They explain how a large republic could control factions, why power should be divided between branches that check one another, and why a stronger national government was needed.","why":"They are the most important commentary on the American Constitution and a classic of political theory about how to design institutions for people who are not angels.","inside":"Eighty-five numbered essays; Madison's No. 10 on factions and No. 51 on checks and balances are the most famous.","start":"Read No. 10, then No. 51.","line":{"text":"If men were angels, no government would be necessary.","where":"No. 51","tr":null},"islands":[],"related":["montesquieu-spirit-of-the-laws","locke-two-treatises","paine-common-sense"],"shelf":"rights","tags":["checks and balances","separation of powers","factions","federalism","constitution","republicanism","american founding","political philosophy","democracy"]},{"id":"equiano-interesting-narrative","title":"The Interesting Narrative of the Life of Olaudah Equiano","original":null,"author":null,"authorName":"Olaudah Equiano","year":1789,"approx":false,"form":"autobiography","length":"about 250 pages","level":1,"topics":["society","ethics","self"],"summary":"Equiano describes being kidnapped as a child from his home in what he calls Eboe (Igbo country), surviving the Middle Passage, being enslaved in the Caribbean and Virginia, serving at sea, and buying his own freedom. He goes on to describe his travels, his Christian conversion and his work against the slave trade. Some scholars have questioned his account of his birthplace, but the book's testimony about slavery is not in doubt.","why":"It was a bestseller that strengthened the British campaign to abolish the slave trade. It is a founding text of Black Atlantic writing and an argument for human equality made from experience.","inside":"Twelve chapters in two volumes, moving from his childhood in Africa through enslavement and freedom to his campaigning.","start":"Read the first five chapters, from his childhood to his life in slavery.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["douglass-narrative","wollstonecraft-vindication-woman"],"shelf":"rights","tags":["slavery","abolitionism","slave trade","middle passage","autobiography","freedom","human dignity","christianity","testimony"]},{"id":"gouges-declaration-rights-of-woman","title":"Declaration of the Rights of Woman and of the Female Citizen","original":"Déclaration des droits de la femme et de la citoyenne (French)","author":null,"authorName":"Olympe de Gouges","year":1791,"approx":false,"form":"pamphlet","length":"about 15 pages","level":1,"topics":["politics","society"],"summary":"De Gouges rewrote the French Revolution's 1789 Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen, article by article, to include women. She argued that if women could be sent to the scaffold, they should also have a voice in politics, and she proposed a new form of marriage contract between equals.","why":"It is one of the first public demands for full political equality for women. De Gouges was guillotined in 1793, and the pamphlet has become a landmark of feminist political thought.","inside":"A dedication to the Queen, a preamble, seventeen articles mirroring the 1789 Declaration, a postscript calling on women to wake up, and a model social contract between a man and a woman.","start":"Read it alongside the 1789 Declaration to see each change she made.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["wollstonecraft-vindication-woman","paine-rights-of-man"],"shelf":"rights","tags":["women's rights","feminism","french revolution","gender equality","citizenship","human rights","marriage","political philosophy"]},{"id":"godwin-political-justice","title":"An Enquiry Concerning Political Justice","original":null,"author":"godwin","authorName":"William Godwin","year":1793,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 800 pages","level":3,"topics":["politics","ethics"],"summary":"Godwin argues that we should act from impartial reason and benevolence, doing whatever most benefits humanity. On this view, private loyalties, promises and even gratitude carry no special weight, which leads to his notorious example of saving a great archbishop from a fire rather than one's own mother. He holds that government is a necessary evil that will gradually become unnecessary as people grow more rational.","why":"It is the founding text of philosophical anarchism and an early, uncompromising form of consequentialism. Godwin married Mary Wollstonecraft, and their daughter Mary Shelley wrote Frankenstein.","inside":"Eight books, covering the powers of man, principles of society, government, the operation of opinion, legislation, crime and punishment, and property.","start":"Read Book 2, chapter 2 on justice, which contains the fire example.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["wollstonecraft-vindication-woman","paine-rights-of-man","bentham-principles-morals-legislation"],"shelf":"rights","tags":["anarchism","utilitarianism","impartiality","archbishop and the chambermaid","benevolence","perfectibility","critique of government","property","political philosophy"]},{"id":"hegel-science-of-logic","title":"Science of Logic","original":"Wissenschaft der Logik (German)","author":"hegel","authorName":"G. W. F. Hegel","year":1812,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 800 pages in English","level":3,"topics":["metaphysics","logic"],"summary":"This is not a textbook of formal logic but Hegel's attempt to show how the most basic concepts of thought develop out of one another. It starts from the emptiest concept, pure being, finds that it cannot be told apart from nothing, and shows the two combining in becoming. From there it works through quality, quantity, essence, appearance and finally 'the Concept' and 'the Idea'.","why":"It is the core of Hegel's system and the fullest statement of his dialectical method, which shaped Marx and much of later Continental philosophy. It is famously difficult.","inside":"Published in parts between 1812 and 1816, in three books: the Doctrine of Being, the Doctrine of Essence and the Doctrine of the Concept.","start":"Read a good introduction first, then the opening chapter on being, nothing and becoming.","line":{"text":"Being, pure being, without any further determination.","where":"Book One, Section One, Chapter 1","tr":"Miller"},"islands":[],"related":["hegel-phenomenology","hegel-philosophy-of-right"],"shelf":"hegel-marx","tags":["dialectic","being and nothing","becoming","german idealism","absolute idea","logic","essence","the concept","metaphysics"]},{"id":"emerson-nature","title":"Nature","original":null,"author":null,"authorName":"Ralph Waldo Emerson","year":1836,"approx":false,"form":"essay","length":"about 50 pages","level":1,"topics":["religion","beauty","self"],"summary":"Emerson urges his readers to stop living through tradition and to seek 'an original relation to the universe'. He describes how nature serves us in rising ways: as a resource, as beauty, as the source of language, as a teacher, and finally as an expression of spirit. Alone in the woods, he says, a person can feel the universal being flowing through them.","why":"Published anonymously, it became the founding text of American Transcendentalism and shaped Thoreau, Whitman and the American nature-writing tradition.","inside":"An introduction and eight short chapters: Nature, Commodity, Beauty, Language, Discipline, Idealism, Spirit and Prospects.","start":"Read the introduction and the first chapter.","line":{"text":"I become a transparent eye-ball; I am nothing; I see all","where":"Chapter 1, Nature","tr":null},"islands":[],"related":["emerson-self-reliance","thoreau-walden"],"shelf":"americas","tags":["transcendentalism","nature","spirit","beauty","transparent eyeball","self-reliance","idealism","religion","romanticism"]},{"id":"hegel-philosophy-of-history","title":"Lectures on the Philosophy of History","original":"Vorlesungen über die Philosophie der Geschichte (German)","author":"hegel","authorName":"G. W. F. Hegel","year":1837,"approx":false,"form":"lectures","length":"about 450 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["politics","society"],"summary":"Compiled after Hegel's death from his lectures of the 1820s, it presents world history as the story of freedom becoming conscious of itself. Hegel claims that in the ancient East only one person (the ruler) knew himself free, in Greece and Rome some were free, and in the modern Germanic world all are known to be free. Reason, he argues, works through the passions of individuals and the rise and fall of states.","why":"It is the most accessible way into Hegel's thought and shaped Marx's and many later ideas of historical progress. It is also notorious for dismissing Africa and other peoples as outside history, a view that later critics have examined closely.","inside":"A long introduction on method and the idea of freedom, then four parts: the Oriental World, the Greek World, the Roman World and the Germanic World.","start":"Read the introduction, which stands on its own.","line":{"text":"What experience and history teach is this, — that peoples and governments never have learned anything from history","where":"Introduction","tr":"Sibree"},"islands":[],"related":["hegel-philosophy-of-right","vico-new-science","marx-theses-on-feuerbach"],"shelf":"hegel-marx","tags":["philosophy of history","freedom","world spirit","cunning of reason","progress","german idealism","the state","dialectic","history"]},{"id":"proudhon-what-is-property","title":"What Is Property?","original":"Qu'est-ce que la propriété ? (French)","author":"proudhon","authorName":"Pierre-Joseph Proudhon","year":1840,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 300 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["politics","society"],"summary":"Proudhon answers his title question with a slogan: property is theft. He means property that lets owners take income without working, such as rent and interest, rather than the things people actually use. He examines the standard defences of property, from first occupation and labour to law, and argues that each one, followed through, leads to equality instead.","why":"It is a founding text of socialism and anarchism; in it Proudhon became the first writer to call himself an anarchist. Marx admired it at first and later attacked Proudhon.","inside":"Five chapters, from method and the idea of property as a natural right, through critiques of occupation and labour, to Proudhon's own view of society.","start":"Read the first chapter, then the last.","line":{"text":"Property is theft!","where":"Chapter 1","tr":null},"islands":[],"related":["marx-capital","locke-two-treatises","stirner-ego-and-its-own"],"shelf":"hegel-marx","tags":["property is theft","anarchism","private property","equality","socialism","critique of capitalism","mutualism","political philosophy","labour"]},{"id":"kierkegaard-concept-of-anxiety","title":"The Concept of Anxiety","original":"Begrebet Angest (Danish)","author":"kierkegaard","authorName":"Søren Kierkegaard","year":1844,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 160 pages in English","level":3,"topics":["self","religion","mind"],"summary":"Written under the pen name Vigilius Haufniensis, it asks what state of mind lies behind the first sin. Kierkegaard's answer is anxiety: not fear of any particular thing, but the unsettling awareness of our own freedom and possibilities. Anxiety, he argues, can either trap us or, rightly understood, educate us towards faith.","why":"It was the first major philosophical study of anxiety and shaped Heidegger, Sartre and existential psychology.","inside":"An introduction and five chapters, moving from Adam's first sin to anxiety in later generations, to anxiety about good and evil, and finally to anxiety as something that saves through faith.","start":"Read the introduction to the idea in chapter 1, then the final chapter.","line":{"text":"anxiety is the dizziness of freedom","where":"Chapter 2","tr":"Thomte"},"islands":[],"related":["kierkegaard-sickness-unto-death","kierkegaard-fear-trembling","heidegger-being-and-time"],"shelf":"inner-life","tags":["anxiety","original sin","freedom","dizziness of freedom","existentialism","possibility","faith","christianity","psychology"]},{"id":"stirner-ego-and-its-own","title":"The Ego and Its Own","original":"Der Einzige und sein Eigentum (German)","author":"stirner","authorName":"Max Stirner","year":1844,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 350 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["self","ethics","politics"],"summary":"Stirner argues that God, humanity, morality, the state and even 'Man' are 'spooks': abstractions that people invent and then serve as if they were masters. Young Hegelians like Feuerbach got rid of God only to worship humanity instead. The individual, 'the unique one', should own himself and treat everything else as his property to use, joining others only in free 'unions of egoists'.","why":"It is the most radical statement of individualist egoism and a key source for individualist anarchism. Marx and Engels wrote a long, mocking reply in The German Ideology.","inside":"Two parts, 'Man' and 'I', framed by short opening and closing sections that both begin and end with the same declaration.","start":"Read the short opening section and the beginning of Part Two.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["marx-theses-on-feuerbach","nietzsche-genealogy","proudhon-what-is-property"],"shelf":"hegel-marx","tags":["egoism","individualism","spooks","anarchism","union of egoists","critique of morality","young hegelians","nihilism","the self","critique of humanism"]},{"id":"marx-theses-on-feuerbach","title":"Theses on Feuerbach","original":"Thesen über Feuerbach (German)","author":"marx","authorName":"Karl Marx","year":1845,"approx":false,"form":"notes","length":"about 3 pages","level":2,"topics":["society","knowledge","politics"],"summary":"Eleven short notes that Marx wrote for himself in 1845, published by Engels in 1888. They criticise Ludwig Feuerbach and earlier materialists for treating reality as something we only observe, rather than as human practical activity. Marx argues that the human essence is 'the ensemble of the social relations', and that philosophy must aim at changing the world.","why":"They are the shortest statement of the turn from philosophy to practice at the heart of Marx's thought, and the eleventh thesis is carved on his tombstone in London.","inside":"Eleven numbered theses, most only a few lines long.","start":"Read all eleven; it takes five minutes.","line":{"text":"The philosophers have only interpreted the world, in various ways; the point is to change it.","where":"Thesis XI","tr":null},"islands":[],"related":["marx-communist-manifesto","marx-capital","hegel-philosophy-of-history"],"shelf":"hegel-marx","tags":["historical materialism","praxis","philosophers have only interpreted the world","materialism","critique of feuerbach","human nature","social relations","marxism"]},{"id":"kierkegaard-sickness-unto-death","title":"The Sickness unto Death","original":"Sygdommen til Døden (Danish)","author":"kierkegaard","authorName":"Søren Kierkegaard","year":1849,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 130 pages in English","level":3,"topics":["self","religion"],"summary":"Written under the pen name Anti-Climacus, it analyses despair, which Kierkegaard sees as a sickness of the self rather than a passing mood. We despair when we refuse to be the self we are, or try desperately to be a self of our own making. The cure, he argues, is faith, in which the self rests transparently in the power that made it.","why":"Its account of the self as a relation and its typology of despair influenced Heidegger, Sartre, Camus and existential theology.","inside":"Two parts: the first on despair and its forms, the second on despair as sin.","start":"Read the opening pages of Part One, then the forms of despair that follow.","line":{"text":"The self is a relation that relates itself to itself","where":"Part One, A","tr":"Hong"},"islands":[],"related":["kierkegaard-concept-of-anxiety","kierkegaard-either-or","kierkegaard-fear-trembling"],"shelf":"inner-life","tags":["despair","the self","existentialism","faith","sin","christianity","authenticity","selfhood"]},{"id":"douglass-what-to-the-slave-is-the-fourth-of-july","title":"What to the Slave Is the Fourth of July?","original":null,"author":null,"authorName":"Frederick Douglass","year":1852,"approx":false,"form":"speech","length":"about 25 pages","level":1,"topics":["politics","ethics","society"],"summary":"Invited to speak in Rochester, New York, on 5 July 1852, Douglass praised the founders of the United States and then turned on his audience: their celebration of liberty, he said, is a sham while millions are enslaved. He argues that the wrongness of slavery needs no argument, and that what is needed is scorching irony and moral outrage. He ends with hope, reading the Constitution as an anti-slavery document.","why":"It is one of the greatest speeches in American history and a classic of political moral argument, showing how a nation can be held to its own declared principles.","inside":"A single speech, moving from praise of the Revolution, to a fierce indictment of slavery and the hypocrisy of churches and laws, to a hopeful close.","start":"Read the whole speech; it is meant to be heard and works well read aloud.","line":{"text":"What, to the American slave, is your 4th of July?","where":"Middle of the speech","tr":null},"islands":[],"related":["douglass-narrative","thoreau-civil-disobedience","paine-common-sense"],"shelf":"americas","tags":["slavery","abolitionism","hypocrisy","liberty","american founding","moral outrage","constitution","racism","rhetoric","justice"]},{"id":"thoreau-walden","title":"Walden","original":null,"author":null,"authorName":"Henry David Thoreau","year":1854,"approx":false,"form":"memoir","length":"about 300 pages","level":1,"topics":["self","life and death","society"],"summary":"Thoreau describes the two years he spent living in a cabin he built beside Walden Pond in Massachusetts, compressed into a single year of seasons. He argues that most people work themselves to exhaustion for things they do not need, and that a simple, deliberate life leaves time for thought, nature and freedom. It mixes economics, natural observation and philosophy.","why":"It is a classic of American literature and of the philosophy of simple living, and it has inspired environmentalists and people rethinking work and consumption ever since.","inside":"Eighteen chapters, beginning with the long 'Economy' and moving through the seasons to 'Spring' and a 'Conclusion'.","start":"Read 'Economy' (or at least its opening) and 'Where I Lived, and What I Lived For'.","line":{"text":"The mass of men lead lives of quiet desperation.","where":"Economy","tr":null},"islands":[],"related":["emerson-nature","thoreau-civil-disobedience","seneca-on-the-shortness-of-life"],"shelf":"americas","tags":["simple living","self-reliance","nature","transcendentalism","living deliberately","solitude","consumerism","freedom","minimalism","economy"]},{"id":"dostoevsky-notes-from-underground","title":"Notes from Underground","original":"Zapiski iz podpolya (Russian)","author":null,"authorName":"Fyodor Dostoevsky","year":1864,"approx":false,"form":"novella","length":"about 130 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["self","ethics","mind"],"summary":"A bitter, retired civil servant in St Petersburg addresses his imaginary readers from his 'underground'. He attacks the idea that people always act in their own rational interest, insisting that humans will act against their own good just to prove they are free and not piano keys. The second part shows how this philosophy plays out in his humiliating dealings with old schoolfellows and a young woman, Liza.","why":"It is often called the first existentialist novel, and it is a sharp critique of rational egoism and utopian planning. Nietzsche, Sartre and Camus all saw it as a forerunner of their concerns.","inside":"Two parts: 'Underground', a philosophical monologue, and 'Apropos of the Wet Snow', a story from his younger years.","start":"Read Part One first, then Part Two to see the ideas in action.","line":{"text":"I am a sick man.... I am a spiteful man.","where":"Opening line","tr":"Garnett"},"islands":[],"related":["nietzsche-genealogy","camus-myth-of-sisyphus","sartre-existentialism-is-a-humanism"],"shelf":"inner-life","tags":["existentialism","free will","critique of rational egoism","spite","the underground man","alienation","consciousness","human nature","irrationality","piano keys"]},{"id":"nietzsche-birth-of-tragedy","title":"The Birth of Tragedy","original":"Die Geburt der Tragödie (German)","author":"nietzsche","authorName":"Friedrich Nietzsche","year":1872,"approx":false,"form":"book","length":"about 150 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["beauty","life and death"],"summary":"Nietzsche's first book argues that Greek tragedy arose from the meeting of two forces: the Apollonian, the art of clear form, images and dreams, and the Dionysian, the art of music, intoxication and the dissolving of the individual. Tragedy let the Greeks face the horror of existence and still affirm it. He claims that Socrates' faith in reason killed tragedy, and hopes Wagner's music will bring it back.","why":"It changed how people read Greek culture and introduced the Apollonian/Dionysian contrast into the wider culture. It also contains the seeds of Nietzsche's later idea that life must be affirmed, not explained away.","inside":"Twenty-five sections; the 1886 edition added a sharp 'Attempt at a Self-Criticism' as a preface.","start":"Read sections 1 to 5 and 'Attempt at a Self-Criticism'.","line":{"text":"it is only as an aesthetic phenomenon that existence and the world are eternally justified","where":"Section 5","tr":"Kaufmann"},"islands":[],"related":["aristotle-poetics","schopenhauer-world-as-will","nietzsche-twilight-of-the-idols"],"shelf":"inner-life","tags":["apollonian and dionysian","tragedy","aesthetics","music","greek tragedy","pessimism","art","critique of socrates","affirmation of life","wagner"]},{"id":"mill-autobiography","title":"Autobiography","original":null,"author":"mill","authorName":"John Stuart Mill","year":1873,"approx":false,"form":"autobiography","length":"about 200 pages","level":1,"topics":["self","ethics"],"summary":"Mill describes his extraordinary education by his father, James Mill, who taught him Greek at three, and how he was raised to be a reformer for Bentham's utilitarianism. At twenty he fell into a depression in which his goals lost all meaning, and he found his way out partly through Wordsworth's poetry. He also describes his partnership with Harriet Taylor, whom he credits with much of his later work.","why":"It is one of the great intellectual autobiographies and a vivid case study of what happens when a philosophy of happiness meets real unhappiness. Its account of his crisis led Mill to a richer utilitarianism.","inside":"Seven chapters, published shortly after his death; chapter 5, on his mental crisis, is the most famous.","start":"Read chapters 1 and 5.","line":{"text":"Ask yourself whether you are happy, and you cease to be so.","where":"Chapter 5","tr":null},"islands":[],"related":["mill-utilitarianism","mill-on-liberty","mill-subjection-women"],"shelf":"britain","tags":["autobiography","education","utilitarianism","depression","mental crisis","poetry","happiness","feminism","meaning of life"]},{"id":"peirce-how-to-make-our-ideas-clear","title":"How to Make Our Ideas Clear","original":null,"author":"peirce","authorName":"Charles Sanders Peirce","year":1878,"approx":false,"form":"paper","length":"about 20 pages","level":2,"topics":["knowledge","language","science"],"summary":"Peirce argues that the meaning of an idea lies in its practical consequences: to understand what 'hard' or 'heavy' means, ask what effects we would expect from something that is hard or heavy. Ideas that make no possible difference to experience are empty. He applies this test to the ideas of force and reality, defining the real as what inquiry would eventually agree on.","why":"This is the paper that stated the 'pragmatic maxim', the founding principle of American pragmatism, later popularised by William James.","inside":"Four sections, published in Popular Science Monthly as the second of Peirce's 'Illustrations of the Logic of Science'.","start":"Read section 2, which states the maxim.","line":{"text":"Consider what effects, that might conceivably have practical bearings, we conceive the object of our conception to have. Then, our conception of these effects is the whole of our conception of the object.","where":"Section 2","tr":null},"islands":[],"related":["peirce-fixation-belief","james-pragmatism"],"shelf":"pragmatism","tags":["pragmatism","pragmatic maxim","meaning","clarity","practical consequences","truth","reality","inquiry","philosophy of science"]},{"id":"frege-begriffsschrift","title":"Begriffsschrift (Concept Script)","original":"Begriffsschrift (German)","author":"frege","authorName":"Gottlob Frege","year":1879,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 90 pages","level":3,"topics":["logic","language"],"summary":"Frege invents a 'concept script', a formal language for pure thought modelled on arithmetic, so that proofs can be written out with no gaps. In doing so he introduces quantifiers ('for all', 'there is') and the analysis of sentences into functions and arguments rather than subjects and predicates. With this he could represent reasoning that Aristotle's logic could not handle.","why":"It is often called the most important single work in the history of logic since Aristotle, founding modern predicate logic and, through it, analytic philosophy and computer science.","inside":"A preface and three parts: the notation, the representation and derivation of some judgements, and an application to the theory of sequences. It uses an unusual two-dimensional notation.","start":"Read the preface, then a modern introduction to how quantifiers work.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["frege-foundations-arithmetic","frege-sense-reference","russell-on-denoting"],"shelf":"logic","tags":["formal logic","predicate logic","quantifiers","function and argument","logicism","philosophy of mathematics","analytic philosophy","logic","concept script"]},{"id":"tolstoy-death-of-ivan-ilyich","title":"The Death of Ivan Ilyich","original":"Smert Ivana Ilyicha (Russian)","author":"tolstoy","authorName":"Leo Tolstoy","year":1886,"approx":false,"form":"novella","length":"about 70 pages in English","level":1,"topics":["life and death","self"],"summary":"Ivan Ilyich, a successful judge who has lived a correct, comfortable and conventional life, is struck by a fatal illness after a minor fall. As he dies, he realises that everyone around him is pretending, and slowly faces the terrible question of whether his whole life was lived wrongly. Only the honest care of his servant Gerasim comforts him.","why":"It is perhaps the most powerful short work ever written about facing death, and philosophers from Heidegger onward have used it to explore what it means to grasp one's own mortality.","inside":"Twelve short chapters, opening with Ivan's funeral and then going back to tell his life and his dying.","start":"Read it straight through.","line":{"text":"Ivan Ilych's life had been most simple and most ordinary and therefore most terrible.","where":"Chapter 2","tr":"Maude"},"islands":[],"related":["heidegger-being-and-time","seneca-on-the-shortness-of-life","plato-phaedo"],"shelf":"inner-life","tags":["death","mortality","meaning of life","authenticity","conventional life","suffering","compassion","dying","self-deception"]},{"id":"nakae-three-drunkards-on-government","title":"A Discourse by Three Drunkards on Government","original":"Sansuijin keirin mondō (Japanese)","author":null,"authorName":"Nakae Chōmin","year":1887,"approx":false,"form":"dialogue","length":"about 140 pages in English","level":1,"topics":["politics"],"summary":"Three men drink and argue about Japan's future in a rapidly modernising world. The Gentleman of Western Learning calls for full democracy and disarmament; the Champion of the East urges military expansion; their host, Master Nankai, offers a cautious middle path. Nakae leaves it to the reader to weigh the views.","why":"Nakae, who translated Rousseau's Social Contract and was called 'the Rousseau of the East', was a leading thinker of Japan's Freedom and People's Rights Movement. The dialogue is a classic of modern Japanese political thought.","inside":"A single dialogue in three voices over one evening, ending with the guests' departures.","start":"Read it straight through; it is witty and fast-moving.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["rousseau-social-contract","fukuzawa-encouragement-learning"],"shelf":"asia-modern","tags":["democracy","pacifism","militarism","modernisation","japanese philosophy","liberalism","political philosophy","dialogue","imperialism"]},{"id":"bergson-time-and-free-will","title":"Time and Free Will","original":"Essai sur les données immédiates de la conscience (French)","author":null,"authorName":"Henri Bergson","year":1889,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 250 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["mind","metaphysics","self"],"summary":"Bergson's doctoral thesis argues that we wrongly think about inner life using ideas taken from space: measuring, counting and setting things side by side. Lived time, which he calls duration, is a flowing whole in which each moment colours the next. Once we see this, he argues, the debate over determinism dissolves, and free acts are those that spring from the whole self.","why":"It introduced Bergson's key idea of duration, which made him one of the most famous philosophers in the world and influenced William James, Proust and phenomenology.","inside":"Three chapters: on the intensity of mental states, on the multiplicity of mental states and the idea of duration, and on free will.","start":"Read chapter 2, on duration.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["bergson-creative-evolution","james-principles-psychology"],"shelf":"inner-life","tags":["duration","time","free will","determinism","consciousness","the self","process philosophy","inner life","intuition"]},{"id":"nietzsche-twilight-of-the-idols","title":"Twilight of the Idols","original":"Götzen-Dämmerung (German)","author":"nietzsche","authorName":"Friedrich Nietzsche","year":1889,"approx":false,"form":"book","length":"about 100 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["ethics","metaphysics","religion"],"summary":"Subtitled 'How to Philosophize with a Hammer', it is a fast summary of Nietzsche's late views, written in 1888 just before his collapse. He attacks Socrates, the idea of a 'true world' behind appearances, Christian morality and modern German culture, and diagnoses many moral ideas as symptoms of declining life.","why":"It is often recommended as the best short introduction to the mature Nietzsche, and its brief history of how the 'true world' became a fable is a classic.","inside":"A preface and short chapters including 'Maxims and Arrows', 'The Problem of Socrates', ''Reason' in Philosophy', 'How the 'True World' Finally Became a Fable' and 'Morality as Anti-Nature'.","start":"Read 'Maxims and Arrows', then 'How the True World Finally Became a Fable'.","line":{"text":"What does not destroy me, makes me stronger.","where":"Maxims and Arrows, 8","tr":"Kaufmann"},"islands":[],"related":["nietzsche-genealogy","nietzsche-beyond-good-evil","nietzsche-birth-of-tragedy"],"shelf":"inner-life","tags":["philosophizing with a hammer","critique of socrates","true world","critique of christianity","morality as anti-nature","decadence","nihilism","aphorisms","appearance and reality"]},{"id":"marti-our-america","title":"Our America","original":"Nuestra América (Spanish)","author":null,"authorName":"José Martí","year":1891,"approx":false,"form":"essay","length":"about 12 pages","level":1,"topics":["politics","society"],"summary":"Martí, the Cuban poet and independence leader, calls on the nations of Latin America to know and govern themselves according to their own realities, including their Indigenous, African and mixed peoples, rather than copying European or North American models. He warns of the growing power of the United States and denounces race hatred.","why":"It is one of the founding texts of Latin American political thought and of the idea of a shared 'Nuestra América', and it is still widely taught across the Americas.","inside":"A single short essay written in a dense, poetic style.","start":"Read it straight through, slowly.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["du-bois-souls-of-black-folk","fanon-wretched-of-the-earth"],"shelf":"americas","tags":["latin american philosophy","anti-imperialism","identity","independence","anti-racism","self-determination","colonialism","political philosophy"]},{"id":"cooper-voice-from-the-south","title":"A Voice from the South","original":null,"author":null,"authorName":"Anna Julia Cooper","year":1892,"approx":false,"form":"essays","length":"about 300 pages","level":1,"topics":["society","politics","self"],"summary":"Subtitled 'By a Black Woman of the South', it argues that Black women's education and leadership are essential to the progress of their race and of the nation. Cooper criticises both white feminists who ignored race and Black men who ignored women, and she writes on education, literature and American culture.","why":"It is a founding text of Black feminist thought, decades ahead of its time in seeing how race and gender combine. Cooper went on to earn a doctorate at the Sorbonne in her sixties.","inside":"Two parts of essays; the first, on women, includes 'Womanhood a Vital Element in the Regeneration and Progress of a Race', the second, on race and American culture.","start":"Read 'Womanhood a Vital Element in the Regeneration and Progress of a Race'.","line":{"text":"Only the BLACK WOMAN can say \"when and where I enter, in the quiet, undisputed dignity of my womanhood, without violence and without suing or special patronage, then and there the whole Negro race enters with me.\"","where":"Womanhood a Vital Element in the Regeneration and Progress of a Race","tr":null},"islands":[],"related":["du-bois-souls-of-black-folk","douglass-narrative","davis-women-race-and-class"],"shelf":"americas","tags":["black feminism","intersectionality","race","feminism","education of women","racism","african american experience","women"]},{"id":"du-bois-conservation-of-races","title":"The Conservation of Races","original":null,"author":"du_bois","authorName":"W. E. B. Du Bois","year":1897,"approx":false,"form":"essay","length":"about 15 pages","level":1,"topics":["society","self"],"summary":"Delivered to the newly founded American Negro Academy, the essay asks what a race is and argues that races are best understood by shared history, traditions and ideals rather than by physical differences alone. Du Bois urges African Americans to preserve their distinct identity and cultural gifts rather than dissolve into white America, while remaining loyal Americans.","why":"It is a classic early attempt to define race philosophically, and Kwame Anthony Appiah's critique of it started a major modern debate about whether races are real.","inside":"A short essay followed by an 'Academy Creed' of numbered principles.","start":"Read it straight through, then Appiah's In My Father's House for the critique.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["du-bois-souls-of-black-folk","appiah-in-my-fathers-house"],"shelf":"society-20","tags":["philosophy of race","race","racial identity","pan-africanism","african american experience","culture","double consciousness","identity"]},{"id":"james-varieties-of-religious-experience","title":"The Varieties of Religious Experience","original":null,"author":"james","authorName":"William James","year":1902,"approx":false,"form":"lectures","length":"about 500 pages","level":2,"topics":["religion","mind"],"summary":"Given as the Gifford Lectures in Edinburgh, it studies religion through first-hand experience rather than doctrine or churches. James collects vivid accounts of conversion, saintliness and mysticism, and contrasts 'healthy-minded' religion with that of the 'sick soul' who has faced despair. He judges religious experience by its fruits in people's lives, staying open to the possibility that it puts us in touch with something real.","why":"It founded the psychology of religion and is still one of the most readable and fair-minded books ever written about religious experience, whether or not one is religious.","inside":"Twenty lectures, including two on healthy-mindedness, two on the sick soul, one on the divided self, two on conversion, five on saintliness and its value, and two on mysticism.","start":"Read Lecture 2 for his definition of religion, then the lectures on the sick soul and on mysticism.","line":{"text":"the feelings, acts, and experiences of individual men in their solitude, so far as they apprehend themselves to stand in relation to whatever they may consider the divine","where":"Lecture 2","tr":null},"islands":[],"related":["james-will-to-believe","james-pragmatism","hume-dialogues"],"shelf":"pragmatism","tags":["religious experience","mysticism","conversion","the sick soul","healthy-mindedness","psychology of religion","pragmatism","saintliness","divided self","philosophy of religion"]},{"id":"kropotkin-mutual-aid","title":"Mutual Aid: A Factor of Evolution","original":null,"author":"kropotkin","authorName":"Peter Kropotkin","year":1902,"approx":false,"form":"book","length":"about 300 pages","level":1,"topics":["science","society","politics"],"summary":"Kropotkin, a Russian geographer and anarchist, answers those who read Darwin as showing that life is a ruthless struggle of each against all. Drawing on his own observations of animals in Siberia and on history, he argues that cooperation within species is just as important a factor in survival. He traces mutual aid from animal societies through tribal life and medieval cities to modern trade unions and neighbourly help.","why":"It is an important early challenge to 'social Darwinism', a founding text of anarchist thought, and a forerunner of later scientific work on the evolution of cooperation.","inside":"Eight chapters, first published as magazine articles in the 1890s: two on animals, then early human societies, medieval cities, and the present day, with appendices.","start":"Read the introduction and the first chapter on mutual aid among animals.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["darwin-origin-of-species","proudhon-what-is-property","midgley-beast-and-man"],"shelf":"hegel-marx","tags":["mutual aid","cooperation","evolution","anarchism","critique of social darwinism","altruism","solidarity","philosophy of biology","community"]},{"id":"okakura-book-of-tea","title":"The Book of Tea","original":null,"author":"okakura","authorName":"Okakura Kakuzō","year":1906,"approx":false,"form":"essay","length":"about 100 pages","level":1,"topics":["beauty","society"],"summary":"Written in English for Western readers, it uses the Japanese tea ceremony to explain an Asian way of life and art. Okakura connects tea with Daoism and Zen, and with an aesthetic that honours simplicity, imperfection and the beauty of everyday things. It is also a gentle criticism of Western assumptions of superiority.","why":"It became one of the most widely read introductions to East Asian aesthetics in the West, and it remains a short classic on beauty, humility and attention.","inside":"Seven short chapters: The Cup of Humanity, The Schools of Tea, Taoism and Zennism, The Tea-Room, Art Appreciation, Flowers, and Tea-Masters, which ends with the death of the tea master Rikyū.","start":"Read the first chapter and 'Taoism and Zennism'.","line":{"text":"Teaism is a cult founded on the adoration of the beautiful among the sordid facts of everyday existence.","where":"Chapter 1, The Cup of Humanity","tr":null},"islands":[],"related":["kenko-essays-in-idleness","laozi-daodejing","kuki-structure-of-iki"],"shelf":"asia-modern","tags":["tea ceremony","aesthetics","zen","taoism","simplicity","beauty of imperfection","japanese philosophy","art","wabi-sabi"]},{"id":"gandhi-hind-swaraj","title":"Hind Swaraj","original":"Hind Swaraj (Gujarati)","author":null,"authorName":"Mohandas K. Gandhi","year":1909,"approx":false,"form":"dialogue","length":"about 100 pages in English","level":1,"topics":["politics","ethics"],"summary":"Written in Gujarati in ten days on a ship from London to South Africa, it is a dialogue between an impatient young 'Reader' and an 'Editor' who speaks for Gandhi. Gandhi argues that true self-rule (swaraj) means ruling oneself, not just replacing British rulers with Indian ones. He condemns modern industrial civilisation, and defends non-violent 'soul-force' or passive resistance against violence.","why":"It is the clearest statement of the ideas behind Gandhi's later campaigns for Indian independence, and it shaped non-violent movements around the world.","inside":"Twenty short chapters of question and answer, with an appendix listing books by Tolstoy, Thoreau, Ruskin and others that influenced him. Gandhi made his own English translation in 1910.","start":"Read the chapters on 'What Is Swaraj?', on civilisation, and on passive resistance.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["thoreau-civil-disobedience","ambedkar-annihilation-of-caste","tagore-nationalism"],"shelf":"asia-20","tags":["non-violence","swaraj","self-rule","satyagraha","passive resistance","critique of modern civilisation","anti-colonialism","indian independence","political philosophy"]},{"id":"tagore-nationalism","title":"Nationalism","original":null,"author":null,"authorName":"Rabindranath Tagore","year":1917,"approx":false,"form":"lectures","length":"about 100 pages","level":1,"topics":["politics","society"],"summary":"In lectures given in Japan and the United States during the First World War, the Nobel Prize-winning poet warns against the modern 'Nation', which he sees as a machine for organised power and profit that crushes the human and moral life of peoples. He criticises Western imperialism, Japan's imitation of it, and Indian nationalists who would copy it too, and he hopes for a cooperative world of many cultures.","why":"It is a powerful early critique of nationalism from the colonised world, and its debate with Gandhi and others about how India should resist empire is still discussed.","inside":"Three essays: 'Nationalism in the West', 'Nationalism in Japan' and 'Nationalism in India', followed by a poem, 'The Sunset of the Century'.","start":"Read 'Nationalism in the West'.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["gandhi-hind-swaraj","ambedkar-annihilation-of-caste"],"shelf":"asia-20","tags":["critique of nationalism","nationalism","imperialism","cosmopolitanism","anti-colonialism","modernity","humanism","political philosophy"]},{"id":"du-bois-darkwater","title":"Darkwater: Voices from Within the Veil","original":null,"author":"du_bois","authorName":"W. E. B. Du Bois","year":1920,"approx":false,"form":"essays","length":"about 250 pages","level":1,"topics":["society","politics"],"summary":"Written after the First World War and the racial violence that followed it, Darkwater mixes essays, poems and stories. Du Bois links racism in America to European colonialism in Africa and argues that the war grew out of imperial competition for colonies. 'The Damnation of Women' argues for women's economic independence and pays tribute to Black women.","why":"It is more radical and global than The Souls of Black Folk and is one of the first works to analyse 'whiteness' itself as a political idea, in 'The Souls of White Folk'.","inside":"Ten chapters, each followed by a poem or short piece, including Du Bois's 'Credo'.","start":"Read 'The Souls of White Folk' and 'The Damnation of Women'.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["du-bois-souls-of-black-folk","du-bois-conservation-of-races","cooper-voice-from-the-south"],"shelf":"society-20","tags":["race","racism","colonialism","imperialism","feminism","the veil","pan-africanism","african american experience","world war i"]},{"id":"liang-shuming-eastern-western-cultures","title":"Eastern and Western Cultures and Their Philosophies","original":"Dong-Xi wenhua ji qi zhexue (Chinese)","author":null,"authorName":"Liang Shuming","year":1921,"approx":false,"form":"lectures","length":"about 250 pages","level":2,"topics":["society","ethics"],"summary":"At a time when many Chinese intellectuals were rejecting Confucianism, Liang compared three cultures by their basic attitude to life. The West strives forward to satisfy desires and master nature; China seeks harmony and balance; India turns back from desire altogether. He argued that after the West's material success, the world would turn to a renewed Confucian way of life.","why":"It was a bestseller that started a major debate about East and West and made Liang a founder of modern New Confucianism. His biographer called him 'the last Confucian'.","inside":"Based on lectures; it surveys the three cultures, then their philosophies, then the future of world culture.","start":"Read a short introduction to the May Fourth era first, then the chapters comparing the three cultural paths.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["confucius-analects","wangyangming-instructions","tagore-nationalism"],"shelf":"asia-20","tags":["comparative philosophy","confucianism","new confucianism","chinese philosophy","culture","east and west","buddhism","modernity","harmony"]},{"id":"buber-i-and-thou","title":"I and Thou","original":"Ich und Du (German)","author":null,"authorName":"Martin Buber","year":1923,"approx":false,"form":"book","length":"about 130 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["religion","self","ethics"],"summary":"Buber distinguishes two basic ways of being in the world. In the 'I-It' attitude we experience and use things and people as objects; in the 'I-Thou' relation we meet another being wholly, as a partner rather than a thing. Modern life is dominated by I-It, but every true I-Thou meeting gives a glimpse of the 'eternal Thou', God.","why":"It is one of the most influential books of twentieth-century religious thought, and its picture of genuine encounter shaped theology, education, psychotherapy and the ethics of dialogue.","inside":"Three parts written in short, poetic numbered passages: the two basic words, the world of It in history and culture, and the eternal Thou.","start":"Read Part One slowly.","line":{"text":"All real living is meeting.","where":"Part One","tr":"Smith"},"islands":[],"related":["kierkegaard-fear-trembling","noddings-caring"],"shelf":"phenomenology","tags":["i-thou","i-it","dialogue","relationships","encounter","jewish philosophy","existentialism","god","the other","philosophy of religion"]},{"id":"moore-defence-of-common-sense","title":"A Defence of Common Sense","original":null,"author":"moore","authorName":"G. E. Moore","year":1925,"approx":false,"form":"paper","length":"about 30 pages","level":2,"topics":["knowledge"],"summary":"Moore lists ordinary truths he says he knows with certainty: that he has a body, that it has been near the earth's surface, that the earth existed long before he was born, and that other people know similar things. He argues that philosophers who deny such things, for example by saying time or matter is unreal, contradict their own beliefs. The real task of philosophy is to analyse these truths, not to doubt them.","why":"It helped turn British philosophy away from idealism and towards analysis of ordinary claims, and it provoked Wittgenstein's last work, On Certainty.","inside":"Numbered sections; the first and longest sets out the list of truisms and the argument about them.","start":"Read the first section.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["reid-inquiry-into-the-human-mind","wittgenstein-on-certainty","descartes-meditations"],"shelf":"cambridge-oxford","tags":["common sense","skepticism","certainty","external world","epistemology","analytic philosophy","truisms","philosophical analysis"]},{"id":"russell-why-i-am-not-a-christian","title":"Why I Am Not a Christian","original":null,"author":"russell","authorName":"Bertrand Russell","year":1927,"approx":false,"form":"lecture","length":"about 20 pages","level":1,"topics":["religion"],"summary":"In a lecture to the National Secular Society in London, Russell explains why he does not believe in God or in Christ as the best and wisest of men. He goes through the classic arguments for God, including the first cause, design and morality, and finds each one wanting. He argues that religion rests mainly on fear, and that we should face the world with courage and knowledge instead.","why":"It is one of the most famous and readable statements of twentieth-century secular humanism, and it is a good way to see the traditional arguments for God set out and questioned in plain language.","inside":"A single lecture: what a Christian is, the arguments for God, the character of Christ, the emotional basis of religion, and what we must do.","start":"Read it straight through.","line":{"text":"Religion is based, I think, primarily and mainly upon fear.","where":"Fear, the Foundation of Religion","tr":null},"islands":[],"related":["hume-dialogues","clifford-ethics-belief","russell-problems-of-philosophy"],"shelf":"cambridge-oxford","tags":["atheism","existence of god","critique of religion","cosmological argument","argument from design","moral argument","fear","secularism","philosophy of religion"]},{"id":"wittgenstein-lecture-on-ethics","title":"A Lecture on Ethics","original":null,"author":"wittgenstein","authorName":"Ludwig Wittgenstein","year":1929,"approx":true,"form":"lecture","length":"about 12 pages","level":2,"topics":["ethics","language","religion"],"summary":"The only popular lecture Wittgenstein ever gave, to a Cambridge society. He distinguishes 'relative' value, such as a good chair for sitting, from 'absolute' value, and argues that sentences trying to express absolute value, such as wonder at the existence of the world or feeling absolutely safe, are strictly nonsense. Yet he ends by expressing deep respect for this human urge to run against the limits of language.","why":"It is the clearest short window into the ethical and religious side of the Tractatus, and it is far easier to read than his major works. It was published only in 1965.","inside":"A single short lecture built around three personal examples of experiences of absolute value.","start":"Read it straight through before trying the Tractatus.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["wittgenstein-tractatus","ayer-language-truth-and-logic"],"shelf":"cambridge-oxford","tags":["ethics","absolute value","limits of language","nonsense","wonder at existence","the mystical","philosophy of language","metaethics"]},{"id":"heidegger-what-is-metaphysics","title":"What Is Metaphysics?","original":"Was ist Metaphysik? (German)","author":"heidegger","authorName":"Martin Heidegger","year":1929,"approx":false,"form":"lecture","length":"about 20 pages in English","level":3,"topics":["metaphysics"],"summary":"In his inaugural lecture at Freiburg, Heidegger asks about something science ignores: the nothing. He argues that in the mood of anxiety, beings as a whole slip away from us, and we face the nothing, which reveals that there is something rather than nothing at all. Metaphysics, he says, is this going beyond beings, and it belongs to human existence itself.","why":"It is the shortest way into Heidegger's thought. Rudolf Carnap attacked its talk of 'the nothing' as a prime example of meaningless metaphysics, making it a famous flashpoint between analytic and Continental philosophy.","inside":"A short lecture in three steps: unfolding a question, working it out, and answering it. Heidegger later added a postscript and an introduction.","start":"Read the lecture itself before the later additions.","line":{"text":"Why are there beings at all, and why not rather nothing?","where":"Final sentence","tr":"Krell"},"islands":[],"related":["heidegger-being-and-time","ayer-language-truth-and-logic","leibniz-monadology"],"shelf":"phenomenology","tags":["nothingness","anxiety","being","why is there something rather than nothing","metaphysics","existentialism","phenomenology","the nothing"]},{"id":"woolf-room-of-ones-own","title":"A Room of One's Own","original":null,"author":null,"authorName":"Virginia Woolf","year":1929,"approx":false,"form":"essay","length":"about 120 pages","level":1,"topics":["society","beauty","self"],"summary":"Based on lectures given at two women's colleges in Cambridge, it asks why there have been so few great women writers. Woolf's answer is material: to write, one needs money and privacy, which women have been denied. She invents Shakespeare's equally gifted sister, Judith, to show how such talent would have been crushed.","why":"It is one of the founding texts of modern feminist thought, and its argument that creativity depends on material conditions is still widely cited.","inside":"Six chapters in which a fictional narrator wanders through Oxbridge, the British Museum and her own bookshelves.","start":"Read chapters 1 and 3, which contains Judith Shakespeare.","line":{"text":"a woman must have money and a room of her own if she is to write fiction","where":"Chapter 1","tr":null},"islands":[],"related":["wollstonecraft-vindication-woman","beauvoir-second-sex","mill-subjection-women"],"shelf":"society-20","tags":["feminism","women writers","judith shakespeare","creativity","money and independence","women","literature","gender equality","privacy"]},{"id":"kuki-structure-of-iki","title":"The Structure of Iki","original":"Iki no kōzō (Japanese)","author":null,"authorName":"Kuki Shūzō","year":1930,"approx":false,"form":"essay","length":"about 100 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["beauty","society"],"summary":"Kuki analyses iki, a Japanese ideal of refined, urbane stylishness that grew up in the pleasure quarters of Edo (old Tokyo). He finds three elements in it: allure towards another person, a proud and spirited composure, and a Buddhist-tinged resignation. He then shows iki in colours, patterns, gestures and architecture.","why":"Kuki studied in Europe with Rickert and Heidegger, and this book uses European phenomenology to describe a distinctly Japanese aesthetic. It is a classic of Japanese philosophy and of the philosophy of taste.","inside":"Six short chapters: an introduction on method, the internal structure of iki, its relation to nearby ideas, its natural and artistic expressions, and a conclusion.","start":"Read the chapter on the internal structure of iki.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["okakura-book-of-tea","heidegger-being-and-time","kenko-essays-in-idleness"],"shelf":"asia-20","tags":["iki","aesthetics","japanese philosophy","style","phenomenology","resignation","allure","culture"]},{"id":"ross-right-and-the-good","title":"The Right and the Good","original":null,"author":"ross","authorName":"W. D. Ross","year":1930,"approx":false,"form":"book","length":"about 180 pages","level":2,"topics":["ethics"],"summary":"Ross argues that we have several basic moral duties, including keeping promises, making up for wrongs, gratitude, justice, helping others, improving ourselves, and not harming others. Each is a 'prima facie' duty: binding unless outweighed by a stronger duty in the case at hand. There is no single master rule, and we must use judgement when duties conflict.","why":"It is the classic statement of moral pluralism and intuitionism, and Ross's list of prima facie duties is still widely used in practical and medical ethics.","inside":"Chapter 2, 'What Makes Right Acts Right?', sets out the list of duties.","start":"Read chapter 2.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["moore-principia-ethica","mill-utilitarianism","kant-groundwork"],"shelf":"cambridge-oxford","tags":["prima facie duties","moral pluralism","intuitionism","deontology","moral judgement","promise-keeping","conflicting duties","ethics"]},{"id":"iqbal-reconstruction-of-religious-thought","title":"The Reconstruction of Religious Thought in Islam","original":null,"author":null,"authorName":"Muhammad Iqbal","year":1930,"approx":false,"form":"lectures","length":"about 200 pages","level":3,"topics":["religion","self"],"summary":"The poet-philosopher of South Asia argues that Islam must be rethought in dialogue with modern philosophy and science. Drawing on the Qur'an, Sufism, Bergson and others, he presents reality as dynamic and creative, and the human self (khudi) as free and growing. He calls for renewed independent reasoning (ijtihad) in Islamic law.","why":"It is one of the most important works of modern Islamic philosophy, and Iqbal is regarded as the spiritual father of Pakistan.","inside":"Six lectures delivered in India in 1928 and 1929, published in Lahore in 1930; a seventh, 'Is Religion Possible?', was added in 1934.","start":"Read the first lecture, on knowledge and religious experience, and the fourth, on the human ego.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["alghazali-deliverance","bergson-creative-evolution","averroes-decisive-treatise"],"shelf":"asia-20","tags":["islamic philosophy","khudi","the self","ijtihad","modernism","faith and reason","sufism","process philosophy","religious reform"]},{"id":"husserl-cartesian-meditations","title":"Cartesian Meditations","original":"Méditations cartésiennes (French)","author":"husserl","authorName":"Edmund Husserl","year":1931,"approx":false,"form":"lectures","length":"about 160 pages in English","level":3,"topics":["mind","knowledge"],"summary":"Developed from lectures given at the Sorbonne in 1929, it presents phenomenology as a radical renewal of Descartes's project of starting philosophy from the thinking self. Husserl explains how 'bracketing' our belief in the world lets us study consciousness and how it is always consciousness of something. In the Fifth Meditation he tackles how I can know other minds at all.","why":"It is the most compact introduction to Husserl's mature phenomenology, and its account of other people shaped Sartre, Merleau-Ponty and Levinas, who co-translated it into French.","inside":"Five meditations; the long Fifth Meditation, on intersubjectivity, is the most discussed. It first appeared in French; the German text came out in 1950.","start":"Read the First Meditation, then the Fifth.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["descartes-meditations","husserl-ideas","merleau-ponty-phenomenology-of-perception"],"shelf":"phenomenology","tags":["phenomenology","intersubjectivity","other minds","epoche","transcendental ego","intentionality","consciousness","cartesianism"]},{"id":"weil-iliad-poem-of-force","title":"The Iliad, or the Poem of Force","original":"L'Iliade ou le poème de la force (French)","author":null,"authorName":"Simone Weil","year":1940,"approx":false,"form":"essay","length":"about 35 pages in English","level":1,"topics":["ethics","life and death","beauty"],"summary":"Written as France fell to Germany, Weil's essay reads Homer's Iliad as a poem about force: the power that turns a human being into a thing, literally a corpse or, while still alive, a slave or a suppliant. Force crushes victims and intoxicates those who wield it, until they in turn are crushed. The Iliad's greatness, she argues, lies in its equal bitterness for both sides.","why":"It is one of the most admired essays of the twentieth century on war and violence, and an accessible way into Weil's moral thought.","inside":"A single essay, first published under a pen name in a Marseille journal, moving through scenes from the Iliad and ending with comparisons to the Gospels and Greek tragedy.","start":"Read it straight through, with the Iliad nearby if possible.","line":{"text":"The true hero, the true subject, the center of the Iliad is force.","where":"Opening sentence","tr":"McCarthy"},"islands":[],"related":["arendt-human-condition","camus-myth-of-sisyphus"],"shelf":"phenomenology","tags":["force","violence","war","power","suffering","compassion","literature","ethics"]},{"id":"camus-the-stranger","title":"The Stranger","original":"L'Étranger (French)","author":"camus","authorName":"Albert Camus","year":1942,"approx":false,"form":"novel","length":"about 120 pages in English","level":1,"topics":["life and death","self","ethics"],"summary":"Meursault, a clerk in Algiers, reacts to his mother's death with apparent indifference, drifts into a relationship and some friendships, and then shoots an Arab man on a beach almost without reason. At his trial he is judged as much for not crying at his mother's funeral as for the killing. In his cell awaiting execution, he comes to accept the 'gentle indifference of the world'.","why":"It is the fictional companion to The Myth of Sisyphus and one of the most widely read novels of the twentieth century. Later readers have also questioned its treatment of the nameless Arab victim and of colonial Algeria.","inside":"Two parts: the first leads up to the killing, the second covers the trial and Meursault's final night.","start":"Read it straight through, then The Myth of Sisyphus.","line":{"text":"Maman died today. Or yesterday maybe, I don't know.","where":"Opening lines","tr":"Ward"},"islands":[],"related":["camus-myth-of-sisyphus","sartre-existentialism-is-a-humanism","dostoevsky-notes-from-underground"],"shelf":"phenomenology","tags":["absurdism","the absurd","indifference of the universe","existentialism","alienation","death","authenticity","death penalty","meaning of life"]},{"id":"merleau-ponty-phenomenology-of-perception","title":"Phenomenology of Perception","original":"Phénoménologie de la perception (French)","author":null,"authorName":"Maurice Merleau-Ponty","year":1945,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 500 pages in English","level":3,"topics":["mind","self","knowledge"],"summary":"Merleau-Ponty argues that perception is not a mind receiving data from a body, but the activity of a living body already engaged with the world. Our bodies 'know' how to reach, walk and see in ways that come before conscious thought. He draws on psychology and neurology, such as phantom limbs and the case of a brain-injured patient, to show the limits of both empiricism and intellectualism.","why":"It is the classic account of embodiment, and it has strongly influenced feminist philosophy, cognitive science and the philosophy of mind.","inside":"A famous preface on what phenomenology is, an introduction against 'traditional prejudices', then three parts: the body, the perceived world, and being-for-itself and being-in-the-world.","start":"Read the preface, then the chapter on the body as object and mechanistic physiology, which discusses phantom limbs.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["husserl-cartesian-meditations","heidegger-being-and-time","beauvoir-second-sex"],"shelf":"phenomenology","tags":["phenomenology","embodiment","perception","the lived body","phantom limbs","embodied cognition","philosophy of mind","being-in-the-world","critique of empiricism"]},{"id":"tanabe-philosophy-as-metanoetics","title":"Philosophy as Metanoetics","original":"Zangedō to shite no tetsugaku (Japanese)","author":null,"authorName":"Tanabe Hajime","year":1946,"approx":false,"form":"treatise","length":"about 300 pages in English","level":3,"topics":["religion","self"],"summary":"Written as Japan faced defeat in the Second World War, it begins with Tanabe's confession that philosophy, including his own, had failed. He argues that reason cannot ground itself and must pass through repentance (zange), giving itself up to an 'Other-power' that returns it renewed. He draws on the Pure Land Buddhism of Shinran, as well as Kant, Hegel and Kierkegaard.","why":"It is a major work of the Kyoto School and a rare example of a philosopher publicly reckoning with his own failures in wartime.","inside":"A preface explaining its origins, followed by chapters moving from the idea of metanoetics through critiques of Kant, Hegel and Nishida to Shinran's thought.","start":"Read the preface.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["nishida-inquiry-into-the-good","kierkegaard-sickness-unto-death","nishitani-religion-and-nothingness"],"shelf":"asia-20","tags":["metanoetics","repentance","other-power","pure land buddhism","kyoto school","japanese philosophy","limits of reason","buddhism","self-surrender"]},{"id":"horkheimer-adorno-dialectic-of-enlightenment","title":"Dialectic of Enlightenment","original":"Dialektik der Aufklärung (German)","author":"horkheimer","authorName":"Max Horkheimer and Theodor W. Adorno","year":1947,"approx":false,"form":"book","length":"about 250 pages in English","level":3,"topics":["society","knowledge","beauty"],"summary":"Written in American exile during the Second World War, it asks why humanity, instead of entering a truly human condition, was sinking into a new kind of barbarism. The authors argue that Enlightenment reason, in seeking to master nature, turned into a tool of domination over people too, and so reverts to a new form of myth. They apply this to Homer's Odysseus, the Marquis de Sade, Hollywood's 'culture industry' and antisemitism.","why":"It is the central text of the Frankfurt School's critical theory, and its chapter on the culture industry shaped how people think about mass media and entertainment.","inside":"An essay on the concept of enlightenment, two excursuses (on Odysseus and on Juliette, or morality), chapters on the culture industry and on antisemitism, and closing notes and sketches.","start":"Read 'The Culture Industry: Enlightenment as Mass Deception'.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["kant-what-is-enlightenment","marx-capital","arendt-origins-of-totalitarianism"],"shelf":"society-20","tags":["critical theory","frankfurt school","culture industry","instrumental reason","critique of enlightenment","domination","myth","antisemitism","mass culture"]},{"id":"quine-on-what-there-is","title":"On What There Is","original":null,"author":"quine","authorName":"W. V. O. Quine","year":1948,"approx":false,"form":"paper","length":"about 20 pages","level":2,"topics":["metaphysics","language","logic"],"summary":"Quine asks how we can deny that something exists, such as Pegasus, without seeming to refer to it. Using Russell's theory of descriptions, he shows that names can be paraphrased away, so we need not believe in non-existent objects. His test for what a theory says exists is the things its variables must range over.","why":"It set the agenda for much of analytic metaphysics, and its slogan 'to be is to be the value of a variable' is one of the most quoted lines in twentieth-century philosophy.","inside":"A single paper, framed as a debate with two imaginary philosophers, McX and Wyman, who believe in more things than Quine does.","start":"Read it straight through; the opening pages are witty and clear.","line":{"text":"A curious thing about the ontological problem is its simplicity. It can be put in three Anglo-Saxon monosyllables: 'What is there?'","where":"Opening sentences","tr":null},"islands":[],"related":["quine-two-dogmas","russell-on-denoting","plato-republic"],"shelf":"language-science","tags":["ontology","ontological commitment","to be is to be the value of a variable","plato's beard","existence","theory of descriptions","nominalism","metaphysics","philosophy of language"]},{"id":"cesaire-discourse-on-colonialism","title":"Discourse on Colonialism","original":"Discours sur le colonialisme (French)","author":null,"authorName":"Aimé Césaire","year":1950,"approx":false,"form":"essay","length":"about 50 pages in English","level":1,"topics":["politics","society"],"summary":"The Martinican poet and politician attacks the claim that colonialism brought civilisation. He argues that colonisation degrades the coloniser as well as the colonised, turning people into instruments and objects. Most provocatively, he says that what Europe could not forgive Hitler was applying to Europeans the methods it had used on colonised peoples.","why":"It is a founding text of anti-colonial thought, written by a creator of the Négritude movement and a teacher of Frantz Fanon.","inside":"A single, fiery essay, first published in 1950 and revised in 1955.","start":"Read it straight through.","line":{"text":"colonization = \"thingification.\"","where":"Middle of the essay","tr":"Pinkham"},"islands":[],"related":["fanon-wretched-of-the-earth","fanon-black-skin-white-masks","du-bois-darkwater"],"shelf":"global","tags":["colonialism","anti-colonialism","racism","critique of european civilisation","negritude","dehumanisation","postcolonialism","political philosophy"]},{"id":"arendt-origins-of-totalitarianism","title":"The Origins of Totalitarianism","original":null,"author":"arendt","authorName":"Hannah Arendt","year":1951,"approx":false,"form":"book","length":"about 500 pages","level":3,"topics":["politics","society"],"summary":"Arendt traces how Nazism and Stalinism, a new form of government based on ideology and terror, could arise in modern Europe. She follows the roots of this through modern antisemitism and nineteenth-century imperialism, then analyses movements, propaganda, secret police and the camps. Totalitarianism, she argues, seeks to make human beings superfluous and feeds on mass loneliness.","why":"It established Arendt as a major political thinker and remains one of the most important attempts to understand the catastrophes of the twentieth century.","inside":"Three parts: Antisemitism, Imperialism and Totalitarianism. The closing chapter, 'Ideology and Terror', was added in 1958.","start":"Read 'Ideology and Terror', then the chapter on the decline of the nation-state and the end of the rights of man.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["arendt-human-condition","arendt-eichmann-in-jerusalem","popper-open-society"],"shelf":"justice","tags":["totalitarianism","nazism","stalinism","antisemitism","imperialism","ideology and terror","loneliness","propaganda","statelessness","political philosophy"]},{"id":"fanon-black-skin-white-masks","title":"Black Skin, White Masks","original":"Peau noire, masques blancs (French)","author":"fanon","authorName":"Frantz Fanon","year":1952,"approx":false,"form":"book","length":"about 200 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["society","self","mind"],"summary":"Fanon, a psychiatrist from Martinique, examines how colonial racism shapes the inner lives of Black people. He looks at language, love relationships and psychology, and describes the shock of being 'fixed' by a white gaze that turns him into an object. He refuses to accept either white definitions of him or a simple celebration of blackness, and ends by demanding to be recognised as a free human being.","why":"It is a founding work of postcolonial theory and critical philosophy of race, combining phenomenology, psychoanalysis and personal experience.","inside":"An introduction and eight chapters; chapter 5, translated as 'The Fact of Blackness' or 'The Lived Experience of the Black Man', is the best known.","start":"Read chapter 5, then the conclusion.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["fanon-wretched-of-the-earth","cesaire-discourse-on-colonialism","du-bois-souls-of-black-folk"],"shelf":"global","tags":["racism","colonialism","the white gaze","black identity","recognition","psychology of oppression","language","postcolonialism","philosophy of race","existentialism"]},{"id":"nishitani-religion-and-nothingness","title":"Religion and Nothingness","original":"Shūkyō to wa nani ka (Japanese)","author":null,"authorName":"Nishitani Keiji","year":1961,"approx":false,"form":"book","length":"about 300 pages in English","level":3,"topics":["religion","metaphysics","self"],"summary":"Nishitani, a student of Nishida and of Heidegger, asks what religion is in an age of nihilism, when science and modern life seem to empty the world of meaning. He argues that nihilism must be lived through and gone beyond, not avoided, by reaching the Buddhist standpoint of emptiness (śūnyatā), in which things and the self appear as they truly are. He draws on Zen, Meister Eckhart, Nietzsche and Heidegger.","why":"It is the best-known work of the later Kyoto School and a key text in dialogue between Buddhist and Western philosophy. Its Japanese title means 'What Is Religion?'","inside":"Six chapters, beginning with 'What Is Religion?' and moving through nihilism and emptiness to time and history.","start":"Read chapter 1, 'What Is Religion?'.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["nishida-inquiry-into-the-good","tanabe-philosophy-as-metanoetics","nietzsche-gay-science"],"shelf":"global","tags":["nihilism","emptiness","kyoto school","zen","buddhism","japanese philosophy","philosophy of religion","nothingness","the self"]},{"id":"austin-how-to-do-things-with-words","title":"How to Do Things with Words","original":null,"author":null,"authorName":"J. L. Austin","year":1962,"approx":false,"form":"lectures","length":"about 170 pages","level":2,"topics":["language"],"summary":"Austin noticed that many sentences do not describe anything but do something: saying 'I do' at a wedding, 'I promise' or 'I name this ship' performs an act. Starting from these 'performatives', he develops a general theory of speech acts, separating what we say, what we do in saying it (warning, ordering, promising), and what effects we cause by saying it.","why":"It founded speech act theory, which transformed philosophy of language and linguistics and later influenced law and gender theory.","inside":"Twelve lectures, given at Harvard in 1955 and published after Austin's death, building step by step from performatives to the general theory of speech acts.","start":"Read Lectures 1 and 2, then skim to Lecture 8.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["wittgenstein-philosophical-investigations","ryle-concept-of-mind","frege-sense-reference"],"shelf":"language-science","tags":["speech acts","performatives","illocutionary acts","philosophy of language","ordinary language philosophy","promising","meaning","pragmatics"]},{"id":"freire-pedagogy-of-the-oppressed","title":"Pedagogy of the Oppressed","original":"Pedagogia do oprimido (Portuguese)","author":null,"authorName":"Paulo Freire","year":1968,"approx":false,"form":"book","length":"about 180 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["society","politics","knowledge"],"summary":"Freire, a Brazilian educator who taught poor adults to read, attacks the 'banking' model of education, in which teachers deposit facts into passive students. He proposes instead a 'problem-posing' education based on dialogue, in which learners name and question their own world. The goal is critical consciousness, through which oppressed people come to understand and transform their situation.","why":"It is one of the most cited books in the history of education and is read worldwide in teaching, social work and community organising.","inside":"Four chapters: the justification for a pedagogy of the oppressed, the banking concept, dialogue, and theories of action. Written in exile in Chile in 1968; first published in English in 1970.","start":"Read chapter 2, on the banking concept of education.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["dewey-democracy-and-education","fanon-wretched-of-the-earth","rousseau-emile"],"shelf":"global","tags":["philosophy of education","critical pedagogy","banking model of education","critical consciousness","oppression","dialogue","liberation","praxis","literacy"]},{"id":"mbiti-african-religions-and-philosophy","title":"African Religions and Philosophy","original":null,"author":"mbiti","authorName":"John S. Mbiti","year":1969,"approx":false,"form":"book","length":"about 300 pages","level":2,"topics":["religion","society","self"],"summary":"Mbiti, a Kenyan theologian, surveys religious beliefs and practices across many African peoples, including ideas of God, spirits, the living dead, birth, marriage and death. He argues that traditional African life is deeply religious and communal, so that a person exists through their relations with others. He also makes a much-debated claim that many African languages treat time as a long past and a short future.","why":"It was one of the first major works to present African religions as serious systems of thought, and its line about community is one of the best-known statements of African communal philosophy, though critics such as Hountondji questioned its method.","inside":"Its chapters move from time and God through the human life cycle to change and modern religions in Africa.","start":"Read the chapters on the concept of time and on kinship and the individual.","line":{"text":"I am, because we are; and since we are, therefore I am.","where":"Discussion of the individual and the community","tr":null},"islands":[],"related":["wiredu-philosophy-and-an-african-culture","hountondji-african-philosophy-myth-and-reality"],"shelf":"global","tags":["african philosophy","african religion","communalism","i am because we are","concept of time","ancestors","god","personhood","death"]},{"id":"wittgenstein-on-certainty","title":"On Certainty","original":"Über Gewißheit (German)","author":"wittgenstein","authorName":"Ludwig Wittgenstein","year":1969,"approx":false,"form":"notes","length":"about 90 pages","level":2,"topics":["knowledge","language"],"summary":"Written in the last eighteen months of Wittgenstein's life and published after his death, these notes respond to G. E. Moore's claims to know things like 'here is a hand'. Wittgenstein argues that such certainties are not pieces of knowledge we have proved, but the 'hinges' on which our questions and doubts turn. Doubt itself only makes sense against a background that is not doubted.","why":"It offers one of the most original answers to scepticism and has inspired a whole field of 'hinge epistemology'.","inside":"676 numbered remarks, written in several stages; they read like a working notebook.","start":"Read remarks 1 to 65, then dip in at random.","line":{"text":"If you do know that here is one hand, we'll grant you all the rest.","where":"§1","tr":"Paul and Anscombe"},"islands":[],"related":["moore-defence-of-common-sense","wittgenstein-philosophical-investigations","descartes-meditations"],"shelf":"cambridge-oxford","tags":["certainty","hinge propositions","skepticism","here is a hand","doubt","epistemology","language games","common sense","forms of life"]},{"id":"nagel-the-absurd","title":"The Absurd","original":null,"author":"nagel","authorName":"Thomas Nagel","year":1971,"approx":false,"form":"paper","length":"about 15 pages","level":1,"topics":["life and death","self"],"summary":"Nagel rejects common arguments that life is absurd because it is short or small in the cosmos. The real source of absurdity, he argues, is that we take our lives seriously while also being able to step back and see everything we care about as arbitrary. This clash is built into self-conscious creatures, and the right response is not despair or defiance but irony.","why":"It is one of the clearest and most-assigned papers on the meaning of life, and it questions Camus's heroic response to the absurd.","inside":"A short paper in numbered sections: bad arguments, the real source of absurdity, possible escapes, and the right response.","start":"Read it straight through.","line":{"text":"If sub specie aeternitatis there is no reason to believe that anything matters, then that does not matter either","where":"Final paragraph","tr":null},"islands":[],"related":["camus-myth-of-sisyphus","nagel-what-is-it-like-to-be-a-bat","tolstoy-death-of-ivan-ilyich"],"shelf":"good-life","tags":["the absurd","meaning of life","absurdism","irony","self-consciousness","view from nowhere","existentialism","death"]},{"id":"hountondji-african-philosophy-myth-and-reality","title":"African Philosophy: Myth and Reality","original":"Sur la « philosophie africaine » (French)","author":null,"authorName":"Paulin Hountondji","year":1976,"approx":false,"form":"book","length":"about 200 pages in English","level":2,"topics":["knowledge","society"],"summary":"The Beninese philosopher criticises 'ethnophilosophy', the practice, begun by Placide Tempels's Bantu Philosophy, of describing an unspoken worldview supposedly shared by a whole African people. Hountondji argues that this treats Africans as having only collective beliefs, not individual critical thinkers. African philosophy, he says, should be the open, argued literature that African philosophers write.","why":"It sparked one of the central debates in modern African philosophy, about what African philosophy is and who makes it.","inside":"A collection of essays first published in French in 1976 and in English in 1983, including studies of Tempels, Kwame Nkrumah and Anton Wilhelm Amo.","start":"Read the opening essay on 'An Alienated Literature'.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["mbiti-african-religions-and-philosophy","wiredu-philosophy-and-an-african-culture","amo-impassivity-of-the-human-mind"],"shelf":"global","tags":["african philosophy","ethnophilosophy","critique of ethnophilosophy","unanimism","metaphilosophy","postcolonialism","critical thinking","science"]},{"id":"williams-moral-luck","title":"Moral Luck","original":null,"author":"williams","authorName":"Bernard Williams","year":1976,"approx":false,"form":"paper","length":"about 25 pages","level":2,"topics":["ethics","self"],"summary":"Williams challenges the idea, central to Kant, that moral worth is immune to luck. His examples include a painter like Gauguin, who abandons his family to paint, and whose choice may be justified only if, by luck, he succeeds, and a lorry driver who, through no fault of his own, runs over a child and feels a special 'agent-regret'. Luck, he argues, reaches deep into how we judge ourselves and others.","why":"It opened a major debate in ethics, together with Thomas Nagel's reply of the same name, and it is a classic of Williams's challenge to tidy moral theories.","inside":"A single paper, first given at a symposium in 1976 and reprinted as the title essay of Williams's 1981 collection.","start":"Read it straight through, then Nagel's paper of the same name.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["williams-utilitarianism-for-and-against","kant-groundwork","nussbaum-fragility-of-goodness"],"shelf":"good-life","tags":["moral luck","agent-regret","gauguin","critique of kant","moral responsibility","luck","ethics","blame"]},{"id":"midgley-beast-and-man","title":"Beast and Man: The Roots of Human Nature","original":null,"author":null,"authorName":"Mary Midgley","year":1978,"approx":false,"form":"book","length":"about 380 pages","level":1,"topics":["science","ethics","self"],"summary":"Midgley argues that humans are animals and that philosophy must take our evolved nature seriously. She attacks both crude biology that reduces us to selfish genes and philosophies that treat us as blank slates or pure free choices. Studying other animals, she argues, helps us understand human motives, and our animal nature is not something to be ashamed of.","why":"It was one of the first serious philosophical engagements with ethology and sociobiology, written in a wise, witty and jargon-free style that made Midgley a public philosopher.","inside":"Its parts move from the problem of human nature and the use of comparisons with other animals to instinct, reason and what is distinctive about humans.","start":"Read the introduction and the first part.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["darwin-origin-of-species","kropotkin-mutual-aid","singer-animal-liberation"],"shelf":"mind","tags":["human nature","animals","evolution","critique of reductionism","instinct","ethology","sociobiology","selfish gene","philosophy of biology"]},{"id":"singer-practical-ethics","title":"Practical Ethics","original":null,"author":"singer","authorName":"Peter Singer","year":1979,"approx":false,"form":"book","length":"about 350 pages","level":1,"topics":["ethics","life and death"],"summary":"Singer applies a utilitarian principle of equal consideration of interests to real moral problems. He discusses racial and sexual equality, the treatment of animals, abortion, euthanasia, global poverty, refugees and the environment, reaching conclusions that many readers find challenging. Each chapter sets out arguments clearly so readers can test them.","why":"It became one of the most widely used textbooks in applied ethics and remains one of the most debated, including protests over its views on disability and euthanasia.","inside":"Revised in 1993 and 2011; about a dozen chapters, beginning with 'About Ethics' and equality, then moving to specific issues.","start":"Read chapter 1 and the chapter on rich and poor.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["singer-famine-affluence-morality","singer-animal-liberation","mill-utilitarianism"],"shelf":"right-wrong","tags":["applied ethics","utilitarianism","equal consideration of interests","animal rights","abortion","euthanasia","global poverty","environmental ethics","refugees","equality"]},{"id":"davis-women-race-and-class","title":"Women, Race and Class","original":null,"author":"davis_angela","authorName":"Angela Y. Davis","year":1981,"approx":false,"form":"book","length":"about 250 pages","level":1,"topics":["society","politics"],"summary":"Davis traces the history of the women's movement in the United States from slavery onwards, showing how racism and class divided it. She examines Black women's labour under slavery, the split between suffragists and abolitionists, the myth of the Black rapist, birth control and sterilisation abuse, and the economics of housework.","why":"It is a classic of Black feminist and Marxist thought and an early, influential account of how race, gender and class must be understood together.","inside":"Thirteen chapters in roughly historical order, from the legacy of slavery to the approaching obsolescence of housework.","start":"Read the first chapter, on the legacy of slavery.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["cooper-voice-from-the-south","beauvoir-second-sex","lorde-sister-outsider"],"shelf":"critical","tags":["black feminism","intersectionality","racism","class","slavery","feminism","women's suffrage","reproductive rights","housework","marxism"]},{"id":"gilligan-in-a-different-voice","title":"In a Different Voice","original":null,"author":null,"authorName":"Carol Gilligan","year":1982,"approx":false,"form":"book","length":"about 200 pages","level":1,"topics":["ethics","self"],"summary":"Gilligan, a psychologist, argues that leading theories of moral development, especially Lawrence Kohlberg's, were built mainly from studies of boys and men. Listening to women and girls, she finds a different moral 'voice' that focuses on care, relationships and responsibility, rather than on abstract rules and rights. She contrasts how two children, Jake and Amy, answer the same moral dilemma.","why":"It launched the 'ethics of care', which philosophers such as Nel Noddings developed into a major ethical theory.","inside":"Six chapters, drawing on interview studies including one of women deciding whether to have an abortion.","start":"Read chapter 2, which contains the Jake and Amy example.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["noddings-caring","kant-groundwork","rawls-theory-of-justice"],"shelf":"good-life","tags":["ethics of care","moral development","feminist ethics","care vs justice","moral psychology","gender","relationships","kohlberg"]},{"id":"lorde-sister-outsider","title":"Sister Outsider","original":null,"author":null,"authorName":"Audre Lorde","year":1984,"approx":false,"form":"essays","length":"about 190 pages","level":1,"topics":["society","self","politics"],"summary":"In essays and speeches, Lorde, a Black lesbian poet, writes about racism, sexism, homophobia and class, and about how differences between women can be a source of strength rather than division. She argues that poetry and feeling are forms of knowledge, that anger can be used productively, and that silence will not protect us.","why":"It is one of the most influential works of Black feminist and queer thought, and several of its essay titles have become well-known phrases.","inside":"Fifteen essays and speeches, including 'Poetry Is Not a Luxury', 'The Transformation of Silence into Language and Action', 'The Master's Tools Will Never Dismantle the Master's House' and 'The Uses of Anger'.","start":"Read 'The Transformation of Silence into Language and Action' and 'The Master's Tools'.","line":{"text":"For the master's tools will never dismantle the master's house.","where":"The Master's Tools Will Never Dismantle the Master's House","tr":null},"islands":[],"related":["davis-women-race-and-class","beauvoir-second-sex","butler-gender-trouble"],"shelf":"critical","tags":["black feminism","intersectionality","the master's tools","anger","silence","difference","queer theory","poetry","racism","feminism"]},{"id":"nussbaum-fragility-of-goodness","title":"The Fragility of Goodness","original":null,"author":"nussbaum","authorName":"Martha C. Nussbaum","year":1986,"approx":false,"form":"book","length":"about 500 pages","level":3,"topics":["ethics","beauty","life and death"],"summary":"Nussbaum asks how far a good human life depends on luck: on things we cannot control, such as other people, our bodies and political events. She reads Greek tragedies alongside Plato and Aristotle, arguing that tragedy shows how valuable things can conflict and be lost, and that Plato's attempt to make the good life invulnerable gives up too much. She defends Aristotle's view that a vulnerable, human-scale goodness is worth having.","why":"It helped bring literature back into moral philosophy and revived interest in Greek ethics and in the role of luck in a good life.","inside":"Three parts: on tragedy, on Plato (including the Symposium and Phaedrus), and on Aristotle, with an interlude on tragedy and philosophy.","start":"Read the first chapter, then the chapter on Plato's Symposium.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["aristotle-nicomachean-ethics","plato-symposium","williams-moral-luck"],"shelf":"good-life","tags":["moral luck","tragedy","vulnerability","eudaimonia","aristotelianism","greek tragedy","the good life","virtue ethics","conflicting values","love"]},{"id":"appiah-in-my-fathers-house","title":"In My Father's House","original":null,"author":null,"authorName":"Kwame Anthony Appiah","year":1992,"approx":false,"form":"book","length":"about 250 pages","level":2,"topics":["society","self"],"summary":"Appiah, a Ghanaian-British philosopher, argues that there are no biological races in the sense earlier thinkers assumed, and criticises even anti-racist thinkers such as Du Bois for keeping the idea. He questions the idea of a single African identity or worldview and examines African literature, ethnophilosophy and modernity, drawing on his own family in Ghana and England.","why":"It is a central work in the philosophy of race and of African identity, and it launched Appiah's influential career as a public philosopher.","inside":"Nine essays plus an epilogue describing his father's funeral in Kumasi.","start":"Read the first two chapters, on race and on Du Bois.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["du-bois-conservation-of-races","hountondji-african-philosophy-myth-and-reality","appiah-cosmopolitanism"],"shelf":"global","tags":["philosophy of race","race","african identity","pan-africanism","ethnophilosophy","african philosophy","identity","modernity","critique of du bois"]},{"id":"rawls-political-liberalism","title":"Political Liberalism","original":null,"author":"rawls","authorName":"John Rawls","year":1993,"approx":false,"form":"book","length":"about 450 pages","level":3,"topics":["politics"],"summary":"Rawls asks how a society can be stable and just when its citizens hold deeply different religious, moral and philosophical views that reasonable people will never agree on. His answer is a 'political' conception of justice that people can endorse from within their own different worldviews, an 'overlapping consensus', and a duty to argue about basic political questions in terms of 'public reason' that all can accept.","why":"It reshaped Rawls's theory of justice for pluralistic societies and set the terms for decades of debate about religion, reason and politics.","inside":"Three parts, on basic elements, main ideas and the institutional framework; later editions add 'The Idea of Public Reason Revisited'.","start":"Read the introduction and Lecture 4, on the idea of an overlapping consensus.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["rawls-theory-of-justice","locke-letter-concerning-toleration","nozick-anarchy-state-and-utopia"],"shelf":"justice","tags":["political liberalism","overlapping consensus","public reason","reasonable pluralism","liberalism","justice","legitimacy","political philosophy","stability"]},{"id":"mills-racial-contract","title":"The Racial Contract","original":null,"author":null,"authorName":"Charles W. Mills","year":1997,"approx":false,"form":"book","length":"about 150 pages","level":1,"topics":["politics","society"],"summary":"Mills, a Jamaican-born philosopher, rereads the social contract tradition of Hobbes, Locke, Rousseau and Kant. He argues that the real contract underlying modern Western states was a 'Racial Contract' among white people, which treated non-white peoples as less than full persons and justified colonialism and slavery. Mainstream political philosophy, he says, has ignored this.","why":"It is a short, provocative classic of critical philosophy of race and a powerful challenge to how political philosophy is usually taught.","inside":"An introduction and three chapters: overview, details, and the 'naturalised' merits of the idea.","start":"Read the introduction and the first chapter.","line":{"text":"White supremacy is the unnamed political system that has made the modern world what it is today.","where":"Opening sentence","tr":null},"islands":[],"related":["locke-two-treatises","hobbes-leviathan","rawls-theory-of-justice"],"shelf":"critical","tags":["racial contract","social contract","racism","white supremacy","colonialism","slavery","philosophy of race","critique of liberalism","political philosophy"]},{"id":"clark-chalmers-extended-mind","title":"The Extended Mind","original":null,"author":"chalmers","authorName":"Andy Clark and David Chalmers","year":1998,"approx":false,"form":"paper","length":"about 12 pages","level":1,"topics":["mind"],"summary":"Clark and Chalmers ask where the mind stops and the rest of the world begins. They compare Inga, who remembers the way to a museum, with Otto, who has Alzheimer's disease and relies on a notebook he always carries. If the notebook plays the same role for Otto as memory does for Inga, they argue, it is part of his mind.","why":"It is one of the most discussed papers in recent philosophy of mind, and it has taken on new life with smartphones and AI tools.","inside":"A short paper: the opening question, examples of active externalism, the Otto and Inga case, and replies to objections.","start":"Read it straight through.","line":{"text":"Where does the mind stop and the rest of the world begin?","where":"Opening sentence","tr":null},"islands":[],"related":["chalmers-conscious-mind","descartes-meditations","turing-computing-machinery-and-intelligence"],"shelf":"mind","tags":["extended mind","otto's notebook","active externalism","cognitive science","philosophy of mind","memory","thought experiment","embodied cognition"]},{"id":"macintyre-dependent-rational-animals","title":"Dependent Rational Animals","original":null,"author":"macintyre","authorName":"Alasdair MacIntyre","year":1999,"approx":false,"form":"book","length":"about 170 pages","level":2,"topics":["ethics","society"],"summary":"MacIntyre argues that moral philosophy has wrongly pictured humans as independent, fully rational adults. In fact we are animals who are vulnerable to illness, injury and old age, and who depend on others throughout our lives. He develops 'virtues of acknowledged dependence', including just generosity, that a flourishing community needs.","why":"It revised MacIntyre's earlier work by bringing in biology and disability, and it is an important bridge between virtue ethics and the ethics of care.","inside":"Short chapters, first given as lectures, comparing humans with dolphins and other intelligent animals before turning to virtues and communities.","start":"Read chapter 1.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["macintyre-after-virtue","noddings-caring","aristotle-nicomachean-ethics"],"shelf":"good-life","tags":["dependence","vulnerability","virtue ethics","aristotelianism","disability","animals","community","just generosity","human nature"]},{"id":"foot-natural-goodness","title":"Natural Goodness","original":null,"author":"foot","authorName":"Philippa Foot","year":2001,"approx":false,"form":"book","length":"about 120 pages","level":2,"topics":["ethics"],"summary":"Foot argues that moral goodness is a kind of natural goodness, like the goodness of a tree's deep roots or a wolf's cooperation in hunting. We judge living things good or defective by what their kind needs to flourish, and humans need virtues such as justice, courage and honesty. On this view moral judgements are factual, not mere expressions of feeling.","why":"It is a key text of 'neo-Aristotelian' ethical naturalism, a short and lucid challenge to the idea that facts and values must be kept apart.","inside":"Short chapters moving from a critique of non-cognitivism to natural normativity, practical rationality and happiness.","start":"Read the introduction and chapter 2.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["anscombe-modern-moral-philosophy","aristotle-nicomachean-ethics","foot-problem-of-abortion"],"shelf":"good-life","tags":["natural goodness","virtue ethics","neo-aristotelianism","naturalism","critique of non-cognitivism","metaethics","flourishing","practical rationality","happiness"]},{"id":"bostrom-simulation-argument","title":"Are You Living in a Computer Simulation?","original":null,"author":"bostrom","authorName":"Nick Bostrom","year":2003,"approx":false,"form":"paper","length":"about 15 pages","level":2,"topics":["metaphysics","knowledge"],"summary":"Bostrom argues that at least one of three claims is true: almost all civilisations like ours die out before they can run detailed simulations of their ancestors; almost none of those that could are interested in doing so; or we are almost certainly living in a computer simulation. He does not say which is true, but argues we cannot easily dismiss the third.","why":"It turned an old sceptical worry, like Descartes's dream or demon, into a probabilistic argument linked to technology, and it is widely discussed inside and outside philosophy.","inside":"A short paper: the technological assumptions, the core probability argument, and an interpretation of the three possibilities.","start":"Read it straight through, skipping the formula on first reading.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["descartes-meditations","plato-republic"],"shelf":"language-science","tags":["simulation argument","simulation hypothesis","skepticism","posthumanism","probability","metaphysics","artificial intelligence","brain in a vat","future of humanity"]},{"id":"appiah-cosmopolitanism","title":"Cosmopolitanism: Ethics in a World of Strangers","original":null,"author":null,"authorName":"Kwame Anthony Appiah","year":2006,"approx":false,"form":"book","length":"about 200 pages","level":1,"topics":["ethics","society"],"summary":"Appiah defends a 'rooted cosmopolitanism' in which we owe obligations to all human beings while respecting the real differences between ways of life. He argues against both moral relativism and the belief that one culture has all the answers, and says that people can live together through conversation and shared practices without first agreeing on values.","why":"It is one of the best-known recent works of public philosophy, a readable guide to ethics in a globalised world.","inside":"Short chapters full of stories from Ghana, where Appiah grew up, and elsewhere, ending with a chapter on kindness to strangers.","start":"Read the introduction and the chapter on 'The Primacy of Practice'.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["appiah-in-my-fathers-house","singer-famine-affluence-morality","kant-perpetual-peace"],"shelf":"global","tags":["cosmopolitanism","global ethics","moral relativism","pluralism","conversation","identity","strangers","culture"]},{"id":"fricker-epistemic-injustice","title":"Epistemic Injustice","original":null,"author":null,"authorName":"Miranda Fricker","year":2007,"approx":false,"form":"book","length":"about 180 pages","level":2,"topics":["knowledge","ethics","society"],"summary":"Fricker identifies a kind of injustice done to people as knowers. 'Testimonial injustice' happens when prejudice makes a listener give a speaker less credibility, as when Tom Robinson's testimony is disbelieved in To Kill a Mockingbird. 'Hermeneutical injustice' happens when a group lacks the shared concepts to make sense of their experience, as women once did before the term 'sexual harassment' existed.","why":"It created a new field linking ethics and the theory of knowledge, and its concepts are now used widely in law, medicine and social science.","inside":"Seven chapters, the first five on testimonial injustice and the virtue of testimonial justice, and the last two on hermeneutical injustice.","start":"Read the introduction and chapter 1.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["gettier-justified-true-belief","beauvoir-second-sex"],"shelf":"critical","tags":["epistemic injustice","testimonial injustice","hermeneutical injustice","testimony","prejudice","social epistemology","feminist epistemology","epistemology","credibility"]},{"id":"parfit-on-what-matters","title":"On What Matters","original":null,"author":"parfit","authorName":"Derek Parfit","year":2011,"approx":false,"form":"book","length":"two volumes, about 1,400 pages (a third volume followed in 2017)","level":3,"topics":["ethics"],"summary":"Parfit argues that there are objective truths about what we have reason to do and that ethics is not just a matter of desire or culture. He then argues that the leading moral theories, Kantian ethics, contractualism and consequentialism, when properly revised, converge on the same 'Triple Theory'. In his image, their followers are climbing the same mountain on different sides.","why":"It is the major late work of one of the most influential moral philosophers of the past century, and it provoked replies from many leading ethicists, some printed in the second volume.","inside":"Volume one covers reasons and Kant's ethics; volume two contains commentaries by other philosophers, Parfit's replies, and his defence of moral realism.","start":"Read the summary at the start of volume one and the chapters on Kant's formula of universal law.","line":null,"islands":[],"related":["parfit-reasons-and-persons","kant-groundwork","korsgaard-sources-of-normativity"],"shelf":"right-wrong","tags":["moral realism","metaethics","triple theory","kantian ethics","contractualism","consequentialism","reasons","climbing the same mountain","objective values"]}];
